/* SQE1 January 2027 — consolidated bank */
QUESTIONS.push(...[{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0368","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Conveyancing — exchange by formula","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two solicitors hold their respective clients' signed residential contracts and agree to exchange by telephone using a recognised Law Society formula, with completion fixed for a later date.\n\nWhat is the key legal consequence of a valid exchange?","options":{"A":"Legal title automatically passes to the buyer during the telephone call.","B":"Exchange has no legal effect until completion monies are paid.","C":"The parties become contractually bound on the agreed sale terms, subject to the contract, even though legal title transfers only on completion and registration.","D":"The buyer becomes registered proprietor immediately.","E":"Either party remains free to withdraw without contractual consequences until registration."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Exchange creates the binding sale contract. Completion later implements the transaction by transfer and payment, with registration required to complete transfer of a registered legal estate.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Correct. Exchange creates the binding sale contract. Completion later implements the transaction by transfer and payment, with registration required to complete transfer of a registered legal estate.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1989, s 2; Law Society exchange formulae and conveyancing practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Valid exchange creates contractual commitment before legal title passes.","common_trap":"Confusing contractual exchange with completion and registration of legal title.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7866f7c823c933c2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0369","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Theft — appropriation with consent","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An elderly owner voluntarily gives a valuable item to a defendant because of the defendant's dishonest manipulation. The defendant argues there can be no appropriation because the owner consented to the transfer.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the Theft Act concept of appropriation?","options":{"A":"Appropriation is impossible whenever the owner consents.","B":"Theft always requires a physical snatching of property.","C":"A valid civil-law gift necessarily prevents theft in every circumstance.","D":"An appropriation can occur even where the owner consents to the transfer; consent does not by itself prevent an assumption of the owner's rights.","E":"Appropriation requires violence."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The House of Lords has held that appropriation can include an assumption of an owner's rights even with the owner's consent. The remaining elements of theft, including dishonesty and intention permanently to deprive, must still be proved.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Correct. The House of Lords has held that appropriation can include an assumption of an owner's rights even with the owner's consent. The remaining elements of theft, including dishonesty and intention permanently to deprive, must still be proved.","E":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Theft Act 1968, s 3; R v Hinks [2000] UKHL 53.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Owner consent does not by itself exclude appropriation for theft.","common_trap":"Importing lack of consent into appropriation as a separate essential element.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1f46e22760b03341"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0370","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Bail — exceptions to right to bail","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant before the magistrates' court is within the general statutory right to bail, but there are substantial grounds for believing that, if released, the defendant would fail to surrender. No condition could adequately address that risk.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"The general right to bail means bail can never be refused.","B":"Bail must be refused whenever the prosecution objects.","C":"Only the Crown Court can impose bail conditions.","D":"Risk of failure to surrender is legally irrelevant.","E":"The court may refuse bail where a statutory exception is established, including qualifying substantial grounds concerning failure to surrender, subject to the Bail Act framework."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The Bail Act creates a general right to bail subject to statutory exceptions. For imprisonable offences, substantial grounds for specified risks such as failure to surrender can justify refusal, with the full statutory framework and relevant factors considered.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","B":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","C":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","D":"Incorrect. This option does not apply the controlling rule identified in the rationale and authority to these facts.","E":"Correct. The Bail Act creates a general right to bail subject to statutory exceptions. For imprisonable offences, substantial grounds for specified risks such as failure to surrender can justify refusal, with the full statutory framework and relevant factors considered."},"primary_legal_authority":"Bail Act 1976, ss 4 and Sch 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The general right to bail is qualified by statutory exceptions, including sufficiently established failure-to-surrender risk.","common_trap":"Treating the right to bail as absolute or, conversely, treating prosecution opposition as enough by itself.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2924f12fb7595b93"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0371","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — dividends and distributions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A profitable private company has ample cash in its bank account, but its latest relevant accounts show no accumulated realised profits available for distribution after accumulated realised losses. The directors nevertheless propose a cash dividend because liquidity is strong.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"Cash availability alone is insufficient; a company may make a distribution only out of profits available for the purpose under the statutory capital-maintenance rules.","B":"A company may distribute any cash it possesses.","C":"A unanimous board decision always validates a dividend.","D":"Only insolvency law limits dividends.","E":"A dividend is lawful whenever the company can pay debts on the payment date."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The statutory test for distributions is based on accumulated realised profits less accumulated realised losses, using the relevant accounts; liquidity alone does not create distributable profits.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The statutory test for distributions is based on accumulated realised profits less accumulated realised losses, using the relevant accounts; liquidity alone does not create distributable profits.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 829–830 and Part 23.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A lawful distribution requires profits available for distribution, not merely cash or solvency.","common_trap":"Confusing cash-flow capacity with the Companies Act profits test for distributions.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"273e58410c8fb595"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0372","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Directors — declaration of interest","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A director knows that a company is about to enter a contract with a supplier owned by the director's spouse. The director has an indirect interest in the proposed transaction and the board has not otherwise been informed of it.\n\nWhat statutory duty should the director consider before the company enters the transaction?","options":{"A":"Only the duty to promote success after the contract is completed.","B":"The duty to declare the nature and extent of an interest in a proposed transaction or arrangement with the company.","C":"No disclosure duty can apply to an indirect interest.","D":"The director must automatically resign.","E":"Only shareholders, never directors, need disclose connected-person interests."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 177 requires a director who is directly or indirectly interested in a proposed transaction or arrangement with the company to declare the nature and extent of that interest to the other directors, subject to statutory exceptions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Correct. Section 177 requires a director who is directly or indirectly interested in a proposed transaction or arrangement with the company to declare the nature and extent of that interest to the other directors, subject to statutory exceptions.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, s 177.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A director must disclose a qualifying direct or indirect interest in a proposed company transaction before it is entered.","common_trap":"Assuming indirect interests through connected persons fall outside the declaration regime.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8729875423e89bfb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0373","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — preferences","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An insolvent company repays a debt owed to a director shortly before liquidation. The repayment puts the director in a better position on liquidation than otherwise. The director is a connected person, so the statutory presumption concerning desire is potentially engaged.\n\nWhich additional concept is central to a preference challenge?","options":{"A":"Whether the transaction involved no consideration at all.","B":"Whether the creditor demanded payment forcefully, which always establishes a preference.","C":"Whether the company was influenced by a desire to produce the preferential effect, subject to the statutory presumption applicable to connected persons.","D":"Whether the debt was originally created by deed.","E":"Whether the company had more than one shareholder."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A preference requires the statutory effect and the company to have been influenced by a desire to prefer. For connected persons, the legislation can presume that influence, subject to the statutory framework.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Correct. A preference requires the statutory effect and the company to have been influenced by a desire to prefer. For connected persons, the legislation can presume that influence, subject to the statutory framework.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, s 239.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Preference analysis includes the desire-to-prefer requirement and the connected-person presumption.","common_trap":"Confusing a preference with a transaction at an undervalue, or assuming every pre-insolvency repayment is automatically avoidable.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b72c9d16ab288ead"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0374","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Agency — apparent authority","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company represents to a supplier that an employee has authority to negotiate and conclude a class of contracts. The supplier reasonably relies on that representation. Internally, the employee's actual authority had been secretly restricted.\n\nWhich doctrine may nevertheless bind the company?","options":{"A":"The employee's secret restriction necessarily binds the supplier.","B":"Only actual express authority can ever bind a company.","C":"The indoor management rule makes every employee a director.","D":"Apparent or ostensible authority arising from the principal's representation and the third party's reliance.","E":"Agency authority can arise only through a deed."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Apparent authority can bind a principal where the principal represents that the agent has authority and the third party relies on that representation. Secret internal limits do not necessarily defeat the external appearance created by the principal.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Correct. Apparent authority can bind a principal where the principal represents that the agent has authority and the third party relies on that representation. Secret internal limits do not necessarily defeat the external appearance created by the principal.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Freeman & Lockyer v Buckhurst Park Properties; agency principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A principal can be bound by apparent authority created by its representation despite undisclosed internal limits.","common_trap":"Treating internal authority restrictions as automatically effective against an outsider who reasonably relied on the principal's representation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"45cddc49431682cd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0375","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Summary judgment — realistic prospect","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant files a defence containing bare denials but identifies no evidence or legal basis capable of supporting the pleaded case. The claimant applies for summary judgment.\n\nWhich test should the court apply?","options":{"A":"Whether the claimant can prove the case beyond reasonable doubt.","B":"Whether the defence is merely arguable in the sense of not being fancifully worded.","C":"Whether the claimant has already won at a full trial.","D":"Whether the amount claimed exceeds the small-claims limit.","E":"Whether the defendant has a real prospect of successfully defending the claim and whether there is some other compelling reason for trial."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"CPR Part 24 permits summary judgment where a party has no real prospect of succeeding and there is no other compelling reason for a trial. A real prospect must be realistic rather than fanciful.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Correct. CPR Part 24 permits summary judgment where a party has no real prospect of succeeding and there is no other compelling reason for a trial. A real prospect must be realistic rather than fanciful."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 24; Swain v Hillman.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Summary judgment asks whether there is a realistic prospect of success and any compelling reason for trial.","common_trap":"Applying a criminal standard or treating any bare assertion as enough to require trial.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"969eb66089ec7656"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0376","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Service — deemed service","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant posts a claim form using a permitted first-class service method shortly before expiry of the period for serving the claim form. The claimant assumes that posting within the four-month period is necessarily enough, regardless of the CPR step required for the chosen method.\n\nWhich is the best approach?","options":{"A":"Compliance must be assessed under the specific CPR rules governing the permitted method and the step required for service of a claim form; mere informal notions of posting in time are insufficient.","B":"Any posting before midnight on the final day is always valid service.","C":"Deemed service rules never apply to claim forms.","D":"A claim form can be served at any time after issue without limit.","E":"Only personal service is permitted."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Claim-form service is governed by CPR Part 6, including the permitted methods and the step that must be completed within the period specified by CPR 7.5. The rules should be applied precisely rather than by analogy with ordinary correspondence.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Claim-form service is governed by CPR Part 6, including the permitted methods and the step that must be completed within the period specified by CPR 7.5. The rules should be applied precisely rather than by analogy with ordinary correspondence.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, rr 6.14 and 7.5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Timely service of a claim form depends on satisfying the CPR method-specific service requirements within CPR 7.5.","common_trap":"Assuming ordinary postal timing rules replace the specific procedural regime for service of originating process.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"88e6a0d22996e288"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0377","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Privilege — legal advice privilege: lawyer acting in non-legal capacity","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A qualified solicitor employed by a company is asked purely for commercial investment advice, not legal advice. A later dispute arises and the company claims legal advice privilege solely because the communication was with a solicitor.\n\nWhat is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Legal advice privilege does not arise merely from the recipient's professional status; the communication must be made in the relevant lawyer-client relationship for the purpose of giving or obtaining legal advice.","B":"Every communication with a qualified solicitor is privileged whatever its purpose.","C":"Commercial advice is automatically privileged if marked confidential.","D":"Privilege depends only on whether litigation has already begun.","E":"Legal advice privilege applies only to communications made in court."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Legal advice privilege protects confidential lawyer-client communications made for the purpose of giving or obtaining legal advice. A lawyer acting purely in another professional or commercial capacity does not make every communication privileged.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Legal advice privilege protects confidential lawyer-client communications made for the purpose of giving or obtaining legal advice. A lawyer acting purely in another professional or commercial capacity does not make every communication privileged.","B":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. The capacity and purpose of the communication matter: confidentiality plus lawyer status alone do not establish legal advice privilege.","C":"Incorrect. This states an absolute rule where the governing doctrine is conditional or contains statutory/equitable limits. The capacity and purpose of the communication matter: confidentiality plus lawyer status alone do not establish legal advice privilege.","D":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. The capacity and purpose of the communication matter: confidentiality plus lawyer status alone do not establish legal advice privilege.","E":"Incorrect. This identifies a consequence or requirement that is not the legal test engaged by these facts. The capacity and purpose of the communication matter: confidentiality plus lawyer status alone do not establish legal advice privilege."},"primary_legal_authority":"R (Prudential plc) v Special Commissioner of Income Tax [2013] UKSC 1; legal advice privilege principles","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The capacity and purpose of the communication matter: confidentiality plus lawyer status alone do not establish legal advice privilege.","common_trap":"Equating 'communication with a lawyer' with privilege without asking whether legal advice is being sought or given.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2c748c38279729e8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0378","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Conduct — misleading the court","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor discovers before a hearing that a factual assertion previously made to the court on the client's instructions is false. The client orders the solicitor to repeat the assertion and not correct the position.\n\nWhich professional duty is paramount?","options":{"A":"Client instructions override duties to the court.","B":"Confidentiality always requires a solicitor to affirm a client's false evidence.","C":"The solicitor must not mislead or attempt to mislead the court and cannot knowingly repeat the false assertion.","D":"The solicitor may knowingly mislead if doing so advances the client's best interests.","E":"Only barristers owe duties concerning misleading courts."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The SRA Code expressly prohibits misleading or attempting to mislead the court or others, whether by acts or omissions. A client's instruction cannot require a solicitor to breach that professional obligation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Correct. The SRA Code expressly prohibits misleading or attempting to mislead the court or others, whether by acts or omissions. A client's instruction cannot require a solicitor to breach that professional obligation.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 1.4; SRA Principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A solicitor cannot knowingly mislead the court even when the client demands it.","common_trap":"Treating the duty to act for a client as overriding duties to the administration of justice.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d35b85f46341f242"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0379","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Conduct — own-interest conflict","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor negligently misses a limitation deadline. The same solicitor proposes advising the client on whether the client has a professional-negligence claim against the solicitor and negotiating any settlement personally.\n\nWhich conflict rule is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"There is no conflict because both solicitor and client want settlement.","B":"Only conflicts between two different clients are regulated.","C":"Written consent always cures an own-interest conflict.","D":"An own-interest conflict: the solicitor's personal interest in avoiding liability conflicts with the client's interest in independent advice.","E":"The conflict disappears if the solicitor believes no negligence occurred."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Code prohibits acting where there is an own-interest conflict or a significant risk of one. Advising on one's own potential negligence is a paradigm situation requiring independent handling.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Correct. The Code prohibits acting where there is an own-interest conflict or a significant risk of one. Advising on one's own potential negligence is a paradigm situation requiring independent handling.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 6.1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A solicitor should not act where personal exposure conflicts with the client's need for independent advice.","common_trap":"Assuming conflict rules concern only competing clients and not the solicitor's own interests.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1048ccae3ed7aa1c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0380","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Third-party rights — express identification","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contract between A and B states that a named third party C may enforce a specified promise. The contract does not exclude the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999. C is identified by name but provides no consideration.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"C can never enforce because privity admits no statutory exceptions.","B":"C must have provided consideration to A.","C":"Only an assignee can ever enforce another person's contract.","D":"C becomes a party to every term of the contract automatically.","E":"C may be able to enforce the term under the 1999 Act despite not providing consideration, subject to the Act and the contract's proper construction."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The 1999 Act permits an identified third party to enforce a contractual term where the statutory test is satisfied, including where the contract expressly provides for enforcement. The third party need not supply consideration.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Correct. The 1999 Act permits an identified third party to enforce a contractual term where the statutory test is satisfied, including where the contract expressly provides for enforcement. The third party need not supply consideration."},"primary_legal_authority":"Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999, s 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An expressly identified third-party beneficiary can acquire statutory enforcement rights without furnishing consideration.","common_trap":"Applying the historic privity rule without considering the statutory exception.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e3fd1fe094ce9a7f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0381","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Misrepresentation — section 2(1) damages","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contracting party makes a negligent misrepresentation inducing the contract. The representor cannot prove that there were reasonable grounds to believe, and that they did believe up to contracting, that the represented facts were true.\n\nWhich statutory remedy is particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"Damages under section 2(1) of the Misrepresentation Act 1967, subject to its requirements.","B":"Only nominal damages are possible.","C":"Section 2(1) applies only to fraudulent representations.","D":"The claimant must prove a contractual warranty before any remedy exists.","E":"Misrepresentation never gives damages."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Section 2(1) provides a damages route for an inducing misrepresentation where the representor cannot establish the statutory reasonable-belief defence. Its measure has distinctive authority.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Section 2(1) provides a damages route for an inducing misrepresentation where the representor cannot establish the statutory reasonable-belief defence. Its measure has distinctive authority.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Misrepresentation Act 1967, s 2(1); Royscot Trust Ltd v Rogerson.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Section 2(1) shifts the burden to the representor to establish reasonable grounds and belief once its conditions are engaged.","common_trap":"Treating section 2(1) as requiring the claimant to prove common-law fraud.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"cfdf45edf0ec3631"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0382","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Exclusion clauses — incorporation by notice","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A customer pays for a hotel room at reception. Only after the contract is concluded does the customer enter the room and see a notice purporting to exclude liability for loss of property.\n\nWhich incorporation principle is most relevant?","options":{"A":"Any notice displayed anywhere on the premises is automatically incorporated.","B":"A term first communicated after contract formation is ordinarily too late to be incorporated by notice into that contract.","C":"Exclusion clauses need never be incorporated.","D":"A post-contract notice retrospectively amends every contract.","E":"Incorporation depends only on whether the customer actually reads the notice."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Reasonable notice of a contractual term must ordinarily be given before or at formation. A notice encountered only after the contract has been made is generally too late to incorporate the term by notice.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Correct. Reasonable notice of a contractual term must ordinarily be given before or at formation. A notice encountered only after the contract has been made is generally too late to incorporate the term by notice.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Olley v Marlborough Court Ltd; Thornton v Shoe Lane Parking Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Notice of a term must ordinarily precede or accompany contract formation to incorporate it.","common_trap":"Focusing on physical prominence while ignoring the timing of the notice.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"97a6e212db78ba1c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0383","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Occupiers' liability — children","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A museum invites families onto its premises. A low, unguarded feature presents little risk to an adult but is foreseeably attractive and dangerous to young children. A child visitor is injured.\n\nWhich statutory consideration is particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"Children are held to the identical self-protective standard as adults under the 1957 Act.","B":"An occupier owes children no duty if their parents are present.","C":"An occupier must be prepared for children to be less careful than adults when assessing the common duty of care.","D":"Attractive dangers are always harmless as a matter of law.","E":"The Occupiers' Liability Act applies only to adult visitors."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Section 2(3)(a) specifically recognises that occupiers must be prepared for children to be less careful than adults. The overall question remains whether the premises were reasonably safe for the child visitor in the circumstances.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Correct. Section 2(3)(a) specifically recognises that occupiers must be prepared for children to be less careful than adults. The overall question remains whether the premises were reasonably safe for the child visitor in the circumstances.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Occupiers' Liability Act 1957, s 2(3)(a); Glasgow Corporation v Taylor.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The common duty of care accounts for children's lesser caution and the nature of dangers attractive to them.","common_trap":"Applying an adult standard of self-protection mechanically to child visitors.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e029f50f2d3f6b67"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0384","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Contributory negligence — apportionment","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant proves the defendant's negligence caused injury, but the claimant's own unreasonable failure to take care for personal safety materially contributed to the damage.\n\nWhat is the ordinary effect if contributory negligence is established?","options":{"A":"The claim automatically fails in full.","B":"The defendant becomes free from all legal responsibility.","C":"The claimant's damages automatically fall by exactly 50%.","D":"The damages are reduced to the extent the court considers just and equitable having regard to the claimant's share in responsibility; the claim is not necessarily defeated entirely.","E":"Contributory negligence applies only to contract."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The 1945 Act replaces the old complete bar with apportionment: recoverable damages are reduced by the percentage the court considers just and equitable having regard to the claimant's responsibility.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Correct. The 1945 Act replaces the old complete bar with apportionment: recoverable damages are reduced by the percentage the court considers just and equitable having regard to the claimant's responsibility.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law Reform (Contributory Negligence) Act 1945, s 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contributory negligence generally reduces damages proportionately rather than automatically defeating the claim.","common_trap":"Treating contributory negligence as an all-or-nothing defence or assuming a fixed percentage reduction.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"31cd24da9f159235"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0385","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligent misstatement — assumption of responsibility","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A professional gives specific advice directly to a known recipient, knowing the recipient will rely on it for a particular transaction. The recipient reasonably relies and suffers pure economic loss when the advice is carelessly wrong.\n\nWhich doctrine may support a duty of care despite the loss being purely economic?","options":{"A":"The rule in Rylands v Fletcher.","B":"Vicarious liability without an underlying tort.","C":"Private nuisance.","D":"Trespass to land.","E":"Assumption of responsibility under the negligent-misstatement line of authority."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Pure economic loss can be recoverable for negligent statements where the relationship and circumstances establish an assumption of responsibility and reasonable reliance within the Hedley Byrne principle.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Correct. Pure economic loss can be recoverable for negligent statements where the relationship and circumstances establish an assumption of responsibility and reasonable reliance within the Hedley Byrne principle."},"primary_legal_authority":"Hedley Byrne & Co Ltd v Heller & Partners Ltd; Caparo Industries plc v Dickman.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A special relationship involving assumption of responsibility and reasonable reliance can support recovery for negligent misstatement.","common_trap":"Applying the general bar on pure economic loss without considering the recognised negligent-statement exception.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9a0446ba3860ed72"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0386","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — promptness","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant learns immediately of a public decision but waits almost three months without justification before filing a judicial-review claim. The claim is still technically within the applicable three-month longstop.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"Judicial review must be brought promptly and in any event within the applicable maximum period, so filing within three months does not necessarily satisfy the promptness requirement.","B":"Any claim filed within three months is automatically timely.","C":"Judicial review has a fixed six-year limitation period.","D":"Delay can never affect public-law remedies.","E":"Promptness applies only to appeals, not judicial review."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"CPR 54.5 requires judicial-review claims to be filed promptly and in any event within the stated maximum period, subject to shorter statutory periods for some categories. Unjustified delay can therefore matter before the maximum expires.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. CPR 54.5 requires judicial-review claims to be filed promptly and in any event within the stated maximum period, subject to shorter statutory periods for some categories. Unjustified delay can therefore matter before the maximum expires.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 54.5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The maximum period is not a licence to delay: judicial review also carries an independent promptness requirement.","common_trap":"Treating the outer time limit as the sole timeliness test.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"030036dc223bc465"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0387","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Human Rights Act — horizontal effect","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two private individuals are litigating a common-law privacy dispute. One lawyer says Convention rights are wholly irrelevant because neither litigant is a public authority.\n\nWhich is the better analysis?","options":{"A":"Convention rights can never influence litigation between private parties.","B":"Courts themselves are public authorities under the Human Rights Act and must develop and apply the law compatibly with Convention rights within the proper legal framework, so rights can affect disputes between private parties indirectly.","C":"The HRA directly makes every private individual a public authority for all purposes.","D":"Private litigation is automatically transferred to Strasbourg.","E":"Common law ceases to apply whenever a Convention right is mentioned."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The HRA generally binds public authorities rather than imposing all Convention duties directly on private persons, but courts are public authorities and must act compatibly, producing an important indirect effect in private-law adjudication.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Correct. The HRA generally binds public authorities rather than imposing all Convention duties directly on private persons, but courts are public authorities and must act compatibly, producing an important indirect effect in private-law adjudication.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, s 6; Campbell v MGN Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Convention rights can influence private disputes through the duties of courts, without converting all private persons into public authorities.","common_trap":"Choosing between the false extremes of no private-law relevance and direct universal horizontal liability.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4490b4e55c37370b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0388","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Delegated legislation — ultra vires","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A minister makes regulations under an enabling Act. The regulations impose obligations that the Act gives the minister no power to create.\n\nWhich public-law challenge is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Delegated legislation can never be reviewed by courts.","B":"A minister may legislate beyond the parent Act whenever policy reasons are strong.","C":"The regulations may be substantively ultra vires because they exceed the power conferred by the enabling Act.","D":"The regulations automatically amend the enabling Act.","E":"Only Parliament can interpret the scope of delegated powers."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Delegated legislation must remain within the scope of authority Parliament conferred. Measures outside that scope can be quashed or declared unlawful through judicial review.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Correct. Delegated legislation must remain within the scope of authority Parliament conferred. Measures outside that scope can be quashed or declared unlawful through judicial review.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Delegated-legislation and judicial-review principles; enabling statute controls.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Delegated legislation exceeding its enabling power is vulnerable as ultra vires.","common_trap":"Assuming a statutory instrument has the same unconstrained law-making authority as primary legislation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c7238e052eed0f2b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0389","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Will execution — acknowledgment","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A testator signed a will earlier in private. Later, in the simultaneous presence of two witnesses, the testator clearly acknowledges the existing signature. Each witness then attests and signs in the testator's presence.\n\nCan the execution requirement concerning the testator's signature be satisfied?","options":{"A":"No. The testator must always physically sign while both witnesses watch the pen move.","B":"No will can be valid if signed before witnesses arrive.","C":"Only notarisation can validate an earlier signature.","D":"Yes. The Wills Act permits the testator to sign or acknowledge the signature in the simultaneous presence of two witnesses, provided the other statutory requirements are met.","E":"Acknowledgment is effective with only one witness."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 9 expressly permits acknowledgment of the testator's signature in the presence of two witnesses present at the same time, followed by each witness attesting and signing in the required manner.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Correct. Section 9 expressly permits acknowledgment of the testator's signature in the presence of two witnesses present at the same time, followed by each witness attesting and signing in the required manner.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837, s 9.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A prior signature can be acknowledged before two simultaneously present witnesses under section 9.","common_trap":"Assuming the witnesses must necessarily observe the physical act of the testator signing.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a05c3e584a1dc608"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0390","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Ademption — specific gifts","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A will specifically gives 'my 500 shares in X plc' to a beneficiary. Before death the testator sells all those shares and owns none at death. No relevant statutory or equitable exception applies.\n\nWhat is the ordinary consequence?","options":{"A":"The beneficiary automatically receives the sale proceeds.","B":"The residuary beneficiary must buy replacement shares.","C":"The gift becomes a pecuniary legacy equal to the date-of-death value.","D":"The sale revokes the entire will.","E":"The specific gift is adeemed and the beneficiary ordinarily receives no substitute value from the estate."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Ademption occurs where specifically gifted property is no longer part of the testator's estate at death. The beneficiary ordinarily receives nothing in substitution unless an exception applies.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Correct. Ademption occurs where specifically gifted property is no longer part of the testator's estate at death. The beneficiary ordinarily receives nothing in substitution unless an exception applies."},"primary_legal_authority":"Common-law ademption principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A specific legacy ordinarily fails by ademption if the specifically identified asset is no longer in the estate.","common_trap":"Treating a specific gift as if it were a general monetary entitlement protected against disposal of the asset.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b861cd90e433d7db"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0391","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Family provision — eligible applicants","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A deceased person domiciled in England and Wales leaves a will making no provision for a financially dependent adult who was being maintained wholly by the deceased immediately before death. The dependent is not a spouse, child or cohabitant.\n\nWhich statutory regime should be considered?","options":{"A":"The Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975, which includes certain persons maintained by the deceased among potential applicants.","B":"Only intestacy rules can provide any remedy.","C":"A dependent who is not a relative can never apply.","D":"The Wills Act automatically rewrites the will.","E":"The Human Rights Act gives the dependent the estate automatically."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The 1975 Act identifies several categories of applicant, including a person who immediately before death was being maintained, wholly or partly, by the deceased, subject to the statutory requirements and time limits.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The 1975 Act identifies several categories of applicant, including a person who immediately before death was being maintained, wholly or partly, by the deceased, subject to the statutory requirements and time limits.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975, ss 1–2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Financial dependants can fall within the statutory family-provision regime even without the specified family relationships.","common_trap":"Assuming eligibility is confined to spouses and children.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b6e409f12b1da397"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0392","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Withdrawals from client account","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A cashier proposes withdrawing money from client account to pay a third party even though there is no corresponding client ledger balance or proper authority for that withdrawal.\n\nWhich is the best regulatory position?","options":{"A":"Any employee may withdraw client money if a third party requests payment.","B":"Withdrawals from client account must be properly authorised and made only for permitted purposes in accordance with the Accounts Rules; a client account is not a general source of firm liquidity.","C":"Client account balances may be borrowed temporarily by the firm.","D":"A negative client ledger is acceptable whenever corrected by year end.","E":"Authorisation is unnecessary for electronic transfers."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Accounts Rules tightly control withdrawals of client money and require systems ensuring withdrawals are appropriately authorised and related to the client money held. Firms must safeguard client funds rather than use them as working capital.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Correct. The Accounts Rules tightly control withdrawals of client money and require systems ensuring withdrawals are appropriately authorised and related to the client money held. Firms must safeguard client funds rather than use them as working capital.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, rr 5.1 and 5.2; safeguarding obligations.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Client-account withdrawals require proper authority and a permitted connection to money held for the relevant purpose.","common_trap":"Treating aggregate cash in client account as interchangeable firm funds regardless of individual client entitlements.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"caf974f952c6ba48"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0393","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Certainty of objects — discretionary trusts","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A settlor creates a discretionary trust 'for any of my employees and former employees whom the trustees select'. The class can be conceptually defined, although it may be administratively large.\n\nWhich certainty test is principally relevant?","options":{"A":"Whether a complete list of every beneficiary can be drawn up before the trust takes effect.","B":"Whether each beneficiary has a fixed share from the outset.","C":"Whether it can be said of any given person that the person is or is not within the class of potential beneficiaries.","D":"Whether the trustees know every beneficiary personally.","E":"Whether all beneficiaries consent to the trust."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"For discretionary trusts, the McPhail v Doulton test asks whether it can be said of any given person that they are or are not a member of the class, subject also to issues such as administrative unworkability.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Correct. For discretionary trusts, the McPhail v Doulton test asks whether it can be said of any given person that they are or are not a member of the class, subject also to issues such as administrative unworkability.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"McPhail v Doulton; Re Baden (No 2).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Discretionary trusts use the 'is or is not' certainty-of-objects test rather than the fixed-trust complete-list test.","common_trap":"Applying the complete-list requirement for fixed trusts to a discretionary trust.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e9f51d32ad8d2c11"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0394","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Dishonest assistance","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A non-trustee knowingly helps a trustee divert trust assets for the trustee's personal benefit. On the facts, the assistant's conduct is objectively dishonest in light of the assistant's actual knowledge and beliefs.\n\nWhich equitable liability is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Knowing receipt necessarily, even though the assistant receives no trust property.","B":"Strict liability merely for knowing the trustee socially.","C":"A resulting trust over all the assistant's property.","D":"Dishonest assistance in a breach of trust or fiduciary duty.","E":"Proprietary estoppel."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Dishonest assistance requires assistance in a breach of trust or fiduciary duty coupled with dishonesty assessed objectively by reference to the defendant's actual state of knowledge or belief.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Correct. Dishonest assistance requires assistance in a breach of trust or fiduciary duty coupled with dishonesty assessed objectively by reference to the defendant's actual state of knowledge or belief.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Royal Brunei Airlines v Tan; Ivey v Genting Casinos; Group Seven Ltd v Notable Services LLP.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A person who dishonestly assists a fiduciary breach can incur accessory liability without personally receiving the trust asset.","common_trap":"Confusing dishonest assistance with knowing receipt, which requires beneficial receipt of trust property.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3ec543cd94f2b16d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0395","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Registered land — priority","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A purchaser for valuable consideration completes a registrable disposition of registered land and becomes registered. An earlier unprotected equitable interest is not overriding and is not otherwise protected on the register.\n\nWhich priority rule is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Earlier equitable interests always prevail regardless of registration or overriding status.","B":"Section 28 alone always gives absolute priority to the earlier interest even after a qualifying disposition for value.","C":"Registration has no role in priority disputes.","D":"Only legal mortgages are subject to the Land Registration Act priority rules.","E":"On a qualifying registered disposition for value, section 29 can postpone an earlier interest whose priority is not protected."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Although section 28 states the basic priority rule, section 29 modifies it on a registered disposition for valuable consideration: the disponee can take subject only to interests whose priority is protected in the ways specified by the Act.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Correct. Although section 28 states the basic priority rule, section 29 modifies it on a registered disposition for valuable consideration: the disponee can take subject only to interests whose priority is protected in the ways specified by the Act."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002, ss 28–29.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A qualifying registered disposition for value can postpone an earlier unprotected non-overriding interest under section 29.","common_trap":"Applying the basic first-in-time rule without considering the statutory special priority rule for registered dispositions for value.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e2bce5bf0f505ec9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0396","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Lease or licence — exclusive possession","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An occupier is granted possession of a flat for a fixed term at rent. The written agreement calls the arrangement a 'licence', but in substance the occupier has exclusive possession and no exceptional circumstance displaces the ordinary inference.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"The court looks to substance rather than label; exclusive possession for a term can indicate a tenancy despite the word 'licence'.","B":"Calling the document a licence conclusively prevents a tenancy.","C":"Rent is the sole test for a lease.","D":"Exclusive possession is legally irrelevant.","E":"A tenancy can exist only if executed by deed."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Street v Mountford establishes that the substance of the arrangement governs. Exclusive possession for a term, subject to recognised exceptions, is a central indicator of a tenancy notwithstanding the parties' label.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Street v Mountford establishes that the substance of the arrangement governs. Exclusive possession for a term, subject to recognised exceptions, is a central indicator of a tenancy notwithstanding the parties' label.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Street v Mountford [1985] AC 809.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The legal character of occupation depends on substantive rights, particularly exclusive possession, not merely contractual labels.","common_trap":"Allowing the parties' chosen label to override the rights actually granted.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6a383a47183304f7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0397","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Pre-completion searches — OS1 priority","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer of registered land is ready to complete. The buyer's solicitor wants to protect the forthcoming application for registration against adverse entries made after the pre-completion search but before the buyer's application is lodged.\n\nWhich search is normally used?","options":{"A":"A local authority search.","B":"An official search with priority of the whole of a registered title (OS1), followed by timely registration within the priority period.","C":"An environmental search.","D":"A bankruptcy search against the seller only.","E":"A Companies House search."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"An OS1 search gives a priority period for the applicant's intended registrable disposition. The registration application must be lodged within that period to obtain the intended priority protection.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Correct. An OS1 search gives a priority period for the applicant's intended registrable disposition. The registration application must be lodged within that period to obtain the intended priority protection.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Rules 2003; HM Land Registry Practice Guide 12/31 and conveyancing practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"OS1 is the standard pre-completion priority search for a disposition of the whole registered title.","common_trap":"Confusing pre-contract due-diligence searches with the Land Registry priority search protecting the registration gap.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9f3ff0ee2979e081"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0398","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Mortgages — redemption on completion","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A seller's registered property is subject to a mortgage. The sale is completing with vacant title promised, and the seller's solicitor has given the usual professional undertaking concerning redemption.\n\nWhat is the solicitor ordinarily expected to ensure?","options":{"A":"That the buyer automatically assumes the seller's mortgage.","B":"That the charge remains indefinitely without disclosure.","C":"That the existing mortgage is redeemed from completion monies and the steps necessary to discharge the registered charge are carried out in accordance with the undertaking.","D":"That HM Land Registry pays the lender from public funds.","E":"That the buyer's solicitor personally becomes liable for the mortgage debt."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"On a sale intended to complete free of the seller's mortgage, the seller's solicitor commonly undertakes to redeem the charge from completion funds and procure its discharge. Professional undertakings must be performed.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Correct. On a sale intended to complete free of the seller's mortgage, the seller's solicitor commonly undertakes to redeem the charge from completion funds and procure its discharge. Professional undertakings must be performed.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Conveyancing practice; SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 1.3; Land Registration discharge procedures.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Completion machinery must ensure redemption and discharge of the seller's charge where title is to pass free of it.","common_trap":"Treating the seller's secured debt as automatically transferred to the buyer merely because the land is sold.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"46108172ef95dffd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0399","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Attempts — more than merely preparatory","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant intends to commit burglary and takes steps towards the offence. Police intervene before entry. The prosecution charges attempt.\n\nWhat statutory threshold must the prosecution establish in addition to the required intent?","options":{"A":"Any thought about committing burglary is sufficient.","B":"Preparation can never amount to any criminal offence.","C":"The full burglary must have been completed.","D":"The defendant's act must have gone beyond merely preparatory conduct toward commission of the offence.","E":"The defendant must have caused physical injury."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Criminal Attempts Act requires an act that is more than merely preparatory to commission of the offence, together with the requisite intent. Drawing the line is fact-sensitive and informed by authority.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Correct. The Criminal Attempts Act requires an act that is more than merely preparatory to commission of the offence, together with the requisite intent. Drawing the line is fact-sensitive and informed by authority.","E":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Attempts Act 1981, s 1; R v Gullefer; R v Jones.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Attempt liability requires conduct that is more than merely preparatory plus the necessary intent.","common_trap":"Treating mere intention or early preparation as automatically satisfying the actus reus of attempt.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3f348541ebe1c5e0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0400","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Guilty plea — sentence reduction","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant indicates an unequivocal guilty plea at the first stage of proceedings at which the plea can properly be indicated. No statutory exception alters the position.\n\nWhich sentencing principle is relevant?","options":{"A":"A guilty plea can never affect sentence.","B":"The maximum reduction is available whenever the plea is entered, including on the first day of trial.","C":"Pleading guilty automatically halves every sentence.","D":"The prosecution alone determines the reduction.","E":"An early guilty plea can attract the maximum reduction available under the applicable guilty-plea guideline, with the reduction generally decreasing as the plea is entered later."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The Sentencing Council guideline provides credit for a guilty plea, with the greatest reduction ordinarily available when indicated at the first stage of proceedings and progressively lower credit thereafter, subject to the guideline's detailed rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. It conflicts with the governing rule or applies a different doctrine to these facts; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Correct. The Sentencing Council guideline provides credit for a guilty plea, with the greatest reduction ordinarily available when indicated at the first stage of proceedings and progressively lower credit thereafter, subject to the guideline's detailed rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"Sentencing Council, Reduction in Sentence for a Guilty Plea guideline; Sentencing Code.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Timing of the guilty plea affects the available sentencing reduction under the guideline.","common_trap":"Assuming plea credit is either unavailable or fixed regardless of when responsibility is accepted.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c88299fbff8d897e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0401","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — allotment authority","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company has only one class of shares in issue and proposes to allot further shares of that same class. Its articles do not restrict the statutory default and no special circumstance applies.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the directors' authority to allot?","options":{"A":"For a private company with only one class of shares, directors may have statutory authority to allot shares of that class unless the articles prohibit or restrict it.","B":"Directors always need a special resolution before any allotment.","C":"Only Companies House can authorise an allotment.","D":"An allotment is valid only if every existing shareholder consents.","E":"Directors can never allot shares without court approval."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Section 550 gives directors of a private company with only one class of shares statutory authority to allot shares of that class, subject to the company's articles and the statutory framework.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Section 550 gives directors of a private company with only one class of shares statutory authority to allot shares of that class, subject to the company's articles and the statutory framework.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, s 550.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A single-class private company can benefit from the section 550 default allotment authority.","common_trap":"Assuming the section 551 authority mechanism applies identically to every company and every allotment.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4e8cc95f64407bdc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0402","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Shares — statutory pre-emption","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company proposes to allot new ordinary shares for cash to an outside investor. Existing ordinary shareholders have statutory pre-emption rights and those rights have not been validly excluded or disapplied.\n\nWhat must ordinarily happen first?","options":{"A":"The outsider automatically has priority because it is paying cash.","B":"The new equity securities must ordinarily be offered to existing qualifying shareholders pro rata on equivalent or more favourable terms before being allotted to the outsider.","C":"Pre-emption applies only to transfers of existing shares.","D":"The directors may ignore statutory pre-emption whenever they believe the investor is desirable.","E":"Only preference shareholders can have statutory pre-emption rights."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The statutory pre-emption regime generally requires new equity securities allotted for cash to be offered first to existing ordinary shareholders in proportion to their holdings, subject to statutory exceptions and valid disapplication.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Correct. The statutory pre-emption regime generally requires new equity securities allotted for cash to be offered first to existing ordinary shareholders in proportion to their holdings, subject to statutory exceptions and valid disapplication.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 561–577.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Cash allotments of equity securities ordinarily engage statutory pro-rata pre-emption rights.","common_trap":"Confusing an allotment of new shares with a transfer of existing shares or assuming board preference overrides statutory rights.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5a33cb12a9744a1e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0403","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Partnership — fiduciary duty and secret profits","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A partner uses information obtained through the partnership to secure a personal commission from a transaction falling within the scope of the partnership business. The other partners did not consent.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"A partner may retain every secret commission if the partnership itself suffers no loss.","B":"Partners owe no fiduciary obligations to one another.","C":"The partner may have to account to the firm for the private benefit obtained from the partnership transaction or use of partnership property or connection.","D":"Only company directors are subject to no-profit obligations.","E":"The commission belongs automatically to the customer rather than the partnership."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Partnership Act requires a partner to account to the firm for benefits derived without consent from partnership transactions or use of partnership property, name or business connection.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Correct. The Partnership Act requires a partner to account to the firm for benefits derived without consent from partnership transactions or use of partnership property, name or business connection.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Partnership Act 1890, ss 28–30.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Partners must account for unauthorised private benefits obtained through the partnership relationship.","common_trap":"Assuming lack of measurable partnership loss permits a partner to retain a secret profit.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"20d1af0cc830e3bf"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0404","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — administration purpose","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An insolvent company enters administration. Rescue of the company as a going concern is not reasonably practicable, but an administrator concludes that achieving a better result for creditors as a whole than immediate winding up is reasonably practicable.\n\nWhich statutory objective may the administrator pursue?","options":{"A":"Maximising returns only for the directors.","B":"Continuing the company indefinitely regardless of creditor outcomes.","C":"Paying shareholders before creditors.","D":"Achieving a better result for the company's creditors as a whole than would be likely on winding up, where rescue as a going concern is not reasonably practicable.","E":"Administration has no statutory hierarchy of objectives."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Schedule B1 sets a hierarchy: rescue as a going concern is the primary objective unless not reasonably practicable or the alternative would achieve a better result for creditors as a whole; the administrator may then pursue the second objective.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Correct. Schedule B1 sets a hierarchy: rescue as a going concern is the primary objective unless not reasonably practicable or the alternative would achieve a better result for creditors as a whole; the administrator may then pursue the second objective.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, Sch B1, para 3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Administration includes a statutory objective of achieving a better result for creditors as a whole where the hierarchy permits it.","common_trap":"Treating administration as having no ordered statutory purposes or as primarily protecting shareholders.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1cf01e271da07959"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0405","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Amendment — limitation after expiry","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"After the limitation period expires, a claimant seeks to amend a statement of case to add a new claim arising from substantially the same facts already in issue in the existing claim.\n\nWhich is the best procedural analysis?","options":{"A":"No amendment can ever be allowed after a limitation period expires.","B":"Limitation becomes irrelevant once any claim form has been issued.","C":"The claimant may add any unrelated cause of action as of right.","D":"Only the defendant may amend after limitation expires.","E":"The court has a limited statutory and CPR power to permit certain amendments after limitation expiry, including claims arising from substantially the same facts, but the specific conditions must be satisfied."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Post-limitation amendments are restricted. The Limitation Act and CPR Part 17 permit specified new claims in defined circumstances, including where the new claim arises out of substantially the same facts as an existing claim.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Correct. Post-limitation amendments are restricted. The Limitation Act and CPR Part 17 permit specified new claims in defined circumstances, including where the new claim arises out of substantially the same facts as an existing claim."},"primary_legal_authority":"Limitation Act 1980, s 35; Civil Procedure Rules, r 17.4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Adding a new claim after limitation requires satisfaction of the special post-limitation amendment regime.","common_trap":"Assuming issue of one claim permanently stops limitation for every later and unrelated claim.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1632d5a098b00ed1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0406","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Relief from sanctions — Denton","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A party breaches a procedural rule and a sanction takes effect. The party applies promptly for relief. The breach is significant, there is a credible explanation, and the court must decide the application in the context of efficient and proportionate litigation.\n\nWhich framework should the court apply?","options":{"A":"The CPR 3.9 relief-from-sanctions test as structured by Denton: seriousness/significance, reasons for default, and all the circumstances including efficient litigation and compliance.","B":"Relief is automatic whenever an application is prompt.","C":"Any breach permanently ends the claim.","D":"The court considers only whether the defaulting party acted dishonestly.","E":"The merits of the substantive claim are the sole consideration."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Denton identifies a three-stage approach to CPR 3.9: assess seriousness and significance, consider why the default occurred, then evaluate all circumstances so the application is dealt with justly, including the specified CPR factors.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Denton identifies a three-stage approach to CPR 3.9: assess seriousness and significance, consider why the default occurred, then evaluate all circumstances so the application is dealt with justly, including the specified CPR factors.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 3.9; Denton v TH White Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Relief from sanctions requires the structured CPR 3.9/Denton analysis rather than an automatic rule.","common_trap":"Treating either promptness or the existence of a breach as dispositive without the three-stage assessment.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"459b0d9ae42d63e5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0407","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Costs — general rule and discretion","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant succeeds at trial on the main issues. There are no unusual circumstances, but the claimant argues that recovery of every penny of legal expenditure is automatic.\n\nWhich statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"The successful party automatically recovers every amount spent.","B":"The general rule is that the unsuccessful party pays the successful party's costs, but costs remain subject to the court's discretion and assessment.","C":"Each party must always bear its own costs.","D":"Costs can be awarded only where a Part 36 offer exists.","E":"The court has no discretion over costs after judgment."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"CPR 44.2 states the general rule that the unsuccessful party pays the successful party's costs, but the court retains a broad discretion as to whether costs are payable, their amount and when they are paid.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Correct. CPR 44.2 states the general rule that the unsuccessful party pays the successful party's costs, but the court retains a broad discretion as to whether costs are payable, their amount and when they are paid.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 44.2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The loser-pays rule is a starting point within a wider judicial costs discretion.","common_trap":"Confusing the general rule with an automatic entitlement to complete reimbursement of actual expenditure.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"181b51578f9c6571"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0408","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Confidentiality — former clients","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor previously acted for Client A and holds confidential information material to a new matter. Client B now asks the solicitor to act against A in that matter. A has not consented, and there are no effective measures eliminating the real risk of disclosure.\n\nWhich is the best professional position?","options":{"A":"Confidentiality ends when the former retainer ends.","B":"The solicitor may freely use former-client information for a new client.","C":"The solicitor should not act where there is a real risk of disclosure of the former client's confidential information unless the applicable exception and safeguards are satisfied.","D":"Only current clients are protected by confidentiality.","E":"Client B's informed consent alone overrides A's confidentiality."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The SRA Code protects confidential information of current and former clients and restricts acting against a former client where relevant confidential information creates a real risk of disclosure, absent informed consent or effective safeguards meeting the Code.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Correct. The SRA Code protects confidential information of current and former clients and restricts acting against a former client where relevant confidential information creates a real risk of disclosure, absent informed consent or effective safeguards meeting the Code.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, paras 6.3–6.5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Former-client confidentiality can restrict later adverse representation where relevant confidential information is at risk.","common_trap":"Assuming termination of a retainer ends the duty of confidentiality.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f4edd7b933f987aa"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0409","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"AML — customer due diligence","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm within the regulated sector begins a new business relationship presenting an ordinary money-laundering risk. The fee earner proposes postponing all identity and verification work indefinitely until the transaction has completed.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"CDD is optional unless money laundering has already been proved.","B":"Verification may always wait until after completion.","C":"Only banks, not solicitors, perform CDD.","D":"The firm must apply customer due diligence in accordance with the Money Laundering Regulations, ordinarily identifying and verifying the customer and relevant beneficial owner at the required stage, subject to limited timing exceptions.","E":"Existing professional obligations prohibit solicitors from checking identity."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Regulated firms must conduct risk-based customer due diligence, including identification and verification requirements, with timing governed by the Regulations and only limited exceptions allowing completion during establishment of a relationship.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Correct. Regulated firms must conduct risk-based customer due diligence, including identification and verification requirements, with timing governed by the Regulations and only limited exceptions allowing completion during establishment of a relationship.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Money Laundering Regulations 2017, regs 27–30.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"CDD is a preventive regulatory obligation and ordinarily cannot be deferred merely for convenience.","common_trap":"Treating CDD as an investigative step triggered only after proven criminality.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8b7ca36245508954"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0410","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Formation — battle of forms","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer sends a purchase order on its standard terms. The seller replies with an acknowledgment expressly accepting only on the seller's materially different standard terms. The buyer then accepts delivery without further objection.\n\nWhich issue is central to deciding the contractual terms?","options":{"A":"The buyer's terms always prevail because the buyer communicated first.","B":"The seller's terms always prevail because the seller supplied the goods.","C":"Conflicting standard terms automatically cancel the contract.","D":"The court combines every inconsistent clause into one contract.","E":"The objective sequence of offer and counter-offer, including which set of terms was last accepted by words or conduct."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Battle-of-forms cases are analysed using ordinary formation principles. A purported acceptance on materially different terms may be a counter-offer, and subsequent conduct can objectively accept that counter-offer.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Correct. Battle-of-forms cases are analysed using ordinary formation principles. A purported acceptance on materially different terms may be a counter-offer, and subsequent conduct can objectively accept that counter-offer."},"primary_legal_authority":"Butler Machine Tool Co Ltd v Ex-Cell-O Corp.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Competing standard terms are resolved through offer, counter-offer and objective acceptance analysis.","common_trap":"Assuming an automatic first-shot or seller-always-wins rule instead of analysing the parties' communications and conduct.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"15c8ea48acd648c9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0411","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Consumer Rights Act — satisfactory quality","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trader sells a new household appliance to a consumer. The appliance functions intermittently and is substantially less durable than a reasonable consumer would expect, taking account of description, price and other relevant circumstances.\n\nWhich statutory term is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"The implied term that goods supplied under a consumer contract are of satisfactory quality.","B":"The consumer must prove fraudulent misrepresentation before any statutory right arises.","C":"Quality terms apply only to second-hand goods.","D":"The Act implies only a term about title, never quality.","E":"Durability is legally irrelevant to satisfactory quality."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Consumer Rights Act implies a term that goods are of satisfactory quality, judged by the standard a reasonable person would consider satisfactory in light of description, price and relevant circumstances; quality includes matters such as durability.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Consumer Rights Act implies a term that goods are of satisfactory quality, judged by the standard a reasonable person would consider satisfactory in light of description, price and relevant circumstances; quality includes matters such as durability.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Consumer Rights Act 2015, s 9.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Satisfactory quality under the CRA includes durability and is assessed objectively in context.","common_trap":"Treating consumer quality rights as dependent on proof of fraud or ignoring durability.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"879b3dad2bff44ea"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0412","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Penalties — legitimate interest","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A commercial contract negotiated by sophisticated parties requires a substantial payment on breach. The sum is not a genuine pre-estimate of loss, but it protects a legitimate commercial interest and is not exorbitant, extravagant or unconscionable relative to that interest.\n\nWhich is the best analysis under the modern penalty doctrine?","options":{"A":"Every clause exceeding estimated loss is automatically a penalty.","B":"The clause is not necessarily penal merely because it is not a genuine pre-estimate of loss; the question includes whether the detriment is out of proportion to the innocent party's legitimate interest.","C":"Penalty doctrine applies equally to every primary payment obligation even without breach.","D":"Sophisticated parties can never be subject to the penalty rule.","E":"A penalty clause is enforceable whenever written in capital letters."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Cavendish reformulated the penalty test: a secondary obligation is penal where the detriment imposed is out of all proportion to a legitimate interest in enforcement of the primary obligation. A genuine pre-estimate remains relevant but is not the exclusive test.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Correct. Cavendish reformulated the penalty test: a secondary obligation is penal where the detriment imposed is out of all proportion to a legitimate interest in enforcement of the primary obligation. A genuine pre-estimate remains relevant but is not the exclusive test.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Cavendish Square Holding BV v Makdessi; ParkingEye Ltd v Beavis.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Modern penalty analysis focuses on proportionality to a legitimate interest, not solely pre-estimated loss.","common_trap":"Applying the older genuine-pre-estimate formula as an exclusive rule after Cavendish.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"08639e78047a50fb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0413","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Product liability — defective product","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A consumer is injured by a product whose safety is below what persons generally are entitled to expect, considering all the statutory circumstances. The producer exercised substantial care in manufacturing it.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the Consumer Protection Act regime?","options":{"A":"The claimant must always prove the producer was negligent.","B":"A product is defective only if it fails to match its advertising exactly.","C":"The producer can face statutory liability for damage caused by a defective product without the claimant proving common-law negligence, subject to the Act's requirements and defences.","D":"The Act creates liability only for contractual buyers.","E":"Reasonable manufacturing care is always a complete defence."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Part I of the Consumer Protection Act imposes liability for damage caused by defective products. Defect turns on the safety persons generally are entitled to expect, and liability does not require proof of negligence, though statutory defences apply.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Correct. Part I of the Consumer Protection Act imposes liability for damage caused by defective products. Defect turns on the safety persons generally are entitled to expect, and liability does not require proof of negligence, though statutory defences apply.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Consumer Protection Act 1987, ss 2–4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"CPA product liability is defect-based rather than dependent on proof of negligence.","common_trap":"Importing the negligence fault requirement into the statutory product-liability regime.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6bb34ef3fb5084e3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0414","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Companies — directors' conflicts: authorisation","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company director identifies a proposed transaction in which the director has an indirect personal interest. The company's constitution does not prohibit board authorisation and the statutory conditions for authorisation can be met.\n\nWhat is the best general position on authorising the conflict?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","C":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","D":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding.","E":"The non-conflicted directors may authorise the situation if the Companies Act conditions, including quorum and voting requirements, are satisfied."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Directors' conflict situations can sometimes be authorised in a private company under the statutory regime; authorisation is not automatically impossible.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Correct. Directors' conflict situations can sometimes be authorised in a private company under the statutory regime; authorisation is not automatically impossible."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 175(5)–(6)","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Directors' conflict situations can sometimes be authorised in a private company under the statutory regime; authorisation is not automatically impossible.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"27788fdbfb9dc1cb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0415","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Occupiers' liability — trespassers","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An occupier knows that trespassers frequently enter a disused structure and knows of a concealed danger there. The danger is one against which, in all the circumstances, the occupier may reasonably be expected to offer some protection.\n\nWhich statutory regime should be considered?","options":{"A":"The Occupiers' Liability Act 1957 applies identically to every trespasser as a lawful visitor.","B":"An occupier can never owe any duty to a trespasser.","C":"Only contract law can regulate injuries to trespassers.","D":"The Defamation Act governs hidden dangers on land.","E":"The Occupiers' Liability Act 1984, which can impose a duty to non-visitors where its statutory conditions are satisfied."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The 1984 Act governs specified duties to persons other than visitors. A duty can arise where the occupier knows of the danger, knows or has reasonable grounds to believe the person is in its vicinity, and the risk is one against which protection may reasonably be expected.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Correct. The 1984 Act governs specified duties to persons other than visitors. A duty can arise where the occupier knows of the danger, knows or has reasonable grounds to believe the person is in its vicinity, and the risk is one against which protection may reasonably be expected."},"primary_legal_authority":"Occupiers' Liability Act 1984, s 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Trespasser liability is governed by the 1984 Act's conditional statutory duty.","common_trap":"Using the visitor regime unchanged or assuming trespass automatically eliminates every occupier duty.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6e725cfac3eb2390"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0416","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Precedent — Supreme Court departure","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"The Supreme Court is asked to reconsider one of its own earlier decisions. A party argues that the Court is absolutely bound by every previous Supreme Court or House of Lords decision.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"The Supreme Court may depart from its own previous decisions when it appears right to do so, exercising that power cautiously.","B":"The Supreme Court is absolutely bound by all its previous decisions.","C":"Every lower court may overrule Supreme Court authority whenever it appears wrong.","D":"The Practice Statement abolished precedent entirely.","E":"Only Parliament can acknowledge that an earlier Supreme Court decision was wrong."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The 1966 Practice Statement permits the House of Lords, and now the Supreme Court, to depart from its own previous decisions where it appears right, while recognising the importance of certainty and cautious use of the power.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The 1966 Practice Statement permits the House of Lords, and now the Supreme Court, to depart from its own previous decisions where it appears right, while recognising the importance of certainty and cautious use of the power.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Practice Statement (Judicial Precedent) [1966] 1 WLR 1234; Supreme Court precedent practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The Supreme Court has a limited power to depart from its own precedent.","common_trap":"Confusing the strong value of precedent with absolute self-binding at the UK's highest court.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0298c320a500d0f1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0417","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — legitimate expectation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A public authority makes a clear, unambiguous and unqualified representation to an individual about the procedure it will follow. The individual relies on it, but the authority later proposes to depart from the promised procedure without adequate public-interest justification.\n\nWhich public-law doctrine is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Private contractual consideration.","B":"Procedural legitimate expectation.","C":"Adverse possession.","D":"The rule against penalties.","E":"Res ipsa loquitur."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A clear representation or settled practice can create a legitimate expectation concerning procedure. Fairness and any sufficient public-interest justification for departure must then be assessed within public-law principles.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Correct. A clear representation or settled practice can create a legitimate expectation concerning procedure. Fairness and any sufficient public-interest justification for departure must then be assessed within public-law principles.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v North and East Devon Health Authority, ex p Coughlan; legitimate-expectation principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A sufficiently clear public-authority representation can generate a procedural legitimate expectation.","common_trap":"Treating a public-law expectation as requiring all elements of a private contract.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f4c37ce6885313ba"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0418","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Separation of powers — judicial function","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A minister directs a judge how to decide the merits of an individual pending case because the government considers one outcome politically preferable.\n\nWhich constitutional principle is most directly threatened?","options":{"A":"Parliamentary privilege gives ministers authority to direct judges.","B":"Collective cabinet responsibility requires judicial compliance.","C":"Judicial independence and the separation of powers.","D":"The rule of precedent requires judges to follow ministerial instructions.","E":"Royal assent authorises executive control of individual judgments."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"An independent judiciary determines cases according to law, free from improper executive direction. Constitutional and statutory protections reinforce that judicial function.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Correct. An independent judiciary determines cases according to law, free from improper executive direction. Constitutional and statutory protections reinforce that judicial function.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Constitutional Reform Act 2005, s 3; constitutional separation-of-powers principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Executive direction of the outcome of an individual case conflicts with judicial independence and separation of powers.","common_trap":"Confusing political accountability of ministers with authority over adjudication.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"926589fd02488af0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0419","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Intestacy — spouse and issue","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person dies intestate leaving a surviving spouse and children. The estate is large enough that the statutory spouse legacy does not exhaust it. Assume the current statutory scheme applies.\n\nWhich is the best structural description of distribution?","options":{"A":"The children take the entire estate and the spouse takes nothing.","B":"The spouse always takes the entire estate whenever children survive.","C":"The estate passes equally to spouse, children and parents.","D":"The spouse takes the statutory personal chattels and statutory legacy plus a share of the residue, while the issue take the remaining share of residue on the statutory trusts.","E":"The court distributes solely according to financial need."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Under the intestacy regime, where a spouse and issue survive and the estate exceeds the statutory legacy, the spouse receives specified entitlements and the residue is divided in the statutory proportions, with issue taking under the statutory trusts.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Correct. Under the intestacy regime, where a spouse and issue survive and the estate exceeds the statutory legacy, the spouse receives specified entitlements and the residue is divided in the statutory proportions, with issue taking under the statutory trusts.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925, s 46, as amended.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Spouse-plus-issue intestacy uses the statutory legacy/chattels and residue-sharing structure.","common_trap":"Assuming either spouse or children automatically exclude the other from intestate succession.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e9526b3facd7b1f0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0420","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"IHT — potentially exempt transfers","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An individual makes an outright lifetime gift to another individual. No immediate exemption applies. The donor survives seven years from the date of the gift.\n\nWhich is the ordinary inheritance-tax consequence of the gift as a potentially exempt transfer?","options":{"A":"The gift remains chargeable at the death rate forever.","B":"Every PET is immediately taxed as a chargeable lifetime transfer.","C":"Survival for three years makes every PET exempt.","D":"PET treatment applies only to gifts to companies.","E":"The transfer becomes exempt if the donor survives the statutory seven-year period, subject to the relevant IHT rules."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"An outright qualifying transfer between individuals is generally a potentially exempt transfer. If the donor survives seven years, it becomes exempt; death within seven years can bring it into the death-transfer calculation subject to the statutory rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Correct. An outright qualifying transfer between individuals is generally a potentially exempt transfer. If the donor survives seven years, it becomes exempt; death within seven years can bring it into the death-transfer calculation subject to the statutory rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984, s 3A.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A PET becomes exempt on seven-year survival.","common_trap":"Confusing PETs with immediately chargeable lifetime transfers or with taper-relief mechanics.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"511832df905f66a9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0421","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Executors — authority before grant","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A will validly appoints an executor. Immediately on the testator's death, before probate is granted, the executor begins taking steps to preserve estate assets.\n\nWhich statement best describes the source of the executor's authority?","options":{"A":"An executor's authority derives from the will and arises on death; probate formally confirms that authority, although a grant is needed for many practical dealings.","B":"An executor has no legal authority of any kind until probate is issued.","C":"The executor's authority derives solely from beneficiaries.","D":"Only administrators can act before a grant.","E":"Probate creates the will itself."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An executor's title derives from the will and takes effect on death, whereas an administrator's authority depends on the grant. Probate is nevertheless required as proof of title for many transactions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. An executor's title derives from the will and takes effect on death, whereas an administrator's authority depends on the grant. Probate is nevertheless required as proof of title for many transactions.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925; probate principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An executor's authority originates in the will at death, with probate confirming it.","common_trap":"Treating executors and administrators as having identical sources and timing of authority.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5e25ebf9a4b2cedc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0422","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Interest on client money","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm holds a substantial amount of client money for a significant period. Its policy states that it never pays clients interest in any circumstances.\n\nWhich is the best regulatory analysis?","options":{"A":"The Accounts Rules prohibit payment of interest to clients.","B":"The firm must account to clients or third parties for a fair sum of interest on client money held, subject to a written policy and the circumstances permitted by the Accounts Rules.","C":"A firm may keep all interest regardless of amount and duration.","D":"Interest is due only if the client obtains a court order.","E":"Interest rules apply only to business accounts."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The SRA Accounts Rules require firms to account fairly for interest on client money and to have a written policy for doing so. The policy can set reasonable de minimis provisions but cannot simply disregard the rule.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Correct. The SRA Accounts Rules require firms to account fairly for interest on client money and to have a written policy for doing so. The policy can set reasonable de minimis provisions but cannot simply disregard the rule.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, r 7.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Firms need a fair written approach to interest on client money rather than a blanket no-interest rule.","common_trap":"Assuming interest generated by client money necessarily belongs to the firm.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"26aea825d1088796"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0423","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Certainty of intention","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A property owner tells a friend, 'I hope you will use this money to help my daughter,' but transfers the money outright and uses no imperative language or surrounding conduct showing an intention to impose a binding trust obligation.\n\nWhich trust requirement is most doubtful?","options":{"A":"Certainty of subject matter because money can never be trust property.","B":"Certainty of objects because a named daughter can never be a beneficiary.","C":"Certainty of intention to create a trust.","D":"Constitution, because every trust requires a deed.","E":"Perpetuity, because all family trusts are void."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A trust requires sufficiently certain intention to impose binding trust obligations. Merely precatory words of hope or confidence may be insufficient when construed in context.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Correct. A trust requires sufficiently certain intention to impose binding trust obligations. Merely precatory words of hope or confidence may be insufficient when construed in context.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Lambe v Eames; Re Adams and Kensington Vestry; certainty-of-intention principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The settlor must manifest an intention to impose binding trust obligations, not merely express a wish.","common_trap":"Treating moral or precatory language automatically as a declaration of trust.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4cd35a0d424e8155"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0424","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Knowing receipt","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A third party beneficially receives trust property transferred in breach of trust and later deals with it while possessing knowledge making retention of the benefit unconscionable.\n\nWhich personal equitable claim is potentially engaged?","options":{"A":"Dishonest assistance even if the recipient gave no assistance.","B":"Promissory estoppel.","C":"Resulting trust automatically over all assets.","D":"Knowing receipt.","E":"Proprietary estoppel."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Knowing receipt is a personal equitable liability involving beneficial receipt of assets traceable to a breach of fiduciary duty and knowledge making retention unconscionable. It is distinct from accessory liability for dishonest assistance.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Correct. Knowing receipt is a personal equitable liability involving beneficial receipt of assets traceable to a breach of fiduciary duty and knowledge making retention unconscionable. It is distinct from accessory liability for dishonest assistance.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Ivey v Genting Casinos; Byers v Saudi National Bank [2023] UKSC 51; knowing-receipt principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Beneficial receipt plus the requisite unconscionable knowledge can generate knowing-receipt liability.","common_trap":"Confusing receipt-based liability with assistance-based accessory liability.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"bb4ea052a6c04a21"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0425","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Easements — characteristics","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A landowner claims an easement benefiting one parcel over neighbouring land. The alleged right benefits the dominant land, the dominant and servient land are owned or occupied by different persons, and the right is capable of forming the subject matter of a grant.\n\nWhich classic framework is relevant?","options":{"A":"The requirements for a resulting trust.","B":"The rule in Saunders v Vautier.","C":"The test for adverse possession only.","D":"The doctrine of frustration.","E":"The characteristics identified in Re Ellenborough Park for a right to qualify as an easement."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Re Ellenborough Park identifies the classic characteristics of an easement: dominant and servient tenements, accommodation of the dominant tenement, different ownership/occupation, and a right capable of forming the subject matter of a grant.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Correct. Re Ellenborough Park identifies the classic characteristics of an easement: dominant and servient tenements, accommodation of the dominant tenement, different ownership/occupation, and a right capable of forming the subject matter of a grant."},"primary_legal_authority":"Re Ellenborough Park [1956] Ch 131.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A claimed easement must satisfy the classic Re Ellenborough Park characteristics.","common_trap":"Assuming any personal convenience exercised over neighbouring land automatically constitutes an easement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f4f163fb994adf82"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0426","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Restrictive covenants — equitable burden","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A purchaser acquires freehold land subject to a negative covenant benefiting identifiable neighbouring land. The covenant was intended to run with the land and the purchaser has the requisite notice or the burden is appropriately protected under the modern registration regime.\n\nWhich doctrine may allow the burden to bind the successor in equity?","options":{"A":"The rule originating in Tulk v Moxhay concerning restrictive covenants, subject to its requirements and modern registration rules.","B":"Positive covenants always run identically at common law.","C":"Every personal promise binds all future owners automatically.","D":"Only easements can bind successors.","E":"Restrictive covenants cease on every transfer."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Equity can enforce the burden of a restrictive covenant against successors where the doctrinal requirements are satisfied, alongside the applicable registered or unregistered land protection rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Equity can enforce the burden of a restrictive covenant against successors where the doctrinal requirements are satisfied, alongside the applicable registered or unregistered land protection rules.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Tulk v Moxhay; Law of Property Act 1925, s 79; Land Registration Act 2002.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Restrictive covenant burdens can run in equity where the Tulk requirements and priority/protection rules are met.","common_trap":"Applying the rule to positive obligations or ignoring modern land-registration requirements.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"82d0d9a5f3bfe3cc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0427","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Pre-contract searches — local authority search","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer's solicitor wants information about matters such as local land charges, planning entries and certain highway or local-authority matters affecting a residential property.\n\nWhich search is ordinarily relevant?","options":{"A":"An OS1 priority search only.","B":"A local authority search, comprising the appropriate local land charges and enquiries components.","C":"A bankruptcy search against the buyer.","D":"A Companies House incorporation search.","E":"A probate search."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A local authority search is a standard pre-contract investigation addressing specified matters held by the local authority. It serves a different function from Land Registry priority searches and environmental searches.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Correct. A local authority search is a standard pre-contract investigation addressing specified matters held by the local authority. It serves a different function from Land Registry priority searches and environmental searches.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Conveyancing practice; LLC1/CON29 framework and current local land charges arrangements.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Local authority searches investigate specified public-law and local-land matters affecting the property.","common_trap":"Confusing pre-contract due diligence with the pre-completion Land Registry priority search.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"16724a56f944c772"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0428","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"SDLT — chargeable consideration","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer acquires land for a cash price and also assumes a qualifying secured debt that counts as consideration under the SDLT rules. The buyer assumes SDLT is calculated only by reference to the cash physically transferred on completion.\n\nWhich is the best general analysis?","options":{"A":"Only physical cash can ever constitute SDLT consideration.","B":"SDLT is charged solely on the property's market value in every transaction.","C":"SDLT chargeable consideration can include forms of consideration beyond cash, including qualifying assumption or release of debt under the statutory rules.","D":"Debt is always ignored for SDLT.","E":"The seller, never the buyer, is the person liable to submit an SDLT return."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"SDLT uses the statutory concept of chargeable consideration, which can include money and money's worth and, in specified circumstances, debt assumed or released. The precise calculation depends on the statutory rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Correct. SDLT uses the statutory concept of chargeable consideration, which can include money and money's worth and, in specified circumstances, debt assumed or released. The precise calculation depends on the statutory rules.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Finance Act 2003, Sch 4; SRA FLK2 taxation specification.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Chargeable consideration for SDLT is broader than the cash price alone.","common_trap":"Assuming conveyancing cash flow and statutory chargeable consideration are identical concepts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"462428e364f1dcf1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0429","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Secondary liability — intention to assist","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant intentionally supplies equipment to the principal, intending to assist the principal to commit an offence and knowing the essential facts that make the principal's conduct criminal. The principal commits that offence.\n\nWhich basis of liability is potentially engaged?","options":{"A":"The defendant can be liable only if physically present at the scene.","B":"Mere foresight is always the sole and sufficient test after Jogee.","C":"Secondary parties can be convicted only of summary offences.","D":"Secondary liability for intentionally assisting or encouraging the principal offence, subject to the required mental elements.","E":"Assistance is irrelevant unless the defendant personally completes the actus reus of the principal offence."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Secondary liability can arise where a person intentionally assists or encourages commission of the principal offence with the requisite knowledge or belief as to the essential facts. Jogee rejected foresight as the legal test of intent, though foresight may be evidence.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Correct. Secondary liability can arise where a person intentionally assists or encourages commission of the principal offence with the requisite knowledge or belief as to the essential facts. Jogee rejected foresight as the legal test of intent, though foresight may be evidence.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Accessories and Abettors Act 1861, s 8; R v Jogee [2016] UKSC 8.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Intentional assistance or encouragement with the required mental state can found secondary liability.","common_trap":"Requiring physical presence or treating foresight alone as the post-Jogee mens rea test.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6b1d2e90f68bef43"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0430","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Allocation — either-way offences","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant charged with an either-way offence appears before the magistrates. After plea before venue, the magistrates consider the offence suitable for summary trial. The defendant nevertheless elects trial by jury.\n\nWhat ordinarily follows?","options":{"A":"The magistrates must try the case because they accepted jurisdiction.","B":"The defendant has no choice of venue for an either-way offence.","C":"The case goes directly to the Court of Appeal.","D":"Election is available only after conviction.","E":"The case is sent to the Crown Court for trial because a defendant charged with an either-way offence retains the right to elect Crown Court trial after magistrates accept jurisdiction."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"For an either-way offence, if magistrates accept jurisdiction, the defendant is then asked whether to consent to summary trial; election for Crown Court trial results in the case being sent for trial.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative either states a materially different rule, overstates the governing principle, or omits a required element identified in the key rule and authority.","E":"Correct. For an either-way offence, if magistrates accept jurisdiction, the defendant is then asked whether to consent to summary trial; election for Crown Court trial results in the case being sent for trial."},"primary_legal_authority":"Magistrates' Courts Act 1980; Crime and Disorder Act 1998; allocation procedure.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A defendant retains the election for jury trial in an either-way case after magistrates accept summary jurisdiction.","common_trap":"Assuming magistrates' acceptance of jurisdiction conclusively fixes the mode of trial.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c5a7e8292b3179c0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0431","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — substantial property transactions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company proposes to buy a valuable property from one of its directors. The property's value is substantial for the statutory regime and no exception applies. The board wants to complete immediately without involving members.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"Member approval is ordinarily required before the company enters into the substantial property transaction with the director, subject to the statutory rules and exceptions.","B":"Board approval alone is always sufficient for any transaction with a director.","C":"The transaction is automatically void and incapable of approval.","D":"Member approval is required only after completion.","E":"The regime applies only to public companies."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Companies Act 2006 section 190 restricts substantial non-cash asset transactions between a company and its director or connected person unless member approval is obtained, subject to statutory qualifications.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Companies Act 2006 section 190 restricts substantial non-cash asset transactions between a company and its director or connected person unless member approval is obtained, subject to statutory qualifications.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 190–196.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A qualifying substantial property transaction with a director ordinarily requires prior member approval.","common_trap":"Treating ordinary board management powers as displacing a specific member-approval requirement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"04d323cdc3d5c63c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0432","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Directors — duty of care, skill and diligence","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A finance director with extensive professional accounting experience approves complex accounts without reading obvious warning material. The director argues that liability should be judged only by the low level of skill personally expected of an inexperienced director.\n\nWhich standard applies?","options":{"A":"Only the director's subjective personal abilities matter.","B":"The statutory standard combines an objective minimum with the director's actual higher knowledge, skill and experience.","C":"All directors are judged solely by the standard of the most expert accountant.","D":"A director owes no duty of care when acting honestly.","E":"The duty applies only to executive directors of public companies."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 174 uses a dual objective and subjective standard: the care, skill and diligence reasonably expected of a person carrying out the director's functions, plus any greater knowledge, skill and experience the particular director actually has.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Correct. Section 174 uses a dual objective and subjective standard: the care, skill and diligence reasonably expected of a person carrying out the director's functions, plus any greater knowledge, skill and experience the particular director actually has.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, s 174.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Section 174 sets an objective floor that can be raised by the director's actual expertise.","common_trap":"Assuming a highly skilled director can reduce the standard by relying on personal inexperience or honesty.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9dd89452772fb4ed"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0433","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Corporation tax — allowable deductions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trading company incurs an expense solely for the purposes of its trade. It is revenue rather than capital expenditure, and no specific statutory prohibition applies. The company asks whether it may generally deduct the expense in calculating trading profits.\n\nWhich is the best answer?","options":{"A":"Every business payment is deductible regardless of purpose.","B":"No revenue expense is deductible for corporation tax.","C":"The expense may generally be deductible because it is incurred wholly and exclusively for the purposes of the trade, subject to the tax rules.","D":"Only capital expenditure can be deducted from trading income.","E":"A company can deduct an expense only if a shareholder personally incurred it."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Trading-profit computation generally permits revenue expenses incurred wholly and exclusively for the purposes of the trade, subject to statutory exclusions and adjustments.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Correct. Trading-profit computation generally permits revenue expenses incurred wholly and exclusively for the purposes of the trade, subject to statutory exclusions and adjustments.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Corporation Tax Act 2009, s 54.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The wholly-and-exclusively test is central to deductibility of trading revenue expenditure.","common_trap":"Assuming commercial expenditure is automatically deductible without applying the statutory purpose test.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"27f2e119bf37ab17"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0434","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — transaction at undervalue","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two years before entering insolvent liquidation, a company gives a valuable asset to a connected person for no consideration. The company was unable to pay its debts as a result of the transaction, and no statutory defence applies.\n\nWhich avoidance provision is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"A preference necessarily, because every gift is a preference.","B":"Wrongful trading only.","C":"A floating charge challenge only.","D":"A transaction at an undervalue under section 238 of the Insolvency Act 1986.","E":"The transaction cannot be challenged because ownership already transferred."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A company enters a transaction at an undervalue if it makes a gift or receives consideration significantly less than the value provided. Within the relevant period and insolvency conditions, a liquidator or administrator can seek statutory relief.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Correct. A company enters a transaction at an undervalue if it makes a gift or receives consideration significantly less than the value provided. Within the relevant period and insolvency conditions, a liquidator or administrator can seek statutory relief.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, ss 238 and 240.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A gratuitous or materially under-value pre-insolvency transaction can be challenged under section 238 if the statutory conditions are met.","common_trap":"Confusing an undervalue transaction with a preference, which focuses on improving a creditor's position and desire to prefer.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1d432b337eef6318"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0435","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Default judgment — discretionary setting aside of regular judgment","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant failed to respond in time and default judgment was regularly entered. The defendant promptly applies to set it aside and produces evidence of a real prospect of successfully defending the claim.\n\nWhich rule governs the application?","options":{"A":"The discretionary regime for setting aside or varying a regularly entered default judgment, including the real-prospect/other-good-reason test and the requirement to act promptly.","B":"The mandatory rule for a judgment that was wrongly entered applies automatically.","C":"A regular default judgment can never be set aside.","D":"The defendant must first succeed at a full trial before seeking relief.","E":"The claimant's consent is the only legal basis on which the judgment can be set aside."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Where default judgment was regularly entered, CPR 13.3 gives the court discretion to set it aside or vary it where the defendant has a real prospect of successfully defending or there is another good reason, with promptness relevant.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Where default judgment was regularly entered, CPR 13.3 gives the court discretion to set it aside or vary it where the defendant has a real prospect of successfully defending or there is another good reason, with promptness relevant.","B":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. The key distinction is between mandatory setting aside under CPR 13.2 and discretionary relief from a regularly entered judgment under CPR 13.3.","C":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. The key distinction is between mandatory setting aside under CPR 13.2 and discretionary relief from a regularly entered judgment under CPR 13.3.","D":"Incorrect. This gives the wrong legal consequence for the facts and does not satisfy the governing test. The key distinction is between mandatory setting aside under CPR 13.2 and discretionary relief from a regularly entered judgment under CPR 13.3.","E":"Incorrect. This incorrectly makes a non-exclusive factor the sole legal test. The key distinction is between mandatory setting aside under CPR 13.2 and discretionary relief from a regularly entered judgment under CPR 13.3."},"primary_legal_authority":"CPR r 13.3","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The key distinction is between mandatory setting aside under CPR 13.2 and discretionary relief from a regularly entered judgment under CPR 13.3.","common_trap":"Applying the headline doctrine without identifying the distinct consequence, exception or procedural stage tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7ee1286257e6fa10"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0436","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Interim injunctions — American Cyanamid","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant seeks an interim prohibitory injunction pending trial. There is a serious issue to be tried, and the court must decide whether interim relief should preserve the position.\n\nWhich consideration forms part of the orthodox approach?","options":{"A":"Whether damages would be an adequate remedy and where the balance of convenience lies, within the American Cyanamid framework.","B":"The claimant must prove the entire case conclusively at the interim hearing.","C":"An injunction must issue whenever the claimant pleads breach of contract.","D":"The court may never consider harm to the defendant.","E":"Interim injunctions are available only after final judgment."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The American Cyanamid approach asks whether there is a serious question to be tried, considers adequacy of damages for the parties, and assesses the balance of convenience and other relevant factors.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The American Cyanamid approach asks whether there is a serious question to be tried, considers adequacy of damages for the parties, and assesses the balance of convenience and other relevant factors.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"American Cyanamid Co v Ethicon Ltd; Civil Procedure Rules, Part 25.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Interim relief ordinarily turns on serious issue, adequacy of damages and balance of convenience rather than a mini-trial.","common_trap":"Requiring final proof of liability at the interlocutory stage.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3605c86ecb004c45"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0437","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Dispute Resolution — amendment after limitation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"After the limitation period expires, a claimant seeks to add a genuinely new cause of action arising from substantially the same facts already in issue in the existing proceedings.\n\nWhich consideration is central to whether the amendment may be permitted?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","C":"The post-limitation amendment regime allows certain new claims where the statutory and CPR tests are satisfied, including the 'same or substantially the same facts' requirement.","D":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Post-limitation amendment is specially controlled rather than governed only by the ordinary amendment discretion.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Correct. Post-limitation amendment is specially controlled rather than governed only by the ordinary amendment discretion.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Limitation Act 1980, s 35; CPR r 17.4","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Post-limitation amendment is specially controlled rather than governed only by the ordinary amendment discretion.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9a355eac898296cd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0438","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Conduct — undertakings","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor gives a clear professional undertaking to another firm to discharge a registered charge from completion monies. After completion, compliance becomes inconvenient and expensive, but remains possible.\n\nWhich is the best professional position?","options":{"A":"Undertakings are merely informal aspirations with no professional significance.","B":"The solicitor may withdraw unilaterally after receiving completion monies.","C":"The solicitor must perform the undertaking within the agreed time or, if none, within a reasonable time; inconvenience does not permit it simply to be ignored.","D":"Only undertakings given to courts matter professionally.","E":"The undertaking ends automatically when the client's retainer ends."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The SRA Code requires solicitors to perform undertakings they give within the agreed timescale or otherwise within a reasonable time. Professional undertakings are serious obligations and may also be enforceable through other mechanisms.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Correct. The SRA Code requires solicitors to perform undertakings they give within the agreed timescale or otherwise within a reasonable time. Professional undertakings are serious obligations and may also be enforceable through other mechanisms.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 1.3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A solicitor must honour a professional undertaking notwithstanding later inconvenience.","common_trap":"Treating an undertaking as revocable merely because performance becomes commercially inconvenient.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b55db3211e4c00b0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0439","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Client care — costs information","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"At the outset of a matter, a solicitor can provide a reliable estimate of likely overall costs and explain the charging basis. The solicitor proposes giving no costs information unless the client later asks.\n\nWhich is the better regulatory approach?","options":{"A":"Costs information is required only after the final bill.","B":"A solicitor must always quote a fixed fee even when impossible.","C":"Costs information is optional for individual clients.","D":"The client should receive the best possible information about how the matter will be priced and its likely overall cost, both at engagement and as appropriate as the matter progresses.","E":"Only the court may discuss legal costs with a client."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The SRA Code requires clients to receive the best possible information about pricing and likely overall cost at engagement and when appropriate during the matter.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Correct. The SRA Code requires clients to receive the best possible information about pricing and likely overall cost at engagement and when appropriate during the matter.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 8.7.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Client care includes timely, intelligible information about pricing and likely overall costs.","common_trap":"Assuming costs transparency arises only when a client expressly requests it.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fe1593b3e5f05037"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0440","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Terms — innominate terms","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contract term is neither classified by statute nor clearly intended as a condition or warranty. A breach occurs, but its consequences are relatively minor and do not substantially deprive the innocent party of the contract's benefit.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"Every breach of an innominate term automatically permits termination.","B":"An innominate term can never support damages.","C":"All unlabelled terms are conditions.","D":"The parties' consequences are irrelevant to classification and remedy.","E":"If the term is innominate, termination depends on the seriousness of the consequences; a minor breach may justify damages but not termination."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"For an innominate term, the remedial consequence depends on the gravity and effect of the breach. Termination is available only where the breach substantially deprives the innocent party of the contract's intended benefit.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Correct. For an innominate term, the remedial consequence depends on the gravity and effect of the breach. Termination is available only where the breach substantially deprives the innocent party of the contract's intended benefit."},"primary_legal_authority":"Hongkong Fir Shipping Co Ltd v Kawasaki Kisen Kaisha Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Breach of an innominate term permits termination only when its consequences are sufficiently serious.","common_trap":"Treating every contractual breach as conferring an automatic right to terminate.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7b5b6be3af33c00f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0441","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Economic duress — causation and illegitimate pressure","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A supplier threatens unlawfully to breach an existing contract unless the customer pays substantially more. The customer has no practical alternative and the threat is a significant cause of its agreement to pay.\n\nWhich doctrine is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Economic duress, requiring illegitimate pressure that causes the claimant to enter the transaction and leaves no practical choice in the relevant sense.","B":"Undue influence necessarily, because all commercial pressure is relational influence.","C":"Frustration.","D":"Promissory estoppel only.","E":"The postal rule."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Unlawful threats to breach a contract can amount to illegitimate pressure. Economic duress examines the nature of the pressure, causation and whether the victim had a practical alternative, among the relevant factors.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Unlawful threats to breach a contract can amount to illegitimate pressure. Economic duress examines the nature of the pressure, causation and whether the victim had a practical alternative, among the relevant factors.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Universe Tankships Inc of Monrovia v ITWF (The Universe Sentinel); DSND Subsea Ltd v Petroleum Geo Services ASA.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Illegitimate commercial pressure that induces agreement in circumstances of no practical choice can constitute economic duress.","common_trap":"Equating ordinary hard bargaining with duress or applying an unrelated equitable doctrine.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a974adc2fd6b7a59"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0442","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Remedies — mitigation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"After the defendant breaches a supply contract, the claimant could reasonably obtain a substitute at modest additional cost but deliberately does nothing, allowing avoidable losses to accumulate.\n\nHow does mitigation affect damages?","options":{"A":"The claimant must take every imaginable step regardless of cost or risk.","B":"The claimant cannot ordinarily recover loss that could reasonably have been avoided after the breach.","C":"Failure to mitigate extinguishes the entire cause of action automatically.","D":"Mitigation is relevant only to tort, never contract.","E":"The defendant bears all post-breach losses even if deliberately increased by the claimant."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The innocent party must take reasonable steps to mitigate loss. The rule does not impose an onerous duty, but losses reasonably avoidable through mitigation are not recoverable.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Correct. The innocent party must take reasonable steps to mitigate loss. The rule does not impose an onerous duty, but losses reasonably avoidable through mitigation are not recoverable.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"British Westinghouse Electric v Underground Electric Railways.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contract damages exclude losses the claimant could reasonably have avoided.","common_trap":"Treating mitigation either as requiring extraordinary measures or as irrelevant to recoverable loss.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6bd92aec49a27b57"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0443","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Professional negligence — standard of care","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor advises on an ordinary transaction. The advice falls below the standard reasonably expected of a competent solicitor performing that work, causing recoverable loss.\n\nWhich standard is principally relevant?","options":{"A":"The solicitor avoids liability whenever acting honestly.","B":"The standard is whatever the particular solicitor personally believed was adequate.","C":"The objective standard of the reasonably competent professional carrying out that work, informed by professional-negligence principles.","D":"Professionals are strictly liable for every adverse outcome.","E":"Only the client's subjective expectations determine breach."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Professionals must exercise the reasonable skill and care expected of an ordinarily competent member of the profession undertaking the relevant work. Honest intentions alone do not answer whether the professional standard was met.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Correct. Professionals must exercise the reasonable skill and care expected of an ordinarily competent member of the profession undertaking the relevant work. Honest intentions alone do not answer whether the professional standard was met.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Bolam v Friern Hospital Management Committee; Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board where applicable; professional negligence principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Professional negligence uses an objective competent-professional standard, applied to the particular task.","common_trap":"Replacing the professional standard with subjective good faith or a guarantee of successful outcome.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"07cd74cbf2ea1284"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0444","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Causation — but-for test","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant proves that a defendant breached a duty of care, but the evidence shows the claimant would have suffered exactly the same injury at the same time even if the defendant had exercised reasonable care.\n\nWhat is the ordinary causation consequence?","options":{"A":"Breach of duty automatically establishes causation.","B":"Causation is irrelevant once negligence is shown.","C":"The claimant always recovers nominal personal-injury damages.","D":"Factual causation ordinarily fails because the breach was not a but-for cause of the injury.","E":"The defendant is liable whenever the injury was foreseeable, regardless of causation."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The ordinary factual-causation inquiry asks whether the damage would have occurred but for the defendant's breach. If it would have occurred identically in any event, factual causation ordinarily fails, subject to exceptional doctrines.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Correct. The ordinary factual-causation inquiry asks whether the damage would have occurred but for the defendant's breach. If it would have occurred identically in any event, factual causation ordinarily fails, subject to exceptional doctrines.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Barnett v Chelsea & Kensington Hospital Management Committee.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A breach must ordinarily be a factual cause of the damage, commonly tested by the but-for test.","common_trap":"Assuming proof of breach and foreseeability removes the need to establish causation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a1fb9c3609688b89"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0445","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Private nuisance — locality","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A resident complains of noise from an activity in an area characterised by substantial existing commercial and industrial use. The court must decide whether the interference with use and enjoyment of land is unreasonable.\n\nWhich factor may legitimately affect that assessment?","options":{"A":"Locality is always irrelevant to nuisance.","B":"Any noise audible from neighbouring land is automatically actionable.","C":"Planning permission automatically authorises every nuisance.","D":"The claimant's ownership period is the sole relevant factor.","E":"The character of the locality, particularly for amenity nuisance, while recognising that locality does not make physical damage reasonable."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The character of the neighbourhood is relevant to the reasonableness of amenity interference: what is unreasonable in a quiet residential area may differ in an industrial locality. Physical damage is treated differently.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Correct. The character of the neighbourhood is relevant to the reasonableness of amenity interference: what is unreasonable in a quiet residential area may differ in an industrial locality. Physical damage is treated differently."},"primary_legal_authority":"Sturges v Bridgman; Coventry v Lawrence.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Locality can inform whether amenity interference is unreasonable in private nuisance.","common_trap":"Applying an identical noise threshold regardless of neighbourhood character or treating planning permission as automatic immunity.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ec17afe60d16aa9f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0446","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Human Rights Act — public authorities","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A local authority exercising a statutory public function interferes with a Convention right. No primary legislation requires it to act incompatibly.\n\nWhich Human Rights Act provision is central?","options":{"A":"Section 6, under which it is unlawful for a public authority to act incompatibly with a Convention right unless a statutory exception applies.","B":"Section 4 automatically invalidates every public-authority decision.","C":"The HRA applies only to Parliament.","D":"Public authorities are never directly bound by Convention rights domestically.","E":"Only Strasbourg can consider public-authority conduct."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Section 6 makes it unlawful for a public authority to act incompatibly with Convention rights, subject to the statutory qualifications, including circumstances involving primary legislation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Section 6 makes it unlawful for a public authority to act incompatibly with Convention rights, subject to the statutory qualifications, including circumstances involving primary legislation.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, s 6.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Public authorities are directly subject to the section 6 compatibility duty within the statutory framework.","common_trap":"Confusing the declaration-of-incompatibility mechanism for legislation with the direct duty governing public authorities.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f5ec3c13bfaddcf2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0447","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — improper purpose","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An Act gives a regulator a power to acquire property for a specified statutory purpose. The regulator uses the power primarily to punish a political critic, a purpose unrelated to the statute.\n\nWhich ground of review is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Wednesbury irrationality is the only possible public-law ground.","B":"Illegality through use of a statutory power for an improper purpose.","C":"The decision is immune because the regulator possesses the power in general terms.","D":"Promissory estoppel.","E":"Private nuisance."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A statutory power must be exercised for the purposes for which Parliament conferred it. Using it to pursue an extraneous or improper purpose is an error going to legality.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Correct. A statutory power must be exercised for the purposes for which Parliament conferred it. Using it to pursue an extraneous or improper purpose is an error going to legality.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Padfield v Minister of Agriculture; public-law improper-purpose principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Exercise of statutory power for an unauthorised purpose is reviewable for illegality.","common_trap":"Assuming possession of a statutory power permits its use for any objective the decision-maker prefers.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6ae79e50424ff4d4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0448","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Statutory interpretation — purposive approach","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A statutory phrase is capable of more than one plausible reading. One reading better advances the evident legislative purpose and fits the statutory scheme.\n\nWhich interpretive approach focuses most directly on that consideration?","options":{"A":"The literal approach requires courts always to ignore statutory purpose.","B":"The rule of precedent determines the meaning without interpreting the statute.","C":"The purposive approach.","D":"Judicial review prohibits consideration of purpose.","E":"The separation of powers requires choosing the longest possible meaning."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The purposive approach interprets statutory language in light of the purpose and scheme of the legislation, while remaining anchored to the enacted text and legitimate interpretive materials.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Correct. The purposive approach interprets statutory language in light of the purpose and scheme of the legislation, while remaining anchored to the enacted text and legitimate interpretive materials.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Statutory interpretation principles; Pepper v Hart where its separate conditions for Hansard use are relevant.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Statutory purpose can guide selection between plausible readings of enacted language.","common_trap":"Treating purposive interpretation as a licence to disregard the statutory text altogether.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"feb8ac2707ea7a29"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0449","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — revocation by marriage","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person makes a valid will and later marries. The will was not expressed to be made in contemplation of that particular marriage and no statutory exception applies.\n\nWhat is the ordinary effect?","options":{"A":"Marriage never affects an existing will.","B":"The will is revoked only if the new spouse signs a revocation.","C":"Only divorce can revoke a will.","D":"The marriage revokes the earlier will under the statutory rule.","E":"Marriage converts every specific gift into residue."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Subject to the statutory exceptions, marriage revokes a will. A will expressed to be made in contemplation of a particular marriage can avoid that result where the statutory requirements are met.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Correct. Subject to the statutory exceptions, marriage revokes a will. A will expressed to be made in contemplation of a particular marriage can avoid that result where the statutory requirements are met.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837, s 18.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Marriage ordinarily revokes a pre-existing will unless a statutory exception applies.","common_trap":"Assuming testamentary documents remain unaffected by a subsequent marriage in all circumstances.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c887cce1a10e6ffa"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0450","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Estate administration — assent","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Personal representatives have completed the necessary administration work relating to a specific estate property and decide to vest it in the beneficiary entitled under the will.\n\nWhich instrument or act is commonly used to vest the legal estate in the beneficiary?","options":{"A":"A Part 36 offer.","B":"A codicil executed by the deceased after death.","C":"A charging order.","D":"An acknowledgment of service.","E":"An assent by the personal representatives, complying with applicable formalities."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Personal representatives may assent to property vesting in the person entitled, with statutory and land-registration formalities applying according to the property involved.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Correct. Personal representatives may assent to property vesting in the person entitled, with statutory and land-registration formalities applying according to the property involved."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925, s 36; land-registration requirements.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An assent is the estate-administration mechanism by which personal representatives vest estate property in the entitled beneficiary.","common_trap":"Confusing post-death administration machinery with instruments used in litigation or lifetime testamentary amendment.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f4f2c5ae26bdcc93"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0451","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"IHT — spouse exemption","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A UK-domiciled deceased leaves a substantial outright legacy to a UK-domiciled surviving spouse. No unusual statutory limitation applies.\n\nWhich inheritance-tax treatment is ordinarily relevant?","options":{"A":"The spouse exemption generally exempts the transfer from inheritance tax.","B":"The legacy is always taxed at the death rate without exemption.","C":"Spouse exemption applies only to lifetime gifts.","D":"Marriage is irrelevant to inheritance tax.","E":"The surviving spouse must disclaim the gift to obtain exemption."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Transfers between spouses or civil partners are generally exempt from IHT, subject to statutory qualifications, including special rules where domicile status differs.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Transfers between spouses or civil partners are generally exempt from IHT, subject to statutory qualifications, including special rules where domicile status differs.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984, s 18.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An outright transfer to a qualifying surviving spouse is ordinarily covered by the spouse exemption.","common_trap":"Ignoring a central IHT exemption when calculating the taxable estate.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1cbc4673f8637ba3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0452","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Client money — prompt return","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A matter has concluded and the firm still holds a small residual balance belonging to the client. The client can readily be located and there is no proper reason to retain the money.\n\nWhat do the Accounts Rules ordinarily require?","options":{"A":"The firm may retain residual balances indefinitely.","B":"The firm should return client money promptly as soon as there is no longer a proper reason to hold it.","C":"The balance automatically becomes the firm's money when the file closes.","D":"Client money can be donated without contacting an available client.","E":"Residual balances need be reviewed only every ten years."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Accounts Rules require client money to be returned promptly once there is no longer a proper reason to hold it, with separate provisions addressing genuinely unclaimed residual balances.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Correct. The Accounts Rules require client money to be returned promptly once there is no longer a proper reason to hold it, with separate provisions addressing genuinely unclaimed residual balances.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, r 2.5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Client money should not remain in client account after the proper purpose for holding it has ended.","common_trap":"Treating administrative convenience or file closure as transferring beneficial ownership to the firm.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4b80cfb5172163b1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0453","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Constitution — Strong v Bird","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A donor intends to make an immediate gift to a donee but fails to complete the transfer. The intention to give continues unchanged until the donor's death, and the donee is then appointed executor so that legal title vests in the donee in that capacity.\n\nWhich doctrine may perfect the imperfect lifetime gift if its requirements are met?","options":{"A":"The rule in Saunders v Vautier.","B":"The beneficiary principle.","C":"The rule in Strong v Bird.","D":"The doctrine of frustration.","E":"The rule in Tulk v Moxhay."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Strong v Bird can perfect an imperfect inter vivos gift where the donor intended an immediate gift, that intention continued until death, and legal title later vests in the intended donee as executor or administrator.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Correct. Strong v Bird can perfect an imperfect inter vivos gift where the donor intended an immediate gift, that intention continued until death, and legal title later vests in the intended donee as executor or administrator.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Strong v Bird (1874) LR 18 Eq 315.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Continuous donative intention plus later vesting of legal title in the donee as personal representative can perfect the gift.","common_trap":"Assuming every imperfect gift is cured at death regardless of continuing intention and vesting of title.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5cb443b3fc978e4c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0454","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Beneficiaries — Saunders v Vautier","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trust fund is held for one adult beneficiary absolutely. The beneficiary has full capacity and is solely entitled to the entire beneficial interest, but the trust instrument directs payment at age 30. The beneficiary is 25 and wants the fund now.\n\nWhich principle is most relevant?","options":{"A":"The settlor's stated age can never be displaced even by the sole absolutely entitled adult beneficiary.","B":"A beneficiary can terminate a trust only with court permission in every case.","C":"The rule applies only to charitable trusts.","D":"The rule in Saunders v Vautier may allow the absolutely entitled adult beneficiary to terminate the trust and require transfer before the stated age.","E":"The trustee becomes beneficial owner until the beneficiary reaches 30."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Where all beneficiaries are of full age and capacity and together absolutely entitled to the trust property, they can ordinarily require the trustees to transfer the property and terminate the trust notwithstanding postponement in the instrument.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Correct. Where all beneficiaries are of full age and capacity and together absolutely entitled to the trust property, they can ordinarily require the trustees to transfer the property and terminate the trust notwithstanding postponement in the instrument.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Saunders v Vautier (1841) 4 Beav 115.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Absolute adult beneficial entitlement can permit early termination under Saunders v Vautier.","common_trap":"Treating administrative postponement as overriding the collective rights of absolutely entitled competent beneficiaries.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d1077b7ff73a3b7a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0455","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Co-ownership — overreaching","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two trustees of land sell the registered property to a purchaser, and the purchase money is paid to both trustees. A beneficiary's equitable interest under the trust is capable of being overreached.\n\nWhat is the ordinary effect of valid overreaching?","options":{"A":"The beneficiary's equitable interest necessarily remains attached to the land forever.","B":"Overreaching destroys the beneficiary's interest without substituting any interest in proceeds.","C":"Payment to two trustees is irrelevant to overreaching.","D":"Only legal easements can be overreached.","E":"The beneficiary's equitable interest is detached from the land and transferred to the sale proceeds, so the purchaser can take the land free of that interest."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Where statutory conditions are satisfied, including payment of capital money to at least two trustees or a trust corporation, overreaching shifts qualifying beneficial interests from the land to the purchase money.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Correct. Where statutory conditions are satisfied, including payment of capital money to at least two trustees or a trust corporation, overreaching shifts qualifying beneficial interests from the land to the purchase money."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, ss 2 and 27; City of London Building Society v Flegg.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Overreaching protects purchasers while preserving the beneficiary's interest in the proceeds.","common_trap":"Confusing overreaching with extinguishment of the beneficial interest or overlooking the two-trustee/trust-corporation machinery.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e006fe5fe038f5f5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0456","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Adverse possession — registered land","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A squatter has been in adverse possession of registered land for ten years and applies to be registered as proprietor. The registered proprietor is notified and serves a valid counter-notice. No statutory exception favouring the squatter applies.\n\nWhich is the best general analysis?","options":{"A":"The application will ordinarily be rejected following the counter-notice unless one of the Schedule 6 conditions for registration despite objection is satisfied.","B":"Ten years' adverse possession automatically transfers registered title without any application.","C":"The registered proprietor cannot object to an adverse-possession application.","D":"The Limitation Act automatically extinguishes registered title after twelve years in the same way as unregistered land.","E":"Adverse possession is impossible for registered land."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The LRA 2002 regime generally allows an application after ten years' adverse possession but protects the registered proprietor through notice and counter-notice; registration despite objection depends on specified Schedule 6 conditions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The LRA 2002 regime generally allows an application after ten years' adverse possession but protects the registered proprietor through notice and counter-notice; registration despite objection depends on specified Schedule 6 conditions.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002, Sch 6.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Ten years permits an application, not automatic acquisition, and the registered proprietor can normally defeat it unless an exception applies.","common_trap":"Importing the old unregistered-land limitation model into the LRA 2002 registered-land regime.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"64783d85c050e681"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0457","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Contracts — deposit","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"On exchange of a residential sale contract incorporating standard conditions, the buyer pays the usual contractual deposit. The parties have not agreed unusual terms.\n\nWhat is the deposit's principal contractual function?","options":{"A":"It transfers legal title to the buyer immediately.","B":"It is part-payment of the price and also provides security for the buyer's performance, with consequences on default governed by the contract and law.","C":"It is always refundable regardless of buyer default.","D":"It is the seller's solicitor's personal fee.","E":"It replaces the need to pay the balance on completion."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A contractual deposit is ordinarily part-payment of the purchase price and earnest/security for performance. Its treatment if the transaction fails depends on the contract, the nature of the default and equitable/statutory principles.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Correct. A contractual deposit is ordinarily part-payment of the purchase price and earnest/security for performance. Its treatment if the transaction fails depends on the contract, the nature of the default and equitable/statutory principles.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Standard Conditions of Sale; Law of Property Act 1925, s 49(2) where relevant.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A conveyancing deposit combines part-payment with security for contractual performance.","common_trap":"Treating exchange deposit as either legal-title consideration completing the transfer or an automatically refundable holding sum.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2975e918540624e2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0458","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Planning and building regulations","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer discovers that a recent extension had planning permission but there is no evidence of required building-regulations approval. The seller argues that planning permission necessarily proves compliance with building regulations.\n\nWhich is the best advice?","options":{"A":"Planning permission automatically constitutes building-regulations approval.","B":"Building regulations apply only where planning permission is refused.","C":"Planning control and building regulations are distinct regimes; planning permission does not itself establish building-regulations compliance.","D":"Neither regime can affect conveyancing due diligence.","E":"A local authority search can never reveal relevant entries."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Planning control concerns development and land use, while building regulations govern matters such as construction standards and safety. A project may require compliance with both independently.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Correct. Planning control concerns development and land use, while building regulations govern matters such as construction standards and safety. A project may require compliance with both independently.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Town and Country Planning Act 1990; Building Act 1984 and Building Regulations 2010.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Planning permission and building-regulations approval address different legal requirements.","common_trap":"Assuming approval under one public-law regime conclusively satisfies the other.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d0772996135cac05"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0459","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Criminal damage — recklessness","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant deliberately starts a small fire beside property belonging to another. The defendant actually foresees a risk that the fire will spread and damage that property but unreasonably takes the risk. Damage results.\n\nWhich mens rea concept is most directly relevant to basic criminal damage?","options":{"A":"Negligence alone is always sufficient for criminal damage.","B":"Recklessness is assessed solely by risks a reasonable person would have foreseen even if the defendant did not.","C":"Criminal damage always requires an intention to destroy the property completely.","D":"Subjective recklessness: awareness of a risk and, in the circumstances known to the defendant, unreasonable taking of that risk.","E":"Dishonesty is an element of every criminal-damage offence."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"For basic criminal damage, recklessness follows the subjective approach in R v G: the defendant must be aware of a risk and it must be unreasonable, in the circumstances known to the defendant, to take it.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Correct. For basic criminal damage, recklessness follows the subjective approach in R v G: the defendant must be aware of a risk and it must be unreasonable, in the circumstances known to the defendant, to take it.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Damage Act 1971, s 1(1); R v G [2003] UKHL 50.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Basic criminal-damage recklessness requires the defendant's awareness of the relevant risk.","common_trap":"Applying the former objective Caldwell approach or adding a dishonesty requirement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7b401f6cf055ae22"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0460","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Evidence — confession and oppression","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"During police questioning, a suspect makes a confession after officers use oppressive treatment. The prosecution wants to rely on the confession at trial.\n\nWhich statutory rule is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Every confession is automatically admissible if written down.","B":"Only section 78 can ever affect confession evidence.","C":"Oppression is relevant only to sentence after conviction.","D":"A confession can be excluded only if the defendant proves factual innocence.","E":"PACE section 76, under which a confession obtained by oppression, or in consequence of things likely to render it unreliable, must be excluded where the statutory conditions are established."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"PACE section 76 specifically governs confessions and requires exclusion where the prosecution cannot prove beyond reasonable doubt that the confession was not obtained by oppression or in consequence of words or conduct likely to render it unreliable once the issue is properly raised.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative applies a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Correct. PACE section 76 specifically governs confessions and requires exclusion where the prosecution cannot prove beyond reasonable doubt that the confession was not obtained by oppression or in consequence of words or conduct likely to render it unreliable once the issue is properly raised."},"primary_legal_authority":"Police and Criminal Evidence Act 1984, s 76.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Confessions obtained through oppression or reliability-compromising conduct are subject to the mandatory section 76 exclusion regime.","common_trap":"Treating confession admissibility as purely discretionary or unrelated to the manner in which the confession was obtained.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"048ed87a21e02c61"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0461","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — redemption and buyback of shares","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company proposes to buy back some of its own fully paid shares. The directors assume that an ordinary asset purchase agreement signed by the board is enough and that the statutory capital-maintenance regime is irrelevant.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"A company purchase of its own shares must comply with the Companies Act statutory regime, including the applicable approval, funding and procedural requirements.","B":"A company may buy its own shares on any terms solely by board decision.","C":"A buyback is legally identical to a transfer between two shareholders.","D":"A company can purchase only partly paid shares.","E":"The statutory buyback rules apply only to listed companies."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A company's acquisition of its own shares is an exception to ordinary capital-maintenance principles and is governed by Part 18 of the Companies Act 2006. The precise approval and funding route depends on the type of buyback and company.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A company's acquisition of its own shares is an exception to ordinary capital-maintenance principles and is governed by Part 18 of the Companies Act 2006. The precise approval and funding route depends on the type of buyback and company.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, Part 18, including ss 690–708.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A share buyback requires compliance with the specific statutory regime rather than ordinary board contracting powers alone.","common_trap":"Treating a company's purchase of its own shares as an ordinary asset acquisition or shareholder-to-shareholder transfer.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d7af7a31f8e36589"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0462","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — directors' conflicts and authorisation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A director of a private company identifies a business opportunity that falls within the company's activities and wants to exploit it personally. The company's constitution does not invalidate the statutory private-company authorisation mechanism, and the interested director does not participate in the authorisation decision.\n\nWhich route may prevent the opportunity from amounting to a breach of the statutory conflict duty?","options":{"A":"The director may simply keep the opportunity secret.","B":"Proper authorisation of the conflict by the non-conflicted directors in accordance with section 175 and the company's constitution.","C":"Only retrospective approval by Companies House can authorise the conflict.","D":"The conflict duty does not apply to opportunities the company has not yet contracted to pursue.","E":"Resignation after taking the opportunity automatically erases any breach."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"For a private company, section 175 permits board authorisation of certain conflict situations where the constitution does not invalidate that mechanism and the statutory quorum/voting requirements are observed.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Correct. For a private company, section 175 permits board authorisation of certain conflict situations where the constitution does not invalidate that mechanism and the statutory quorum/voting requirements are observed.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, s 175(5)–(6).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A private-company conflict can in appropriate circumstances be authorised prospectively under the section 175 mechanism.","common_trap":"Assuming a corporate opportunity is outside the conflict duty merely because the company has not yet entered a contract for it.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"aff03a3eeac9b571"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0463","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — preference: desire to prefer","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An insolvent company pays a debt guaranteed by a director shortly before liquidation, improving the director's position. The payment is challenged as a preference.\n\nWhat additional element is generally important to a preference challenge?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","C":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","D":"The company must have been influenced by a desire to put the relevant creditor, surety or guarantor into a better position on an insolvent liquidation, subject to the statutory presumptions for connected persons.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A preference focuses on both the effect of the transaction and the statutory 'desire to prefer' requirement.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Correct. A preference focuses on both the effect of the transaction and the statutory 'desire to prefer' requirement.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, s 239","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A preference focuses on both the effect of the transaction and the statutory 'desire to prefer' requirement.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"85e7a8cc33bd28b9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0464","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Tax — chargeable gains of companies","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trading company sells an investment asset at a gain. The finance director proposes calculating corporation tax only on the company's trading income and ignoring the gain because companies do not pay capital gains tax as individuals do.\n\nWhich is the best general analysis?","options":{"A":"Companies pay no tax on disposals of capital assets.","B":"The company pays individual capital gains tax separately from corporation tax.","C":"Every disposal gain is treated as trading turnover.","D":"A company's chargeable gains are brought within the corporation tax regime and must be included in the corporation tax computation subject to applicable rules and reliefs.","E":"Chargeable gains are relevant only to partnerships."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Companies are generally subject to corporation tax on chargeable gains rather than the separate capital gains tax charged on individuals. The gain is computed under the applicable chargeable-gains rules and included in the corporation tax calculation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Correct. Companies are generally subject to corporation tax on chargeable gains rather than the separate capital gains tax charged on individuals. The gain is computed under the applicable chargeable-gains rules and included in the corporation tax calculation.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Corporation Tax Act 2009 and Taxation of Chargeable Gains Act 1992; corporation tax on chargeable gains.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Corporate chargeable gains form part of the corporation tax basis of charge.","common_trap":"Confusing the absence of a separate corporate CGT charge with exemption of companies' chargeable gains.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f98d58e4eb8ea853"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0465","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Statements of case — strike out","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant's particulars disclose no legally recognisable cause of action even if every pleaded fact is assumed true. The defendant wants the court to dispose of the defective pleading without a full trial.\n\nWhich case-management power is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Default judgment under CPR Part 12.","B":"A freezing injunction under Part 25.","C":"A charging order under Part 73.","D":"A Part 36 offer.","E":"Strike out under CPR 3.4 for a statement of case disclosing no reasonable grounds for bringing the claim."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"CPR 3.4 permits the court to strike out a statement of case where it discloses no reasonable grounds for bringing or defending the claim, as well as on other specified grounds.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Correct. CPR 3.4 permits the court to strike out a statement of case where it discloses no reasonable grounds for bringing or defending the claim, as well as on other specified grounds."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 3.4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A legally defective statement of case can be struck out under CPR 3.4.","common_trap":"Confusing strike out of a defective pleading with summary judgment's assessment of prospects on a properly articulated case.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"dcf8ec7682261377"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0466","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Witness evidence — hearsay notice in civil proceedings","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A party intends to rely at a civil trial on a witness statement containing an out-of-court statement for the truth of its contents. The evidence is admissible under the modern civil hearsay regime, but procedural requirements apply.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Civil hearsay is not automatically inadmissible, but the party must comply with the applicable notice and procedural rules and the court may consider hearsay features when assessing weight.","B":"All hearsay remains absolutely inadmissible in civil proceedings.","C":"Hearsay is admissible only if the maker is dead.","D":"No procedural notice can ever be required for civil hearsay.","E":"Hearsay automatically carries the same weight as live tested evidence."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Civil Evidence Act 1995 abolished the general exclusionary rule for hearsay in civil proceedings. CPR Part 33 regulates notice and related procedure, while statutory factors can affect weight.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Civil Evidence Act 1995 abolished the general exclusionary rule for hearsay in civil proceedings. CPR Part 33 regulates notice and related procedure, while statutory factors can affect weight.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Evidence Act 1995; Civil Procedure Rules, Part 33.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Civil hearsay can be admissible while remaining subject to procedural safeguards and weight assessment.","common_trap":"Importing the historic absolute hearsay exclusion into modern civil procedure or assuming admissibility determines weight.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"39f6339b76a2533b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0467","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Offers to settle — defendant's Part 36 offer","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant wins at trial but obtains a judgment less advantageous than a defendant's valid Part 36 offer. The offer remained open and the relevant period expired before trial. It would not be unjust to apply the normal consequences.\n\nWhich costs consequence ordinarily follows from the end of the relevant period?","options":{"A":"The claimant automatically receives indemnity costs for the whole case.","B":"The defendant is ordinarily entitled to its costs from expiry of the relevant period, together with the other consequences specified by CPR 36.17, because the claimant failed to beat the defendant's offer.","C":"The Part 36 offer has no effect because the claimant technically won.","D":"The claimant must automatically pay all damages back to the defendant.","E":"Part 36 applies only to claimant offers."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Where a claimant fails to obtain a judgment more advantageous than a defendant's Part 36 offer, CPR 36.17 ordinarily shifts costs consequences from the end of the relevant period, unless unjust.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Correct. Where a claimant fails to obtain a judgment more advantageous than a defendant's Part 36 offer, CPR 36.17 ordinarily shifts costs consequences from the end of the relevant period, unless unjust.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 36.17.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A claimant can win the claim yet suffer adverse Part 36 costs consequences by failing to beat a defendant's offer.","common_trap":"Assuming only overall liability success matters, rather than comparing the judgment with the formal settlement offer.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f65431429d0d9abb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0468","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Professional conduct — client conflict exception","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two commercial clients ask one solicitor to act on a joint project. Their interests are not presently adverse, but there is a significant risk of conflict. They have a substantially common interest, both give informed consent in writing, and the solicitor reasonably believes appropriate safeguards permit competent representation.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"A significant risk of client conflict can never be subject to any exception.","B":"Oral consent from only one client is sufficient in every conflict case.","C":"The substantially-common-interest exception may permit acting if all requirements of the SRA conflict rule are satisfied.","D":"The exception applies to an own-interest conflict involving the solicitor personally.","E":"The solicitor may act whenever the clients are commercially sophisticated, without further conditions."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The SRA Code permits limited exceptions to the prohibition on acting where clients conflict or significantly risk conflicting, including substantially common interests, but only where informed written consent and the other safeguards are satisfied.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Correct. The SRA Code permits limited exceptions to the prohibition on acting where clients conflict or significantly risk conflicting, including substantially common interests, but only where informed written consent and the other safeguards are satisfied.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, paras 6.1–6.2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The client-conflict exceptions are narrow and conditional; they do not remove the separate prohibition on own-interest conflicts.","common_trap":"Treating informed consent as the sole requirement or confusing client conflicts with own-interest conflicts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3e8938c4134fdb4b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0469","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"AML — tipping off","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor in the regulated sector has made a suspicious activity report. The client asks why a transaction has unexpectedly been delayed. The solicitor considers telling the client expressly that a report has been made and that law-enforcement authorities are examining the suspected money laundering, in circumstances where the disclosure is likely to prejudice an investigation.\n\nWhich risk is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"There is a professional duty always to reveal the existence of a SAR to the client.","B":"Once a SAR is made, confidentiality requires publication of it.","C":"Tipping off is relevant only to tax disputes.","D":"The criminal prohibition on tipping off or prejudicing an investigation, subject to the precise statutory provisions and exceptions.","E":"The solicitor must give the client a copy of every report submitted."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"POCA contains offences relating to disclosures in the regulated sector that reveal a suspicious activity report or related investigation where the statutory conditions, including prejudice where applicable, are satisfied. Solicitors must manage client communications carefully within the statutory framework.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Correct. POCA contains offences relating to disclosures in the regulated sector that reveal a suspicious activity report or related investigation where the statutory conditions, including prejudice where applicable, are satisfied. Solicitors must manage client communications carefully within the statutory framework.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Proceeds of Crime Act 2002, ss 333A–333D and related provisions.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A solicitor must avoid disclosures that constitute prohibited tipping off or prejudicing an investigation.","common_trap":"Assuming ordinary client-information duties override specific anti-money-laundering criminal restrictions.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"167ab08f55fe7d4a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0470","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Consideration — existing contractual duty","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contractor is already contractually bound to complete work by a deadline. The customer promises extra payment after unexpected practical difficulties arise. The contractor provides no additional legal obligation, but the customer's promise secures a real practical benefit and is not procured by economic duress or fraud.\n\nWhich authority is most relevant to whether consideration may exist?","options":{"A":"Stilk v Myrick creates an absolute rule that existing-duty performance can never be consideration in any circumstances.","B":"Foakes v Beer automatically governs every promise to pay more for services.","C":"Consideration exists only if the contractor supplies new physical materials.","D":"Practical benefit is relevant only to deeds.","E":"Williams v Roffey Bros, recognising that performance of an existing contractual duty can in appropriate circumstances provide consideration where the promisor obtains a practical benefit."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Williams v Roffey Bros recognises that a promisor's practical benefit from performance of an existing contractual duty may support consideration for a promise of additional payment, provided the relevant conditions are satisfied and there is no duress or fraud.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Correct. Williams v Roffey Bros recognises that a promisor's practical benefit from performance of an existing contractual duty may support consideration for a promise of additional payment, provided the relevant conditions are satisfied and there is no duress or fraud."},"primary_legal_authority":"Williams v Roffey Bros & Nicholls (Contractors) Ltd [1991] 1 QB 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Performance of an existing duty can sometimes support a promise to pay more through the practical-benefit doctrine.","common_trap":"Treating the older existing-duty rule as absolute or confusing promises to pay more with part-payment of debt.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1fbe64302538bc76"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0471","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Frustration — self-induced event","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A party claims a contract was frustrated after deliberately choosing a course of action that made its own performance impossible, even though another available course would have allowed performance.\n\nWhich is the best general analysis?","options":{"A":"Frustration will not ordinarily assist where the alleged frustrating event is self-induced by the party relying on it.","B":"Any impossibility automatically frustrates a contract regardless of its cause.","C":"Frustration depends only on whether performance became more expensive.","D":"A self-induced event always renders the contract void from formation.","E":"Frustration is available only where both parties expressly agree after the event."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The doctrine generally requires a supervening event not due to the fault or election of the party relying on frustration. Self-induced impossibility ordinarily prevents reliance on the doctrine.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The doctrine generally requires a supervening event not due to the fault or election of the party relying on frustration. Self-induced impossibility ordinarily prevents reliance on the doctrine.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Maritime National Fish Ltd v Ocean Trawlers Ltd; frustration principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A party generally cannot rely on frustration where its own choice or fault produced the alleged impossibility.","common_trap":"Ignoring the requirement that the frustrating event be external rather than self-induced.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5f3a408532d26d99"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0472","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Assignment — statutory legal assignment","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A creditor assigns the whole of a presently existing contractual debt absolutely to an assignee in writing. Written notice of the assignment is then given to the debtor.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"A debt can never be assigned without the debtor's consent.","B":"The assignment may satisfy the statutory requirements for a legal assignment under section 136 of the Law of Property Act 1925.","C":"Only future debts can be legally assigned.","D":"A statutory legal assignment must always be made by deed.","E":"Notice to the debtor is irrelevant to section 136."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 136 permits an absolute assignment in writing of a debt or other legal thing in action, not purporting to be by way of charge only, with express written notice to the debtor or other relevant person.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Correct. Section 136 permits an absolute assignment in writing of a debt or other legal thing in action, not purporting to be by way of charge only, with express written notice to the debtor or other relevant person.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, s 136.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An absolute written assignment of the whole qualifying chose, coupled with written notice, can take effect as a statutory legal assignment.","common_trap":"Assuming debtor consent or a deed is universally required, or overlooking the notice requirement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"846ae5b19a195c03"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0473","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Vicarious liability — relationship and close connection","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An employee commits a tort while carrying out an assigned customer-facing function. The wrongdoing is closely connected with the field of activities entrusted to the employee. The employer argues that deliberate wrongdoing can never generate vicarious liability.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"Employers are liable only for negligent, never intentional, torts.","B":"Every tort committed by an employee at any time is automatically the employer's liability.","C":"Deliberate wrongdoing does not automatically prevent vicarious liability; the court considers the relevant relationship and whether the tort is sufficiently closely connected with the employee's assigned activities.","D":"Vicarious liability requires the employer personally to authorise the tort.","E":"The doctrine applies only to property damage."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Modern vicarious-liability analysis asks whether the relationship is one capable of giving rise to the doctrine and whether the wrongful conduct is sufficiently connected with the employee's authorised field of activities. Intentional torts are not categorically excluded.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Correct. Modern vicarious-liability analysis asks whether the relationship is one capable of giving rise to the doctrine and whether the wrongful conduct is sufficiently connected with the employee's authorised field of activities. Intentional torts are not categorically excluded.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Various Claimants v Catholic Child Welfare Society; Mohamud v WM Morrison Supermarkets plc; WM Morrison Supermarkets plc v Various Claimants.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Intentional wrongdoing can fall within vicarious liability where the required close connection exists.","common_trap":"Using the intentional nature of the tort as an automatic bar or, conversely, treating employment status alone as sufficient.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c430596d7154f0d6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0474","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Remoteness — type of damage","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant negligently causes an accident. Personal injury of the general type suffered by the claimant was reasonably foreseeable, although the precise sequence and extent of injury were unusual.\n\nWhich principle is most relevant to remoteness?","options":{"A":"Every detail of the causal sequence must have been foreseeable.","B":"A defendant is liable for every consequence, however unforeseeable, once factual causation is shown.","C":"Remoteness applies only to contractual damages.","D":"It is generally the kind or type of damage that must be reasonably foreseeable, not the precise manner or full extent in which it occurs.","E":"The claimant must prove the defendant predicted the exact injury."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Wagon Mound remoteness principle focuses on reasonable foreseeability of the relevant kind of damage. The precise manner of occurrence need not ordinarily be foreseen if the type of harm is within the foreseeable scope.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Correct. The Wagon Mound remoteness principle focuses on reasonable foreseeability of the relevant kind of damage. The precise manner of occurrence need not ordinarily be foreseen if the type of harm is within the foreseeable scope.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Overseas Tankship (UK) Ltd v Morts Dock (The Wagon Mound No 1); Hughes v Lord Advocate.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Foreseeability for remoteness concerns the relevant type of damage rather than exact mechanics or magnitude.","common_trap":"Demanding prediction of the precise accident sequence or ignoring remoteness entirely once causation exists.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"800131080e8b6a4b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0475","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Employers' liability — personal duty","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An employer delegates workplace safety arrangements to a competent manager. The manager negligently fails to maintain a safe system of work and an employee is injured. The employer argues that delegation itself eliminates the employer's duty.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"Delegation always transfers the employer's legal duty entirely to the manager.","B":"Employers owe employees no common-law safety duty.","C":"Only criminal health-and-safety law can apply.","D":"The employee must sue the manager and can never sue the employer.","E":"An employer's core duty to take reasonable care for employees' safety is personal and cannot simply be escaped by delegating performance to another person."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"An employer owes personal duties concerning matters such as competent staff, adequate plant and a safe system of work. Tasks can be delegated, but the employer does not thereby shed the legal responsibility to take reasonable care.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Correct. An employer owes personal duties concerning matters such as competent staff, adequate plant and a safe system of work. Tasks can be delegated, but the employer does not thereby shed the legal responsibility to take reasonable care."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wilsons & Clyde Coal Co Ltd v English [1938] AC 57.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The employer's common-law safety duty is personal even though performance of tasks may be delegated.","common_trap":"Confusing delegation of performance with delegation of the underlying legal duty.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0e5bbf00eeb62109"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0476","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Human Rights Act — remedial orders","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A final court decision has identified primary legislation as incompatible with a Convention right. The government is considering the statutory mechanism for amending legislation following that finding.\n\nWhich HRA mechanism is potentially relevant?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"A minister may in the statutory circumstances use the remedial-order procedure to amend legislation to remove incompatibility, subject to the safeguards in the Act.","C":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","D":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A declaration of incompatibility does not itself amend legislation; the HRA separately provides a remedial-order mechanism.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Correct. A declaration of incompatibility does not itself amend legislation; the HRA separately provides a remedial-order mechanism.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, s 10 and Sch 2","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A declaration of incompatibility does not itself amend legislation; the HRA separately provides a remedial-order mechanism.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fa90e57c6abc4c26"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0477","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — standing","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A campaigning organisation seeks judicial review of a public decision closely connected with its established work. It has expertise in the field and the issue affects the public interest, although the organisation does not itself own property directly affected by the decision.\n\nWhich threshold issue must the court consider?","options":{"A":"Whether the claimant has a contractual cause of action against the government.","B":"Whether the claimant has a sufficient interest in the matter to which the application relates.","C":"Whether the claimant owns land is the exclusive standing test.","D":"Only the Attorney General can ever seek judicial review.","E":"Standing is irrelevant in judicial review."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 31(3) of the Senior Courts Act requires sufficient interest. The assessment is contextual and can accommodate representative or public-interest claimants depending on the issue and their connection to it.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Correct. Section 31(3) of the Senior Courts Act requires sufficient interest. The assessment is contextual and can accommodate representative or public-interest claimants depending on the issue and their connection to it.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Senior Courts Act 1981, s 31(3); R v Inland Revenue Commissioners, ex p National Federation of Self-Employed; R v Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs, ex p World Development Movement.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Judicial-review standing turns on sufficient interest, assessed in context rather than a rigid property-right requirement.","common_trap":"Assuming only a person with a private proprietary right can challenge public action.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"890a24545b66589c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0478","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Retained/assimilated EU law — domestic status","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A lawyer analysing a post-Brexit domestic dispute assumes that every rule formerly described as retained EU law continues indefinitely with exactly the same status, terminology and supremacy rules that applied before the end of 2023.\n\nWhich is the better general approach?","options":{"A":"All EU-derived domestic law automatically ceased to exist at the end of 2020.","B":"Pre-Brexit EU supremacy continues universally over every later Act of Parliament.","C":"The lawyer must apply the post-2023 statutory framework for assimilated law and the current rules on interpretation, priority and domestic effect rather than assuming the former retained-EU-law regime remains unchanged.","D":"EU law remains a freestanding source identical to membership-era law in all domestic cases.","E":"Brexit made statutory interpretation unnecessary for EU-derived rules."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023 changed terminology and important features of the domestic framework from the end of 2023. Candidates must apply the current statutory position to the particular category and timing of law in issue.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Correct. The Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023 changed terminology and important features of the domestic framework from the end of 2023. Candidates must apply the current statutory position to the particular category and timing of law in issue.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018, as amended; Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Post-Brexit EU-derived law must be analysed under the current domestic statutory framework, including assimilated-law rules.","common_trap":"Freezing the legal position at an earlier Brexit stage and assuming historic terminology and supremacy principles remain universally unchanged.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"60efbd2bf3eb38f6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0479","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — knowledge and approval","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An elderly testator signs a formally valid will prepared by a beneficiary who receives an unexpectedly large gift. Evidence raises a real suspicion about whether the testator understood and approved the contents, although testamentary capacity itself is not in issue.\n\nWhich validity requirement is particularly engaged?","options":{"A":"Formal execution alone conclusively proves the testator approved every provision.","B":"Knowledge and approval is identical to testamentary capacity and has no separate role.","C":"A beneficiary may never prepare a will under any circumstances.","D":"Knowledge and approval of the will's contents, with the evidential burden capable of shifting where suspicious circumstances arise.","E":"The will is automatically valid because two witnesses signed it."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A valid will requires the testator to know and approve its contents. Due execution can support a presumption, but suspicious circumstances may require those propounding the will to satisfy the court that knowledge and approval existed.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Correct. A valid will requires the testator to know and approve its contents. Due execution can support a presumption, but suspicious circumstances may require those propounding the will to satisfy the court that knowledge and approval existed.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Gill v Woodall; Sherrington v Sherrington; Wills validity principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Knowledge and approval is a distinct validity requirement, and suspicious circumstances can affect the evidential burden.","common_trap":"Assuming formal execution or capacity conclusively resolves whether the testator understood and approved the document.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"941e14d9ae428726"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0480","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — burden when capacity challenged","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A will appears rational on its face and was duly executed. A disappointed relative produces evidence raising a real doubt about the testator's testamentary capacity at execution. The will's propounder seeks probate.\n\nWhich is the best general evidential position?","options":{"A":"The challenger always bears the legal burden throughout, regardless of evidence.","B":"Capacity is conclusively presumed from the presence of witnesses.","C":"The criminal standard of proof applies to probate validity.","D":"Only a medical expert can legally determine capacity.","E":"Once a real doubt as to testamentary capacity is raised, the person propounding the will bears the burden of establishing capacity on the balance of probabilities."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Probate law begins with presumptions that may assist the propounder, but where sufficient evidence raises a real doubt about capacity, the evidential/legal burden operates so that the person propounding the will must establish testamentary capacity on the civil standard.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Correct. Probate law begins with presumptions that may assist the propounder, but where sufficient evidence raises a real doubt about capacity, the evidential/legal burden operates so that the person propounding the will must establish testamentary capacity on the civil standard."},"primary_legal_authority":"Banks v Goodfellow; Key v Key; probate evidential principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A genuine capacity challenge can place the burden on the will's propounder to establish capacity.","common_trap":"Assuming due execution makes capacity irrebuttable or applying the criminal standard.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2a3af538eaa1e602"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0481","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Administration — section 27 notices","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Personal representatives want to distribute an estate but are concerned about unknown creditors. They advertise for claims in the manner and for the period required by the statutory trustee-notice procedure before distributing.\n\nWhat protection can this procedure principally provide?","options":{"A":"It can protect personal representatives from personal liability to certain unknown claimants when distributing after proper notices, without extinguishing the underlying claimant's rights against recipients where otherwise available.","B":"It extinguishes every estate debt absolutely.","C":"It guarantees that no beneficiary can ever be asked to restore assets.","D":"It replaces the need to identify known creditors.","E":"It transfers all estate liabilities to the Probate Registry."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Section 27 permits trustees and personal representatives to advertise for claims and, after the prescribed period, distribute with protection from personal liability in respect of claims of which they had no notice. The claimant's substantive rights are not necessarily extinguished.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Section 27 permits trustees and personal representatives to advertise for claims and, after the prescribed period, distribute with protection from personal liability in respect of claims of which they had no notice. The claimant's substantive rights are not necessarily extinguished.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 1925, s 27.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Statutory notices protect representatives personally against unknown claims; they do not erase the debt itself.","common_trap":"Treating the notice procedure as substantive cancellation of creditors' rights or protection against known claims.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2c4aed1887ca4509"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0482","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Mixed receipts — client and business money","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm receives one electronic payment containing £9,000 client money for completion and £500 that is immediately due to the firm for billed costs. The whole payment initially arrives in client account.\n\nWhich is the best general approach?","options":{"A":"Because any part is client money, the entire £9,500 must remain in client account indefinitely.","B":"The firm must promptly allocate the receipt correctly and transfer the business-money element out of client account in accordance with the Accounts Rules.","C":"The entire receipt becomes business money because some billed costs are included.","D":"Mixed receipts are prohibited and must be returned automatically.","E":"The firm may leave business money in client account as a permanent reserve."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Accounts Rules require firms to keep client money separate from business money and to correct mixed receipts promptly, transferring the appropriate element to the proper account once identified.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Correct. The Accounts Rules require firms to keep client money separate from business money and to correct mixed receipts promptly, transferring the appropriate element to the proper account once identified.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, including rr 2 and 4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A mixed receipt must be promptly allocated so client and business money are held in the correct accounts.","common_trap":"Assuming the character of one component determines the treatment of the entire mixed receipt.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ada349cd93c3f110"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0483","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Constitution — Re Rose principle","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A donor executes the correct share-transfer form and delivers it with the share certificate to the company for registration, having done everything within the donor's own power required to transfer the shares. The donor dies before the company registers the transfer.\n\nWhich equitable principle may be relevant?","options":{"A":"Milroy v Lord requires every imperfect gift to fail even after the donor has completed all required steps personally.","B":"The rule in Strong v Bird applies because the donee automatically becomes executor.","C":"The Re Rose principle, under which equity may treat the transfer as effective once the donor has done everything necessary within the donor's power to effect it.","D":"Saunders v Vautier.","E":"The beneficiary principle."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Re Rose recognises an exception to the general rule against perfecting imperfect gifts where the donor has done everything necessary within the donor's power to transfer the property and only third-party registration remains.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Correct. Re Rose recognises an exception to the general rule against perfecting imperfect gifts where the donor has done everything necessary within the donor's power to transfer the property and only third-party registration remains.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Re Rose [1952] Ch 499; Milroy v Lord.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Equity can recognise a transfer once the donor has completed all steps required of the donor, even though registration remains outstanding.","common_trap":"Applying the maxim that equity will not perfect an imperfect gift without considering the recognised Re Rose exception.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"961f38cc1fd9a1d5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0484","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Fiduciaries — profits: account despite principal's inability to profit","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A fiduciary obtains an unauthorised profit by exploiting an opportunity arising from the fiduciary position. The fiduciary argues that the principal could not itself have taken the opportunity.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The fiduciary may still be required to account: the no-profit rule can apply even where the principal could not itself have obtained the benefit.","B":"The fiduciary keeps the profit whenever the principal could not have made it.","C":"Liability arises only if the principal proves an equivalent financial loss.","D":"The no-profit rule applies only to express trustees and never to other fiduciaries.","E":"Disclosure after the profit is made automatically eliminates liability."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The fiduciary no-profit rule is strict. Liability to account does not ordinarily depend on proving that the principal could itself have secured the opportunity or suffered corresponding loss.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The fiduciary no-profit rule is strict. Liability to account does not ordinarily depend on proving that the principal could itself have secured the opportunity or suffered corresponding loss.","B":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. The remedy protects fiduciary loyalty and is not merely compensatory for the principal's lost opportunity.","C":"Incorrect. This incorrectly makes a non-exclusive factor the sole legal test. The remedy protects fiduciary loyalty and is not merely compensatory for the principal's lost opportunity.","D":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. The remedy protects fiduciary loyalty and is not merely compensatory for the principal's lost opportunity.","E":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. The remedy protects fiduciary loyalty and is not merely compensatory for the principal's lost opportunity."},"primary_legal_authority":"Boardman v Phipps [1967] 2 AC 46; Regal (Hastings) Ltd v Gulliver [1967] 2 AC 134","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The remedy protects fiduciary loyalty and is not merely compensatory for the principal's lost opportunity.","common_trap":"Applying the headline doctrine without identifying the distinct consequence, exception or procedural stage tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"791d972d884b5ff2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0485","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Co-ownership — severance by notice","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two people hold the beneficial interest in a home as joint tenants. One wants to sever the beneficial joint tenancy unilaterally and serves an effective written notice expressing an immediate intention to sever on the other joint tenant.\n\nWhat is the ordinary effect?","options":{"A":"The legal estate is converted into a legal tenancy in common.","B":"Severance automatically transfers the entire property to the person serving notice.","C":"A beneficial joint tenancy can never be severed unilaterally.","D":"The notice revokes the other owner's registered legal title.","E":"The beneficial joint tenancy is severed in equity so the parties thereafter hold beneficial shares as tenants in common, while the legal estate remains jointly held."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Section 36(2) permits severance of a beneficial joint tenancy by written notice. Legal co-ownership remains a joint tenancy because a legal estate cannot be held as tenants in common.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Correct. Section 36(2) permits severance of a beneficial joint tenancy by written notice. Legal co-ownership remains a joint tenancy because a legal estate cannot be held as tenants in common."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, s 36(2); Williams v Hensman.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Written notice can sever the beneficial joint tenancy without changing the joint legal title.","common_trap":"Confusing severance of equitable co-ownership with the form in which the legal estate must be held.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9ec48cba2cf6f121"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0486","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Overriding interests — actual occupation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A purchaser for value becomes registered proprietor. At the time of the disposition, another person has a proprietary interest and is in actual occupation. The occupation would have been obvious on a reasonably careful inspection and no statutory exception removes overriding status.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"The occupier's interest may override the registered disposition under Schedule 3 paragraph 2 despite not being entered on the register.","B":"No unregistered interest can ever bind a registered purchaser.","C":"Actual occupation automatically creates a proprietary interest even where none otherwise exists.","D":"Every person physically present on completion has an overriding interest.","E":"Section 29 always defeats actual-occupation interests."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Schedule 3 paragraph 2 can give overriding status to an existing proprietary interest belonging to a person in actual occupation, subject to statutory exceptions. Actual occupation protects an interest; it does not itself create one.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Schedule 3 paragraph 2 can give overriding status to an existing proprietary interest belonging to a person in actual occupation, subject to statutory exceptions. Actual occupation protects an interest; it does not itself create one.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002, Sch 3 para 2; Williams & Glyn's Bank v Boland.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A qualifying proprietary interest coupled with actual occupation can override a registered disposition.","common_trap":"Treating registration as eliminating all overriding interests or assuming occupation itself creates property rights.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b9a72565508922f7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0487","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Contract — exclusion clauses: contra proferentem and negligence","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A professionally negotiated contract contains an ambiguous exclusion clause. One party argues that the clause excludes liability for negligence, but the wording does not expressly mention negligence.\n\nWhat is the best approach?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","C":"The clause must be interpreted in its contractual context; clear language is required before negligence liability is excluded, while modern interpretation gives primary weight to the wording and context rather than mechanically applying a presumption.","D":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Exclusion clauses are interpreted using ordinary contractual principles, with particular care where negligence liability is said to be excluded.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Correct. Exclusion clauses are interpreted using ordinary contractual principles, with particular care where negligence liability is said to be excluded.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Canada Steamship Lines Ltd v The King [1952] AC 192; Persimmon Homes Ltd v Ove Arup & Partners Ltd [2017] EWCA Civ 373","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Exclusion clauses are interpreted using ordinary contractual principles, with particular care where negligence liability is said to be excluded.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d3137318b7e28a25"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0488","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Exchange — Law Society formulae","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two conveyancers are ready to exchange residential contracts by telephone. Each holds a counterpart signed by their own client and has authority to exchange. They want a recognised mechanism governing the telephone exchange and handling of the counterparts.\n\nWhich practice mechanism is directly relevant?","options":{"A":"An OS1 search.","B":"A DS1 discharge.","C":"The Law Society's formulae for exchange of contracts.","D":"A bankruptcy search.","E":"A section 27 Trustee Act notice."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Law Society formulae provide recognised procedures for exchange by telephone where conveyancers hold signed counterparts and have appropriate authority, specifying how and when exchange becomes binding and documents are dealt with.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Correct. The Law Society formulae provide recognised procedures for exchange by telephone where conveyancers hold signed counterparts and have appropriate authority, specifying how and when exchange becomes binding and documents are dealt with.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law Society Formulae for Exchange of Contracts; conveyancing practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Telephone exchange can be conducted using the Law Society's recognised exchange formulae.","common_trap":"Confusing the mechanism for contract exchange with Land Registry priority, mortgage discharge or probate procedures.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b6ac6dfa0b1c5cd5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0489","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Intoxication — specific intent","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant voluntarily becomes intoxicated and is charged with an offence of specific intent. On the evidence, intoxication may mean the defendant did not in fact form the specific intent required for that offence.\n\nWhich is the best general analysis?","options":{"A":"Voluntary intoxication is always a complete defence to every offence.","B":"Intoxication is legally irrelevant to whether a specific intent was actually formed.","C":"A voluntarily intoxicated defendant is automatically deemed to have every possible intent.","D":"Voluntary intoxication may prevent proof of the specific intent if the defendant genuinely lacked it, although liability for an available basic-intent alternative may still need consideration.","E":"Specific-intent offences become strict liability when alcohol is consumed."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"For specific-intent offences, voluntary intoxication can be relevant to whether the prosecution proves the required intent. The doctrine does not amount to a free-standing defence, and a basic-intent alternative may sometimes remain.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Correct. For specific-intent offences, voluntary intoxication can be relevant to whether the prosecution proves the required intent. The doctrine does not amount to a free-standing defence, and a basic-intent alternative may sometimes remain.","E":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"DPP v Majewski; R v Sheehan and Moore; intoxication principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Intoxication can negate proof of a specific intent actually required by the offence, without providing universal immunity.","common_trap":"Treating intoxication either as an automatic defence or as wholly irrelevant to proof of specific intent.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7a4256db3f045b2d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0490","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Bail — general right and exceptions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant appears before the magistrates charged with an imprisonable offence. The prosecution seeks remand in custody, asserting a substantial risk that the defendant will fail to surrender and identifying evidence supporting that risk.\n\nWhich is the best general framework?","options":{"A":"Every defendant charged with an imprisonable offence must be remanded in custody.","B":"Bail can be refused only after conviction.","C":"The prosecution's objection automatically determines the issue.","D":"The Bail Act creates an absolute right to unconditional bail in every case.","E":"The court begins with the statutory right to bail but may refuse bail where a Schedule 1 exception is established, such as substantial grounds for believing the defendant would fail to surrender, subject to the statutory framework."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The Bail Act establishes a general right to bail, but Schedule 1 provides exceptions, including substantial grounds for believing an accused would fail to surrender, commit offences on bail or interfere with witnesses or obstruct justice, depending on the case.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","B":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","C":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","D":"Incorrect. This alternative states a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and authority.","E":"Correct. The Bail Act establishes a general right to bail, but Schedule 1 provides exceptions, including substantial grounds for believing an accused would fail to surrender, commit offences on bail or interfere with witnesses or obstruct justice, depending on the case."},"primary_legal_authority":"Bail Act 1976, ss 4 and Sch 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Bail is the statutory starting point, subject to defined exceptions assessed on evidence and relevant factors.","common_trap":"Treating bail as either automatic detention or an unconditional absolute entitlement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2d39c377721303a9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0491","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — ordinary and special resolutions","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company's members are asked to approve two matters at a general meeting: an ordinary business decision requiring an ordinary resolution and an amendment to the company's articles.\n\nWhich voting thresholds ordinarily apply, assuming no special provision changes the statutory defaults?","options":{"A":"The ordinary resolution requires a simple majority of votes cast, while amendment of the articles requires a special resolution with at least 75% of votes cast.","B":"Both matters require unanimity.","C":"Both matters require exactly two-thirds of all issued shares.","D":"The articles can be amended by directors without any member resolution.","E":"An ordinary resolution requires 75%, while a special resolution requires only a simple majority."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Under the Companies Act, an ordinary resolution is passed by a simple majority, whereas a special resolution requires at least 75%. Amendment of articles is ordinarily by special resolution.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Under the Companies Act, an ordinary resolution is passed by a simple majority, whereas a special resolution requires at least 75%. Amendment of articles is ordinarily by special resolution.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 21, 282 and 283.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Ordinary and special resolutions have different statutory voting thresholds, and alteration of articles ordinarily requires a special resolution.","common_trap":"Reversing the voting thresholds or assuming directors can amend articles using ordinary management powers.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8ed0c638a6811593"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0492","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Directors — duty to promote success","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A director honestly considers several statutory factors before approving a strategy that the director genuinely believes, in good faith, would promote the company's success for the benefit of members as a whole. A minority shareholder later argues that the court should substitute its own commercial judgment merely because the strategy performed badly.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"Section 172 makes directors strictly liable whenever a commercial decision loses money.","B":"Section 172 principally asks whether the director acted in good faith in the way the director considered would promote the company's success, while requiring regard to the statutory factors.","C":"The court always substitutes the decision it would itself have made.","D":"A director must maximise immediate shareholder distributions regardless of other statutory factors.","E":"The duty is owed personally to each individual shareholder."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 172 is framed around the director's good-faith judgment as to promoting the company's success for members as a whole, with regard to listed factors. Poor outcome alone does not establish breach.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Correct. Section 172 is framed around the director's good-faith judgment as to promoting the company's success for members as a whole, with regard to listed factors. Poor outcome alone does not establish breach.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, s 172.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The statutory success duty centres on the director's good-faith judgment, not hindsight review of commercial success.","common_trap":"Equating a bad commercial outcome with breach or treating section 172 as a purely objective profit-maximisation test.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"51713402d2a1e6ec"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0493","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — legal personality and shareholder liability","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company validly contracts for equipment. The company later cannot pay the supplier. Its sole shareholder did not guarantee the debt and there is no basis for piercing the corporate veil.\n\nWho is ordinarily liable on the contract?","options":{"A":"The sole shareholder is automatically personally liable for every company debt.","B":"The directors and shareholder are jointly liable whenever the company is insolvent.","C":"The company, as a separate legal person; the shareholder is not personally liable merely because the shareholder owns all the shares.","D":"Companies have no separate legal personality until they have multiple shareholders.","E":"The supplier may disregard incorporation whenever recovery from the company is difficult."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Incorporation creates a legal person distinct from its members. Shareholders' exposure is ordinarily limited according to the company's structure, absent a separate basis such as guarantee or exceptional veil doctrine.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Correct. Incorporation creates a legal person distinct from its members. Shareholders' exposure is ordinarily limited according to the company's structure, absent a separate basis such as guarantee or exceptional veil doctrine.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006; Salomon v A Salomon & Co Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A company's contractual debts are its own; sole ownership does not by itself impose personal liability on the shareholder.","common_trap":"Treating economic control or insolvency as sufficient to disregard separate corporate personality.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"dc7346afd9489385"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0494","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — wrongful trading","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Directors know that their insolvent company has no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation, yet continue trading without taking every step they ought to take to minimise potential loss to creditors. Losses increase.\n\nWhich provision may expose them to a contribution order?","options":{"A":"A preference under section 239, because directors continued trading.","B":"The rule against penalties.","C":"The Partnership Act 1890.","D":"The wrongful-trading regime under section 214 of the Insolvency Act 1986, subject to the statutory requirements and current law.","E":"Directors can never face personal consequences for post-insolvency trading decisions."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Wrongful trading can permit a contribution order where the statutory knowledge threshold concerning unavoidable insolvent liquidation is met and the director fails to take the required steps to minimise creditor loss, subject to the legislation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Correct. Wrongful trading can permit a contribution order where the statutory knowledge threshold concerning unavoidable insolvent liquidation is met and the director fails to take the required steps to minimise creditor loss, subject to the legislation.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, s 214.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Wrongful trading focuses on directors' conduct once the statutory insolvency-prospect threshold is crossed.","common_trap":"Confusing director liability for continuing an insolvent business with transaction-avoidance provisions such as preferences.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6c57f810d2fc08c0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0495","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Limitation — simple contract debt","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A supplier's cause of action for an unpaid contractual debt accrued on 1 March 2021. No acknowledgment, part payment, fraud, concealment, disability or contractual specialty changes the position.\n\nWhat is the ordinary limitation period for bringing an action founded on simple contract?","options":{"A":"Three years from knowledge in every contract case.","B":"Twelve years for every contract regardless of form.","C":"One year from invoice date.","D":"There is no limitation period for contractual debts.","E":"Six years from accrual of the cause of action."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Section 5 of the Limitation Act provides a six-year limitation period for actions founded on simple contract, running from accrual of the cause of action, subject to applicable postponement or extension rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Correct. Section 5 of the Limitation Act provides a six-year limitation period for actions founded on simple contract, running from accrual of the cause of action, subject to applicable postponement or extension rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"Limitation Act 1980, s 5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A simple-contract claim is ordinarily subject to a six-year limitation period from accrual.","common_trap":"Importing the personal-injury knowledge regime or the specialty period into an ordinary simple-contract debt claim.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c0c26f3069090b68"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0496","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Disclosure — adverse documents","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"In litigation governed by the standard disclosure regime, a party identifies a document that materially harms its own case but supports the opponent's case. It is not privileged.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The document falls within standard disclosure because the obligation includes documents adversely affecting the party's own case or supporting another party's case.","B":"A party discloses only documents that help its own case.","C":"Adverse documents may always be destroyed once proceedings begin.","D":"Disclosure applies only to documents intended to be used at trial.","E":"Privilege and adverse effect are the same concept."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Where CPR 31.6 standard disclosure applies, it extends beyond relied-on documents to those adversely affecting a party's own or another party's case or supporting another party's case. Privilege affects inspection, not the definition of relevance for disclosure.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Where CPR 31.6 standard disclosure applies, it extends beyond relied-on documents to those adversely affecting a party's own or another party's case or supporting another party's case. Privilege affects inspection, not the definition of relevance for disclosure.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 31.6, where applicable.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Standard disclosure can require disclosure of non-privileged documents harmful to the disclosing party's position.","common_trap":"Treating disclosure as a partisan selection of favourable evidence only.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d9b15a82ca692209"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0497","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Expert evidence — overriding duty","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A party instructs an expert witness and tells the expert to present the most favourable possible opinion, even if that requires suppressing qualifications that materially affect the expert's conclusion.\n\nWhich duty governs the expert?","options":{"A":"The expert's sole duty is to maximise the instructing party's prospects.","B":"The expert's overriding duty is to help the court on matters within the expert's expertise, overriding any obligation to the instructing party.","C":"An expert may omit material qualifications if the client requests it.","D":"Experts are advocates and owe no independent duty to the court.","E":"The expert's duty arises only after cross-examination begins."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"CPR 35.3 states that experts must help the court on matters within their expertise and that this duty overrides obligations to the person instructing or paying them.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Correct. CPR 35.3 states that experts must help the court on matters within their expertise and that this duty overrides obligations to the person instructing or paying them.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 35.3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An expert is independent and owes an overriding duty to the court rather than a partisan advocacy duty.","common_trap":"Treating a party-appointed expert as equivalent to an advocate instructed to advance only one side.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"981c910d5ce0fbd9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0498","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Professional conduct — supervision","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor with management responsibility delegates substantive work to a junior employee but establishes no system to supervise the work or ensure that services delivered to clients remain competent and timely.\n\nWhich professional obligation is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Delegation removes all professional responsibility from the supervising solicitor.","B":"Supervision is required only for trainees, never other staff.","C":"The obligation to ensure effective supervision of client matters and those carrying out work for which the solicitor has responsibility.","D":"The SRA Code regulates honesty but not supervision.","E":"A client must personally supervise delegated legal work."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The SRA Code requires effective supervision of client matters and appropriate supervision of those whose work a solicitor manages. Delegation does not remove regulatory responsibility for competent service.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Correct. The SRA Code requires effective supervision of client matters and appropriate supervision of those whose work a solicitor manages. Delegation does not remove regulatory responsibility for competent service.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, paras 3.5–3.6.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Solicitors with supervisory responsibility must maintain effective systems and oversight of delegated legal work.","common_trap":"Assuming delegation transfers regulatory responsibility entirely to the junior worker.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f70c591327ad72a6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0499","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Professional conduct — confidentiality and disclosure","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor holds information confidential to Client A that would materially help Client B in an unrelated matter. A has not consented to disclosure, and no legal or regulatory exception requires or permits disclosure.\n\nWhat is the ordinary position?","options":{"A":"The solicitor must always disclose any helpful information to another client.","B":"Confidentiality ends whenever the information would improve another client's case.","C":"The duty applies only to information marked 'confidential'.","D":"The solicitor must keep A's information confidential and cannot disclose it merely because doing so would benefit B.","E":"The solicitor may disclose because both people are clients of the same firm."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The duty of confidentiality applies to the affairs of current and former clients unless disclosure is required or permitted by law or the client consents. Duties to another client do not ordinarily authorise misuse of confidential information.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Correct. The duty of confidentiality applies to the affairs of current and former clients unless disclosure is required or permitted by law or the client consents. Duties to another client do not ordinarily authorise misuse of confidential information.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 6.3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Client confidentiality is not displaced merely because disclosure would benefit another client.","common_trap":"Confusing the duty to act in a client's best interests with permission to disclose another client's protected information.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"62a3426ef321962b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0500","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Formation — acceptance and silence","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An offeror writes: 'If I hear nothing from you by Friday, I will treat your silence as acceptance.' The offeree does nothing and has not previously indicated that silence may constitute acceptance.\n\nIs a contract ordinarily formed merely by the offeree's silence?","options":{"A":"Yes. An offeror can always prescribe silence as acceptance.","B":"Yes, because failure to reject is identical to signing.","C":"No contract can ever be accepted by conduct.","D":"Silence always creates a unilateral contract.","E":"No. An offeror cannot ordinarily impose contractual acceptance on an offeree merely by stipulating that silence will count as acceptance."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"As a general rule, acceptance must be communicated by words or conduct and an offeror cannot impose acceptance through the offeree's silence alone. Context or prior dealings can sometimes affect the analysis.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Correct. As a general rule, acceptance must be communicated by words or conduct and an offeror cannot impose acceptance through the offeree's silence alone. Context or prior dealings can sometimes affect the analysis."},"primary_legal_authority":"Felthouse v Bindley; contract-formation principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An offeror ordinarily cannot unilaterally make an offeree's silence amount to acceptance.","common_trap":"Assuming the offeror's prescribed mode can convert inactivity into consent without an objective basis.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"de214ed55cfe433f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0501","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Misrepresentation — rescission and third-party rights","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer acquires goods after an actionable misrepresentation but, before seeking rescission, sells them to an innocent third party who acquires good title. The buyer then attempts to unwind the original contract and recover the goods from that third party.\n\nWhich bar to rescission is particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"Intervention of third-party rights can bar rescission where an innocent third party has acquired rights in the subject matter.","B":"Rescission is always available regardless of subsequent third-party rights.","C":"Only lapse of time can bar rescission.","D":"Misrepresentation automatically invalidates all later transfers.","E":"The buyer must rescind only after obtaining the representor's criminal conviction."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Rescission is an equitable remedy subject to bars. Once an innocent third party has acquired rights in the property before rescission, restoration of the original position may be unavailable and rescission can be barred.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Rescission is an equitable remedy subject to bars. Once an innocent third party has acquired rights in the property before rescission, restoration of the original position may be unavailable and rescission can be barred.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Misrepresentation Act 1967; equitable rescission principles; Car & Universal Finance Co Ltd v Caldwell.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Acquisition of rights by an innocent third party can prevent rescission of the original transaction.","common_trap":"Treating rescission as automatically proprietary against all later innocent transferees.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f2aa956dbed37a8b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0502","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Damages — remoteness in contract","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A seller breaches a supply contract. The buyer claims an unusual consequential loss that would not ordinarily arise from such a breach. The special circumstances producing that loss were never communicated to the seller.\n\nWhich remoteness principle is most relevant?","options":{"A":"All factual consequences of breach are recoverable.","B":"Loss is ordinarily recoverable if it arises naturally in the usual course or was within the parties' reasonable contemplation because relevant special circumstances were known at contracting.","C":"Only losses expressly quantified in the contract can ever be recovered.","D":"Contract remoteness uses exactly the same test as criminal causation.","E":"Uncommunicated exceptional circumstances automatically expand liability."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Hadley v Baxendale distinguishes ordinary losses arising naturally from breach from exceptional losses dependent on special circumstances known to both parties at contracting. Modern authority frames the inquiry around the scope of contractual responsibility.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Correct. Hadley v Baxendale distinguishes ordinary losses arising naturally from breach from exceptional losses dependent on special circumstances known to both parties at contracting. Modern authority frames the inquiry around the scope of contractual responsibility.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Hadley v Baxendale; The Achilleas.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contract damages do not ordinarily extend to unusual losses dependent on special circumstances unknown to the defendant at formation.","common_trap":"Assuming factual causation alone makes every consequential loss recoverable.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"23c64c3c302d192d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0503","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — duty for omissions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A passer-by sees a stranger in danger created entirely by natural causes. The passer-by did not create the danger, assume responsibility, control the source of danger or have any recognised special relationship, but could easily assist and does nothing.\n\nWhich is the best general common-law position?","options":{"A":"Everyone who can easily prevent harm automatically owes a negligence duty to do so.","B":"A duty arises whenever moral blame can be alleged.","C":"There is ordinarily no general duty to rescue or prevent harm caused by a third party or natural event absent a recognised basis for a positive duty.","D":"The law treats omissions identically to positive acts in all circumstances.","E":"Only occupiers can ever owe positive duties."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"English negligence law generally distinguishes causing harm from failing to confer a benefit or prevent harm. Positive duties can arise through recognised exceptions such as assumption of responsibility, creation of danger, control or special relationships.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Correct. English negligence law generally distinguishes causing harm from failing to confer a benefit or prevent harm. Positive duties can arise through recognised exceptions such as assumption of responsibility, creation of danger, control or special relationships.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Smith v Littlewoods Organisation Ltd; Michael v Chief Constable of South Wales Police; Robinson v Chief Constable of West Yorkshire Police.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Negligence ordinarily imposes no general duty to rescue, subject to recognised categories creating positive obligations.","common_trap":"Converting moral expectations into a universal common-law duty of affirmative action.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"edba8f027720ad99"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0504","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Occupiers' liability — independent contractors","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An occupier engages a competent specialist contractor to perform technical maintenance that it was reasonable to entrust to a specialist. The occupier takes reasonable steps to select the contractor and, where appropriate, checks that the work appears properly done. A visitor is later injured by the contractor's negligent workmanship.\n\nWhich statutory provision is particularly relevant to the occupier's potential defence?","options":{"A":"The occupier is automatically strictly liable for every contractor error.","B":"Independent contractors can never affect occupier liability.","C":"Section 2(3)(a), which concerns children, is the only relevant rule.","D":"Section 2(4)(b) of the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957 concerning work entrusted to an independent contractor.","E":"The Defamation Act 2013."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"An occupier may satisfy the common duty in relation to dangers arising from work entrusted to an independent contractor if it was reasonable to entrust the work and reasonable steps were taken concerning contractor competence and, where appropriate, inspection of the work.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Correct. An occupier may satisfy the common duty in relation to dangers arising from work entrusted to an independent contractor if it was reasonable to entrust the work and reasonable steps were taken concerning contractor competence and, where appropriate, inspection of the work.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Occupiers' Liability Act 1957, s 2(4)(b).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Reasonable reliance on a competent independent contractor can satisfy the occupier's duty for specialist work if the statutory conditions are met.","common_trap":"Assuming an occupier is either automatically liable or automatically exempt whenever a contractor performs the work.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"09d10e5b8d9972d0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0505","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Defamation — honest opinion","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A columnist clearly presents a statement as opinion, indicates the basis of that opinion, and an honest person could have held the opinion on the basis of facts existing at the time. The claimant proves serious harm but not that the columnist did not genuinely hold the opinion.\n\nWhich statutory defence is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Truth under section 2 necessarily, because every opinion is a statement of fact.","B":"There is no defence once serious harm is shown.","C":"Qualified privilege is the only defence to commentary.","D":"Opinion can never be defamatory.","E":"Honest opinion under section 3 of the Defamation Act 2013, subject to its statutory requirements and defeat provisions."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Section 3 provides the honest-opinion defence where the statement was opinion, indicated its basis, and an honest person could have held it on the relevant facts. The defence can be defeated in circumstances specified by the Act.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Correct. Section 3 provides the honest-opinion defence where the statement was opinion, indicated its basis, and an honest person could have held it on the relevant facts. The defence can be defeated in circumstances specified by the Act."},"primary_legal_authority":"Defamation Act 2013, s 3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A defamatory opinion can be protected by the statutory honest-opinion defence if its conditions are satisfied.","common_trap":"Confusing the threshold of serious harm with elimination of substantive defences or requiring the opinion itself to be provably true.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1862f4e012441526"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0506","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Parliamentary sovereignty — primary legislation","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A court considers a validly enacted Act of the UK Parliament that clearly changes an earlier inconsistent statutory rule. No special constitutional issue alters the ordinary analysis.\n\nWhich principle is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Parliament can enact or repeal primary legislation, and courts ordinarily give effect to the later valid statute according to constitutional principles governing parliamentary sovereignty.","B":"Courts may disregard any Act they consider unwise.","C":"An earlier Parliament can permanently prevent a later Parliament from legislating on the same subject in every respect.","D":"Government policy automatically overrides an Act.","E":"A minister may repeal primary legislation by announcement."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Parliamentary sovereignty remains a foundational principle: Parliament can make or unmake law, and courts recognise valid primary legislation, subject to the nuanced constitutional framework governing interpretation and particular statutory arrangements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Parliamentary sovereignty remains a foundational principle: Parliament can make or unmake law, and courts recognise valid primary legislation, subject to the nuanced constitutional framework governing interpretation and particular statutory arrangements.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Parliamentary sovereignty principles; R (Miller) v Secretary of State for Exiting the European Union [2017] UKSC 5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Valid Acts of Parliament are given legal effect by courts, and ordinary legislation can change earlier inconsistent statutory law.","common_trap":"Confusing judicial interpretation with a general power to disregard primary legislation on policy grounds.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1806c426aa7daa60"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0507","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Royal prerogative — statutory displacement","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Parliament has enacted a statute regulating a field previously governed by prerogative power. The executive attempts to use the prerogative to bypass the statutory limits and achieve an outcome inconsistent with the legislation.\n\nWhich is the best constitutional analysis?","options":{"A":"Prerogative power always overrides Acts of Parliament.","B":"The executive cannot ordinarily use prerogative power to frustrate or bypass a statutory scheme governing the same subject matter.","C":"A minister may choose freely between statute and prerogative even to evade statutory restrictions.","D":"Courts cannot review the legal scope of prerogative powers.","E":"Statutes never affect pre-existing prerogatives."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Where Parliament has occupied a field or prescribed legal limits, prerogative powers cannot ordinarily be used inconsistently with the statutory scheme or to frustrate Parliament's legislation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Correct. Where Parliament has occupied a field or prescribed legal limits, prerogative powers cannot ordinarily be used inconsistently with the statutory scheme or to frustrate Parliament's legislation.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Attorney General v De Keyser's Royal Hotel Ltd; R (Miller) v Secretary of State for Exiting the European Union.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Statute prevails over inconsistent executive reliance on prerogative powers.","common_trap":"Treating prerogative as a source of executive authority superior to legislation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6c2d656e98be24e0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0508","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — procedural fairness","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A regulator proposes to revoke a professional licence based on serious allegations received from a third party. The licensee is not told the substance of the allegations and is given no meaningful opportunity to respond before the decision.\n\nWhich ground of judicial review is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Only irrationality can ever challenge regulatory procedure.","B":"A public authority never needs to hear a person affected by its decision.","C":"Procedural unfairness, including the common-law requirement of a fair opportunity to know and answer the case where fairness requires it.","D":"Natural justice applies only to criminal courts.","E":"The decision is automatically valid if the regulator acted honestly."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Common-law fairness can require a person significantly affected by an adverse decision to know the gist of the case and have a fair opportunity to respond, with content depending on context.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Correct. Common-law fairness can require a person significantly affected by an adverse decision to know the gist of the case and have a fair opportunity to respond, with content depending on context.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Secretary of State for the Home Department, ex p Doody; common-law procedural fairness.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Procedural fairness may require notice of the substance of adverse material and a meaningful opportunity to make representations.","common_trap":"Assuming honest motive cures an unfair procedure or that natural justice is confined to courts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"54c4596f514088b6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0509","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — anti-lapse for issue","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A testator leaves a gift 'to my daughter absolutely'. The daughter dies before the testator leaving children who survive the testator. The will contains no contrary intention and the statutory conditions are otherwise met.\n\nWhat is the ordinary effect of section 33 of the Wills Act 1837?","options":{"A":"The gift must always lapse into residue whenever any beneficiary predeceases the testator.","B":"Section 33 applies only to gifts to friends.","C":"The surviving grandchildren are ignored unless expressly named.","D":"The gift can pass to the deceased daughter's issue rather than lapse, subject to the statutory conditions.","E":"The entire will is revoked by the daughter's death."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 33 provides an anti-lapse rule for gifts to a testator's child or remoter descendant who predeceases the testator leaving issue who survive, unless the will shows a contrary intention.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Correct. Section 33 provides an anti-lapse rule for gifts to a testator's child or remoter descendant who predeceases the testator leaving issue who survive, unless the will shows a contrary intention.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837, s 33.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A qualifying gift to a deceased descendant can be preserved for that descendant's issue by the statutory anti-lapse rule.","common_trap":"Applying the general lapse rule without considering the statutory descendant exception.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b239bdd55e8b3fd5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0510","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"IHT — gift with reservation of benefit","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A parent gives a house outright to an adult child but continues living in it rent-free for life without any relevant sharing arrangement or other exception.\n\nWhich inheritance-tax concept is particularly relevant on the parent's death?","options":{"A":"The gift is necessarily outside the estate merely because legal title transferred.","B":"Every lifetime gift becomes exempt immediately.","C":"Only capital gains tax can be relevant to retained enjoyment.","D":"The reservation rules apply only to cash gifts.","E":"The gift-with-reservation rules may treat the property as remaining within the donor's estate for IHT purposes despite the lifetime transfer."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Where a donor gives property but retains significant enjoyment or benefit, the gift-with-reservation regime can bring the property back into the donor's estate for IHT purposes despite the earlier transfer of title.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Correct. Where a donor gives property but retains significant enjoyment or benefit, the gift-with-reservation regime can bring the property back into the donor's estate for IHT purposes despite the earlier transfer of title."},"primary_legal_authority":"Finance Act 1986, ss 102–102C.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Retained enjoyment of gifted property can prevent effective removal from the donor's IHT estate.","common_trap":"Equating transfer of legal ownership with complete IHT effectiveness despite retained benefit.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"98cd85a5bb3e9aa4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0511","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Estate administration — abatement","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An estate is solvent as to debts but insufficient to satisfy all testamentary gifts in full after liabilities and administration expenses. The will contains no special direction changing the normal order.\n\nWhich doctrine determines how testamentary gifts are reduced?","options":{"A":"Abatement, under which gifts bear the shortfall according to the applicable order and rules.","B":"Ademption, because every insufficient estate adeems all gifts.","C":"Lapse, because beneficiaries automatically predecease the testator when funds are short.","D":"Hotchpot applies to every will.","E":"The rule against perpetuities."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Abatement governs reduction of testamentary gifts where estate assets are insufficient after liabilities and expenses. It is distinct from ademption, which concerns disappearance of specifically gifted property.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Abatement governs reduction of testamentary gifts where estate assets are insufficient after liabilities and expenses. It is distinct from ademption, which concerns disappearance of specifically gifted property.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of estates principles on abatement.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Abatement allocates an estate shortfall among gifts according to the applicable priority rules.","common_trap":"Confusing insufficiency of the estate generally with ademption of a particular specific asset.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5e77bfe88859daeb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0512","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Client account — banking facility prohibition","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A long-standing client asks a solicitor to receive money into client account and immediately forward it to a third party. The transfer has no underlying legal transaction or regulated service connected with the firm's work; the client merely wants to use the firm's account for convenience.\n\nWhich is the best regulatory position?","options":{"A":"The firm may provide banking services whenever the client is trustworthy.","B":"The firm must not use client account as a banking facility; any payment into or transfer from client account must have a proper connection with the delivery of regulated services.","C":"Client account can be used for any personal payment if no fee is charged.","D":"The prohibition applies only to cash, not electronic transfers.","E":"A client's consent eliminates the Accounts Rules."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"SRA Accounts Rule 3.3 prohibits using client account to provide banking facilities. Receipts and payments must relate to the delivery of regulated services by the firm.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Correct. SRA Accounts Rule 3.3 prohibits using client account to provide banking facilities. Receipts and payments must relate to the delivery of regulated services by the firm.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, r 3.3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Client account cannot be used as a general payment conduit unrelated to the firm's legal work.","common_trap":"Assuming client trustworthiness or consent makes an unrelated banking transaction permissible.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d9efd887f929661c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0513","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Certainty of subject matter — intangible property","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A settlor declares a trust of '50 of my 1,000 identical ordinary shares in X plc' but does not identify particular numbered shares. All 1,000 shares are of the same class and legally indistinguishable.\n\nWhich issue is most directly raised?","options":{"A":"Certainty of intention only; subject matter can never be an issue for shares.","B":"A trust of shares always requires physical segregation of share certificates.","C":"Certainty of subject matter, including whether the trust property is sufficiently identified despite the assets being identical intangible units.","D":"The trust necessarily fails because intangible property cannot be held on trust.","E":"Only certainty of objects matters once a beneficiary is named."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A trust requires certain subject matter. Authority distinguishes some trusts of identical intangible assets from unsegregated tangible goods, so the nature of the property matters to whether the relevant quantity is sufficiently certain.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Correct. A trust requires certain subject matter. Authority distinguishes some trusts of identical intangible assets from unsegregated tangible goods, so the nature of the property matters to whether the relevant quantity is sufficiently certain.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Hunter v Moss; Re London Wine Co; Re Goldcorp Exchange Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The trust property must be sufficiently certain, with different considerations potentially applying to identical intangible assets.","common_trap":"Assuming all assets require physical segregation in precisely the same way or that subject-matter certainty is irrelevant to shares.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"bd28778ca3bbc745"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0514","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustee investment — suitability and diversification","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Trustees invest the entire substantial trust fund in one speculative company without considering whether that investment suits the trust or whether diversification is appropriate. The trust instrument does not exclude the statutory investment duties.\n\nWhich statutory duties are particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"Trustees may invest without considering risk whenever they act unanimously.","B":"Diversification is legally prohibited for trusts.","C":"The Trustee Act requires investment only in government bonds.","D":"The trustees must consider the standard investment criteria, including suitability and the need for diversification so far as appropriate to the trust.","E":"Investment duties apply only to charitable trusts."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"When exercising the general investment power, trustees must have regard to the standard investment criteria: suitability of investments and the need for diversification insofar as appropriate, with periodic review.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Correct. When exercising the general investment power, trustees must have regard to the standard investment criteria: suitability of investments and the need for diversification insofar as appropriate, with periodic review.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 2000, ss 3–4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Trustee investment decisions require consideration of suitability and appropriate diversification.","common_trap":"Treating the broad investment power as eliminating the statutory prudential criteria.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b7f304bd309b82c7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0515","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Mortgages — equity of redemption","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A lender agrees to a mortgage only if the borrower promises that, even after repaying the loan in full, the lender will have a permanent option preventing the borrower from recovering the property free of the mortgage.\n\nWhich equitable principle is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Any term agreed with a lender is automatically valid because of freedom of contract.","B":"The borrower has no right to redeem a mortgage.","C":"Redemption is available only with the lender's voluntary consent.","D":"The rule against clogs applies only to unsecured loans.","E":"A mortgage cannot contain a clog or fetter that improperly prevents or impedes the mortgagor's equity of redemption."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Equity treats a mortgage as security and protects the mortgagor's right to redeem once the secured obligation is satisfied. Terms that make redemption illusory or improperly fetter it can be invalid.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Correct. Equity treats a mortgage as security and protects the mortgagor's right to redeem once the secured obligation is satisfied. Terms that make redemption illusory or improperly fetter it can be invalid."},"primary_legal_authority":"Santley v Wilde; Fairclough v Swan Brewery Co Ltd; mortgage redemption principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The mortgagor's equity of redemption cannot be improperly clogged by the mortgage bargain.","common_trap":"Assuming contractual agreement alone validates a term fundamentally inconsistent with the right to redeem.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9f3a2015455c701b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0516","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Proprietary estoppel — assurance, reliance and detriment","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A landowner repeatedly assures a relative that the relative will receive a specific parcel of land. Relying reasonably on those assurances, the relative works for years for low pay and spends money improving the land. The owner later denies any entitlement.\n\nWhich equitable doctrine is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Proprietary estoppel, requiring the court to consider assurance, reliance and detriment and whether it would be unconscionable to resile, followed by an appropriate remedy.","B":"Promissory estoppel can automatically transfer legal title to land in every case.","C":"Adverse possession necessarily applies because the relative worked on the land.","D":"A resulting trust arises automatically from unpaid labour.","E":"The assurance is enforceable only if made by deed."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Proprietary estoppel can arise from sufficiently clear assurance concerning rights in property, reasonable reliance and detriment in circumstances making it unconscionable for the promisor to resile. Remedy is then tailored to the equity.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Proprietary estoppel can arise from sufficiently clear assurance concerning rights in property, reasonable reliance and detriment in circumstances making it unconscionable for the promisor to resile. Remedy is then tailored to the equity.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Thorner v Major; Guest v Guest [2022] UKSC 27.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Property-related assurances inducing detrimental reliance can found proprietary estoppel even without a completed formal transfer.","common_trap":"Treating the doctrine as automatically granting the precise promised property or confusing it with adverse possession.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a0efecd9c5c8f33f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0517","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Title investigation — registered title","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer's solicitor investigates title to registered freehold land. The seller provides the title number and the solicitor wants authoritative current evidence of the registered proprietor, registered charges and entries affecting the title.\n\nWhich documents are ordinarily obtained from HM Land Registry?","options":{"A":"An epitome of title covering at least fifteen years is always required for registered land.","B":"Official copies of the register and, where relevant, the title plan and filed documents.","C":"Only the seller's old paper deeds can prove registered title.","D":"A local authority search replaces investigation of the register.","E":"Companies House records establish ownership of registered land."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"For registered land, official copies of the register provide authoritative evidence of current registered entries, supplemented where appropriate by the title plan and copies of filed documents.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Correct. For registered land, official copies of the register provide authoritative evidence of current registered entries, supplemented where appropriate by the title plan and copies of filed documents.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002; HM Land Registry practice on official copies.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Investigation of registered title centres on current official Land Registry records.","common_trap":"Applying the unregistered-title deeds approach as the primary proof of ownership for registered land.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6b21b7fb62a88932"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0518","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Completion — late completion and notice to complete","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A residential sale contract incorporates the Standard Conditions of Sale. The contractual completion date passes because the buyer fails to provide completion funds. The seller does not want immediately to treat the contract as ended but wants to make time effectively of the essence under the contractual machinery.\n\nWhat step is ordinarily relevant?","options":{"A":"The contract automatically ends at midnight on the contractual completion date in every case.","B":"Issue an OS1 search against the buyer.","C":"Serve a valid notice to complete in accordance with the contract, triggering the contractual completion period and consequences if the buyer still fails to complete.","D":"Serve a section 27 Trustee Act notice.","E":"Apply for probate."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Under standard conveyancing conditions, failure to complete on the contractual date does not ordinarily terminate the contract automatically. A notice to complete can activate the specified period and make time of the essence for the ensuing contractual consequences.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Correct. Under standard conveyancing conditions, failure to complete on the contractual date does not ordinarily terminate the contract automatically. A notice to complete can activate the specified period and make time of the essence for the ensuing contractual consequences.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Standard Conditions of Sale; conveyancing completion practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A notice to complete is the standard contractual mechanism following failure to complete where the innocent party wants to enforce a final completion timetable.","common_trap":"Assuming every missed completion date automatically rescinds the sale contract.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f1ca17dfbda4ea8d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0519","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Theft — later appropriation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person innocently receives property belonging to another, later discovers the true owner's identity, and then dishonestly decides to keep and deal with the property as their own.\n\nWhich Theft Act principle is particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"Appropriation can occur only at the instant property first comes into the defendant's possession.","B":"An innocent initial receipt permanently prevents theft liability.","C":"Keeping another's property can never involve appropriation.","D":"A later assumption of an owner's right can amount to appropriation even where the property was initially acquired innocently.","E":"Theft requires the property to have been taken by force."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 3 recognises that where property has been acquired without theft, a later assumption of a right of the owner by keeping or dealing with it as owner can constitute appropriation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Correct. Section 3 recognises that where property has been acquired without theft, a later assumption of a right of the owner by keeping or dealing with it as owner can constitute appropriation.","E":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Theft Act 1968, s 3(1).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Appropriation can occur after innocent receipt when the defendant later assumes an owner's rights dishonestly.","common_trap":"Focusing exclusively on the moment of initial possession and overlooking later appropriation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"78c0d71628d1019e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0520","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Police station — right to legal advice","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A detained suspect asks to speak privately with a solicitor before interview. The investigating officer says legal advice is unnecessary because the alleged offence is straightforward.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Police may refuse legal advice whenever they think the case is simple.","B":"Only suspects charged with indictable-only offences can consult a solicitor.","C":"The right arises only after the first interview has finished.","D":"Legal advice at a police station must always be paid for by the suspect.","E":"A detained person has a statutory right to consult a solicitor privately, with delay permitted only in limited circumstances prescribed by law; perceived simplicity of the case is not enough."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"PACE provides a detained person with the right to consult a solicitor privately at any time, subject to tightly defined provisions permitting delay in specified serious cases. The police cannot deny the right merely because they view advice as unnecessary.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option states a different rule, omits a required element, or overstates the governing doctrine; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Correct. PACE provides a detained person with the right to consult a solicitor privately at any time, subject to tightly defined provisions permitting delay in specified serious cases. The police cannot deny the right merely because they view advice as unnecessary."},"primary_legal_authority":"Police and Criminal Evidence Act 1984, s 58; PACE Code C.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Access to independent legal advice is a statutory custody right, subject only to defined exceptions.","common_trap":"Treating police assessment of the case's simplicity as a lawful ground to deny access to a solicitor.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ff2a15470ab641ef"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0521","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — authority to allot shares","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company with more than one class of shares wants its directors to allot new ordinary shares. The articles contain no special authority and the company is not within the statutory exception that gives directors automatic authority for a private company with only one class.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The directors require authority to allot under the Companies Act, ordinarily conferred by the articles or by shareholder resolution within the statutory limits.","B":"Directors of every private company have unlimited automatic authority to allot any class of shares.","C":"Only Companies House can authorise an allotment.","D":"A share allotment never requires corporate authority if cash is paid.","E":"Existing shareholders must unanimously sign every allotment."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Section 549 restricts directors from allotting shares or granting subscription/conversion rights without authority, subject to statutory exceptions. Section 551 provides the general mechanism for authority by articles or company resolution.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Section 549 restricts directors from allotting shares or granting subscription/conversion rights without authority, subject to statutory exceptions. Section 551 provides the general mechanism for authority by articles or company resolution.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 549–551.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Where the single-class private-company exception does not apply, directors need valid statutory authority to allot shares.","common_trap":"Assuming all private-company directors possess unlimited allotment authority regardless of capital structure.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"dee952e8718089fa"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0522","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — statutory pre-emption rights","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company proposes a cash allotment of new ordinary equity securities to a new investor. Existing ordinary shareholders have not waived or disapplied their statutory rights, and no exception applies.\n\nWhat must ordinarily be considered before allotment?","options":{"A":"There are never pre-emption rights on a cash issue of ordinary shares.","B":"The statutory pre-emption regime, which generally requires qualifying equity securities to be offered first to existing ordinary shareholders on a proportionate basis.","C":"Pre-emption rights require the company to offer the shares first to directors only.","D":"Statutory pre-emption applies only to transfers of existing shares between shareholders.","E":"The new investor automatically acquires priority because it offered cash."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Companies Act generally protects existing holders against dilution on certain cash allotments of equity securities by requiring a proportionate first offer, subject to statutory exceptions and lawful disapplication.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Correct. The Companies Act generally protects existing holders against dilution on certain cash allotments of equity securities by requiring a proportionate first offer, subject to statutory exceptions and lawful disapplication.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 561–577.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A qualifying cash issue of equity securities ordinarily engages statutory pre-emption rights unless validly excluded or disapplied.","common_trap":"Confusing an allotment of new equity with a transfer of existing shares or assuming cash consideration removes pre-emption.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"04d8807ab877b922"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0523","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — distributions: unlawful distribution recovery","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A shareholder receives a distribution that contravenes the statutory rules and knew, or had reasonable grounds for believing, that it was unlawful when received.\n\nWhat consequence may follow?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","C":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","D":"The shareholder can be liable to repay the distribution or its value to the company under the statutory recovery rule.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The distribution regime can impose recipient liability where the shareholder had the specified knowledge.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Correct. The distribution regime can impose recipient liability where the shareholder had the specified knowledge.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, s 847","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The distribution regime can impose recipient liability where the shareholder had the specified knowledge.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d5066925d6634624"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0524","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — preferences","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An insolvent company repays a debt owed to the managing director's spouse shortly before liquidation, putting the spouse in a better position than in the liquidation. The transaction falls within the relevant time. The liquidator investigates the statutory mental element.\n\nWhat additional element is ordinarily required for a preference?","options":{"A":"Any payment to a creditor before liquidation is automatically a preference.","B":"The company must have received significantly less value than it gave.","C":"A preference exists only where the creditor demanded payment by court order.","D":"The company must have been influenced by a desire to produce the preferential effect, with statutory presumptions potentially relevant for connected persons.","E":"The creditor must prove that the company was solvent."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 239 requires the company to do something that puts a creditor, surety or guarantor in a better insolvency position and to be influenced by a desire to produce that effect. For connected persons, the statutory presumption concerning desire may apply.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Correct. Section 239 requires the company to do something that puts a creditor, surety or guarantor in a better insolvency position and to be influenced by a desire to produce that effect. For connected persons, the statutory presumption concerning desire may apply.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, ss 239 and 240.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A preference requires the statutory preferential effect plus the relevant desire, subject to connected-person presumptions.","common_trap":"Confusing preferences with transactions at an undervalue or assuming every pre-insolvency payment is avoidable.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"11d40b217c501a84"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0525","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Service — claim form within jurisdiction","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant issues a claim form in England and Wales and serves it on a defendant within the jurisdiction. No special rule alters the ordinary period for completing the relevant service step.\n\nWhat timing rule is central?","options":{"A":"A claim form within the jurisdiction is valid for service for twelve months automatically.","B":"Service can occur at any time before trial.","C":"The limitation period and the claim-form service period are identical.","D":"The defendant must serve the claim form on itself.","E":"The claimant must complete the applicable step required by CPR 7.5 before midnight on the calendar day four months after issue of the claim form."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"CPR 7.5 sets the ordinary four-month period for completing the specified service step where the claim form is to be served within the jurisdiction, subject to extensions and other applicable rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Correct. CPR 7.5 sets the ordinary four-month period for completing the specified service step where the claim form is to be served within the jurisdiction, subject to extensions and other applicable rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 7.5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Issue does not give an unlimited period for service; CPR 7.5 imposes a distinct service deadline.","common_trap":"Confusing the substantive limitation period with the procedural period for serving an issued claim form.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"464e9bc754100b2d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0526","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Relief from sanctions — Denton approach","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A party misses a court-ordered deadline and a sanction takes effect. It applies promptly for relief, explaining the circumstances and relying on overall compliance in the litigation.\n\nWhich framework should the court ordinarily apply?","options":{"A":"The CPR 3.9 relief-from-sanctions framework as explained in Denton: assess seriousness and significance, consider why the default occurred, then evaluate all the circumstances so the application is dealt with justly.","B":"Relief must automatically be granted whenever an application is prompt.","C":"Any breach permanently bars relief regardless of seriousness.","D":"The court considers only whether the defaulting party apologised.","E":"Relief from sanctions is governed exclusively by Part 36."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Denton explains the structured approach to CPR 3.9: first assess seriousness and significance of the breach, second consider the reason, and third evaluate all circumstances, including efficient and proportionate litigation and compliance with rules and orders.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Denton explains the structured approach to CPR 3.9: first assess seriousness and significance of the breach, second consider the reason, and third evaluate all circumstances, including efficient and proportionate litigation and compliance with rules and orders.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 3.9; Denton v TH White Ltd [2014] EWCA Civ 906.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Relief from sanctions requires the structured Denton assessment rather than an automatic result.","common_trap":"Reducing the analysis to a single factor such as promptness, reason or seriousness.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b3724a7d88b9d989"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0527","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Privilege — inadvertent disclosure","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"During disclosure, a privileged document is accidentally provided to the opposing solicitor, who immediately recognises that the disclosure was an obvious mistake.\n\nWhat is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","C":"The receiving party cannot assume accidental disclosure automatically destroys privilege; use of an obviously inadvertently disclosed privileged document can be restrained by the court.","D":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Inadvertent disclosure raises specific procedural and equitable protections and does not necessarily amount to intentional waiver.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Correct. Inadvertent disclosure raises specific procedural and equitable protections and does not necessarily amount to intentional waiver.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"CPR r 31.20; Al Fayed v Commissioner of Police of the Metropolis [2002] EWCA Civ 780","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Inadvertent disclosure raises specific procedural and equitable protections and does not necessarily amount to intentional waiver.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f15f57ce32cc4445"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0528","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Professional conduct — misleading the court","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor discovers before a hearing that a factual assertion in a filed witness statement, on which the solicitor intended to rely, is materially false. The client instructs the solicitor to say nothing and continue relying on it.\n\nWhat is the solicitor's professional position?","options":{"A":"Client instructions require the solicitor to rely on any evidence the client chooses.","B":"The duty to the client always overrides duties concerning the administration of justice.","C":"The solicitor must not mislead or attempt to mislead the court and cannot knowingly continue to rely on the false assertion as though it were true.","D":"A solicitor may knowingly mislead the court if the opponent could discover the truth.","E":"The rule applies only to criminal proceedings."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The SRA Code prohibits misleading or attempting to mislead the court or others, whether by acts or omissions or by allowing another person to be misled. Client instructions cannot require professional misconduct.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Correct. The SRA Code prohibits misleading or attempting to mislead the court or others, whether by acts or omissions or by allowing another person to be misled. Client instructions cannot require professional misconduct.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 1.4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A solicitor's duties to the administration of justice prohibit knowing reliance on materially false evidence.","common_trap":"Treating client autonomy or adversarial procedure as permission knowingly to mislead the tribunal.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"027bcc935290750f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0529","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"AML — customer due diligence","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm in the regulated sector is about to establish a business relationship with a new corporate client. No simplified-due-diligence basis has been established.\n\nWhich is the best general requirement?","options":{"A":"The firm may postpone all identity checks indefinitely if the client appears respectable.","B":"CDD is required only after money laundering has been proved.","C":"Corporate clients are exempt from beneficial-ownership enquiries.","D":"The firm must apply the customer-due-diligence measures required by the Money Laundering Regulations, including identifying and verifying the customer and, where applicable, beneficial owners.","E":"The SRA Accounts Rules replace the Money Laundering Regulations."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The MLR 2017 require regulated persons to apply CDD in specified circumstances, including establishment of a business relationship, and to identify and verify customers and relevant beneficial owners on a risk-sensitive basis.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Correct. The MLR 2017 require regulated persons to apply CDD in specified circumstances, including establishment of a business relationship, and to identify and verify customers and relevant beneficial owners on a risk-sensitive basis.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Money Laundering Regulations 2017, regs 27–28.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A regulated firm must carry out prescribed CDD when establishing a business relationship.","common_trap":"Treating CDD as a response only after criminality is established or assuming corporate status removes beneficial-ownership checks.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"15e19917c97d1454"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0530","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Formation — revocation of offer","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An offeror posts a letter revoking an offer. Before the revocation reaches the offeree, the offeree validly accepts the offer using an authorised instantaneous method. No option contract exists.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"Posting revocation always terminates the offer immediately.","B":"An offer can never be revoked once made.","C":"The postal rule always makes revocation effective on posting.","D":"Revocation requires a deed.","E":"Revocation is ordinarily ineffective until communicated to the offeree, so a valid acceptance before communication of revocation can form a contract."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Revocation of an offer generally must be communicated to the offeree before acceptance. The postal acceptance rule does not ordinarily make a revocation effective merely on posting.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Correct. Revocation of an offer generally must be communicated to the offeree before acceptance. The postal acceptance rule does not ordinarily make a revocation effective merely on posting."},"primary_legal_authority":"Byrne & Co v Leon Van Tienhoven & Co.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An uncommunicated revocation does not ordinarily terminate an offer before a valid acceptance.","common_trap":"Applying the postal rule for acceptance to revocation or treating posting alone as sufficient communication.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"537001fa9cc932bc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0531","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Contract — damages: mitigation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A supplier repudiates a contract. The innocent buyer could reasonably obtain an equivalent substitute promptly at the market price but deliberately refuses to do so and allows avoidable losses to accumulate.\n\nHow does mitigation affect damages?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The claimant cannot ordinarily recover loss that could reasonably have been avoided after breach, although the burden is on the defendant to establish failure to mitigate.","C":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","D":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Mitigation limits recoverable loss; it is not a positive duty owed to the defendant.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Correct. Mitigation limits recoverable loss; it is not a positive duty owed to the defendant.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"British Westinghouse Electric v Underground Electric Railways [1912] AC 673","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Mitigation limits recoverable loss; it is not a positive duty owed to the defendant.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"795bedf878f625c9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0532","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Consumer rights — satisfactory quality","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A consumer buys a new household appliance from a trader. The appliance has a serious latent manufacturing defect inconsistent with the standard a reasonable person would regard as satisfactory, taking account of description, price and other relevant circumstances.\n\nWhich statutory term is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"The Sale of Goods Act applies to the exclusion of the CRA in every consumer sale.","B":"The Consumer Rights Act 2015 term that goods supplied under a consumer contract must be of satisfactory quality.","C":"A trader gives no statutory quality obligation unless an express warranty is signed.","D":"Only fitness for a specifically disclosed purpose can ever be relevant.","E":"Consumer quality rights apply only to second-hand goods."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The CRA implies a term that goods are of satisfactory quality, judged by the standard a reasonable person would consider satisfactory in light of description, price and other relevant circumstances, including statutory aspects of quality.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Correct. The CRA implies a term that goods are of satisfactory quality, judged by the standard a reasonable person would consider satisfactory in light of description, price and other relevant circumstances, including statutory aspects of quality.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Consumer Rights Act 2015, s 9.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Consumer goods must satisfy the CRA's statutory satisfactory-quality standard.","common_trap":"Assuming consumer quality protection depends solely on an express warranty or a specifically communicated purpose.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a09244b479e47027"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0533","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — contributory negligence","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant's negligence causes an accident, but the claimant's own lack of reasonable care materially contributes to the damage suffered. The claimant's conduct does not break the chain of causation.\n\nWhat is the ordinary effect if contributory negligence is established?","options":{"A":"The claim is automatically defeated in full.","B":"The defendant becomes free of all liability whenever the claimant was careless.","C":"The claimant's damages may be reduced to the extent the court considers just and equitable having regard to the claimant's share in responsibility.","D":"Contributory negligence increases damages as punishment.","E":"The doctrine applies only to contractual claims."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The 1945 Act replaces the former complete bar with apportionment: recoverable damages are reduced by the proportion the court considers just and equitable in light of the claimant's responsibility for the damage.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Correct. The 1945 Act replaces the former complete bar with apportionment: recoverable damages are reduced by the proportion the court considers just and equitable in light of the claimant's responsibility for the damage.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law Reform (Contributory Negligence) Act 1945, s 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contributory negligence ordinarily reduces damages rather than extinguishing the claim.","common_trap":"Treating claimant fault as an automatic complete defence rather than a basis for apportionment.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5d31d953eabd32a2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0534","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Occupiers' liability — children","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A museum knows that a low barrier around an attractive mechanical exhibit is likely to tempt young children to climb. A six-year-old visitor does so and is injured. The museum argues that an adult would have appreciated the risk.\n\nWhich principle is particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"Children are always treated as having an adult's appreciation of danger.","B":"Occupiers owe no duty to child visitors accompanied by parents.","C":"A child can never recover under the 1957 Act.","D":"An occupier must be prepared for children to be less careful than adults, so the common duty is assessed with that statutory consideration in mind.","E":"The museum is strictly liable for every injury to a child."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 2(3)(a) requires an occupier to be prepared for children to be less careful than adults. The overall duty remains one of reasonable safety, with circumstances including parental supervision potentially relevant.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Correct. Section 2(3)(a) requires an occupier to be prepared for children to be less careful than adults. The overall duty remains one of reasonable safety, with circumstances including parental supervision potentially relevant.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Occupiers' Liability Act 1957, s 2(3)(a); Glasgow Corporation v Taylor.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The reasonable occupier must account for children's lesser care when assessing visitor safety.","common_trap":"Applying an adult standard of appreciation to a young child or, conversely, assuming strict liability.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2103c181204147f3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0535","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Product liability — Consumer Protection Act","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A consumer is injured because a product is not as safe as persons generally are entitled to expect. The producer exercised considerable care in manufacturing it and argues that absence of negligence is a complete answer.\n\nWhich is the best general analysis?","options":{"A":"The claimant must always prove that the producer personally acted negligently.","B":"The Act applies only where the producer intended injury.","C":"Product liability exists only through contract.","D":"A product is defective only if it fails to last forever.","E":"Liability under Part I of the Consumer Protection Act 1987 is based on a defective product and does not require the claimant to prove negligence, subject to the statutory framework and defences."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The CPA 1987 establishes a statutory regime imposing liability for damage caused by a defect, defined by reference to safety persons generally are entitled to expect. Proof of negligence is not an element, although statutory defences apply.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Correct. The CPA 1987 establishes a statutory regime imposing liability for damage caused by a defect, defined by reference to safety persons generally are entitled to expect. Proof of negligence is not an element, although statutory defences apply."},"primary_legal_authority":"Consumer Protection Act 1987, ss 2–4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The statutory product-liability claim focuses on defect, damage and causation rather than proof of producer negligence.","common_trap":"Importing the fault requirement of negligence into the CPA's statutory defective-product regime.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"94e2923ea82ed559"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0536","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Human Rights Act — section 3 interpretation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A statutory provision is capable of a Convention-compatible interpretation that remains within the legitimate interpretive possibilities available under the legislation.\n\nWhich Human Rights Act duty is central?","options":{"A":"So far as possible, primary and subordinate legislation must be read and given effect compatibly with Convention rights under section 3.","B":"The court must issue a declaration of incompatibility without first considering interpretation.","C":"Section 3 authorises courts to repeal Acts of Parliament.","D":"Convention rights can never affect statutory interpretation.","E":"Section 3 applies only to legislation enacted after 1998."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Section 3 requires legislation, so far as possible, to be read and given effect in a way compatible with Convention rights. The interpretive obligation is powerful but has constitutional limits.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Section 3 requires legislation, so far as possible, to be read and given effect in a way compatible with Convention rights. The interpretive obligation is powerful but has constitutional limits.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, s 3; Ghaidan v Godin-Mendoza.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Courts must first apply the section 3 compatibility obligation where a permissible interpretation is available.","common_trap":"Jumping directly to section 4 or treating section 3 as a power judicially to rewrite legislation without limit.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ff0ee9ff2fd0e451"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0537","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Property — lease assignment: qualified covenant","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A commercial lease prohibits assignment without the landlord's consent. The covenant is qualified rather than absolute, and the tenant applies for consent.\n\nWhat statutory overlay should be considered?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","C":"The landlord is subject to statutory duties concerning consent, including acting within a reasonable time and, where applicable, not unreasonably withholding consent.","D":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Qualified alienation covenants are supplemented by statutory duties; the lease wording is not the whole analysis.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Correct. Qualified alienation covenants are supplemented by statutory duties; the lease wording is not the whole analysis.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Landlord and Tenant Act 1927, s 19(1); Landlord and Tenant Act 1988","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Qualified alienation covenants are supplemented by statutory duties; the lease wording is not the whole analysis.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7b72986c81d3abd0"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0538","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Delegated legislation — ultra vires","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A minister makes regulations under an Act. The regulations impose obligations going materially beyond the scope of the regulation-making power Parliament conferred.\n\nWhich is the most direct legal challenge?","options":{"A":"Delegated legislation can never be reviewed by a court.","B":"A minister may legislate on any subject once any regulation-making power exists.","C":"The regulations may be substantively ultra vires because the delegated legislation exceeds the authority granted by the parent Act.","D":"The regulations automatically amend the parent Act.","E":"Only Parliament can determine whether delegated legislation exceeded statutory power."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Delegated legislation derives legal authority from the enabling Act and can be quashed or treated as unlawful where it exceeds the scope or purpose of the delegated power, subject to the governing public-law principles.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Correct. Delegated legislation derives legal authority from the enabling Act and can be quashed or treated as unlawful where it exceeds the scope or purpose of the delegated power, subject to the governing public-law principles.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Delegated-legislation ultra vires principles; R (Public Law Project) v Lord Chancellor [2016] UKSC 39.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Secondary legislation must remain within the power Parliament delegated.","common_trap":"Treating delegated legislation as possessing the same autonomous law-making authority as primary legislation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f49bef4745760332"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0539","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — effect of divorce","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A testator validly leaves a gift to a spouse and appoints that spouse executor. The couple later divorce. The testator dies without making a new will. The will contains no provision showing a contrary intention relevant to the statutory rule.\n\nHow is the former spouse ordinarily treated under the will?","options":{"A":"Divorce automatically revokes the entire will.","B":"Divorce has no effect whatsoever on gifts or executor appointments to a former spouse.","C":"The former spouse automatically receives the whole estate.","D":"For the relevant testamentary gifts and appointments, the will generally takes effect as if the former spouse had died on the date the marriage was dissolved, subject to the statutory wording and contrary intention.","E":"The will becomes intestate in every respect."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 18A provides that divorce or annulment ordinarily causes specified gifts and appointments in favour of the former spouse to take effect as if that person had died at dissolution, unless a contrary intention appears.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Correct. Section 18A provides that divorce or annulment ordinarily causes specified gifts and appointments in favour of the former spouse to take effect as if that person had died at dissolution, unless a contrary intention appears.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837, s 18A.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Divorce generally affects dispositions and appointments to the former spouse without revoking the whole will.","common_trap":"Confusing the effect of divorce with the separate rule by which marriage can revoke a will.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"63879ab1faa61c39"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0540","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Personal representatives — executor eligibility","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A will appoints two executors. One is an adult with capacity. The other is a minor when the testator dies. The estate requires a grant.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The minor automatically receives a full grant because the will appoints them.","B":"Appointment in a will removes every probate eligibility requirement.","C":"Neither executor can obtain a grant until the minor turns 18.","D":"A minor appointment automatically revokes the will.","E":"The minor cannot ordinarily take a grant while under 18; the adult executor may prove, with the minor's entitlement dealt with under the probate rules until majority."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Although a testator can name a minor as executor, a minor cannot ordinarily take a grant while under 18. The Non-Contentious Probate Rules govern how representation proceeds and preserve relevant rights.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Correct. Although a testator can name a minor as executor, a minor cannot ordinarily take a grant while under 18. The Non-Contentious Probate Rules govern how representation proceeds and preserve relevant rights."},"primary_legal_authority":"Non-Contentious Probate Rules 1987; probate rules concerning minority of executor.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Executor appointment does not eliminate the eligibility requirements for taking a grant.","common_trap":"Assuming testamentary nomination alone entitles every appointee immediately to a grant regardless of minority.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ede31774b06c5e4f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0541","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Probate — letters of administration with will annexed","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A deceased leaves a valid will disposing of the estate, but the sole named executor died before the deceased and there is no substitute executor able to prove. A beneficiary is entitled under the probate priority rules.\n\nWhich grant is ordinarily required?","options":{"A":"Letters of administration with the will annexed.","B":"A grant of probate to the deceased executor.","C":"Letters of administration on intestacy because absence of an executor invalidates the will.","D":"No grant can ever be issued where the named executor predeceased.","E":"A lasting power of attorney."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Where there is a valid will but no executor able and willing to prove, the court can issue letters of administration with the will annexed to the person entitled under the applicable priority rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Where there is a valid will but no executor able and willing to prove, the court can issue letters of administration with the will annexed to the person entitled under the applicable priority rules.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925; Non-Contentious Probate Rules 1987, including r 20.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Failure of executorship does not invalidate the will; administration can proceed with the will annexed.","common_trap":"Confusing absence of an acting executor with intestacy.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"20f1dfe1988366ae"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0542","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Client money — withdrawals","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor wants to withdraw money from client account to pay a third party. The amount belongs to a particular client, the payment is properly authorised for that client's transaction, and sufficient money is held for that client.\n\nWhich is the key Accounts Rules principle?","options":{"A":"A firm may use any aggregate client-account balance for any client's payment.","B":"Client-account withdrawals must be for the purpose for which the money is held or otherwise properly authorised under the Rules, and the firm must not use another client's money.","C":"Client money can be withdrawn for the firm's unrelated overheads whenever the account remains in credit overall.","D":"Authorisation is irrelevant once money reaches client account.","E":"Client account may never be used to pay a third party."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Accounts Rules require withdrawals from client account to be appropriately authorised and attributable to the relevant client or purpose. Firms must maintain sufficient funds for each client and cannot cross-subsidise using another client's money.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Correct. The Accounts Rules require withdrawals from client account to be appropriately authorised and attributable to the relevant client or purpose. Firms must maintain sufficient funds for each client and cannot cross-subsidise using another client's money.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, rr 5.1–5.3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A client-account payment must be properly authorised and supported by funds held for the relevant client or purpose.","common_trap":"Looking only at the aggregate bank balance rather than the individual client's ledger entitlement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5e0764941b8df088"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0543","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Three certainties — certainty of objects","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A settlor declares a discretionary trust 'for such of my relatives and dependants as my trustees select'. The class is conceptually defined so that it can be said of any given person whether they are or are not within it, although it is impossible to compile a complete list of every potential beneficiary.\n\nWhich test is most relevant?","options":{"A":"Every discretionary trust requires a complete list of all possible beneficiaries.","B":"Certainty of objects applies only to fixed trusts.","C":"For a discretionary trust, certainty of objects is generally assessed by the 'is or is not' test rather than requiring a complete list of every beneficiary.","D":"A discretionary trust is valid only if one beneficiary is named individually.","E":"Administrative difficulty and conceptual certainty are identical questions."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"McPhail v Doulton replaced the complete-list requirement for discretionary trusts with the test whether it can be said of any given person that they are or are not a member of the class, subject also to issues such as administrative unworkability.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Correct. McPhail v Doulton replaced the complete-list requirement for discretionary trusts with the test whether it can be said of any given person that they are or are not a member of the class, subject also to issues such as administrative unworkability.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"McPhail v Doulton; Re Baden (No 2).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Discretionary trusts use the 'is or is not' test for conceptual certainty of objects.","common_trap":"Applying the fixed-trust complete-list test to a discretionary trust.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9259501efae15c16"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0544","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Dishonest assistance","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A third party is not a trustee but knowingly helps a trustee carry out a transaction that breaches trust. Considering the third party's actual knowledge and beliefs, the assistance is dishonest by the objective standards of ordinary decent people.\n\nWhich equitable liability is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Knowing receipt, even though the third party received no trust property.","B":"Automatic trusteeship solely because assistance occurred.","C":"Proprietary estoppel.","D":"Dishonest assistance.","E":"Resulting trust."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Dishonest assistance requires a breach of trust or fiduciary duty, assistance by the defendant, and dishonesty assessed using the defendant's actual state of knowledge or belief against the objective standards of ordinary decent people.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Correct. Dishonest assistance requires a breach of trust or fiduciary duty, assistance by the defendant, and dishonesty assessed using the defendant's actual state of knowledge or belief against the objective standards of ordinary decent people.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Royal Brunei Airlines v Tan; Ivey v Genting Casinos; Group Seven Ltd v Notable Services LLP.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A person who dishonestly assists a breach of trust can incur personal equitable liability without receiving trust property.","common_trap":"Confusing assistance-based liability with knowing receipt, which requires beneficial receipt of trust assets.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"26d175a508335f61"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0545","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Freehold covenants — positive burden","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A freehold owner covenants to maintain a boundary wall. The land is later sold. The successor is not bound through any chain of indemnity or separate mechanism, and the claimant argues that the positive covenant's burden automatically runs with the freehold land in equity.\n\nWhich is the orthodox rule?","options":{"A":"All positive freehold covenant burdens automatically run in equity.","B":"Tulk v Moxhay makes positive and restrictive covenants identical.","C":"Registration alone always makes a positive covenant's burden enforceable against successors.","D":"Positive covenants are void even between the original parties.","E":"The burden of a positive freehold covenant does not ordinarily run with the land to bind successors merely because they acquire the land."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"English land law ordinarily does not permit the burden of a positive freehold covenant to run with freehold land, though contractual indemnities and limited doctrines may produce practical alternatives.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Correct. English land law ordinarily does not permit the burden of a positive freehold covenant to run with freehold land, though contractual indemnities and limited doctrines may produce practical alternatives."},"primary_legal_authority":"Austerberry v Oldham Corporation; Rhone v Stephens.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A positive covenant's burden ordinarily remains personal rather than automatically binding successors in title.","common_trap":"Extending the equitable running of restrictive covenants under Tulk v Moxhay to positive obligations.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5fda5bc9e1cb09ed"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0546","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Leases — exclusive possession","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An agreement labelled 'licence' grants a person exclusive possession of a flat for a fixed term at rent. The owner retains no genuine right to share occupation and the arrangement is not within a recognised exceptional category.\n\nWhich is the best analysis?","options":{"A":"The substance may create a lease despite the 'licence' label because exclusive possession for a term is a central indicator of tenancy.","B":"Calling the document a licence conclusively prevents a lease.","C":"Payment of rent always prevents a tenancy.","D":"Exclusive possession is irrelevant to lease classification.","E":"A residential occupier can never hold a lease unless the document is executed as a deed."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Street v Mountford requires the court to examine substance rather than labels. Exclusive possession for a term, subject to recognised exceptions and formal requirements, strongly indicates a tenancy.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Street v Mountford requires the court to examine substance rather than labels. Exclusive possession for a term, subject to recognised exceptions and formal requirements, strongly indicates a tenancy.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Street v Mountford [1985] AC 809.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Parties cannot avoid lease consequences merely by labelling an arrangement a licence when its substance grants exclusive possession.","common_trap":"Allowing contractual labels to override the legal characteristics of the occupation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ebaf1a955ae0ba4c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0547","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Pre-completion — OS1 priority search","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer of registered freehold land is ready to complete. The buyer's solicitor wants to protect the intended transfer against adverse registrable dispositions lodged between the pre-completion search and the buyer's registration application.\n\nWhich search is ordinarily used?","options":{"A":"A local authority search only.","B":"An OS1 official search with priority against the whole of a registered title.","C":"A bankruptcy search against the seller instead of any title search.","D":"An epitome of title.","E":"A section 27 Trustee Act notice."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"An OS1 search can provide a priority period for an application arising from the intended registrable disposition of the whole registered title, provided the later registration application is lodged within that period.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Correct. An OS1 search can provide a priority period for an application arising from the intended registrable disposition of the whole registered title, provided the later registration application is lodged within that period.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Rules 2003; HM Land Registry Practice Guide 12 and official search procedures.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A pre-completion OS1 search protects priority for the buyer's subsequent registration application.","common_trap":"Confusing pre-contract due-diligence searches with the Land Registry priority mechanism immediately before completion.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"68fee2a1e2f879e8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0548","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Commercial leases — consent to assignment","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A commercial lease contains a qualified covenant against assignment without landlord consent. The relevant statutory provisions apply. The tenant makes a proper written application with sufficient information. The landlord simply ignores it for months.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"A qualified covenant allows the landlord to refuse or ignore every request for any reason.","B":"Landlord consent is never required for assignment of a lease.","C":"The landlord may be under a statutory duty to give consent except where it is reasonable not to do so and to respond within a reasonable time, with potential liability for breach.","D":"The tenant automatically becomes freehold owner if the landlord delays.","E":"The statutory duty applies only after the assignment has already occurred."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Where the statutory regime applies to a qualified alienation covenant, the landlord must deal with a written application within a reasonable time and may refuse only on reasonable grounds, subject to the legislation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Correct. Where the statutory regime applies to a qualified alienation covenant, the landlord must deal with a written application within a reasonable time and may refuse only on reasonable grounds, subject to the legislation.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Landlord and Tenant Act 1927, s 19(1); Landlord and Tenant Act 1988.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A landlord cannot treat a qualified covenant as an absolute unrestricted veto where the statutory consent regime applies.","common_trap":"Assuming silence or arbitrary refusal is permitted merely because the lease requires consent.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"575a527872809857"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0549","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Attempts — more than merely preparatory","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant intends to commit burglary and, having equipped themself and travelled to the target, begins forcing open the victim's locked window to enter and steal. Police intervene before entry.\n\nWhich requirement for attempt is most directly in issue and likely satisfied on these facts?","options":{"A":"Attempt requires completion of every element of the full offence.","B":"Mere intention without any act is always enough.","C":"Attempt liability exists only after the substantive offence has been completed.","D":"The defendant has done an act more than merely preparatory to the commission of the intended offence.","E":"Burglary can never be attempted."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Under section 1 of the Criminal Attempts Act, a person intending to commit an indictable offence is guilty of attempt if they do an act more than merely preparatory to its commission. Beginning to force entry can move beyond preparation on appropriate facts.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Correct. Under section 1 of the Criminal Attempts Act, a person intending to commit an indictable offence is guilty of attempt if they do an act more than merely preparatory to its commission. Beginning to force entry can move beyond preparation on appropriate facts.","E":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Attempts Act 1981, s 1; R v Gullefer; R v Jones.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Attempt requires both the necessary intent and conduct that has progressed beyond mere preparation.","common_trap":"Treating preparatory planning alone as sufficient or requiring completion of the substantive offence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"91d8dd40aa939ea2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0550","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Allocation — either-way offence","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An adult defendant pleads not guilty to an either-way offence in the magistrates' court. The magistrates determine that their sentencing powers would be adequate and accept jurisdiction.\n\nWhat procedural choice does the defendant ordinarily have at the next stage?","options":{"A":"The magistrates' acceptance of jurisdiction permanently removes any possibility of Crown Court trial.","B":"The prosecution alone chooses the trial venue after allocation.","C":"Every either-way offence must be tried in the Crown Court.","D":"The defendant can elect Crown Court trial only after conviction.","E":"The defendant may elect trial by jury in the Crown Court rather than consent to summary trial."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"For an either-way offence, after the magistrates accept jurisdiction, the defendant is asked whether they consent to summary trial or elect trial in the Crown Court, subject to the governing allocation procedure.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","C":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option applies a different rule, omits a necessary element, or overstates the governing principle; compare the key rule and cited authority.","E":"Correct. For an either-way offence, after the magistrates accept jurisdiction, the defendant is asked whether they consent to summary trial or elect trial in the Crown Court, subject to the governing allocation procedure."},"primary_legal_authority":"Magistrates' Courts Act 1980; Crime and Disorder Act 1998; allocation and sending procedure.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A defendant charged with an either-way offence retains the statutory election for Crown Court trial after magistrates accept jurisdiction.","common_trap":"Confusing the magistrates' allocation decision with the defendant's subsequent right of election.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0c769a914e5a2627"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0551","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — removal of director","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A public company's shareholders want to remove a director before expiry of the director's term. The articles purport to require a 90% majority for removal. Proper special notice is given and the director is allowed the statutory opportunity to make representations.\n\nWhich is the best general analysis?","options":{"A":"The company may remove the director by ordinary resolution under section 168, notwithstanding contrary provisions in an agreement or articles, although separate contractual compensation rights may remain.","B":"The 90% provision prevents statutory removal unless 90% vote in favour.","C":"A director can be removed only by special resolution.","D":"Only the board can remove a director appointed by shareholders.","E":"Removal automatically extinguishes any contractual claim the director may have."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Section 168 gives a company a statutory power to remove a director by ordinary resolution at a meeting, notwithstanding anything in an agreement or the articles. Section 169 and the special-notice rules protect the director procedurally, while contractual rights may remain.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Section 168 gives a company a statutory power to remove a director by ordinary resolution at a meeting, notwithstanding anything in an agreement or the articles. Section 169 and the special-notice rules protect the director procedurally, while contractual rights may remain.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The 90% provision prevents statutory removal unless 90% vote in favour” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Shareholders' statutory removal power cannot ordinarily be increased to a 90% voting threshold by the articles.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition that “A director can be removed only by special resolution” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Shareholders' statutory removal power cannot ordinarily be increased to a 90% voting threshold by the articles.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Only the board can remove a director appointed by shareholders” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Shareholders' statutory removal power cannot ordinarily be increased to a 90% voting threshold by the articles.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Removal automatically extinguishes any contractual claim the director may have” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Shareholders' statutory removal power cannot ordinarily be increased to a 90% voting threshold by the articles."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 168–169 and 312.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Shareholders' statutory removal power cannot ordinarily be increased to a 90% voting threshold by the articles.","common_trap":"Confusing removal from office with termination of a service contract or assuming the articles can override section 168.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8a5f1f0e615a7771"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0552","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — substantial property transactions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A director proposes to buy a valuable property from the company. Its value is sufficiently substantial for the statutory regime and no exception applies. The board approves the deal, but members have not approved it.\n\nWhich issue is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Board approval alone is always sufficient for any transaction with a director.","B":"A substantial property transaction involving a director generally requires member approval under the Companies Act before the arrangement is entered into, subject to the statutory provisions.","C":"Transactions between a company and director are automatically void in every case.","D":"Member approval is relevant only to loans to directors.","E":"The regime applies only if the director owns more than 50% of the company's shares."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Sections 190–196 regulate substantial non-cash asset transactions involving directors and connected persons. Where the statutory thresholds and conditions are met, member approval is ordinarily required.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Board approval alone is always sufficient for any transaction with a director” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A substantial non-cash asset transaction with a director can require prior member approval.","B":"Correct. Sections 190–196 regulate substantial non-cash asset transactions involving directors and connected persons. Where the statutory thresholds and conditions are met, member approval is ordinarily required.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Transactions between a company and director are automatically void in every case” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A substantial non-cash asset transaction with a director can require prior member approval.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Member approval is relevant only to loans to directors” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A substantial non-cash asset transaction with a director can require prior member approval.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The regime applies only if the director owns more than 50% of the company's shares” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A substantial non-cash asset transaction with a director can require prior member approval."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 190–196.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A substantial non-cash asset transaction with a director can require prior member approval.","common_trap":"Treating ordinary board authority as sufficient despite a statutory member-approval regime.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"34537815cdc174f9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0553","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Business taxation — wholly and exclusively","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A sole trader claims a deduction for an expense incurred partly for the trade and partly to meet a private personal purpose. The expenditure cannot sensibly be apportioned into distinct business and private elements.\n\nWhich is the best general income-tax analysis?","options":{"A":"Any expense with some business benefit is fully deductible.","B":"Private expenditure is deductible whenever paid from the business bank account.","C":"The expense is not deductible if it was not incurred wholly and exclusively for the purposes of the trade, subject to the statutory rules and any identifiable apportionable element.","D":"The wholly-and-exclusively test applies only to companies.","E":"All mixed-purpose expenditure is automatically deductible at 50%."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Trading deductions are generally denied for expenses not incurred wholly and exclusively for the purposes of the trade. Where an expense has separable business and private components, the business component may sometimes be deductible; an indivisible dual-purpose expense is not.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Any expense with some business benefit is fully deductible” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Trading expenditure must satisfy the statutory wholly-and-exclusively requirement, subject to the rules on identifiable business elements.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Private expenditure is deductible whenever paid from the business bank account” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Trading expenditure must satisfy the statutory wholly-and-exclusively requirement, subject to the rules on identifiable business elements.","C":"Correct. Trading deductions are generally denied for expenses not incurred wholly and exclusively for the purposes of the trade. Where an expense has separable business and private components, the business component may sometimes be deductible; an indivisible dual-purpose expense is not.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The wholly-and-exclusively test applies only to companies” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Trading expenditure must satisfy the statutory wholly-and-exclusively requirement, subject to the rules on identifiable business elements.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition that “All mixed-purpose expenditure is automatically deductible at 50%” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Trading expenditure must satisfy the statutory wholly-and-exclusively requirement, subject to the rules on identifiable business elements."},"primary_legal_authority":"Income Tax (Trading and Other Income) Act 2005, s 34.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Trading expenditure must satisfy the statutory wholly-and-exclusively requirement, subject to the rules on identifiable business elements.","common_trap":"Equating a business connection with satisfaction of the stricter statutory purpose test.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6ff005df194b52c3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0554","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — transaction at undervalue","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An insolvent company transfers an asset worth £300,000 to an unconnected person for £20,000 shortly before liquidation. The transaction falls within the relevant statutory period and no applicable defence is established.\n\nWhich avoidance provision is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"The preference regime solely because a transfer occurred before liquidation.","B":"Wrongful trading, because every undervalue is necessarily a director's personal trading decision.","C":"The statutory pre-emption regime for shares.","D":"The transaction-at-an-undervalue regime, because the company received consideration significantly less than the value it provided.","E":"The transaction is immune because some consideration was paid."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 238 applies where a company makes a gift or enters a transaction for consideration whose value in money or money's worth is significantly less than the value provided by the company, subject to the statutory conditions and defence.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The preference regime solely because a transfer occurred before liquidation” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A grossly unequal exchange can constitute a transaction at an undervalue even though some consideration was received.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Wrongful trading, because every undervalue is necessarily a director's personal trading decision” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A grossly unequal exchange can constitute a transaction at an undervalue even though some consideration was received.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The statutory pre-emption regime for shares” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A grossly unequal exchange can constitute a transaction at an undervalue even though some consideration was received.","D":"Correct. Section 238 applies where a company makes a gift or enters a transaction for consideration whose value in money or money's worth is significantly less than the value provided by the company, subject to the statutory conditions and defence.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The transaction is immune because some consideration was paid” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A grossly unequal exchange can constitute a transaction at an undervalue even though some consideration was received."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, ss 238 and 240.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A grossly unequal exchange can constitute a transaction at an undervalue even though some consideration was received.","common_trap":"Assuming an undervalue requires a complete gift or confusing it with a preference, which focuses on improving a creditor's insolvency position.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"cb2ccf01f67e4619"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0555","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Dispute Resolution — default judgment: setting aside mandatory","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant obtains default judgment even though, before judgment was entered, the defendant had filed a defence within the permitted time.\n\nWhich setting-aside rule is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"The court must set aside a default judgment that was wrongly entered because the conditions for obtaining it were not satisfied.","B":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","C":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","D":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"CPR 13 distinguishes mandatory setting aside of wrongly entered judgment from the discretionary regime for regularly entered default judgment.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. CPR 13 distinguishes mandatory setting aside of wrongly entered judgment from the discretionary regime for regularly entered default judgment.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"CPR rr 12.3 and 13.2","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"CPR 13 distinguishes mandatory setting aside of wrongly entered judgment from the discretionary regime for regularly entered default judgment.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e492ab4876032516"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0556","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Interim remedies — freezing injunction","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant has a strong substantive claim and credible evidence that the defendant is rapidly moving assets offshore with a real risk that any eventual judgment will go unsatisfied.\n\nWhich interim remedy is most directly designed to address that risk?","options":{"A":"A freezing injunction restraining dealings with assets so as to prevent unjustified dissipation pending judgment.","B":"A search order solely to obtain disclosure of documents.","C":"Default judgment before the defence deadline.","D":"A declaration of incompatibility.","E":"A charging order before any judgment exists as an automatic right."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A freezing injunction is an exceptional interim remedy designed to prevent a defendant from dissipating assets so as to frustrate enforcement. The applicant must satisfy the relevant requirements and full-and-frank duties for without-notice relief.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A freezing injunction is an exceptional interim remedy designed to prevent a defendant from dissipating assets so as to frustrate enforcement. The applicant must satisfy the relevant requirements and full-and-frank duties for without-notice relief.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition that “A search order solely to obtain disclosure of documents” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A freezing injunction preserves assets against a real risk of unjustified dissipation; it does not give the claimant proprietary security over them.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Default judgment before the defence deadline” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A freezing injunction preserves assets against a real risk of unjustified dissipation; it does not give the claimant proprietary security over them.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition that “A declaration of incompatibility” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A freezing injunction preserves assets against a real risk of unjustified dissipation; it does not give the claimant proprietary security over them.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition that “A charging order before any judgment exists as an automatic right” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A freezing injunction preserves assets against a real risk of unjustified dissipation; it does not give the claimant proprietary security over them."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 25; Mareva Compania Naviera SA v International Bulkcarriers SA; freezing-injunction principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A freezing injunction preserves assets against a real risk of unjustified dissipation; it does not give the claimant proprietary security over them.","common_trap":"Treating the remedy as ordinary pre-judgment security or confusing asset preservation with evidence-preservation orders.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c51d9f0873f551d9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0557","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Costs — general rule and discretion","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"After trial, the claimant succeeds on most issues but pursued one costly allegation unsuccessfully. The judge is deciding costs.\n\nWhich is the best statement of the court's approach?","options":{"A":"The successful party must always recover 100% of costs regardless of conduct or issues.","B":"Although the general rule is that the unsuccessful party pays the successful party's costs, the court has a broad discretion and may make an issue-based or other order after considering all the circumstances.","C":"Each party must always bear its own costs.","D":"Costs are determined solely by which party issued proceedings first.","E":"The court cannot consider offers, conduct or partial success."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"CPR 44.2 establishes the general rule but gives the court discretion over whether costs are payable, the amount and timing, taking into account matters including conduct, partial success and admissible offers.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The successful party must always recover 100% of costs regardless of conduct or issues” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Costs generally follow the event, but the court retains a structured discretion capable of reflecting issue-by-issue success and conduct.","B":"Correct. CPR 44.2 establishes the general rule but gives the court discretion over whether costs are payable, the amount and timing, taking into account matters including conduct, partial success and admissible offers.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Each party must always bear its own costs” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Costs generally follow the event, but the court retains a structured discretion capable of reflecting issue-by-issue success and conduct.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Costs are determined solely by which party issued proceedings first” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Costs generally follow the event, but the court retains a structured discretion capable of reflecting issue-by-issue success and conduct.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The court cannot consider offers, conduct or partial success” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Costs generally follow the event, but the court retains a structured discretion capable of reflecting issue-by-issue success and conduct."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 44.2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Costs generally follow the event, but the court retains a structured discretion capable of reflecting issue-by-issue success and conduct.","common_trap":"Treating the general rule as an inflexible entitlement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a97a562ca3d29d85"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0558","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Professional conduct — undertakings","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor gives a clear professional undertaking to another firm to redeem a charge from completion monies. After completion, the client tells the solicitor not to comply because of a separate dispute with the lender.\n\nWhich is the best professional-conduct analysis?","options":{"A":"A client can unilaterally cancel any professional undertaking given by the solicitor.","B":"Undertakings are merely informal statements with no regulatory significance.","C":"The solicitor must perform the undertaking within the agreed time or, if none is specified, within a reasonable time, unless properly released from it.","D":"A solicitor may ignore an undertaking whenever compliance becomes inconvenient.","E":"Undertakings bind only barristers."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The SRA Code requires solicitors to perform undertakings they give within an agreed timescale or otherwise within a reasonable time. Client instructions do not by themselves release the solicitor from a professional undertaking.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition that “A client can unilaterally cancel any professional undertaking given by the solicitor” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A professional undertaking must be honoured unless the solicitor is properly released or another lawful basis affects performance.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Undertakings are merely informal statements with no regulatory significance” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A professional undertaking must be honoured unless the solicitor is properly released or another lawful basis affects performance.","C":"Correct. The SRA Code requires solicitors to perform undertakings they give within an agreed timescale or otherwise within a reasonable time. Client instructions do not by themselves release the solicitor from a professional undertaking.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition that “A solicitor may ignore an undertaking whenever compliance becomes inconvenient” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A professional undertaking must be honoured unless the solicitor is properly released or another lawful basis affects performance.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Undertakings bind only barristers” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A professional undertaking must be honoured unless the solicitor is properly released or another lawful basis affects performance."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 1.3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A professional undertaking must be honoured unless the solicitor is properly released or another lawful basis affects performance.","common_trap":"Assuming the client's later preference overrides an undertaking on which another party is entitled to rely.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"99f1225838f8022e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0559","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Professional conduct — costs information","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor accepts a new litigation matter but gives the client no information about likely overall costs, billing arrangements or how costs may change, even though meaningful information can be provided.\n\nWhich professional obligation is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"There is no duty to discuss costs until the matter ends.","B":"Costs information is required only for legally aided clients.","C":"A solicitor must guarantee the exact final cost in every matter.","D":"The obligation to ensure clients receive the best possible information about how their matter will be priced and, both at engagement and as it progresses, about likely overall cost.","E":"The duty applies only if the client asks for a written estimate."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The SRA Code requires solicitors to ensure clients receive the best possible information about pricing and likely overall cost at engagement and as the matter progresses. This is not an absolute requirement to predict an exact final figure.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition that “There is no duty to discuss costs until the matter ends” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Clients should receive meaningful, timely information about pricing and likely overall costs.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Costs information is required only for legally aided clients” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Clients should receive meaningful, timely information about pricing and likely overall costs.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition that “A solicitor must guarantee the exact final cost in every matter” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Clients should receive meaningful, timely information about pricing and likely overall costs.","D":"Correct. The SRA Code requires solicitors to ensure clients receive the best possible information about pricing and likely overall cost at engagement and as the matter progresses. This is not an absolute requirement to predict an exact final figure.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The duty applies only if the client asks for a written estimate” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Clients should receive meaningful, timely information about pricing and likely overall costs."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 8.7.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Clients should receive meaningful, timely information about pricing and likely overall costs.","common_trap":"Confusing the absence of certainty about final cost with permission to give no costs information at all.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6b3a46a82150035e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0560","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Terms — innominate terms: anticipatory assessment","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A party announces before performance that it will commit a breach of an innominate term. The innocent party wants immediately to treat the contract as terminated.\n\nWhat must be assessed before treating the threatened breach as repudiatory?","options":{"A":"Whether the threatened breach, judged by its prospective consequences, would deprive the innocent party of substantially the whole benefit of the contract or otherwise amount to repudiatory breach.","B":"Every threatened breach of an innominate term is automatically repudiatory.","C":"An innominate term can never be repudiated before the date for performance.","D":"The innocent party may terminate solely because the term is written in the contract.","E":"The classification means only nominal damages can ever be awarded."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"For an innominate term, repudiatory character depends on the seriousness of the consequences. In an anticipatory setting, that assessment is made prospectively by reference to the threatened non-performance.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. For an innominate term, repudiatory character depends on the seriousness of the consequences. In an anticipatory setting, that assessment is made prospectively by reference to the threatened non-performance.","B":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. The innominate-term analysis remains consequence-based even when breach is anticipatory.","C":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. The innominate-term analysis remains consequence-based even when breach is anticipatory.","D":"Incorrect. This gives the wrong legal consequence for the facts and does not satisfy the governing test. The innominate-term analysis remains consequence-based even when breach is anticipatory.","E":"Incorrect. This incorrectly makes a non-exclusive factor the sole legal test. The innominate-term analysis remains consequence-based even when breach is anticipatory."},"primary_legal_authority":"Hongkong Fir Shipping Co Ltd v Kawasaki Kisen Kaisha Ltd [1962] 2 QB 26; anticipatory breach principles","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The innominate-term analysis remains consequence-based even when breach is anticipatory.","common_trap":"Applying the headline doctrine without identifying the distinct consequence, exception or procedural stage tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"bb105be40eec7438"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0561","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Duress — causation and illegitimate pressure","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A supplier threatens unlawfully to breach a critical contract unless the customer agrees immediately to pay substantially more. The customer has no practical alternative and the threat materially causes the variation.\n\nWhich doctrine is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Economic duress, requiring illegitimate pressure that causes the claimant to enter the transaction and leaves no reasonable practical alternative in the relevant sense.","B":"Undue influence can arise only because the parties are businesses.","C":"Frustration automatically discharges the original contract.","D":"A contractual variation procured by threats is always valid if written.","E":"Promissory estoppel gives the supplier a cause of action for the extra payment."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Economic duress can render a transaction voidable where illegitimate pressure is a significant cause of assent and the circumstances show the victim lacked a reasonable practical alternative, assessed under the authorities.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Economic duress can render a transaction voidable where illegitimate pressure is a significant cause of assent and the circumstances show the victim lacked a reasonable practical alternative, assessed under the authorities.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Undue influence can arise only because the parties are businesses” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A contract variation procured by causative illegitimate commercial pressure may be voidable for economic duress.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Frustration automatically discharges the original contract” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A contract variation procured by causative illegitimate commercial pressure may be voidable for economic duress.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition that “A contractual variation procured by threats is always valid if written” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A contract variation procured by causative illegitimate commercial pressure may be voidable for economic duress.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Promissory estoppel gives the supplier a cause of action for the extra payment” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A contract variation procured by causative illegitimate commercial pressure may be voidable for economic duress."},"primary_legal_authority":"Universe Tankships Inc of Monrovia v ITWF (The Universe Sentinel); DSND Subsea Ltd v Petroleum Geo Services ASA.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A contract variation procured by causative illegitimate commercial pressure may be voidable for economic duress.","common_trap":"Treating hard bargaining as necessarily duress or, conversely, assuming written assent cures coercion.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ab008957b9f070d4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0562","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Damages — mitigation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"After a supplier's breach, the buyer could obtain a reasonable substitute immediately at a modest additional cost but refuses for personal reasons. Months later, the market price has risen dramatically and the buyer claims the entire increased loss.\n\nWhich damages principle is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"The claimant must take every imaginable step regardless of cost or risk.","B":"The claimant cannot ordinarily recover loss that could reasonably have been avoided by taking reasonable mitigating steps after breach.","C":"Failure to mitigate eliminates all damages, including unavoidable loss.","D":"Mitigation is a duty owed to the defendant giving rise to a separate damages claim.","E":"A claimant is always entitled to wait for the market to worsen."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The mitigation principle limits recovery for avoidable post-breach loss. The claimant need only act reasonably and does not owe an actionable duty to the defendant, but unreasonable failure to mitigate can reduce recoverable damages.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The claimant must take every imaginable step regardless of cost or risk” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Damages exclude loss that the claimant could reasonably have avoided after breach.","B":"Correct. The mitigation principle limits recovery for avoidable post-breach loss. The claimant need only act reasonably and does not owe an actionable duty to the defendant, but unreasonable failure to mitigate can reduce recoverable damages.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Failure to mitigate eliminates all damages, including unavoidable loss” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Damages exclude loss that the claimant could reasonably have avoided after breach.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Mitigation is a duty owed to the defendant giving rise to a separate damages claim” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Damages exclude loss that the claimant could reasonably have avoided after breach.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition that “A claimant is always entitled to wait for the market to worsen” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Damages exclude loss that the claimant could reasonably have avoided after breach."},"primary_legal_authority":"British Westinghouse Electric and Manufacturing Co Ltd v Underground Electric Railways Co of London Ltd.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Damages exclude loss that the claimant could reasonably have avoided after breach.","common_trap":"Treating mitigation as either an onerous independent duty or as irrelevant to the recoverable measure.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5a462853e21a78ed"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0563","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Professional negligence — standard of care","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor handling an ordinary conveyance adopts a course no reasonably competent conveyancing solicitor would have taken at the time, causing foreseeable loss. The solicitor argues that personal inexperience should lower the standard.\n\nWhich is the best general analysis?","options":{"A":"A newly practising professional is judged only by their own subjective experience.","B":"Professional defendants are liable only for deliberate wrongdoing.","C":"The solicitor is judged by the objective standard of the reasonably competent practitioner undertaking that work; personal inexperience does not lower the required standard.","D":"Any error by a professional automatically proves negligence.","E":"The standard is whatever the individual client expected, however unreasonable."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Professional negligence applies an objective standard of reasonable competence appropriate to the work undertaken. A practitioner's personal lack of experience does not ordinarily reduce that standard, although negligence is not established merely by showing an error.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition that “A newly practising professional is judged only by their own subjective experience” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A professional undertaking work must meet the objective standard of reasonable competence for that work.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Professional defendants are liable only for deliberate wrongdoing” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A professional undertaking work must meet the objective standard of reasonable competence for that work.","C":"Correct. Professional negligence applies an objective standard of reasonable competence appropriate to the work undertaken. A practitioner's personal lack of experience does not ordinarily reduce that standard, although negligence is not established merely by showing an error.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Any error by a professional automatically proves negligence” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A professional undertaking work must meet the objective standard of reasonable competence for that work.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The standard is whatever the individual client expected, however unreasonable” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A professional undertaking work must meet the objective standard of reasonable competence for that work."},"primary_legal_authority":"Bolam v Friern Hospital Management Committee; professional-negligence principles; Midland Bank Trust Co Ltd v Hett Stubbs & Kemp.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A professional undertaking work must meet the objective standard of reasonable competence for that work.","common_trap":"Lowering the standard to match the defendant's individual experience or treating any adverse outcome as negligence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e7bab4cf09bda8da"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0564","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Causation — intervening acts","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant negligently injures a claimant. Before the claimant's condition is resolved, an independent third party deliberately commits an extraordinary act that causes a new and distinct injury. The court considers whether the defendant remains legally responsible for that later harm.\n\nWhich causation concept is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Res ipsa loquitur.","B":"Volenti non fit injuria.","C":"The rule in Rylands v Fletcher.","D":"Novus actus interveniens: whether the later event breaks the chain of legal causation for the subsequent harm.","E":"Contributory negligence necessarily."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Even where factual causation exists, a sufficiently independent and potent intervening act can break the chain of legal causation for later damage. Whether it does so depends on the nature and foreseeability of the intervention and the scope of the original risk.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Res ipsa loquitur” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A sufficiently independent intervening event may break legal causation for subsequent damage.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Volenti non fit injuria” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A sufficiently independent intervening event may break legal causation for subsequent damage.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The rule in Rylands v Fletcher” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A sufficiently independent intervening event may break legal causation for subsequent damage.","D":"Correct. Even where factual causation exists, a sufficiently independent and potent intervening act can break the chain of legal causation for later damage. Whether it does so depends on the nature and foreseeability of the intervention and the scope of the original risk.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Contributory negligence necessarily” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A sufficiently independent intervening event may break legal causation for subsequent damage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Knightley v Johns; Corr v IBC Vehicles Ltd; intervening-act principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A sufficiently independent intervening event may break legal causation for subsequent damage.","common_trap":"Assuming the original defendant remains liable for every later event merely because the first injury created the occasion.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"116b812ed57bb947"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0565","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Private nuisance — locality","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A resident complains that recurring noise from neighbouring land substantially interferes with use and enjoyment of the home. The defendant argues that the same noise might be acceptable in a heavily industrial district.\n\nWhich factor is relevant to whether the interference is unreasonable?","options":{"A":"Locality is never relevant to private nuisance.","B":"Any audible noise is automatically actionable.","C":"Planning permission always provides a complete defence to nuisance.","D":"Only physical damage to land can constitute private nuisance.","E":"The character of the locality can be relevant to the standard of reasonable user, although it does not make every activity permissible."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Private nuisance balances competing land uses. The character of the locality can influence whether amenity interference is unreasonable, although physical damage and other categories may be treated differently and planning permission does not itself authorise nuisance.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Locality is never relevant to private nuisance” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Locality is relevant when assessing the reasonableness of amenity interference in private nuisance.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Any audible noise is automatically actionable” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Locality is relevant when assessing the reasonableness of amenity interference in private nuisance.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Planning permission always provides a complete defence to nuisance” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Locality is relevant when assessing the reasonableness of amenity interference in private nuisance.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Only physical damage to land can constitute private nuisance” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Locality is relevant when assessing the reasonableness of amenity interference in private nuisance.","E":"Correct. Private nuisance balances competing land uses. The character of the locality can influence whether amenity interference is unreasonable, although physical damage and other categories may be treated differently and planning permission does not itself authorise nuisance."},"primary_legal_authority":"Sturges v Bridgman; Coventry v Lawrence [2014] UKSC 13.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Locality is relevant when assessing the reasonableness of amenity interference in private nuisance.","common_trap":"Treating nuisance as strict liability for any interference or assuming planning status automatically resolves private-law liability.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"cbfcf0e832f15c06"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0566","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Wills — lapse and substitutionary gift","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A will leaves a gift to B, but provides expressly that if B dies before the testator the gift is to pass to C. B predeceases the testator.\n\nWhat is the best general result?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The express substitutionary provision ordinarily directs the gift to C, so the usual lapse consequence is displaced by the will's wording.","C":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","D":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Lapse is a default consequence and can be displaced by an effective substitutionary gift.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Correct. Lapse is a default consequence and can be displaced by an effective substitutionary gift.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"General law of lapse and construction of wills","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Lapse is a default consequence and can be displaced by an effective substitutionary gift.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"dc1d5960da4c078c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0567","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — improper purpose","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A statute gives a minister a power to acquire land for constructing public hospitals. The minister uses the power solely to punish a landowner who publicly criticised the government, with no genuine hospital purpose.\n\nWhich ground of review is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Procedural legitimate expectation only.","B":"Illegality through use of a statutory power for an improper purpose.","C":"The decision is immune because the minister has a statutory power to acquire land.","D":"Private nuisance.","E":"Parliamentary privilege."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A statutory discretion must be exercised to promote the purposes for which Parliament conferred it. Using a power for an extraneous punitive purpose can amount to illegality and abuse of power.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Procedural legitimate expectation only” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Public powers must be used for their statutory purposes rather than collateral or improper objectives.","B":"Correct. A statutory discretion must be exercised to promote the purposes for which Parliament conferred it. Using a power for an extraneous punitive purpose can amount to illegality and abuse of power.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The decision is immune because the minister has a statutory power to acquire land” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Public powers must be used for their statutory purposes rather than collateral or improper objectives.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Private nuisance” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Public powers must be used for their statutory purposes rather than collateral or improper objectives.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Parliamentary privilege” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Public powers must be used for their statutory purposes rather than collateral or improper objectives."},"primary_legal_authority":"Padfield v Minister of Agriculture, Fisheries and Food; Congreve v Home Office; public-law improper-purpose principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Public powers must be used for their statutory purposes rather than collateral or improper objectives.","common_trap":"Assuming the existence of a broad statutory discretion permits its use for any motive whatsoever.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fe622925b36b97ab"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0568","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Statutory interpretation — presumption against retrospectivity","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Parliament enacts legislation using language that does not clearly indicate retrospective operation. Applying it retrospectively would alter substantive legal consequences of transactions completed before commencement.\n\nWhich interpretive principle is relevant?","options":{"A":"All legislation automatically applies retrospectively.","B":"Courts may never apply legislation to any facts beginning before commencement.","C":"There is a general presumption against retrospective alteration of substantive rights unless Parliament has indicated the contrary with sufficient clarity.","D":"The presumption allows courts to disregard clear statutory words requiring retrospectivity.","E":"Retrospectivity is relevant only to delegated legislation."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Courts generally presume that legislation does not retrospectively alter substantive rights or liabilities unless the statutory language or necessary implication indicates otherwise. The presumption yields to sufficiently clear legislative intention.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition that “All legislation automatically applies retrospectively” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Absent sufficient contrary indication, statutes are generally construed against retrospective alteration of substantive legal consequences.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Courts may never apply legislation to any facts beginning before commencement” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Absent sufficient contrary indication, statutes are generally construed against retrospective alteration of substantive legal consequences.","C":"Correct. Courts generally presume that legislation does not retrospectively alter substantive rights or liabilities unless the statutory language or necessary implication indicates otherwise. The presumption yields to sufficiently clear legislative intention.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The presumption allows courts to disregard clear statutory words requiring retrospectivity” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Absent sufficient contrary indication, statutes are generally construed against retrospective alteration of substantive legal consequences.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Retrospectivity is relevant only to delegated legislation” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Absent sufficient contrary indication, statutes are generally construed against retrospective alteration of substantive legal consequences."},"primary_legal_authority":"Yew Bon Tew v Kenderaan Bas Mara; R v Secretary of State for Social Security, ex p Britnell; statutory-interpretation principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Absent sufficient contrary indication, statutes are generally construed against retrospective alteration of substantive legal consequences.","common_trap":"Treating the presumption as absolute or, at the other extreme, assuming every new statute rewrites completed legal events.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f1ce8da77a857577"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0569","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — ademption","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A will gives 'my 1,000 shares in X plc to B'. Before death, the testator sells all X plc shares and uses the proceeds for unrelated purposes. At death the estate contains no X plc shares.\n\nWhat doctrine is most directly relevant to the gift?","options":{"A":"Abatement, because every sold asset must be replaced from residue.","B":"Anti-lapse, because the property rather than the beneficiary disappeared.","C":"The executors must ordinarily buy replacement shares for B.","D":"Ademption: a specific gift ordinarily fails if the specifically identified property is no longer part of the estate at death, subject to any applicable exception.","E":"The gift automatically converts into a general pecuniary legacy equal to the sale proceeds."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Ademption concerns specific gifts where the subject matter is no longer in the testator's estate at death. Ordinarily the specific beneficiary receives nothing in place of the missing asset unless an exception or proper construction produces another result.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Abatement, because every sold asset must be replaced from residue” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A specific legacy generally adeems when its identified subject matter has ceased to belong to the testator before death.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Anti-lapse, because the property rather than the beneficiary disappeared” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A specific legacy generally adeems when its identified subject matter has ceased to belong to the testator before death.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The executors must ordinarily buy replacement shares for B” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A specific legacy generally adeems when its identified subject matter has ceased to belong to the testator before death.","D":"Correct. Ademption concerns specific gifts where the subject matter is no longer in the testator's estate at death. Ordinarily the specific beneficiary receives nothing in place of the missing asset unless an exception or proper construction produces another result.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The gift automatically converts into a general pecuniary legacy equal to the sale proceeds” does not state the governing rule for these facts. A specific legacy generally adeems when its identified subject matter has ceased to belong to the testator before death."},"primary_legal_authority":"Ademption principles; Re Slater [1907] 1 Ch 665.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A specific legacy generally adeems when its identified subject matter has ceased to belong to the testator before death.","common_trap":"Confusing disappearance of a specific asset with abatement for insufficiency of estate assets generally.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6694bcd23340e2f3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0570","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Criminal procedure — confession evidence: oppression","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A suspect makes a confession after questioning involving oppressive treatment. The prosecution seeks to rely on it at trial.\n\nWhich statutory rule is central?","options":{"A":"PACE requires exclusion where the confession was obtained by oppression, or in consequence of things said or done likely to render it unreliable, unless the prosecution proves otherwise to the statutory standard.","B":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","C":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","D":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Confession admissibility is specifically controlled by PACE s 76.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Confession admissibility is specifically controlled by PACE s 76.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Police and Criminal Evidence Act 1984, s 76","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Confession admissibility is specifically controlled by PACE s 76.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"09606b19f29a1e0c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0571","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Inheritance tax — potentially exempt transfer","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An individual makes an outright lifetime gift of cash to an adult child. The gift is not to a trust, no reservation of benefit exists and no exemption fully covers it.\n\nHow is the transfer ordinarily characterised for inheritance-tax purposes at the time it is made?","options":{"A":"It is ordinarily a potentially exempt transfer, becoming exempt if the donor survives the relevant seven-year period, subject to the statutory rules.","B":"It is always immediately chargeable at the lifetime trust rate.","C":"It is never relevant to inheritance tax.","D":"It is a gift with reservation merely because the donee is the donor's child.","E":"It is automatically exempt regardless of survival."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An outright lifetime transfer by an individual to another individual is generally a potentially exempt transfer under section 3A. If the donor survives seven years it becomes exempt; death within that period can bring the transfer into the death calculation, subject to exemptions and reliefs.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. An outright lifetime transfer by an individual to another individual is generally a potentially exempt transfer under section 3A. If the donor survives seven years it becomes exempt; death within that period can bring the transfer into the death calculation, subject to exemptions and reliefs.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition that “It is always immediately chargeable at the lifetime trust rate” does not state the governing rule for these facts. An outright gift between individuals is generally a PET rather than an immediately chargeable lifetime transfer.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition that “It is never relevant to inheritance tax” does not state the governing rule for these facts. An outright gift between individuals is generally a PET rather than an immediately chargeable lifetime transfer.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition that “It is a gift with reservation merely because the donee is the donor's child” does not state the governing rule for these facts. An outright gift between individuals is generally a PET rather than an immediately chargeable lifetime transfer.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition that “It is automatically exempt regardless of survival” does not state the governing rule for these facts. An outright gift between individuals is generally a PET rather than an immediately chargeable lifetime transfer."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984, s 3A.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An outright gift between individuals is generally a PET rather than an immediately chargeable lifetime transfer.","common_trap":"Confusing ordinary individual gifts with chargeable lifetime transfers into relevant-property trusts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"379abf3281c9f2d1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0572","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Trusts — trustee remuneration","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A professional trustee seeks payment for acting as trustee, but the trust instrument is silent and no beneficiary agreement has been obtained.\n\nWhich issue should be analysed?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","C":"A trustee has no unrestricted common-law right to remuneration merely for acting; any entitlement must arise under the trust instrument, statute, court authority or another recognised basis.","D":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Trustee remuneration requires a legal basis rather than arising automatically from professional status.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Correct. Trustee remuneration requires a legal basis rather than arising automatically from professional status.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 2000, s 29 and general equitable principles","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Trustee remuneration requires a legal basis rather than arising automatically from professional status.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7b0912cf0d67deb9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0573","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Land — co-ownership: severance by notice","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"One equitable joint tenant serves on the other joint tenant a written notice showing an immediate intention to sever the beneficial joint tenancy. The notice is validly served.\n\nWhat is the ordinary effect?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","C":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","D":"The beneficial joint tenancy is severed as to that share, which is thereafter held as a tenancy in common in equity.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A valid written notice under s 36(2) is a unilateral statutory method of severance.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Correct. A valid written notice under s 36(2) is a unilateral statutory method of severance.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, s 36(2)","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A valid written notice under s 36(2) is a unilateral statutory method of severance.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"57c0e19b67841a45"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0574","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Beneficiaries — Saunders v Vautier","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trust fund is held for one adult beneficiary absolutely. The beneficiary has full capacity and is solely beneficially entitled to the entire trust property, but the trust instrument says the fund should not be transferred until age 30. The beneficiary is 25.\n\nWhich principle is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Trustees must always retain the fund until the exact age stated, regardless of absolute beneficial entitlement.","B":"A beneficiary can terminate a trust only with the settlor's consent.","C":"The rule applies only to charitable trusts.","D":"Under Saunders v Vautier, an adult beneficiary with full capacity who is absolutely entitled may ordinarily require the trustees to transfer the trust property and terminate the trust early.","E":"The beneficiary must wait until every trustee agrees that early termination is desirable."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Saunders v Vautier principle permits beneficiaries who are adult, of full capacity and together absolutely entitled to the entire beneficial interest to call for the trust property, notwithstanding a postponed distribution direction.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Trustees must always retain the fund until the exact age stated, regardless of absolute beneficial entitlement” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Absolute beneficial owners of full age and capacity can ordinarily collapse the trust and demand the property.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition that “A beneficiary can terminate a trust only with the settlor's consent” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Absolute beneficial owners of full age and capacity can ordinarily collapse the trust and demand the property.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The rule applies only to charitable trusts” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Absolute beneficial owners of full age and capacity can ordinarily collapse the trust and demand the property.","D":"Correct. The Saunders v Vautier principle permits beneficiaries who are adult, of full capacity and together absolutely entitled to the entire beneficial interest to call for the trust property, notwithstanding a postponed distribution direction.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The beneficiary must wait until every trustee agrees that early termination is desirable” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Absolute beneficial owners of full age and capacity can ordinarily collapse the trust and demand the property."},"primary_legal_authority":"Saunders v Vautier (1841) 4 Beav 115.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Absolute beneficial owners of full age and capacity can ordinarily collapse the trust and demand the property.","common_trap":"Treating administrative postponement as overriding the rights of persons collectively entitled to the whole beneficial interest.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"586760738e115c89"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0575","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Co-ownership — overreaching: proceeds after overreaching","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A property is held by two trustees for themselves and another beneficiary. On a sale, the buyer pays the purchase money to both trustees, so the beneficiary's equitable interest is overreached.\n\nAfter completion, where does the beneficiary's equitable interest ordinarily attach?","options":{"A":"It attaches to the capital money/proceeds held on the trust rather than continuing to bind the land sold.","B":"It is automatically extinguished without replacement.","C":"It continues to bind the buyer's land despite valid overreaching.","D":"It becomes a legal easement over the land.","E":"It converts automatically into a debt owed personally by the buyer."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Valid overreaching shifts the equitable interest from the land to the capital money, allowing the purchaser to take the land free of that interest while preserving the beneficiary's interest in the proceeds.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Valid overreaching shifts the equitable interest from the land to the capital money, allowing the purchaser to take the land free of that interest while preserving the beneficiary's interest in the proceeds.","B":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. Overreaching changes the asset to which the equitable interest attaches; it does not simply destroy the beneficiary's interest.","C":"Incorrect. This gives the wrong legal consequence for the facts and does not satisfy the governing test. Overreaching changes the asset to which the equitable interest attaches; it does not simply destroy the beneficiary's interest.","D":"Incorrect. This gives the wrong legal consequence for the facts and does not satisfy the governing test. Overreaching changes the asset to which the equitable interest attaches; it does not simply destroy the beneficiary's interest.","E":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. Overreaching changes the asset to which the equitable interest attaches; it does not simply destroy the beneficiary's interest."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, ss 2 and 27","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Overreaching changes the asset to which the equitable interest attaches; it does not simply destroy the beneficiary's interest.","common_trap":"Applying the headline doctrine without identifying the distinct consequence, exception or procedural stage tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ed35ab7a2ec31219"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0576","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Adverse possession — registered land: second application","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A registered-land adverse possessor's first application is rejected after the proprietor objects. The proprietor then takes no effective steps to remove the squatter, who remains in adverse possession for a further two years.\n\nWhy can the further two-year period matter?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The statutory scheme may permit a further application after two years of continued adverse possession if the registered proprietor has failed to recover possession, subject to the Schedule 6 conditions.","C":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","D":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The LRA 2002 gives the registered proprietor an opportunity to act after a rejected first application, but continued inaction can support a later application.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Correct. The LRA 2002 gives the registered proprietor an opportunity to act after a rejected first application, but continued inaction can support a later application.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002, Sch 6, para 6","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The LRA 2002 gives the registered proprietor an opportunity to act after a rejected first application, but continued inaction can support a later application.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"34333c06571fcd7c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0577","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Property — local search: road adoption","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer's local authority search indicates that the road serving the property is not maintainable at public expense.\n\nWhat practical legal issue should the buyer's solicitor investigate next?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","C":"The solicitor should investigate private rights of access and responsibility for road maintenance and costs rather than assuming the local authority will maintain the road.","D":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Search results must be acted upon; an unadopted road can create access and maintenance issues requiring further enquiries.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Correct. Search results must be acted upon; an unadopted road can create access and maintenance issues requiring further enquiries.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Conveyancing practice; Local Land Charges/CON29 enquiries","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Search results must be acted upon; an unadopted road can create access and maintenance issues requiring further enquiries.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"dec7a1ac442dfb98"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0578","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"SDLT — chargeable consideration","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An individual buys a freehold investment property in England for money. No relief applies. The solicitor is considering the buyer's SDLT obligations and the tax base.\n\nWhich is the best general principle?","options":{"A":"SDLT is always calculated on the seller's original acquisition cost.","B":"The seller, never the buyer, is the taxpayer on an ordinary purchase.","C":"SDLT is generally charged by reference to chargeable consideration for a land transaction, applying the rates and thresholds in force and any relevant reliefs; assessment questions provide monetary rates or thresholds where candidates are required to apply them.","D":"Every land purchase is exempt if the buyer already owns another property.","E":"SDLT is a tax on annual rental income rather than land transactions."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"SDLT applies to chargeable land transactions in England and Northern Ireland and is generally computed by reference to chargeable consideration under the statutory regime. For SQE questions requiring application of monetary rates, thresholds, exemptions or reliefs, the specification explains when figures will be supplied.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition that “SDLT is always calculated on the seller's original acquisition cost” does not state the governing rule for these facts. The buyer's SDLT liability is transaction-based and ordinarily references chargeable consideration, subject to rates, thresholds and reliefs.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The seller, never the buyer, is the taxpayer on an ordinary purchase” does not state the governing rule for these facts. The buyer's SDLT liability is transaction-based and ordinarily references chargeable consideration, subject to rates, thresholds and reliefs.","C":"Correct. SDLT applies to chargeable land transactions in England and Northern Ireland and is generally computed by reference to chargeable consideration under the statutory regime. For SQE questions requiring application of monetary rates, thresholds, exemptions or reliefs, the specification explains when figures will be supplied.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Every land purchase is exempt if the buyer already owns another property” does not state the governing rule for these facts. The buyer's SDLT liability is transaction-based and ordinarily references chargeable consideration, subject to rates, thresholds and reliefs.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition that “SDLT is a tax on annual rental income rather than land transactions” does not state the governing rule for these facts. The buyer's SDLT liability is transaction-based and ordinarily references chargeable consideration, subject to rates, thresholds and reliefs."},"primary_legal_authority":"Finance Act 2003, Parts 4 and relevant schedules; SQE1 Assessment Specification tax guidance.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The buyer's SDLT liability is transaction-based and ordinarily references chargeable consideration, subject to rates, thresholds and reliefs.","common_trap":"Confusing SDLT with capital gains or income taxation, or using the seller's historic cost as the tax base.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6bc77dbfab2bc0c3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0579","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Self-defence — reasonable force","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant honestly believes an imminent attack is about to occur and uses force to protect themself. The prosecution accepts the belief was honestly held but argues the amount of force used was unreasonable in the circumstances as the defendant believed them to be.\n\nWhich is the best general approach?","options":{"A":"Any honest belief in danger makes any amount of force lawful.","B":"Force is lawful only if the perceived attack had actually begun.","C":"The defendant's mistaken belief is irrelevant unless objectively reasonable in every respect.","D":"The court considers whether the force used was reasonable in the circumstances as the defendant honestly believed them to be, subject to the statutory and common-law rules.","E":"Self-defence applies only inside the defendant's home."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Self-defence combines a subjective assessment of the circumstances as the defendant honestly believed them with an objective assessment of whether the degree of force used was reasonable in those believed circumstances, subject to statutory qualifications.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Any honest belief in danger makes any amount of force lawful” does not state the governing rule for these facts. An honestly held belief informs the circumstances, but the force itself must satisfy the legal standard of reasonableness.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Force is lawful only if the perceived attack had actually begun” does not state the governing rule for these facts. An honestly held belief informs the circumstances, but the force itself must satisfy the legal standard of reasonableness.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The defendant's mistaken belief is irrelevant unless objectively reasonable in every respect” does not state the governing rule for these facts. An honestly held belief informs the circumstances, but the force itself must satisfy the legal standard of reasonableness.","D":"Correct. Self-defence combines a subjective assessment of the circumstances as the defendant honestly believed them with an objective assessment of whether the degree of force used was reasonable in those believed circumstances, subject to statutory qualifications.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Self-defence applies only inside the defendant's home” does not state the governing rule for these facts. An honestly held belief informs the circumstances, but the force itself must satisfy the legal standard of reasonableness."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Justice and Immigration Act 2008, s 76; common-law self-defence principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An honestly held belief informs the circumstances, but the force itself must satisfy the legal standard of reasonableness.","common_trap":"Conflating honest belief in the need for force with unlimited permission as to the amount of force.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8890d8a6396282f5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0580","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Guilty pleas — sentence reduction","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant intends to plead guilty and asks why entering the plea at the first reasonable opportunity can matter to sentence.\n\nWhich is the best general explanation?","options":{"A":"A guilty plea always guarantees acquittal.","B":"The timing of a guilty plea can never affect sentence.","C":"A guilty plea automatically reduces every sentence by exactly one half.","D":"Credit for plea is available only after a jury has been sworn.","E":"A guilty plea can justify a reduction in sentence, with the level of credit generally greatest for a plea indicated at the earliest stage and decreasing as the case progresses, subject to the applicable guideline."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The sentencing guideline on reduction for guilty pleas provides credit for the utilitarian benefit of a plea, with the maximum ordinarily associated with indication at the first stage of proceedings and reduced credit for later pleas, subject to specified rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition that “A guilty plea always guarantees acquittal” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Early indication of a guilty plea can materially increase sentence credit under the applicable guideline.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition that “The timing of a guilty plea can never affect sentence” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Early indication of a guilty plea can materially increase sentence credit under the applicable guideline.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition that “A guilty plea automatically reduces every sentence by exactly one half” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Early indication of a guilty plea can materially increase sentence credit under the applicable guideline.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition that “Credit for plea is available only after a jury has been sworn” does not state the governing rule for these facts. Early indication of a guilty plea can materially increase sentence credit under the applicable guideline.","E":"Correct. The sentencing guideline on reduction for guilty pleas provides credit for the utilitarian benefit of a plea, with the maximum ordinarily associated with indication at the first stage of proceedings and reduced credit for later pleas, subject to specified rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"Sentencing Council, Reduction in Sentence for a Guilty Plea guideline; Sentencing Act 2020.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Early indication of a guilty plea can materially increase sentence credit under the applicable guideline.","common_trap":"Treating plea credit as either irrelevant or a fixed automatic fraction regardless of timing and guideline conditions.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"cf22a4d593b9d2eb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0581","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — derivative claims","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A minority shareholder alleges that a director breached duties owed to the company, causing loss to the company. The board will not sue. The shareholder wants to pursue the company's cause of action rather than recover a personal shareholder loss.\n\nWhich procedure is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"A statutory derivative claim, subject to the permission procedure and statutory criteria.","B":"An unfair-prejudice petition is the only possible shareholder procedure.","C":"A personal breach-of-contract claim against the director for the company's loss.","D":"A winding-up petition automatically entitles the shareholder to damages.","E":"The shareholder may sue in the company's name without court permission."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Part 11 of the Companies Act 2006 permits a member to seek permission to continue a derivative claim in respect of a cause of action vested in the company arising from specified director misconduct.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Part 11 of the Companies Act 2006 permits a member to seek permission to continue a derivative claim in respect of a cause of action vested in the company arising from specified director misconduct.","B":"Incorrect. “An unfair-prejudice petition is the only possible shareholder procedure” is not the applicable rule on these facts. A derivative claim is exceptional litigation by a member on the company's cause of action and requires the statutory permission process.","C":"Incorrect. “A personal breach-of-contract claim against the director for the company's loss” is not the applicable rule on these facts. A derivative claim is exceptional litigation by a member on the company's cause of action and requires the statutory permission process.","D":"Incorrect. “A winding-up petition automatically entitles the shareholder to damages” is not the applicable rule on these facts. A derivative claim is exceptional litigation by a member on the company's cause of action and requires the statutory permission process.","E":"Incorrect. “The shareholder may sue in the company's name without court permission” is not the applicable rule on these facts. A derivative claim is exceptional litigation by a member on the company's cause of action and requires the statutory permission process."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 260–264.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A derivative claim is exceptional litigation by a member on the company's cause of action and requires the statutory permission process.","common_trap":"Confusing loss suffered by the company with a personal shareholder cause of action.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4e60070f8a720a14"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0582","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — unfair prejudice","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A majority shareholder causes a small private company to divert business to another company the majority shareholder owns and excludes the minority from management contrary to the basis on which the company was operated. The minority seeks a personal statutory remedy.\n\nWhich remedy is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"A derivative claim is mandatory for every shareholder complaint.","B":"A petition under section 994 alleging that the company's affairs are being conducted in a manner unfairly prejudicial to the minority's interests.","C":"Judicial review of the company's commercial decisions.","D":"Automatic rescission of the company's constitution.","E":"A criminal prosecution under the Companies Act as the minority's only remedy."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 994 permits a member to petition where the company's affairs are or have been conducted in a manner unfairly prejudicial to members' interests. The court has broad remedial powers under section 996.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “A derivative claim is mandatory for every shareholder complaint” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Unfair prejudice protects members against qualifying unfairly prejudicial conduct and can support remedies such as a share-purchase order.","B":"Correct. Section 994 permits a member to petition where the company's affairs are or have been conducted in a manner unfairly prejudicial to members' interests. The court has broad remedial powers under section 996.","C":"Incorrect. “Judicial review of the company's commercial decisions” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Unfair prejudice protects members against qualifying unfairly prejudicial conduct and can support remedies such as a share-purchase order.","D":"Incorrect. “Automatic rescission of the company's constitution” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Unfair prejudice protects members against qualifying unfairly prejudicial conduct and can support remedies such as a share-purchase order.","E":"Incorrect. “A criminal prosecution under the Companies Act as the minority's only remedy” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Unfair prejudice protects members against qualifying unfairly prejudicial conduct and can support remedies such as a share-purchase order."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 994 and 996.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Unfair prejudice protects members against qualifying unfairly prejudicial conduct and can support remedies such as a share-purchase order.","common_trap":"Assuming every shareholder dispute concerns only a cause of action belonging to the company.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d0f8d72f12eb035b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0583","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Partnership — liability of partners","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A partner acting in the ordinary course of the firm's business enters a contract with a supplier. The partner has authority to bind the firm. The firm later defaults.\n\nWhich is the best general statement about partners' liability for the firm's contractual debts?","options":{"A":"Only the partner who signed is ever liable.","B":"Partners have limited liability merely because the firm has a trading name.","C":"Partners are jointly liable for debts and obligations of the firm incurred while they are partners, subject to the statutory framework.","D":"The supplier can recover only from partnership assets and never from partners.","E":"Contractual debts of a partnership are automatically debts of Companies House."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Under the Partnership Act, every partner is jointly liable with the other partners for debts and obligations of the firm incurred while that person is a partner.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Only the partner who signed is ever liable” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Ordinary partnership does not confer the limited-liability structure of a company or LLP.","B":"Incorrect. “Partners have limited liability merely because the firm has a trading name” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Ordinary partnership does not confer the limited-liability structure of a company or LLP.","C":"Correct. Under the Partnership Act, every partner is jointly liable with the other partners for debts and obligations of the firm incurred while that person is a partner.","D":"Incorrect. “The supplier can recover only from partnership assets and never from partners” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Ordinary partnership does not confer the limited-liability structure of a company or LLP.","E":"Incorrect. “Contractual debts of a partnership are automatically debts of Companies House” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Ordinary partnership does not confer the limited-liability structure of a company or LLP."},"primary_legal_authority":"Partnership Act 1890, s 9.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Ordinary partnership does not confer the limited-liability structure of a company or LLP.","common_trap":"Treating an ordinary partnership as though it had separate limited liability equivalent to an LLP.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f146552d260c6d5f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0584","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — administration moratorium","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company enters administration. An unsecured creditor wants immediately to commence ordinary enforcement proceedings against the company without the administrator's consent or permission of the court.\n\nWhich feature of administration is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Administration automatically extinguishes every debt.","B":"Unsecured creditors obtain an unrestricted right to seize company assets once an administrator is appointed.","C":"The moratorium applies only to directors personally.","D":"The statutory moratorium generally restricts commencement or continuation of specified proceedings and enforcement without the required consent or court permission.","E":"Administration has no effect on creditor enforcement."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Administration carries a statutory moratorium restricting various enforcement steps and legal processes, subject to the Insolvency Act and permitted exceptions or consents.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Administration automatically extinguishes every debt” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The administration moratorium protects the collective process from unilateral creditor action.","B":"Incorrect. “Unsecured creditors obtain an unrestricted right to seize company assets once an administrator is appointed” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The administration moratorium protects the collective process from unilateral creditor action.","C":"Incorrect. “The moratorium applies only to directors personally” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The administration moratorium protects the collective process from unilateral creditor action.","D":"Correct. Administration carries a statutory moratorium restricting various enforcement steps and legal processes, subject to the Insolvency Act and permitted exceptions or consents.","E":"Incorrect. “Administration has no effect on creditor enforcement” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The administration moratorium protects the collective process from unilateral creditor action."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, Sch B1, including paras 42–43.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The administration moratorium protects the collective process from unilateral creditor action.","common_trap":"Confusing a temporary procedural restriction on enforcement with discharge of the underlying debt.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b2d3d83a42ac1492"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0585","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Statements of case — amendment after limitation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"After expiry of the relevant limitation period, a claimant seeks to add a new claim to existing proceedings. The new claim arises out of substantially the same facts as a claim already in issue.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Expiry of limitation makes every amendment legally impossible.","B":"A claimant may add any unrelated cause of action after limitation without permission.","C":"Limitation is irrelevant once any claim form has been issued.","D":"The defendant alone decides whether CPR 17 applies.","E":"The court has a limited statutory and procedural power to permit certain post-limitation amendments, including a new claim arising from substantially the same facts as an existing claim, subject to the applicable rules."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Section 35 of the Limitation Act and CPR 17.4 permit specified amendments after expiry of limitation, including certain new claims arising out of substantially the same facts already in issue.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Expiry of limitation makes every amendment legally impossible” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Post-limitation amendment is exceptional but may be permitted within defined statutory categories.","B":"Incorrect. “A claimant may add any unrelated cause of action after limitation without permission” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Post-limitation amendment is exceptional but may be permitted within defined statutory categories.","C":"Incorrect. “Limitation is irrelevant once any claim form has been issued” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Post-limitation amendment is exceptional but may be permitted within defined statutory categories.","D":"Incorrect. “The defendant alone decides whether CPR 17 applies” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Post-limitation amendment is exceptional but may be permitted within defined statutory categories.","E":"Correct. Section 35 of the Limitation Act and CPR 17.4 permit specified amendments after expiry of limitation, including certain new claims arising out of substantially the same facts already in issue."},"primary_legal_authority":"Limitation Act 1980, s 35; Civil Procedure Rules, r 17.4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Post-limitation amendment is exceptional but may be permitted within defined statutory categories.","common_trap":"Assuming either an absolute prohibition or an unrestricted right to introduce unrelated time-barred claims.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"59ab6b8cab96071a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0586","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Evidence — hearsay in civil proceedings","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"In civil proceedings, a party wants to rely at trial on a written statement made outside court as evidence of the truth of what it says. The opponent argues that hearsay is automatically inadmissible.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Hearsay is not automatically inadmissible in civil proceedings, although statutory notice, weight and other evidential rules may apply.","B":"All hearsay is absolutely inadmissible in civil cases.","C":"Hearsay is admissible only if both parties sign a consent form.","D":"A hearsay statement automatically has the same weight as live tested evidence.","E":"The Civil Evidence Act applies only to criminal trials."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Civil Evidence Act 1995 abolished the common-law rule excluding hearsay in civil proceedings. Procedural requirements and factors affecting weight remain important.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Civil Evidence Act 1995 abolished the common-law rule excluding hearsay in civil proceedings. Procedural requirements and factors affecting weight remain important.","B":"Incorrect. “All hearsay is absolutely inadmissible in civil cases” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Civil hearsay is generally admissible subject to the statutory and procedural framework.","C":"Incorrect. “Hearsay is admissible only if both parties sign a consent form” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Civil hearsay is generally admissible subject to the statutory and procedural framework.","D":"Incorrect. “A hearsay statement automatically has the same weight as live tested evidence” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Civil hearsay is generally admissible subject to the statutory and procedural framework.","E":"Incorrect. “The Civil Evidence Act applies only to criminal trials” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Civil hearsay is generally admissible subject to the statutory and procedural framework."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Evidence Act 1995, ss 1–4; CPR Part 33.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Civil hearsay is generally admissible subject to the statutory and procedural framework.","common_trap":"Importing the criminal-law approach or assuming admissibility means the evidence must receive full weight.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4a501f35a866422c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0587","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Settlement — without prejudice save as to costs","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"During genuine settlement negotiations, a party makes an offer headed 'without prejudice save as to costs'. No settlement is reached and the case proceeds to judgment.\n\nWhat is the ordinary significance of that formulation?","options":{"A":"The communication must be shown to the trial judge before liability is decided.","B":"The communication is generally protected from the court deciding the merits, but may be shown when the court later considers costs.","C":"The words make the offer a Part 36 offer automatically.","D":"The communication can never be considered for any purpose.","E":"The label removes privilege from genuine settlement negotiations."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"'Without prejudice save as to costs' ordinarily preserves without-prejudice protection on the merits while allowing the communication to be considered at the costs stage after substantive determination.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “The communication must be shown to the trial judge before liability is decided” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The qualification separates merits-stage privilege from later costs consideration.","B":"Correct. 'Without prejudice save as to costs' ordinarily preserves without-prejudice protection on the merits while allowing the communication to be considered at the costs stage after substantive determination.","C":"Incorrect. “The words make the offer a Part 36 offer automatically” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The qualification separates merits-stage privilege from later costs consideration.","D":"Incorrect. “The communication can never be considered for any purpose” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The qualification separates merits-stage privilege from later costs consideration.","E":"Incorrect. “The label removes privilege from genuine settlement negotiations” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The qualification separates merits-stage privilege from later costs consideration."},"primary_legal_authority":"Without-prejudice principles; Calderbank v Calderbank; CPR costs principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The qualification separates merits-stage privilege from later costs consideration.","common_trap":"Confusing common-law Calderbank-style protection with the distinct formal Part 36 regime.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"81f0bb211c7bd1c2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0588","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Conflicts — former clients","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor is asked to act against a former client in a new matter. The solicitor holds confidential information from the former retainer that is material to the new matter and could be used to the former client's disadvantage. No effective protective measures or informed consent resolve the problem.\n\nWhich is the best professional position?","options":{"A":"Confidentiality ends automatically when a retainer ends.","B":"A former client's information may always be used against them once bills are paid.","C":"The solicitor should not act where there is a real risk that the former client's confidential information would be used or disclosed improperly, subject to the SRA rules.","D":"Only current-client conflicts are regulated.","E":"The solicitor must act because the new client's interests always prevail."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The SRA confidentiality rules continue after the retainer. Acting against a former client can be impermissible where relevant confidential information creates a real risk of misuse or disclosure and the regulatory safeguards are not satisfied.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Confidentiality ends automatically when a retainer ends” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Former-client confidentiality can constrain later adverse representation.","B":"Incorrect. “A former client's information may always be used against them once bills are paid” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Former-client confidentiality can constrain later adverse representation.","C":"Correct. The SRA confidentiality rules continue after the retainer. Acting against a former client can be impermissible where relevant confidential information creates a real risk of misuse or disclosure and the regulatory safeguards are not satisfied.","D":"Incorrect. “Only current-client conflicts are regulated” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Former-client confidentiality can constrain later adverse representation.","E":"Incorrect. “The solicitor must act because the new client's interests always prevail” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Former-client confidentiality can constrain later adverse representation."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, paras 6.3–6.5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Former-client confidentiality can constrain later adverse representation.","common_trap":"Treating termination of the retainer as termination of the confidentiality obligation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"660a125f200fb669"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0589","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Professional conduct — client instructions and legality","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A client instructs a solicitor to structure a transaction in a way the solicitor knows would involve committing a criminal offence. The client insists that confidentiality and the duty to follow instructions require the solicitor to proceed.\n\nWhat should the solicitor do?","options":{"A":"The solicitor must follow every client instruction regardless of legality.","B":"Confidentiality converts an illegal instruction into a lawful one.","C":"The solicitor may proceed if the client signs a disclaimer.","D":"The solicitor must not facilitate unlawful conduct and should decline to carry out the illegal instruction, while dealing appropriately with confidentiality and any other legal duties.","E":"Only a court may refuse a client's illegal instruction."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Solicitors must uphold the rule of law and must not facilitate criminal conduct. Duties to clients operate within legal and regulatory limits and do not require implementation of unlawful instructions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “The solicitor must follow every client instruction regardless of legality” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Client autonomy does not require or permit a solicitor to participate in illegality.","B":"Incorrect. “Confidentiality converts an illegal instruction into a lawful one” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Client autonomy does not require or permit a solicitor to participate in illegality.","C":"Incorrect. “The solicitor may proceed if the client signs a disclaimer” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Client autonomy does not require or permit a solicitor to participate in illegality.","D":"Correct. Solicitors must uphold the rule of law and must not facilitate criminal conduct. Duties to clients operate within legal and regulatory limits and do not require implementation of unlawful instructions.","E":"Incorrect. “Only a court may refuse a client's illegal instruction” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Client autonomy does not require or permit a solicitor to participate in illegality."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Principles; SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Client autonomy does not require or permit a solicitor to participate in illegality.","common_trap":"Treating duties of confidentiality and client service as overriding the law and professional standards.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d320013e1af6f09c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0590","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Consideration — existing duty owed to third party","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A promises B £10,000 if B performs work that B is already contractually obliged to perform for C. B completes that work. No other validity issue arises.\n\nWhich consideration principle is most relevant?","options":{"A":"An existing duty owed to anyone can never be consideration.","B":"Consideration is valid only if B undertakes work not owed to any person.","C":"A promise by A is enforceable only if C releases B from the original contract.","D":"Past consideration is the only relevant doctrine.","E":"Performance of an existing contractual duty owed to a third party can constitute good consideration for a promise made by someone else."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Performance of, or a promise to perform, an existing contractual duty owed to a third party can amount to consideration for a fresh promise from another promisor.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “An existing duty owed to anyone can never be consideration” is not the applicable rule on these facts. A pre-existing duty to C can still be legal value furnished to A.","B":"Incorrect. “Consideration is valid only if B undertakes work not owed to any person” is not the applicable rule on these facts. A pre-existing duty to C can still be legal value furnished to A.","C":"Incorrect. “A promise by A is enforceable only if C releases B from the original contract” is not the applicable rule on these facts. A pre-existing duty to C can still be legal value furnished to A.","D":"Incorrect. “Past consideration is the only relevant doctrine” is not the applicable rule on these facts. A pre-existing duty to C can still be legal value furnished to A.","E":"Correct. Performance of, or a promise to perform, an existing contractual duty owed to a third party can amount to consideration for a fresh promise from another promisor."},"primary_legal_authority":"Shadwell v Shadwell; Scotson v Pegg.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A pre-existing duty to C can still be legal value furnished to A.","common_trap":"Confusing an existing duty owed to the promisor with one owed to a third party.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c071afbeee1251cb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0591","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Privity — Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contract between X and Y expressly states that Z may enforce a specified payment term. The contract identifies Z by name. No exclusion of the 1999 Act applies.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Z may enforce the specified term under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999, subject to the Act.","B":"Z can never enforce because Z supplied no consideration.","C":"Only an assignee can ever enforce a contract made by others.","D":"Z becomes a contracting party for every purpose and every term.","E":"The 1999 Act applies only to deeds."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The 1999 Act permits a third party to enforce a term where the contract expressly provides that the third party may do so or, subject to construction, the term purports to confer a benefit and the statutory identification requirement is met.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The 1999 Act permits a third party to enforce a term where the contract expressly provides that the third party may do so or, subject to construction, the term purports to confer a benefit and the statutory identification requirement is met.","B":"Incorrect. “Z can never enforce because Z supplied no consideration” is not the applicable rule on these facts. An expressly identified third party can receive statutory enforcement rights without becoming an original contracting party.","C":"Incorrect. “Only an assignee can ever enforce a contract made by others” is not the applicable rule on these facts. An expressly identified third party can receive statutory enforcement rights without becoming an original contracting party.","D":"Incorrect. “Z becomes a contracting party for every purpose and every term” is not the applicable rule on these facts. An expressly identified third party can receive statutory enforcement rights without becoming an original contracting party.","E":"Incorrect. “The 1999 Act applies only to deeds” is not the applicable rule on these facts. An expressly identified third party can receive statutory enforcement rights without becoming an original contracting party."},"primary_legal_authority":"Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999, s 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An expressly identified third party can receive statutory enforcement rights without becoming an original contracting party.","common_trap":"Applying the traditional privity rule without considering the statutory exception.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ead19738cffdee4d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0592","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Discharge — frustration and allocation of risk","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contract requires use of a specific venue for a one-off event. Before the event, and without fault of either party, the venue is destroyed. The contract contains no term allocating this risk and performance in the contemplated manner becomes impossible.\n\nWhich doctrine is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Breach automatically by the venue owner makes X and Y liable to each other.","B":"Frustration may automatically discharge future obligations where the supervening event makes performance impossible or radically different and the risk was not allocated by the contract.","C":"The contract remains enforceable exactly as written regardless of impossibility.","D":"Frustration requires one party deliberately to cause the event.","E":"Specific performance is automatically ordered despite destruction."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A supervening event occurring without fault may frustrate a contract where it makes performance impossible or transforms the obligation into something radically different, provided the contract has not allocated the risk.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Breach automatically by the venue owner makes X and Y liable to each other” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Frustration is a narrow doctrine addressing qualifying supervening events outside the parties' allocated risks.","B":"Correct. A supervening event occurring without fault may frustrate a contract where it makes performance impossible or transforms the obligation into something radically different, provided the contract has not allocated the risk.","C":"Incorrect. “The contract remains enforceable exactly as written regardless of impossibility” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Frustration is a narrow doctrine addressing qualifying supervening events outside the parties' allocated risks.","D":"Incorrect. “Frustration requires one party deliberately to cause the event” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Frustration is a narrow doctrine addressing qualifying supervening events outside the parties' allocated risks.","E":"Incorrect. “Specific performance is automatically ordered despite destruction” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Frustration is a narrow doctrine addressing qualifying supervening events outside the parties' allocated risks."},"primary_legal_authority":"Taylor v Caldwell; Davis Contractors Ltd v Fareham UDC.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Frustration is a narrow doctrine addressing qualifying supervening events outside the parties' allocated risks.","common_trap":"Treating every difficulty as frustration or overlooking contractual risk allocation and self-induced events.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f9db2a53882e73ff"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0593","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — psychiatric injury, primary victims","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A driver negligently causes a collision that places a pedestrian within the range of foreseeable physical injury. The pedestrian escapes physical injury but develops a recognised psychiatric illness as a result.\n\nWhich is the best general approach to the pedestrian as a primary victim?","options":{"A":"Recovery is impossible without physical injury.","B":"Primary victims must satisfy all the special control mechanisms applicable to secondary victims.","C":"If personal injury was reasonably foreseeable, a primary victim within the zone of physical danger need not separately establish that psychiatric injury itself was foreseeable.","D":"Mere grief without recognised psychiatric illness is always sufficient.","E":"Psychiatric injury is never actionable in negligence."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"For a primary victim exposed to foreseeable physical injury, foreseeability of personal injury can suffice; the claimant must still prove a recognised psychiatric condition and the ordinary elements of negligence.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Recovery is impossible without physical injury” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Primary-victim analysis differs from the additional control mechanisms governing secondary victims.","B":"Incorrect. “Primary victims must satisfy all the special control mechanisms applicable to secondary victims” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Primary-victim analysis differs from the additional control mechanisms governing secondary victims.","C":"Correct. For a primary victim exposed to foreseeable physical injury, foreseeability of personal injury can suffice; the claimant must still prove a recognised psychiatric condition and the ordinary elements of negligence.","D":"Incorrect. “Mere grief without recognised psychiatric illness is always sufficient” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Primary-victim analysis differs from the additional control mechanisms governing secondary victims.","E":"Incorrect. “Psychiatric injury is never actionable in negligence” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Primary-victim analysis differs from the additional control mechanisms governing secondary victims."},"primary_legal_authority":"Page v Smith [1996] AC 155; psychiatric-injury principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Primary-victim analysis differs from the additional control mechanisms governing secondary victims.","common_trap":"Importing secondary-victim proximity requirements into a claimant directly exposed to physical danger.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4a2a57c5ba83ecfc"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0594","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Vicarious liability — relationship akin to employment","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An organisation engages an individual under arrangements that are not labelled employment. The individual is integrated into the organisation's enterprise, works as part of its business and creates risk on its behalf rather than carrying on an independent business of their own.\n\nWhich issue is central to potential vicarious liability?","options":{"A":"Vicarious liability exists only where the written contract uses the word 'employee'.","B":"Every independent contractor automatically makes the engager vicariously liable.","C":"The claimant must prove the organisation personally authorised the tort.","D":"Whether the relationship is employment or sufficiently akin to employment, assessed substantively rather than solely by contractual label.","E":"Vicarious liability applies only to criminal offences."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The first stage of vicarious-liability analysis asks whether the relationship is employment or sufficiently akin to employment. Modern authority emphasises the substance of the relationship while preserving the distinction for genuinely independent contractors.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Vicarious liability exists only where the written contract uses the word 'employee'” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The legal character of the working relationship, not its label alone, is central.","B":"Incorrect. “Every independent contractor automatically makes the engager vicariously liable” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The legal character of the working relationship, not its label alone, is central.","C":"Incorrect. “The claimant must prove the organisation personally authorised the tort” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The legal character of the working relationship, not its label alone, is central.","D":"Correct. The first stage of vicarious-liability analysis asks whether the relationship is employment or sufficiently akin to employment. Modern authority emphasises the substance of the relationship while preserving the distinction for genuinely independent contractors.","E":"Incorrect. “Vicarious liability applies only to criminal offences” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The legal character of the working relationship, not its label alone, is central."},"primary_legal_authority":"Various Claimants v Catholic Child Welfare Society; Barclays Bank plc v Various Claimants [2020] UKSC 13.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The legal character of the working relationship, not its label alone, is central.","common_trap":"Treating contractual terminology as conclusive or erasing the independent-contractor distinction.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"90395f7536fd38eb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0595","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Defamation — publication on matter of public interest","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A journalist publishes a defamatory allegation on a matter of public interest. The journalist honestly and reasonably believes that publishing the statement is in the public interest after undertaking responsible investigation. The allegation cannot ultimately be proved true.\n\nWhich statutory defence is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Truth under section 2 necessarily succeeds even though truth cannot be proved.","B":"Honest opinion necessarily applies to every factual allegation.","C":"There is no possible defence if the allegation is defamatory.","D":"Consent is the only defence concerning journalism.","E":"Publication on a matter of public interest under section 4 of the Defamation Act 2013."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Section 4 provides a defence where the statement was, or formed part of, a statement on a matter of public interest and the defendant reasonably believed publication was in the public interest, with the court considering all circumstances.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Truth under section 2 necessarily succeeds even though truth cannot be proved” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Public-interest publication is a distinct statutory defence and does not depend on proving the allegation true.","B":"Incorrect. “Honest opinion necessarily applies to every factual allegation” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Public-interest publication is a distinct statutory defence and does not depend on proving the allegation true.","C":"Incorrect. “There is no possible defence if the allegation is defamatory” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Public-interest publication is a distinct statutory defence and does not depend on proving the allegation true.","D":"Incorrect. “Consent is the only defence concerning journalism” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Public-interest publication is a distinct statutory defence and does not depend on proving the allegation true.","E":"Correct. Section 4 provides a defence where the statement was, or formed part of, a statement on a matter of public interest and the defendant reasonably believed publication was in the public interest, with the court considering all circumstances."},"primary_legal_authority":"Defamation Act 2013, s 4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Public-interest publication is a distinct statutory defence and does not depend on proving the allegation true.","common_trap":"Conflating truth, opinion and public-interest defences.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"61e44bdc72be037c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0596","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Precedent — Court of Appeal and Young exceptions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A civil division of the Court of Appeal faces one of its own earlier decisions. Counsel argues that the court is absolutely bound by every previous Court of Appeal decision in all circumstances.\n\nWhich is the best general statement?","options":{"A":"The Court of Appeal is generally bound by its own decisions but recognises limited exceptions traditionally associated with Young v Bristol Aeroplane.","B":"The Court of Appeal is never bound by its own decisions.","C":"It may disregard any earlier case simply because the present judges disagree.","D":"Only the Supreme Court is subject to precedent.","E":"Every first-instance decision binds the Court of Appeal."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Horizontal precedent generally binds the Court of Appeal to its own decisions, subject to limited exceptions, including conflicting prior Court of Appeal authorities, inconsistency with a later Supreme Court decision, and decisions given per incuriam in the relevant sense.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Horizontal precedent generally binds the Court of Appeal to its own decisions, subject to limited exceptions, including conflicting prior Court of Appeal authorities, inconsistency with a later Supreme Court decision, and decisions given per incuriam in the relevant sense.","B":"Incorrect. “The Court of Appeal is never bound by its own decisions” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The Court of Appeal's self-binding rule is strong but not absolute.","C":"Incorrect. “It may disregard any earlier case simply because the present judges disagree” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The Court of Appeal's self-binding rule is strong but not absolute.","D":"Incorrect. “Only the Supreme Court is subject to precedent” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The Court of Appeal's self-binding rule is strong but not absolute.","E":"Incorrect. “Every first-instance decision binds the Court of Appeal” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The Court of Appeal's self-binding rule is strong but not absolute."},"primary_legal_authority":"Young v Bristol Aeroplane Co Ltd [1944] KB 718.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The Court of Appeal's self-binding rule is strong but not absolute.","common_trap":"Replacing defined exceptions with a free-standing discretion to depart whenever a panel prefers another view.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5689e466ca020375"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0597","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — error of law","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A statutory decision-maker interprets a legal condition governing its power incorrectly and refuses an application on that basis. The statute does not make the decision-maker's legal interpretation conclusively unreviewable.\n\nWhich public-law ground is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Procedural unfairness necessarily, even though procedure was flawless.","B":"Illegality through error of law.","C":"Irrationality is the only way courts can review legal mistakes.","D":"Private-law misrepresentation.","E":"Parliamentary privilege."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A public authority must correctly understand the law regulating its decision-making power. Reviewable error of law is ordinarily a form of illegality.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Procedural unfairness necessarily, even though procedure was flawless” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Misunderstanding the legal limits or conditions of a statutory power can make the decision unlawful.","B":"Correct. A public authority must correctly understand the law regulating its decision-making power. Reviewable error of law is ordinarily a form of illegality.","C":"Incorrect. “Irrationality is the only way courts can review legal mistakes” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Misunderstanding the legal limits or conditions of a statutory power can make the decision unlawful.","D":"Incorrect. “Private-law misrepresentation” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Misunderstanding the legal limits or conditions of a statutory power can make the decision unlawful.","E":"Incorrect. “Parliamentary privilege” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Misunderstanding the legal limits or conditions of a statutory power can make the decision unlawful."},"primary_legal_authority":"Anisminic Ltd v Foreign Compensation Commission; R (Cart) v Upper Tribunal and subsequent statutory context where relevant.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Misunderstanding the legal limits or conditions of a statutory power can make the decision unlawful.","common_trap":"Assuming legal interpretation by an administrator is immune unless it is also irrational.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b7d4b9072a54c5e4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0598","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Human Rights Act — declaration of incompatibility","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A senior court concludes that a provision of primary legislation cannot be interpreted compatibly with a Convention right using section 3. The provision remains valid legislation.\n\nWhich remedy may an authorised court consider?","options":{"A":"Automatic repeal of the Act by the court.","B":"Damages against Parliament as an automatic consequence.","C":"A declaration of incompatibility under section 4, which does not itself invalidate or disapply the primary legislation.","D":"A declaration under section 4 that makes the legislation void from enactment.","E":"Refusal to apply any primary legislation whenever incompatibility is alleged."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Where compatible interpretation is not possible, specified higher courts may issue a section 4 declaration. The declaration signals incompatibility but does not affect the validity, continuing operation or enforcement of the provision.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Automatic repeal of the Act by the court” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Section 4 preserves parliamentary sovereignty by leaving the legislation legally effective despite the declaration.","B":"Incorrect. “Damages against Parliament as an automatic consequence” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Section 4 preserves parliamentary sovereignty by leaving the legislation legally effective despite the declaration.","C":"Correct. Where compatible interpretation is not possible, specified higher courts may issue a section 4 declaration. The declaration signals incompatibility but does not affect the validity, continuing operation or enforcement of the provision.","D":"Incorrect. “A declaration under section 4 that makes the legislation void from enactment” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Section 4 preserves parliamentary sovereignty by leaving the legislation legally effective despite the declaration.","E":"Incorrect. “Refusal to apply any primary legislation whenever incompatibility is alleged” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Section 4 preserves parliamentary sovereignty by leaving the legislation legally effective despite the declaration."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, s 4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Section 4 preserves parliamentary sovereignty by leaving the legislation legally effective despite the declaration.","common_trap":"Treating a declaration of incompatibility as judicial invalidation of an Act of Parliament.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"330cba522727a44f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0599","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — knowledge and approval","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A testator duly executes a professionally prepared will but compelling evidence shows the testator signed without knowing that a substantial new gift had been inserted into the document. Capacity and formal execution are not otherwise disputed.\n\nWhich validity requirement is most directly in issue?","options":{"A":"Testamentary capacity only.","B":"The rule against perpetuities.","C":"Ademption.","D":"Knowledge and approval of the contents of the will.","E":"Intestacy entitlement."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A valid will requires the testator to know and approve its contents. Due execution can support a presumption, but suspicious or affirmative evidence may require the propounder to establish knowledge and approval.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Testamentary capacity only” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Formal execution does not by itself resolve a genuine issue whether the testator knew and approved the testamentary contents.","B":"Incorrect. “The rule against perpetuities” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Formal execution does not by itself resolve a genuine issue whether the testator knew and approved the testamentary contents.","C":"Incorrect. “Ademption” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Formal execution does not by itself resolve a genuine issue whether the testator knew and approved the testamentary contents.","D":"Correct. A valid will requires the testator to know and approve its contents. Due execution can support a presumption, but suspicious or affirmative evidence may require the propounder to establish knowledge and approval.","E":"Incorrect. “Intestacy entitlement” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Formal execution does not by itself resolve a genuine issue whether the testator knew and approved the testamentary contents."},"primary_legal_authority":"Gill v Woodall [2010] EWCA Civ 1430; knowledge-and-approval principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Formal execution does not by itself resolve a genuine issue whether the testator knew and approved the testamentary contents.","common_trap":"Confusing understanding of the particular will with the distinct Banks v Goodfellow capacity test.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d11c2048eb6128ea"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0600","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Estate administration — executor's authority","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A valid will appoints an adult executor. Immediately on the testator's death, before the grant of probate is issued, the executor asks about the source of authority to administer the estate.\n\nWhich is the best general statement?","options":{"A":"An executor acquires all authority only when the probate registry creates it by issuing the grant.","B":"An executor has no legal relationship to the estate before probate.","C":"Only administrators can act before a grant.","D":"The beneficiaries automatically become personal representatives until probate.","E":"An executor derives authority from the will and the testator's death; probate confirms that authority, although a grant is practically required for many dealings."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"An executor's title derives from the will and takes effect on death; the grant of probate authenticates and confirms that title. This contrasts with an administrator, whose authority derives from the grant.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “An executor acquires all authority only when the probate registry creates it by issuing the grant” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Probate confirms an executor's pre-existing representative title rather than creating it.","B":"Incorrect. “An executor has no legal relationship to the estate before probate” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Probate confirms an executor's pre-existing representative title rather than creating it.","C":"Incorrect. “Only administrators can act before a grant” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Probate confirms an executor's pre-existing representative title rather than creating it.","D":"Incorrect. “The beneficiaries automatically become personal representatives until probate” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Probate confirms an executor's pre-existing representative title rather than creating it.","E":"Correct. An executor's title derives from the will and takes effect on death; the grant of probate authenticates and confirms that title. This contrasts with an administrator, whose authority derives from the grant."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925; probate principles distinguishing executors and administrators.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Probate confirms an executor's pre-existing representative title rather than creating it.","common_trap":"Applying the rule for administrators to executors.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c00efdfce25d9719"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0601","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Companies — articles and shareholder agreement","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Shareholders enter a private agreement restricting how they will vote, but the company's articles contain a different governance rule. A dispute later concerns the company's own corporate acts.\n\nWhich distinction is important?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"A shareholders' agreement binds its parties as a contract but does not, merely by existing, amend the company's articles or statutory corporate constitution.","C":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","D":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Contractual shareholder arrangements and the company's constitution have distinct legal effects.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Correct. Contractual shareholder arrangements and the company's constitution have distinct legal effects.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 17–18; shareholder-agreement principles","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Contractual shareholder arrangements and the company's constitution have distinct legal effects.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"834a857a416831d2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0602","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Client account — reconciliation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm's accounting records show client liabilities that do not match the adjusted bank balance on its client account. The discrepancy has not been investigated for several months.\n\nWhich Accounts Rules requirement is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Reconciliation is required only once each financial year.","B":"The firm must perform client-account reconciliations at least every five weeks and promptly investigate and resolve differences.","C":"A difference can be ignored if the bank balance is positive.","D":"Only the firm's external accountant may reconcile client account.","E":"Reconciliation is optional where electronic banking is used."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The SRA Accounts Rules require reconciliations of client-account balances with client ledgers and bank records at least every five weeks, with differences promptly investigated and resolved.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Reconciliation is required only once each financial year” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Regular reconciliation is a core control protecting the accuracy and integrity of client-money records.","B":"Correct. The SRA Accounts Rules require reconciliations of client-account balances with client ledgers and bank records at least every five weeks, with differences promptly investigated and resolved.","C":"Incorrect. “A difference can be ignored if the bank balance is positive” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Regular reconciliation is a core control protecting the accuracy and integrity of client-money records.","D":"Incorrect. “Only the firm's external accountant may reconcile client account” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Regular reconciliation is a core control protecting the accuracy and integrity of client-money records.","E":"Incorrect. “Reconciliation is optional where electronic banking is used” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Regular reconciliation is a core control protecting the accuracy and integrity of client-money records."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, r 8.3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Regular reconciliation is a core control protecting the accuracy and integrity of client-money records.","common_trap":"Mistaking a positive aggregate balance for evidence that individual client liabilities are correctly recorded.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"318f9fa84fd0714e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0603","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustee delegation — investment management","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Trustees wish to delegate asset-management functions to a professional investment manager. The trust instrument does not prohibit delegation.\n\nWhich is the best general position under the Trustee Act 2000?","options":{"A":"Trustees can never delegate investment functions.","B":"Delegation permanently transfers all trustee responsibility to the agent.","C":"Trustees may delegate permitted functions subject to statutory safeguards, including appropriate terms, policy documentation where required, and duties of review.","D":"Trustees may delegate every function without restriction or supervision.","E":"Only beneficiaries can appoint an investment manager."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Trustee Act 2000 permits delegation of specified functions, including asset management, but imposes safeguards concerning selection, terms, policy statements where applicable, and ongoing review.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Trustees can never delegate investment functions” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Delegation is permitted but does not amount to abandonment of trustee oversight.","B":"Incorrect. “Delegation permanently transfers all trustee responsibility to the agent” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Delegation is permitted but does not amount to abandonment of trustee oversight.","C":"Correct. The Trustee Act 2000 permits delegation of specified functions, including asset management, but imposes safeguards concerning selection, terms, policy statements where applicable, and ongoing review.","D":"Incorrect. “Trustees may delegate every function without restriction or supervision” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Delegation is permitted but does not amount to abandonment of trustee oversight.","E":"Incorrect. “Only beneficiaries can appoint an investment manager” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Delegation is permitted but does not amount to abandonment of trustee oversight."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 2000, ss 11–15 and 22.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Delegation is permitted but does not amount to abandonment of trustee oversight.","common_trap":"Treating delegation as either categorically prohibited or as complete release from trustee responsibility.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"afcd67d1d061cd9c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0604","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Knowing receipt — beneficial receipt","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A third party receives trust property for their own benefit following a breach of trust. At the relevant time, their knowledge makes it unconscionable for them to retain the benefit.\n\nWhich personal equitable claim is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Dishonest assistance, which never requires receipt and is therefore the only possible claim.","B":"A resulting trust automatically, regardless of knowledge.","C":"Proprietary estoppel.","D":"Knowing receipt.","E":"Breach of a trustee's statutory investment duty by the recipient."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Knowing receipt is a personal equitable claim requiring beneficial receipt of assets traceable to a breach of trust or fiduciary duty and knowledge making retention unconscionable.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Dishonest assistance, which never requires receipt and is therefore the only possible claim” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Beneficial receipt plus the requisite unconscionable knowledge distinguishes knowing receipt from assistance liability.","B":"Incorrect. “A resulting trust automatically, regardless of knowledge” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Beneficial receipt plus the requisite unconscionable knowledge distinguishes knowing receipt from assistance liability.","C":"Incorrect. “Proprietary estoppel” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Beneficial receipt plus the requisite unconscionable knowledge distinguishes knowing receipt from assistance liability.","D":"Correct. Knowing receipt is a personal equitable claim requiring beneficial receipt of assets traceable to a breach of trust or fiduciary duty and knowledge making retention unconscionable.","E":"Incorrect. “Breach of a trustee's statutory investment duty by the recipient” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Beneficial receipt plus the requisite unconscionable knowledge distinguishes knowing receipt from assistance liability."},"primary_legal_authority":"Ivey v Genting Casinos not determinative here; Bank of Credit and Commerce International (Overseas) Ltd v Akindele [2001] Ch 437.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Beneficial receipt plus the requisite unconscionable knowledge distinguishes knowing receipt from assistance liability.","common_trap":"Confusing receipt-based liability with dishonest assistance, where receipt of trust property is unnecessary.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c5c4fecb6ac1bbc5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0605","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Easements — accommodation of dominant tenement","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An owner claims an easement benefiting Blackacre over neighbouring Whiteacre. The claimed right has no connection with the normal use or enjoyment of Blackacre and instead benefits the owner purely in a personal business capacity wherever the owner happens to operate.\n\nWhich characteristic of an easement is most directly problematic?","options":{"A":"An easement never requires dominant and servient land.","B":"Every personal commercial advantage automatically qualifies as an easement.","C":"The dominant and servient owners must always be the same person.","D":"Only rights of way can be easements.","E":"The right must accommodate the dominant tenement rather than confer a merely personal advantage unrelated to that land."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"One of the Re Ellenborough Park characteristics is that the right must accommodate the dominant tenement: it must be connected with and benefit the land, not merely the personal activities of its current owner.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “An easement never requires dominant and servient land” is not the applicable rule on these facts. An easement is appurtenant to land and must benefit the dominant tenement as land.","B":"Incorrect. “Every personal commercial advantage automatically qualifies as an easement” is not the applicable rule on these facts. An easement is appurtenant to land and must benefit the dominant tenement as land.","C":"Incorrect. “The dominant and servient owners must always be the same person” is not the applicable rule on these facts. An easement is appurtenant to land and must benefit the dominant tenement as land.","D":"Incorrect. “Only rights of way can be easements” is not the applicable rule on these facts. An easement is appurtenant to land and must benefit the dominant tenement as land.","E":"Correct. One of the Re Ellenborough Park characteristics is that the right must accommodate the dominant tenement: it must be connected with and benefit the land, not merely the personal activities of its current owner."},"primary_legal_authority":"Re Ellenborough Park [1956] Ch 131.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"An easement is appurtenant to land and must benefit the dominant tenement as land.","common_trap":"Confusing a personal contractual advantage with a proprietary right attached to dominant land.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1cabbbd2d01acd5d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0606","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Mortgages — priority of registered charges","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A registered proprietor grants Bank A a registered legal charge. Later, the proprietor grants Bank B another registered legal charge. No priority agreement, postponement or special statutory exception applies.\n\nWhich is the ordinary priority position?","options":{"A":"Bank A's earlier registered charge ordinarily has priority over Bank B's later registered charge.","B":"Bank B automatically has priority because its loan is later.","C":"Both charges always rank equally regardless of registration.","D":"Priority depends solely on which bank lent more money.","E":"Registration has no relevance to priority of registered charges."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Registered dispositions and the Land Registration Act priority regime generally preserve the priority of an earlier registered charge over a later one, absent a priority-altering event or agreement.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Registered dispositions and the Land Registration Act priority regime generally preserve the priority of an earlier registered charge over a later one, absent a priority-altering event or agreement.","B":"Incorrect. “Bank B automatically has priority because its loan is later” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Earlier protected registered interests ordinarily retain priority over later registered charges.","C":"Incorrect. “Both charges always rank equally regardless of registration” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Earlier protected registered interests ordinarily retain priority over later registered charges.","D":"Incorrect. “Priority depends solely on which bank lent more money” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Earlier protected registered interests ordinarily retain priority over later registered charges.","E":"Incorrect. “Registration has no relevance to priority of registered charges” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Earlier protected registered interests ordinarily retain priority over later registered charges."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002, ss 28–29 and registered-charge priority principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Earlier protected registered interests ordinarily retain priority over later registered charges.","common_trap":"Assuming later lending or larger value itself reverses registered priority.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"086142a2249c0a90"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0607","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Exchange — deposit as stakeholder","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"On exchange of a residential sale contract incorporating the Standard Conditions of Sale, the buyer pays the contractual deposit to the seller's solicitor as stakeholder rather than as agent for the seller.\n\nWhat is the principal consequence of stakeholder status before completion?","options":{"A":"The seller immediately owns and may freely spend the deposit in every case.","B":"The deposit is held pending the contractual event and is not ordinarily available for the seller to use as its own money before entitlement arises.","C":"The buyer can demand the deposit back at any moment despite a binding contract.","D":"Stakeholder status means no contract has been formed.","E":"The deposit must be sent to HM Land Registry."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A stakeholder holds the deposit for the parties pending the event determining entitlement, unlike an agent for the seller who may receive it on the seller's behalf subject to the contract.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “The seller immediately owns and may freely spend the deposit in every case” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Stakeholder status protects the deposit from being treated as immediately available to the seller before contractual entitlement crystallises.","B":"Correct. A stakeholder holds the deposit for the parties pending the event determining entitlement, unlike an agent for the seller who may receive it on the seller's behalf subject to the contract.","C":"Incorrect. “The buyer can demand the deposit back at any moment despite a binding contract” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Stakeholder status protects the deposit from being treated as immediately available to the seller before contractual entitlement crystallises.","D":"Incorrect. “Stakeholder status means no contract has been formed” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Stakeholder status protects the deposit from being treated as immediately available to the seller before contractual entitlement crystallises.","E":"Incorrect. “The deposit must be sent to HM Land Registry” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Stakeholder status protects the deposit from being treated as immediately available to the seller before contractual entitlement crystallises."},"primary_legal_authority":"Standard Conditions of Sale; conveyancing deposit principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Stakeholder status protects the deposit from being treated as immediately available to the seller before contractual entitlement crystallises.","common_trap":"Confusing receipt as stakeholder with receipt as the seller's agent.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e308286171ce6a4f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0608","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Leasehold transactions — rent review","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A commercial lease provides for rent review every five years by reference to open-market rent and contains a detailed hypothetical-lease formula. The review date arrives.\n\nWhich is the best approach when advising on the new rent?","options":{"A":"The landlord may set any rent unilaterally because a review date has arrived.","B":"Rent review always means an automatic increase by inflation.","C":"Apply the contractual rent-review machinery and its stated assumptions and disregards; there is no universal statutory formula replacing the lease terms.","D":"The tenant may ignore the clause because commercial rents cannot be reviewed.","E":"HM Land Registry determines open-market rent for every lease."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Commercial rent review is principally contractual. The lease's review clause specifies the valuation basis, assumptions, disregards, procedure and dispute mechanism, which must be applied according to its construction.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “The landlord may set any rent unilaterally because a review date has arrived” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The lease's own review machinery governs unless legislation or another binding rule modifies it.","B":"Incorrect. “Rent review always means an automatic increase by inflation” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The lease's own review machinery governs unless legislation or another binding rule modifies it.","C":"Correct. Commercial rent review is principally contractual. The lease's review clause specifies the valuation basis, assumptions, disregards, procedure and dispute mechanism, which must be applied according to its construction.","D":"Incorrect. “The tenant may ignore the clause because commercial rents cannot be reviewed” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The lease's own review machinery governs unless legislation or another binding rule modifies it.","E":"Incorrect. “HM Land Registry determines open-market rent for every lease” is not the applicable rule on these facts. The lease's own review machinery governs unless legislation or another binding rule modifies it."},"primary_legal_authority":"Commercial lease rent-review principles and construction of the lease.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The lease's own review machinery governs unless legislation or another binding rule modifies it.","common_trap":"Assuming 'open market' review has a single universal formula independent of the lease wording.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"10b6e849cfafac92"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0609","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Wills — republication by codicil","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A testator validly executes a codicil confirming an earlier will. A question arises about the date from which the will is treated as speaking for certain construction purposes.\n\nWhat is the significance of republication by codicil?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","C":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","D":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding.","E":"A valid codicil can republish the will so that, subject to context, the will is treated as re-executed at the date of the codicil for relevant construction purposes."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"A codicil can affect the operative date of the will without requiring a wholly new will.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Correct. A codicil can affect the operative date of the will without requiring a wholly new will."},"primary_legal_authority":"Republication by codicil; Wills Act 1837 principles","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A codicil can affect the operative date of the will without requiring a wholly new will.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"891175a0ae4768a4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0610","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Mode of trial — indictable-only offences","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An adult is charged with an indictable-only offence. After the required preliminary magistrates' court procedure, the case is to proceed to trial.\n\nWhere is the trial ordinarily held?","options":{"A":"In the magistrates' court if the defendant prefers.","B":"In the County Court.","C":"In the High Court's Administrative Court as a criminal trial.","D":"Before a tribunal chosen by the prosecution.","E":"In the Crown Court."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Indictable-only offences are sent to the Crown Court for trial; the magistrates' court deals with preliminary matters and sending procedure rather than conducting the trial.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “In the magistrates' court if the defendant prefers” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Indictable-only classification determines Crown Court trial jurisdiction.","B":"Incorrect. “In the County Court” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Indictable-only classification determines Crown Court trial jurisdiction.","C":"Incorrect. “In the High Court's Administrative Court as a criminal trial” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Indictable-only classification determines Crown Court trial jurisdiction.","D":"Incorrect. “Before a tribunal chosen by the prosecution” is not the applicable rule on these facts. Indictable-only classification determines Crown Court trial jurisdiction.","E":"Correct. Indictable-only offences are sent to the Crown Court for trial; the magistrates' court deals with preliminary matters and sending procedure rather than conducting the trial."},"primary_legal_authority":"Crime and Disorder Act 1998, s 51; Magistrates' Courts Act 1980; criminal procedure rules.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Indictable-only classification determines Crown Court trial jurisdiction.","common_trap":"Confusing either-way election and allocation with offences that cannot be tried summarily.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"716bcf1f1c3ebffe"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0611","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — loans to directors","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company proposes to make a substantial cash loan to one of its directors. No statutory exception applies. The board has approved the proposal, but the members have not.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The transaction ordinarily requires member approval under the Companies Act before the company enters into the loan.","B":"Board approval is always sufficient for a loan to a director.","C":"Companies are absolutely prohibited from lending to directors in all circumstances.","D":"Member approval is needed only if the director is also a creditor of the company.","E":"A loan to a director is governed only by insolvency law."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Companies Act regulates loans, quasi-loans, credit transactions and related arrangements involving directors. Where the relevant statutory regime applies and no exception is available, member approval is ordinarily required.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Companies Act regulates loans, quasi-loans, credit transactions and related arrangements involving directors. Where the relevant statutory regime applies and no exception is available, member approval is ordinarily required.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “Board approval is always sufficient for a loan to a director” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Certain company loans to directors require shareholder approval notwithstanding board support.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “Companies are absolutely prohibited from lending to directors in all circumstances” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Certain company loans to directors require shareholder approval notwithstanding board support.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “Member approval is needed only if the director is also a creditor of the company” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Certain company loans to directors require shareholder approval notwithstanding board support.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “A loan to a director is governed only by insolvency law” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Certain company loans to directors require shareholder approval notwithstanding board support."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 197–214.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Certain company loans to directors require shareholder approval notwithstanding board support.","common_trap":"Confusing a regulated related-party transaction with either an absolute prohibition or ordinary board authority.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"66a363fa74184743"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0612","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — directors' duty to creditors","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company is insolvent and liquidation is probable. Its directors are deciding whether to transfer remaining assets to shareholders, even though doing so would materially prejudice unpaid creditors.\n\nWhich consideration becomes particularly important in the directors' decision-making?","options":{"A":"Directors must always prefer shareholders even after insolvent liquidation becomes inevitable.","B":"The interests of creditors as a whole, because the creditor-interest duty is engaged as insolvency becomes sufficiently serious under the common-law rule preserved by statute.","C":"Creditors' interests can never be relevant until a liquidator is formally appointed.","D":"Directors owe each individual creditor an independent fiduciary duty enforceable directly in every case.","E":"The duty to promote success under section 172 always requires distribution of remaining assets to members."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The common-law creditor-interest rule, recognised by section 172(3), modifies the ordinary shareholder-focused framework when insolvency reaches the relevant threshold. The Supreme Court has explained that creditor interests gain weight as the company's financial position deteriorates toward insolvent liquidation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “Directors must always prefer shareholders even after insolvent liquidation becomes inevitable” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Where insolvent liquidation is probable or inevitable, directors must give appropriate priority to creditors' interests rather than extracting value for shareholders.","B":"Correct. The common-law creditor-interest rule, recognised by section 172(3), modifies the ordinary shareholder-focused framework when insolvency reaches the relevant threshold. The Supreme Court has explained that creditor interests gain weight as the company's financial position deteriorates toward insolvent liquidation.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “Creditors' interests can never be relevant until a liquidator is formally appointed” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Where insolvent liquidation is probable or inevitable, directors must give appropriate priority to creditors' interests rather than extracting value for shareholders.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “Directors owe each individual creditor an independent fiduciary duty enforceable directly in every case” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Where insolvent liquidation is probable or inevitable, directors must give appropriate priority to creditors' interests rather than extracting value for shareholders.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “The duty to promote success under section 172 always requires distribution of remaining assets to members” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Where insolvent liquidation is probable or inevitable, directors must give appropriate priority to creditors' interests rather than extracting value for shareholders."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, s 172(3); BTI 2014 LLC v Sequana SA [2022] UKSC 25.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Where insolvent liquidation is probable or inevitable, directors must give appropriate priority to creditors' interests rather than extracting value for shareholders.","common_trap":"Assuming the ordinary shareholder-benefit focus remains unchanged throughout insolvency.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f9f9b28348bb4c29"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0613","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — registration of charges","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A UK company grants a registrable charge over its assets to secure a bank loan. The parties want the security to retain its intended effectiveness against an administrator, liquidator and creditors.\n\nWhich Companies Act requirement is particularly important?","options":{"A":"A company charge never needs registration.","B":"Only the company's articles need to mention the charge.","C":"The prescribed particulars and instrument should be delivered for registration within the statutory period, ordinarily 21 days beginning with the day after creation of the charge.","D":"Registration can always be postponed until liquidation without consequence.","E":"Companies House registration converts the secured loan into share capital."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Part 25 requires registration of most company charges. Failure to register within the statutory period can make the security void against a liquidator, administrator and creditor, while the underlying debt remains.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “A company charge never needs registration” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Timely registration is central to preserving the effectiveness of a registrable company charge against insolvency office-holders and creditors.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “Only the company's articles need to mention the charge” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Timely registration is central to preserving the effectiveness of a registrable company charge against insolvency office-holders and creditors.","C":"Correct. Part 25 requires registration of most company charges. Failure to register within the statutory period can make the security void against a liquidator, administrator and creditor, while the underlying debt remains.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “Registration can always be postponed until liquidation without consequence” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Timely registration is central to preserving the effectiveness of a registrable company charge against insolvency office-holders and creditors.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “Companies House registration converts the secured loan into share capital” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Timely registration is central to preserving the effectiveness of a registrable company charge against insolvency office-holders and creditors."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 859A–859H.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Timely registration is central to preserving the effectiveness of a registrable company charge against insolvency office-holders and creditors.","common_trap":"Confusing invalidity of unregistered security against specified parties with extinction of the underlying debt.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"daed94f01afe0afb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0614","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — wrongful trading defence","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A director continued trading after concluding that insolvent liquidation could not reasonably be avoided. The director nevertheless immediately took every step that ought to have been taken to minimise potential loss to creditors.\n\nWhich is the best analysis of wrongful-trading exposure?","options":{"A":"Once the knowledge threshold is reached, liability is automatic regardless of subsequent conduct.","B":"Wrongful trading is a criminal offence carrying mandatory imprisonment in every case.","C":"Only shareholders can bring a wrongful-trading application.","D":"The 'every step' conduct is relevant to the statutory protection against a contribution order, subject to the precise statutory conditions.","E":"A director escapes liability merely by resigning, regardless of creditor loss or conduct."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The wrongful-trading provisions permit the court to order a contribution where the statutory knowledge threshold and other requirements are met, but the legislation protects a director who thereafter took every step that ought to have been taken with a view to minimising potential loss to creditors.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “Once the knowledge threshold is reached, liability is automatic regardless of subsequent conduct” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. After the relevant knowledge point, the director's concrete loss-minimisation conduct remains legally significant.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “Wrongful trading is a criminal offence carrying mandatory imprisonment in every case” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. After the relevant knowledge point, the director's concrete loss-minimisation conduct remains legally significant.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “Only shareholders can bring a wrongful-trading application” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. After the relevant knowledge point, the director's concrete loss-minimisation conduct remains legally significant.","D":"Correct. The wrongful-trading provisions permit the court to order a contribution where the statutory knowledge threshold and other requirements are met, but the legislation protects a director who thereafter took every step that ought to have been taken with a view to minimising potential loss to creditors.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “A director escapes liability merely by resigning, regardless of creditor loss or conduct” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. After the relevant knowledge point, the director's concrete loss-minimisation conduct remains legally significant."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, s 214.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"After the relevant knowledge point, the director's concrete loss-minimisation conduct remains legally significant.","common_trap":"Treating the statutory knowledge threshold as creating automatic liability without examining the director's subsequent steps.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e210b7a8c2524231"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0615","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Part 36 — defendant's offer and costs","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant makes a valid Part 36 offer. The claimant rejects it, proceeds to trial and obtains a judgment less advantageous than the defendant's offer. No injustice exception applies.\n\nWhat is the ordinary costs significance?","options":{"A":"The defendant's offer has no costs effect because only claimant offers matter.","B":"The claimant automatically receives indemnity costs from the defendant after rejecting the offer.","C":"The offer retrospectively ends the proceedings on expiry of the relevant period.","D":"Part 36 consequences depend solely on whether the claimant won something at trial.","E":"The court ordinarily applies the defendant-offer consequences under CPR 36.17 from expiry of the relevant period, subject to the rule's detailed provisions."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Where a claimant fails to obtain a judgment more advantageous than a defendant's Part 36 offer, CPR 36.17 ordinarily shifts specified costs consequences from the end of the relevant period unless the court considers that unjust.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “The defendant's offer has no costs effect because only claimant offers matter” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A claimant can win the case yet suffer Part 36 costs consequences by failing to beat a defendant's qualifying offer.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “The claimant automatically receives indemnity costs from the defendant after rejecting the offer” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A claimant can win the case yet suffer Part 36 costs consequences by failing to beat a defendant's qualifying offer.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “The offer retrospectively ends the proceedings on expiry of the relevant period” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A claimant can win the case yet suffer Part 36 costs consequences by failing to beat a defendant's qualifying offer.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “Part 36 consequences depend solely on whether the claimant won something at trial” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A claimant can win the case yet suffer Part 36 costs consequences by failing to beat a defendant's qualifying offer.","E":"Correct. Where a claimant fails to obtain a judgment more advantageous than a defendant's Part 36 offer, CPR 36.17 ordinarily shifts specified costs consequences from the end of the relevant period unless the court considers that unjust."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, rr 36.5 and 36.17.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A claimant can win the case yet suffer Part 36 costs consequences by failing to beat a defendant's qualifying offer.","common_trap":"Assuming substantive success alone prevents adverse Part 36 consequences.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"be3e859ab41b1004"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0616","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Case management — strike out","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defence contains only bare assertions that disclose no reasonable grounds for defending the claim. The claimant seeks an order disposing of the defective statement of case.\n\nWhich power is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"The court's power to strike out a statement of case under CPR 3.4.","B":"A freezing injunction under CPR Part 25.","C":"A Part 36 offer.","D":"A charging order.","E":"A witness summons."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"CPR 3.4 permits strike-out where a statement of case discloses no reasonable grounds, is an abuse of process or is otherwise likely to obstruct the just disposal of proceedings, or where there has been qualifying non-compliance.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. CPR 3.4 permits strike-out where a statement of case discloses no reasonable grounds, is an abuse of process or is otherwise likely to obstruct the just disposal of proceedings, or where there has been qualifying non-compliance.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “A freezing injunction under CPR Part 25” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Strike-out targets defective or abusive statements of case and specified procedural defaults.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “A Part 36 offer” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Strike-out targets defective or abusive statements of case and specified procedural defaults.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “A charging order” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Strike-out targets defective or abusive statements of case and specified procedural defaults.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “A witness summons” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Strike-out targets defective or abusive statements of case and specified procedural defaults."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, r 3.4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Strike-out targets defective or abusive statements of case and specified procedural defaults.","common_trap":"Confusing disposal of a defective pleading with an interim asset-preservation or enforcement remedy.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c912e6324e61d0f1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0617","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Disclosure — continuing duty","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"After standard disclosure has been completed, a party later discovers a relevant adverse document that falls within the scope of its disclosure obligation.\n\nWhat is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Disclosure obligations end permanently when the first list is served.","B":"The duty of disclosure continues until proceedings conclude, so the party should disclose the document in accordance with the applicable rules.","C":"Adverse documents never need to be disclosed.","D":"A party may destroy a late-discovered document if it harms its case.","E":"Only documents created after proceedings began can be added later."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Under the ordinary CPR disclosure framework, the duty of disclosure continues until proceedings are concluded. If documents later come within the duty, supplemental disclosure is required.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “Disclosure obligations end permanently when the first list is served” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Disclosure is a continuing procedural obligation rather than a one-off snapshot.","B":"Correct. Under the ordinary CPR disclosure framework, the duty of disclosure continues until proceedings are concluded. If documents later come within the duty, supplemental disclosure is required.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “Adverse documents never need to be disclosed” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Disclosure is a continuing procedural obligation rather than a one-off snapshot.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “A party may destroy a late-discovered document if it harms its case” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Disclosure is a continuing procedural obligation rather than a one-off snapshot.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “Only documents created after proceedings began can be added later” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Disclosure is a continuing procedural obligation rather than a one-off snapshot."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, rr 31.6 and 31.11, where Part 31 applies.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Disclosure is a continuing procedural obligation rather than a one-off snapshot.","common_trap":"Assuming completion of the initial disclosure exercise eliminates later duties.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1873d4f6d04058a6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0618","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Professional conduct — independence","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A major commercial client threatens to withdraw all work from a firm unless the solicitor gives legal advice that the solicitor knows is misleading and unsupported by law.\n\nWhat professional principle is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"The size of the client permits the solicitor to alter legal advice to preserve revenue.","B":"A solicitor's independence matters only in litigation.","C":"The solicitor must maintain professional independence and must not allow commercial pressure to compromise legal or regulatory duties.","D":"Client pressure converts misleading advice into acceptable advocacy.","E":"The firm must follow the instruction because the client pays the fees."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"SRA Principles and conduct obligations require solicitors to act with independence and integrity. Commercial pressure from a client cannot justify misleading advice or departure from professional obligations.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “The size of the client permits the solicitor to alter legal advice to preserve revenue” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Professional judgment must remain independent of improper client or commercial pressure.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “A solicitor's independence matters only in litigation” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Professional judgment must remain independent of improper client or commercial pressure.","C":"Correct. SRA Principles and conduct obligations require solicitors to act with independence and integrity. Commercial pressure from a client cannot justify misleading advice or departure from professional obligations.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “Client pressure converts misleading advice into acceptable advocacy” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Professional judgment must remain independent of improper client or commercial pressure.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “The firm must follow the instruction because the client pays the fees” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Professional judgment must remain independent of improper client or commercial pressure."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Principles; SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Professional judgment must remain independent of improper client or commercial pressure.","common_trap":"Treating economic dependence on a client as overriding professional standards.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a5a1d14bddddae2c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0619","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"AML — suspicious activity and tipping off","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor in the regulated sector makes an authorised disclosure concerning suspected money laundering. The client then asks why the transaction has been delayed. The solicitor considers telling the client that a suspicious activity report was made and that law enforcement may investigate.\n\nWhich risk must the solicitor consider?","options":{"A":"A solicitor is always required to tell the client immediately that a SAR has been filed.","B":"Tipping off applies only to judges.","C":"Once a SAR is made, confidentiality ceases entirely.","D":"Whether the proposed disclosure could constitute the tipping-off offence or another prohibited disclosure under POCA, subject to the statutory elements and exceptions.","E":"POCA permits disclosure whenever it would improve client relations."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"POCA contains offences concerning disclosure of information likely to prejudice an investigation after specified money-laundering disclosures, with statutory scope and exceptions that must be considered carefully.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “A solicitor is always required to tell the client immediately that a SAR has been filed” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A regulated professional must not casually reveal a SAR or investigation where doing so could satisfy the tipping-off provisions.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “Tipping off applies only to judges” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A regulated professional must not casually reveal a SAR or investigation where doing so could satisfy the tipping-off provisions.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “Once a SAR is made, confidentiality ceases entirely” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A regulated professional must not casually reveal a SAR or investigation where doing so could satisfy the tipping-off provisions.","D":"Correct. POCA contains offences concerning disclosure of information likely to prejudice an investigation after specified money-laundering disclosures, with statutory scope and exceptions that must be considered carefully.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “POCA permits disclosure whenever it would improve client relations” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A regulated professional must not casually reveal a SAR or investigation where doing so could satisfy the tipping-off provisions."},"primary_legal_authority":"Proceeds of Crime Act 2002, ss 333A–333D.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A regulated professional must not casually reveal a SAR or investigation where doing so could satisfy the tipping-off provisions.","common_trap":"Assuming ordinary client communication duties require disclosure of a SAR regardless of criminal-law restrictions.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"85c8bc75cc297e86"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0620","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Exclusion clauses — reasonableness under UCTA","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two businesses contract on one party's written standard terms. A clause seeks to exclude liability for breach of a contractual obligation, and UCTA makes the clause subject to the reasonableness test.\n\nWhich is the best general approach?","options":{"A":"Every exclusion clause between businesses is automatically valid.","B":"UCTA automatically voids every limitation of liability.","C":"Reasonableness is assessed solely by whether the breach later caused a large loss.","D":"A signed standard term can never be controlled by statute.","E":"The party relying on the term must satisfy the statutory reasonableness requirement assessed in the circumstances contemplated when the contract was made."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Where UCTA subjects a term to section 11, the clause is effective only so far as it satisfies the statutory requirement of reasonableness, assessed by reference to the circumstances known or reasonably contemplated when the contract was made.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “Every exclusion clause between businesses is automatically valid” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Statutory control can apply to business exclusion terms even where they were incorporated into the contract.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “UCTA automatically voids every limitation of liability” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Statutory control can apply to business exclusion terms even where they were incorporated into the contract.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “Reasonableness is assessed solely by whether the breach later caused a large loss” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Statutory control can apply to business exclusion terms even where they were incorporated into the contract.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “A signed standard term can never be controlled by statute” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Statutory control can apply to business exclusion terms even where they were incorporated into the contract.","E":"Correct. Where UCTA subjects a term to section 11, the clause is effective only so far as it satisfies the statutory requirement of reasonableness, assessed by reference to the circumstances known or reasonably contemplated when the contract was made."},"primary_legal_authority":"Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977, ss 3 and 11, Sch 2 where relevant.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Statutory control can apply to business exclusion terms even where they were incorporated into the contract.","common_trap":"Confusing incorporation with enforceability or treating UCTA as either irrelevant or an automatic ban.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fb4130aa772bd152"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0621","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Remedies — liquidated damages and penalties","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A negotiated commercial contract requires a defaulting party to pay a fixed sum on breach. The sum protects a legitimate commercial interest and is not out of all proportion to that interest.\n\nWhich is the best general analysis?","options":{"A":"The clause is not a penalty merely because it exceeds a pre-estimate of loss; the modern test asks whether the detriment is out of all proportion to a legitimate interest in performance.","B":"Any fixed payment triggered by breach is automatically an unenforceable penalty.","C":"A clause is valid only if it is an exact pre-estimate of loss.","D":"Penalty doctrine applies identically to every primary payment obligation not triggered by breach.","E":"Commercial parties cannot agree liquidated damages."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The Supreme Court reformulated the penalty test: the question is whether a secondary obligation imposes a detriment out of all proportion to the innocent party's legitimate interest in enforcement of the primary obligation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The Supreme Court reformulated the penalty test: the question is whether a secondary obligation imposes a detriment out of all proportion to the innocent party's legitimate interest in enforcement of the primary obligation.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “Any fixed payment triggered by breach is automatically an unenforceable penalty” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A commercially justified breach-triggered payment is not automatically penal merely because compensation is not its sole function.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “A clause is valid only if it is an exact pre-estimate of loss” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A commercially justified breach-triggered payment is not automatically penal merely because compensation is not its sole function.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “Penalty doctrine applies identically to every primary payment obligation not triggered by breach” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A commercially justified breach-triggered payment is not automatically penal merely because compensation is not its sole function.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “Commercial parties cannot agree liquidated damages” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A commercially justified breach-triggered payment is not automatically penal merely because compensation is not its sole function."},"primary_legal_authority":"Cavendish Square Holding BV v Makdessi; ParkingEye Ltd v Beavis [2015] UKSC 67.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A commercially justified breach-triggered payment is not automatically penal merely because compensation is not its sole function.","common_trap":"Applying the older 'genuine pre-estimate of loss' formulation as an exclusive modern test.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8011528cf4d7abd1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0622","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Companies — substantial property transaction: connected person","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company proposes to transfer a substantial non-cash asset to the spouse of one of its directors. No exception applies.\n\nWhy is the relationship relevant?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","C":"The statutory substantial-property-transaction regime extends to directors and persons connected with them, so member approval requirements may be engaged.","D":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The regime cannot be avoided simply by transacting with a statutorily connected person rather than the director personally.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Correct. The regime cannot be avoided simply by transacting with a statutorily connected person rather than the director personally.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 190–196, 252–254","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The regime cannot be avoided simply by transacting with a statutorily connected person rather than the director personally.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d2920d61daf323c8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0623","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — rescuers","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A driver negligently creates an immediate danger to a child. A passer-by makes a reasonable rescue attempt and is physically injured in the process. The rescue is not recklessly disproportionate.\n\nWhich is the best general approach to duty and remoteness?","options":{"A":"A rescuer can never recover because intervention always breaks causation.","B":"Every rescue, however reckless, is automatically reasonable.","C":"A reasonable rescue response to danger created by the defendant can fall within the scope of foreseeable consequences, so rescuer status does not by itself defeat recovery.","D":"The defendant owes duties only to the person initially endangered.","E":"Rescue cases are governed solely by contract law."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The law generally treats reasonable rescue as a foreseeable response to danger created by negligence. A reasonable rescuer is not automatically a novus actus, although the circumstances and reasonableness of the intervention remain relevant.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “A rescuer can never recover because intervention always breaks causation” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Reasonable rescue can be within the foreseeable scope of risk created by negligence.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “Every rescue, however reckless, is automatically reasonable” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Reasonable rescue can be within the foreseeable scope of risk created by negligence.","C":"Correct. The law generally treats reasonable rescue as a foreseeable response to danger created by negligence. A reasonable rescuer is not automatically a novus actus, although the circumstances and reasonableness of the intervention remain relevant.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “The defendant owes duties only to the person initially endangered” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Reasonable rescue can be within the foreseeable scope of risk created by negligence.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “Rescue cases are governed solely by contract law” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Reasonable rescue can be within the foreseeable scope of risk created by negligence."},"primary_legal_authority":"Haynes v Harwood [1935] 1 KB 146; Baker v T E Hopkins & Son Ltd [1959] 1 WLR 966.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Reasonable rescue can be within the foreseeable scope of risk created by negligence.","common_trap":"Assuming voluntary intervention necessarily breaks the chain of causation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6311119a5a668ba8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0624","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Land — restrictive covenant: modification or discharge","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A restrictive covenant substantially impedes a reasonable proposed use of land. The owner cannot obtain a consensual release from the beneficiary.\n\nWhich statutory route may be considered?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","C":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","D":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding.","E":"An application to the Upper Tribunal to discharge or modify the restrictive covenant on one or more statutory grounds."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Restrictive covenants may in appropriate cases be modified or discharged through the statutory tribunal jurisdiction.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Correct. Restrictive covenants may in appropriate cases be modified or discharged through the statutory tribunal jurisdiction."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, s 84","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Restrictive covenants may in appropriate cases be modified or discharged through the statutory tribunal jurisdiction.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"619cb8764519cac9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0625","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Defamation — single publication rule","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An online article remains continuously accessible for several years without material republication. The claimant seeks to treat each individual download as restarting limitation.\n\nWhich statutory rule is most relevant?","options":{"A":"The single-publication rule can prevent substantially the same publication by the same publisher from generating a fresh cause of action and limitation period on each access, subject to the statutory test.","B":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","C":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","D":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","E":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Defamation legislation limits repeated limitation accrual for substantially the same publication.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Defamation legislation limits repeated limitation accrual for substantially the same publication.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Defamation Act 2013, s 8","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Defamation legislation limits repeated limitation accrual for substantially the same publication.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a64c4785d14df259"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0626","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Statutory interpretation — ejusdem generis","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A statute regulates 'cars, vans, lorries and other vehicles' in a context suggesting the listed words form a class of road vehicles. A dispute concerns whether 'other vehicles' includes an aircraft.\n\nWhich interpretive principle may assist?","options":{"A":"Ejusdem generis, under which general words following specific items may be construed as limited to the same class where the context supports that reading.","B":"The literal rule requires 'other vehicles' always to include every object capable of movement.","C":"Expressio unius necessarily invalidates the statute.","D":"Judicial precedent prevents courts interpreting lists.","E":"The Human Rights Act automatically determines the meaning."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Ejusdem generis is a contextual linguistic principle under which general words following a list of specific words may take colour from the genus formed by that list.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Ejusdem generis is a contextual linguistic principle under which general words following a list of specific words may take colour from the genus formed by that list.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “The literal rule requires 'other vehicles' always to include every object capable of movement” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. General residual words can be confined by the class indicated by preceding specific words where the statutory context supports it.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “Expressio unius necessarily invalidates the statute” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. General residual words can be confined by the class indicated by preceding specific words where the statutory context supports it.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “Judicial precedent prevents courts interpreting lists” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. General residual words can be confined by the class indicated by preceding specific words where the statutory context supports it.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “The Human Rights Act automatically determines the meaning” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. General residual words can be confined by the class indicated by preceding specific words where the statutory context supports it."},"primary_legal_authority":"Statutory interpretation principles; ejusdem generis canon.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"General residual words can be confined by the class indicated by preceding specific words where the statutory context supports it.","common_trap":"Treating a canon of construction as either mechanically decisive or irrelevant to contextual interpretation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2a86eab3b40409b6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0627","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — procedural fairness and bias","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A licensing appeal is determined by a panel member who has a close financial interest in the success of one party. The losing party challenges the decision.\n\nWhich public-law concern is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Parliamentary sovereignty.","B":"The rule against bias and the requirement of procedural fairness.","C":"The doctrine of frustration.","D":"Vicarious liability.","E":"The rule against perpetuities."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Public decision-making must comply with procedural fairness, including rules against actual or apparent bias. The modern apparent-bias question asks whether the fair-minded and informed observer would conclude there was a real possibility of bias.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “Parliamentary sovereignty” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A decision-maker's disqualifying interest or apparent partiality can render the process unlawful.","B":"Correct. Public decision-making must comply with procedural fairness, including rules against actual or apparent bias. The modern apparent-bias question asks whether the fair-minded and informed observer would conclude there was a real possibility of bias.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “The doctrine of frustration” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A decision-maker's disqualifying interest or apparent partiality can render the process unlawful.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “Vicarious liability” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A decision-maker's disqualifying interest or apparent partiality can render the process unlawful.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “The rule against perpetuities” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A decision-maker's disqualifying interest or apparent partiality can render the process unlawful."},"primary_legal_authority":"Porter v Magill [2001] UKHL 67; common-law procedural fairness.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A decision-maker's disqualifying interest or apparent partiality can render the process unlawful.","common_trap":"Treating a procedurally tainted decision as immune merely because the decision-maker possessed substantive jurisdiction.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1f4fb4d4f9146b16"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0628","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Constitutional law — prerogative and statute","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A statute comprehensively regulates a field previously governed by a prerogative power and provides the legal machinery for exercising the relevant governmental authority. The executive seeks to bypass the statute and rely on the older prerogative instead.\n\nWhich constitutional principle is most relevant?","options":{"A":"Prerogative power always overrides inconsistent Acts of Parliament.","B":"The executive may suspend statutes whenever prerogative power existed historically.","C":"Where statute has occupied the field, the executive cannot ordinarily use prerogative power to frustrate or bypass the statutory scheme.","D":"Statutes cannot affect prerogative powers.","E":"Courts have no role in identifying the legal limits of prerogative power."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Under the De Keyser principle and related authorities, statutory regulation can displace or place prerogative power in abeyance in the regulated field; the executive cannot use prerogative to circumvent Parliament's statutory scheme.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “Prerogative power always overrides inconsistent Acts of Parliament” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Executive prerogative is subordinate to statute where Parliament has legislated inconsistently with its continued use.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “The executive may suspend statutes whenever prerogative power existed historically” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Executive prerogative is subordinate to statute where Parliament has legislated inconsistently with its continued use.","C":"Correct. Under the De Keyser principle and related authorities, statutory regulation can displace or place prerogative power in abeyance in the regulated field; the executive cannot use prerogative to circumvent Parliament's statutory scheme.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “Statutes cannot affect prerogative powers” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Executive prerogative is subordinate to statute where Parliament has legislated inconsistently with its continued use.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “Courts have no role in identifying the legal limits of prerogative power” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Executive prerogative is subordinate to statute where Parliament has legislated inconsistently with its continued use."},"primary_legal_authority":"Attorney General v De Keyser's Royal Hotel Ltd [1920] AC 508; R (Miller) v Secretary of State for Exiting the EU [2017] UKSC 5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Executive prerogative is subordinate to statute where Parliament has legislated inconsistently with its continued use.","common_trap":"Treating historical executive power as superior to legislation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5bda945d4641831a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0629","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — alteration after execution","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"After validly executing a will, a testator writes a new beneficiary's name into a dispositive clause. The alteration is not executed with the formalities required for a will or otherwise validated. The original wording remains apparent.\n\nWhat is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Any handwritten alteration after execution is automatically valid.","B":"An alteration never requires witnesses if the original will was witnessed.","C":"Every ineffective alteration revokes the entire will.","D":"A post-execution alteration is ineffective unless executed with the required formalities, subject to statutory rules; the original apparent wording may continue to operate.","E":"The new beneficiary takes because testamentary intention alone cures formal defects."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 21 addresses alterations made after execution. An alteration is generally ineffective unless executed in the manner required for a will, with statutory treatment depending also on whether the original words remain apparent.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “Any handwritten alteration after execution is automatically valid” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Post-execution amendments ordinarily require testamentary formalities; intention alone is insufficient.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “An alteration never requires witnesses if the original will was witnessed” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Post-execution amendments ordinarily require testamentary formalities; intention alone is insufficient.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “Every ineffective alteration revokes the entire will” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Post-execution amendments ordinarily require testamentary formalities; intention alone is insufficient.","D":"Correct. Section 21 addresses alterations made after execution. An alteration is generally ineffective unless executed in the manner required for a will, with statutory treatment depending also on whether the original words remain apparent.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “The new beneficiary takes because testamentary intention alone cures formal defects” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Post-execution amendments ordinarily require testamentary formalities; intention alone is insufficient."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837, s 21.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Post-execution amendments ordinarily require testamentary formalities; intention alone is insufficient.","common_trap":"Assuming handwriting or clear intention substitutes for statutory execution requirements.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"63686116f5dcbfd9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0630","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Administration — payment of debts","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solvent estate contains sufficient assets to pay all liabilities. Before distributing legacies, the personal representatives ask whether they may simply ignore an undisputed unsecured debt because beneficiaries want immediate payment.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Beneficiaries' wishes always take priority over creditors.","B":"All unsecured debts disappear automatically on death.","C":"Legacies must always be paid before estate liabilities.","D":"Personal representatives are never responsible for proper payment of estate debts.","E":"Personal representatives must administer the estate by paying funeral, testamentary and administration expenses and enforceable debts before distributing the net estate to beneficiaries."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Personal representatives collect and administer estate assets, discharge proper liabilities and only then distribute the remaining estate according to the will or intestacy rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “Beneficiaries' wishes always take priority over creditors” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Beneficiary entitlements are to the net estate after proper administration and liabilities.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “All unsecured debts disappear automatically on death” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Beneficiary entitlements are to the net estate after proper administration and liabilities.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “Legacies must always be paid before estate liabilities” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Beneficiary entitlements are to the net estate after proper administration and liabilities.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “Personal representatives are never responsible for proper payment of estate debts” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Beneficiary entitlements are to the net estate after proper administration and liabilities.","E":"Correct. Personal representatives collect and administer estate assets, discharge proper liabilities and only then distribute the remaining estate according to the will or intestacy rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925; estate-administration principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Beneficiary entitlements are to the net estate after proper administration and liabilities.","common_trap":"Treating testamentary gifts as having priority over enforceable estate debts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"355a2cb271de3eea"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0631","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Inheritance tax — annual exemption","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An individual makes lifetime gifts that are potentially relevant to inheritance tax and asks whether a limited annual statutory exemption may reduce the value transferred.\n\nWhich is the best general statement?","options":{"A":"The IHT regime provides an annual exemption for qualifying lifetime transfers, subject to the statutory amount and carry-forward rules applicable at the relevant time.","B":"There is no annual exemption for lifetime transfers.","C":"The annual exemption eliminates IHT on gifts of any amount.","D":"Unused annual exemption can be accumulated indefinitely.","E":"The exemption applies only to transfers made on death."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"IHT legislation provides an annual exemption for qualifying lifetime transfers, with limited carry-forward of unused exemption to the immediately following year subject to the statutory rules. SQE numerical questions should use figures supplied where required.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. IHT legislation provides an annual exemption for qualifying lifetime transfers, with limited carry-forward of unused exemption to the immediately following year subject to the statutory rules. SQE numerical questions should use figures supplied where required.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “There is no annual exemption for lifetime transfers” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A limited statutory annual exemption can shelter part of qualifying lifetime gifts.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “The annual exemption eliminates IHT on gifts of any amount” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A limited statutory annual exemption can shelter part of qualifying lifetime gifts.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “Unused annual exemption can be accumulated indefinitely” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A limited statutory annual exemption can shelter part of qualifying lifetime gifts.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “The exemption applies only to transfers made on death” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A limited statutory annual exemption can shelter part of qualifying lifetime gifts."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984, s 19.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A limited statutory annual exemption can shelter part of qualifying lifetime gifts.","common_trap":"Confusing a capped exemption with unlimited lifetime gifting or unlimited accumulation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"043c1b5f499a56ea"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0632","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Client money — payment of firm's costs","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm holds client money for a concluded transaction and has delivered a bill of costs to the client. The firm wants to transfer the billed amount from client account to business account.\n\nWhich is the best general approach?","options":{"A":"A firm may take any amount from client account for costs without notifying the client.","B":"The firm may transfer money held for the client to pay the firm's costs only in accordance with the Accounts Rules, including giving a bill or other written notification of costs before transfer.","C":"Client money can never be used to pay the firm's properly billed costs.","D":"The whole client balance becomes business money when a bill is issued.","E":"Costs may be transferred before they are calculated."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Accounts Rules permit transfer of client money for the firm's costs where the amount has been properly determined and the client has first been given a bill or other written notification, subject to the rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “A firm may take any amount from client account for costs without notifying the client” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A bill does not automatically convert all client funds into office money; only the properly notified amount may be transferred in accordance with the rules.","B":"Correct. The Accounts Rules permit transfer of client money for the firm's costs where the amount has been properly determined and the client has first been given a bill or other written notification, subject to the rules.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “Client money can never be used to pay the firm's properly billed costs” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A bill does not automatically convert all client funds into office money; only the properly notified amount may be transferred in accordance with the rules.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “The whole client balance becomes business money when a bill is issued” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A bill does not automatically convert all client funds into office money; only the properly notified amount may be transferred in accordance with the rules.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “Costs may be transferred before they are calculated” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A bill does not automatically convert all client funds into office money; only the properly notified amount may be transferred in accordance with the rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, rr 4.3 and 5.1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A bill does not automatically convert all client funds into office money; only the properly notified amount may be transferred in accordance with the rules.","common_trap":"Treating client account as a source from which unnotified or unquantified fees may be taken.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"78dafa658fd32dc8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0633","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustees — duty of care","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A paid professional trustee exercises an investment function to which the statutory duty of care applies. The trustee argues that only the standard expected of an ordinary layperson is relevant.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"All trustees are judged by an identical minimal lay standard.","B":"Professional expertise is irrelevant to trustee care.","C":"The statutory duty considers the care and skill reasonable in the circumstances, including any special knowledge or experience the trustee has or holds themself out as having and, for a professional trustee, what is reasonably expected of that kind of professional.","D":"The statutory duty imposes strict liability for every investment loss.","E":"Trustees owe no duty of care when investing."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Section 1 of the Trustee Act 2000 sets a contextual standard that expressly takes account of special knowledge or experience and the additional expectations applicable where a trustee acts in the course of a business or profession.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “All trustees are judged by an identical minimal lay standard” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A professional trustee's claimed and expected expertise informs the statutory standard of care.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “Professional expertise is irrelevant to trustee care” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A professional trustee's claimed and expected expertise informs the statutory standard of care.","C":"Correct. Section 1 of the Trustee Act 2000 sets a contextual standard that expressly takes account of special knowledge or experience and the additional expectations applicable where a trustee acts in the course of a business or profession.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “The statutory duty imposes strict liability for every investment loss” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A professional trustee's claimed and expected expertise informs the statutory standard of care.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “Trustees owe no duty of care when investing” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A professional trustee's claimed and expected expertise informs the statutory standard of care."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 2000, s 1 and Sch 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A professional trustee's claimed and expected expertise informs the statutory standard of care.","common_trap":"Reducing a contextual statutory standard to the lowest common lay standard or treating it as strict liability.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"10d48b0c877e1b8c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0634","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Resulting trusts — surplus after purpose fulfilled","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Money is transferred for a specifically limited purpose on terms that do not give the recipient beneficial ownership of any surplus. The purpose is fully carried out, but part of the fund remains.\n\nWhat equitable analysis may apply to the surplus?","options":{"A":"A resulting trust may arise in respect of the surplus if, on proper construction, beneficial ownership of the unused money was not intended to pass to the recipient.","B":"The recipient necessarily takes every surplus beneficially because legal title was transferred.","C":"Any surplus automatically becomes bona vacantia.","D":"The surplus must always be divided equally between all persons involved.","E":"A resulting trust is impossible once part of the stated purpose has been performed."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Where the arrangement shows that beneficial ownership was limited to carrying out a defined purpose, unused money may result back rather than belong beneficially to the recipient.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Where the arrangement shows that beneficial ownership was limited to carrying out a defined purpose, unused money may result back rather than belong beneficially to the recipient.","B":"Incorrect. This gives the wrong legal consequence for the facts and does not satisfy the governing test. The issue is the beneficial ownership of the surplus after the purpose, not merely whether an express trust initially failed.","C":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. The issue is the beneficial ownership of the surplus after the purpose, not merely whether an express trust initially failed.","D":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. The issue is the beneficial ownership of the surplus after the purpose, not merely whether an express trust initially failed.","E":"Incorrect. This gives the wrong legal consequence for the facts and does not satisfy the governing test. The issue is the beneficial ownership of the surplus after the purpose, not merely whether an express trust initially failed."},"primary_legal_authority":"Barclays Bank Ltd v Quistclose Investments Ltd [1970] AC 567; resulting-trust principles","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The issue is the beneficial ownership of the surplus after the purpose, not merely whether an express trust initially failed.","common_trap":"Applying the headline doctrine without identifying the distinct consequence, exception or procedural stage tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"cd362253fb95ef71"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0635","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Proprietary estoppel — assurance, reliance and detriment","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A landowner clearly assures a neighbour that the neighbour will receive a strip of land. In reasonable reliance, the neighbour spends substantial money building access works. The landowner later seeks to deny any right.\n\nWhich equitable doctrine is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Promissory estoppel can only extinguish freehold title automatically.","B":"Adverse possession arises immediately from expenditure.","C":"A resulting trust necessarily arises from every assurance about land.","D":"The neighbour acquires legal title solely because money was spent.","E":"Proprietary estoppel, requiring an assurance or expectation relating to property, reasonable reliance and detriment such that it would be unconscionable to resile, with remedy tailored by the court."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Proprietary estoppel can arise from a sufficiently clear property-related assurance, reliance and detriment in circumstances making departure unconscionable. The remedy is discretionary and seeks to address the equity rather than automatically transferring the promised property.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “Promissory estoppel can only extinguish freehold title automatically” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Property assurances coupled with detrimental reliance can generate an equity even where formal transfer requirements were not satisfied.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “Adverse possession arises immediately from expenditure” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Property assurances coupled with detrimental reliance can generate an equity even where formal transfer requirements were not satisfied.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “A resulting trust necessarily arises from every assurance about land” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Property assurances coupled with detrimental reliance can generate an equity even where formal transfer requirements were not satisfied.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “The neighbour acquires legal title solely because money was spent” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Property assurances coupled with detrimental reliance can generate an equity even where formal transfer requirements were not satisfied.","E":"Correct. Proprietary estoppel can arise from a sufficiently clear property-related assurance, reliance and detriment in circumstances making departure unconscionable. The remedy is discretionary and seeks to address the equity rather than automatically transferring the promised property."},"primary_legal_authority":"Thorner v Major [2009] UKHL 18; Guest v Guest [2022] UKSC 27.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Property assurances coupled with detrimental reliance can generate an equity even where formal transfer requirements were not satisfied.","common_trap":"Assuming the remedy is automatically the full promised property or confusing estoppel with adverse possession.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b7f35013ea3905b5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0636","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Mortgages — undue influence and lender notice","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A wife guarantees her husband's business debts and charges her interest in the family home. The transaction is not to her financial advantage and the bank is put on inquiry. The bank takes no reasonable steps to ensure she receives independent advice.\n\nWhich doctrine is most directly relevant to the bank's ability to enforce against her interest?","options":{"A":"The undue-influence principles governing when a lender is put on inquiry and must take reasonable steps to avoid being fixed with notice of the wrongdoing.","B":"A mortgage is always enforceable if signed, regardless of undue influence.","C":"Only fraud by the bank itself can affect enforcement.","D":"Independent advice is legally irrelevant in surety transactions.","E":"The doctrine of frustration."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Etridge explains when a lender is put on inquiry in non-commercial surety transactions and the reasonable steps ordinarily required to avoid constructive notice of undue influence by the principal debtor.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Etridge explains when a lender is put on inquiry in non-commercial surety transactions and the reasonable steps ordinarily required to avoid constructive notice of undue influence by the principal debtor.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “A mortgage is always enforceable if signed, regardless of undue influence” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A lender alerted to the risk of undue influence must follow the protective steps identified by authority if it is to enforce safely against the surety's interest.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “Only fraud by the bank itself can affect enforcement” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A lender alerted to the risk of undue influence must follow the protective steps identified by authority if it is to enforce safely against the surety's interest.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “Independent advice is legally irrelevant in surety transactions” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A lender alerted to the risk of undue influence must follow the protective steps identified by authority if it is to enforce safely against the surety's interest.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “The doctrine of frustration” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. A lender alerted to the risk of undue influence must follow the protective steps identified by authority if it is to enforce safely against the surety's interest."},"primary_legal_authority":"Royal Bank of Scotland plc v Etridge (No 2) [2001] UKHL 44.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A lender alerted to the risk of undue influence must follow the protective steps identified by authority if it is to enforce safely against the surety's interest.","common_trap":"Treating signature alone as conclusive despite equitable vitiating factors affecting a surety transaction.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8a0429202e47c73d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0637","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Registered title — restrictions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Official copies show a restriction in the proprietorship register requiring a specified consent before a disposition may be registered. The seller proposes to transfer the property without satisfying the restriction.\n\nWhat is the principal conveyancing significance?","options":{"A":"A restriction automatically proves that the seller has no legal estate.","B":"The restriction regulates registration of dispositions, so the buyer's solicitor must ensure its requirements are satisfied or otherwise properly addressed for registration.","C":"Restrictions can be ignored on a transfer for value.","D":"A restriction is identical to a registered charge and must always be redeemed with money.","E":"HM Land Registry must register the transfer despite non-compliance."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A restriction is an entry regulating the circumstances in which a disposition may be registered. Conveyancers must identify and satisfy its terms or obtain appropriate cancellation, withdrawal or other resolution.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “A restriction automatically proves that the seller has no legal estate” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Restrictions are registration controls and can block completion of the buyer's registration if not properly dealt with.","B":"Correct. A restriction is an entry regulating the circumstances in which a disposition may be registered. Conveyancers must identify and satisfy its terms or obtain appropriate cancellation, withdrawal or other resolution.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “Restrictions can be ignored on a transfer for value” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Restrictions are registration controls and can block completion of the buyer's registration if not properly dealt with.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “A restriction is identical to a registered charge and must always be redeemed with money” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Restrictions are registration controls and can block completion of the buyer's registration if not properly dealt with.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “HM Land Registry must register the transfer despite non-compliance” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Restrictions are registration controls and can block completion of the buyer's registration if not properly dealt with."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002, ss 40–47; Land Registration Rules 2003.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Restrictions are registration controls and can block completion of the buyer's registration if not properly dealt with.","common_trap":"Confusing a restriction with substantive ownership or with a charge securing repayment.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b49026a9f3a00a94"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0638","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Completion — vacant possession","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A sale contract requires the seller to give vacant possession on completion. On completion day, the seller leaves a substantial quantity of furniture and building materials that materially prevent the buyer from using a significant part of the property.\n\nWhich is the best general analysis?","options":{"A":"Vacant possession concerns only whether human occupiers remain.","B":"Any object left behind, however trivial, automatically prevents vacant possession.","C":"The seller may be in breach of the obligation to give vacant possession because substantial physical impediments can prevent the buyer enjoying immediate and exclusive possession.","D":"Vacant possession is irrelevant if legal title can be transferred.","E":"The buyer must accept substantial obstructions whenever the property is sold freehold."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Vacant possession requires the purchaser to be able to enjoy immediate, exclusive and substantially unimpeded possession. Significant chattels or rubbish can breach the obligation where they materially interfere with enjoyment of a substantial part.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “Vacant possession concerns only whether human occupiers remain” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Vacant possession can be defeated by substantial physical impediments as well as continuing occupiers or adverse legal claims.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “Any object left behind, however trivial, automatically prevents vacant possession” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Vacant possession can be defeated by substantial physical impediments as well as continuing occupiers or adverse legal claims.","C":"Correct. Vacant possession requires the purchaser to be able to enjoy immediate, exclusive and substantially unimpeded possession. Significant chattels or rubbish can breach the obligation where they materially interfere with enjoyment of a substantial part.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “Vacant possession is irrelevant if legal title can be transferred” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Vacant possession can be defeated by substantial physical impediments as well as continuing occupiers or adverse legal claims.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “The buyer must accept substantial obstructions whenever the property is sold freehold” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Vacant possession can be defeated by substantial physical impediments as well as continuing occupiers or adverse legal claims."},"primary_legal_authority":"Cumberland Consolidated Holdings Ltd v Ireland [1946] KB 264; vacant-possession principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Vacant possession can be defeated by substantial physical impediments as well as continuing occupiers or adverse legal claims.","common_trap":"Reducing vacant possession solely to absence of people or, conversely, treating trivial remnants as necessarily fatal.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"360f04e2e9e381bd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0639","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Secondary liability — intentional assistance or encouragement","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A knows that P plans a robbery. Wanting the robbery to succeed, A deliberately supplies P with a getaway vehicle for use in the offence. P commits the robbery as planned.\n\nWhich is the best general basis for A's secondary liability?","options":{"A":"A can be liable only if physically present at the robbery.","B":"Mere association with P is always enough for conviction.","C":"Secondary liability requires A personally to satisfy the actus reus of robbery as principal.","D":"A intentionally assisted or encouraged P's offence with the requisite mental element concerning the essential matters of that offence.","E":"Supplying assistance before an offence can never create accessory liability."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"An accessory may be liable where they intentionally assist or encourage the principal offence and possess the required intent as to the assistance and the essential matters constituting the offence. Presence at the scene is not required.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “A can be liable only if physically present at the robbery” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Deliberate practical assistance supplied with the requisite knowledge and intent can found secondary liability.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “Mere association with P is always enough for conviction” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Deliberate practical assistance supplied with the requisite knowledge and intent can found secondary liability.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “Secondary liability requires A personally to satisfy the actus reus of robbery as principal” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Deliberate practical assistance supplied with the requisite knowledge and intent can found secondary liability.","D":"Correct. An accessory may be liable where they intentionally assist or encourage the principal offence and possess the required intent as to the assistance and the essential matters constituting the offence. Presence at the scene is not required.","E":"Incorrect. The proposition “Supplying assistance before an offence can never create accessory liability” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Deliberate practical assistance supplied with the requisite knowledge and intent can found secondary liability."},"primary_legal_authority":"Accessories and Abettors Act 1861, s 8; R v Jogee [2016] UKSC 8.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Deliberate practical assistance supplied with the requisite knowledge and intent can found secondary liability.","common_trap":"Confusing accessory liability with principal liability or treating mere association as sufficient.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f560992646c9112d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0640","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Bail — failure to surrender","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant released on bail deliberately fails without reasonable cause to surrender to custody at the time and place appointed by the court.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Failure to surrender has no legal consequence if the original charge remains unproved.","B":"The court must automatically acquit the defendant of the original offence.","C":"Only breach of a curfew, never failure to attend court, can be a bail offence.","D":"The prosecution must first prove the original charge before failure to surrender can be considered.","E":"Failure without reasonable cause to surrender to custody as required can itself constitute an offence under the Bail Act 1976."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The Bail Act creates offences relating to failure without reasonable cause to surrender to custody and failure to surrender at the appointed time, subject to the statutory provisions and available defence concerning reasonable cause.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The proposition “Failure to surrender has no legal consequence if the original charge remains unproved” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Bail imposes a legal obligation to surrender, breach of which can generate separate criminal liability.","B":"Incorrect. The proposition “The court must automatically acquit the defendant of the original offence” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Bail imposes a legal obligation to surrender, breach of which can generate separate criminal liability.","C":"Incorrect. The proposition “Only breach of a curfew, never failure to attend court, can be a bail offence” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Bail imposes a legal obligation to surrender, breach of which can generate separate criminal liability.","D":"Incorrect. The proposition “The prosecution must first prove the original charge before failure to surrender can be considered” does not fit the governing rule on these facts. Bail imposes a legal obligation to surrender, breach of which can generate separate criminal liability.","E":"Correct. The Bail Act creates offences relating to failure without reasonable cause to surrender to custody and failure to surrender at the appointed time, subject to the statutory provisions and available defence concerning reasonable cause."},"primary_legal_authority":"Bail Act 1976, s 6.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Bail imposes a legal obligation to surrender, breach of which can generate separate criminal liability.","common_trap":"Assuming bail obligations are merely administrative and depend on conviction of the underlying charge.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4fb581e151e7677e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0641","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — shareholder resolutions and special notice","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A shareholder wants the company to remove a director at a general meeting. The proposed resolution itself requires only an ordinary majority, but the shareholder gives the company only two days' notice of the intention to move it.\n\nWhich additional procedural requirement is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"A resolution to remove a director requires special notice under the Companies Act, notwithstanding that the resolution itself is an ordinary resolution.","B":"Because it is an ordinary resolution, no advance notice beyond the meeting notice can ever be required.","C":"Removal requires a written resolution rather than a meeting.","D":"Only the director can give notice of the removal resolution.","E":"Special notice converts the resolution into a special resolution requiring 75%."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Section 168 permits removal by ordinary resolution, but section 312 requires special notice of a resolution to remove a director. Special notice concerns advance procedure; it does not change the voting threshold into a special resolution.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Section 168 permits removal by ordinary resolution, but section 312 requires special notice of a resolution to remove a director. Special notice concerns advance procedure; it does not change the voting threshold into a special resolution.","B":"Incorrect. “Because it is an ordinary resolution, no advance notice beyond the meeting notice can ever be required” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Certain ordinary resolutions, including director removal, carry a separate statutory special-notice requirement.","C":"Incorrect. “Removal requires a written resolution rather than a meeting” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Certain ordinary resolutions, including director removal, carry a separate statutory special-notice requirement.","D":"Incorrect. “Only the director can give notice of the removal resolution” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Certain ordinary resolutions, including director removal, carry a separate statutory special-notice requirement.","E":"Incorrect. “Special notice converts the resolution into a special resolution requiring 75%” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Certain ordinary resolutions, including director removal, carry a separate statutory special-notice requirement."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 168, 169 and 312.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Certain ordinary resolutions, including director removal, carry a separate statutory special-notice requirement.","common_trap":"Equating the voting threshold for an ordinary resolution with the separate rules governing advance notice.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b9a6e22caa00f452"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0642","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — directors' service contracts","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company agrees a guaranteed director's service contract under which the director cannot be terminated by the company within three years except for breach. No relevant exception applies.\n\nWhich corporate approval issue should be considered?","options":{"A":"Any director's service contract longer than six months is prohibited.","B":"Member approval is ordinarily required for a director's service contract containing a guaranteed term exceeding two years.","C":"Board approval alone always validates any guaranteed term.","D":"Member approval is required only after the director has completed the guaranteed term.","E":"The rule applies only to contracts with non-director employees."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Companies Act requires member approval for a director's service contract that guarantees employment for a period exceeding two years, subject to the statutory provisions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Any director's service contract longer than six months is prohibited” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. A long guaranteed director service term can trigger a specific shareholder-approval requirement.","B":"Correct. The Companies Act requires member approval for a director's service contract that guarantees employment for a period exceeding two years, subject to the statutory provisions.","C":"Incorrect. “Board approval alone always validates any guaranteed term” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. A long guaranteed director service term can trigger a specific shareholder-approval requirement.","D":"Incorrect. “Member approval is required only after the director has completed the guaranteed term” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. A long guaranteed director service term can trigger a specific shareholder-approval requirement.","E":"Incorrect. “The rule applies only to contracts with non-director employees” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. A long guaranteed director service term can trigger a specific shareholder-approval requirement."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 188–189.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A long guaranteed director service term can trigger a specific shareholder-approval requirement.","common_trap":"Confusing ordinary employment contracting authority with the statutory control of long guaranteed director terms.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"45e48d10875d9da7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0643","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Partnership — expulsion","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Three partners carry on business under a partnership agreement that contains no power to expel a partner. Two partners vote to expel the third because relations have deteriorated.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"A simple majority always has an implied statutory power to expel a partner.","B":"Any partner may expel another unilaterally.","C":"A majority cannot expel a partner unless a power to do so has been conferred by express agreement.","D":"Expulsion is automatic whenever partners disagree.","E":"The court must treat every majority vote as an effective expulsion."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The default rule is that no majority of partners can expel a partner unless a power to do so has been conferred by express agreement between the partners.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “A simple majority always has an implied statutory power to expel a partner” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Expulsion from an ordinary partnership requires an agreed power rather than a free-standing majority right.","B":"Incorrect. “Any partner may expel another unilaterally” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Expulsion from an ordinary partnership requires an agreed power rather than a free-standing majority right.","C":"Correct. The default rule is that no majority of partners can expel a partner unless a power to do so has been conferred by express agreement between the partners.","D":"Incorrect. “Expulsion is automatic whenever partners disagree” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Expulsion from an ordinary partnership requires an agreed power rather than a free-standing majority right.","E":"Incorrect. “The court must treat every majority vote as an effective expulsion” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Expulsion from an ordinary partnership requires an agreed power rather than a free-standing majority right."},"primary_legal_authority":"Partnership Act 1890, s 25.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Expulsion from an ordinary partnership requires an agreed power rather than a free-standing majority right.","common_trap":"Assuming majority management power includes an implied power to remove a co-owner of the partnership business.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5f347d2e8dfdb3a7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0644","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — liquidation priority and floating charge","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An insolvent company enters liquidation. Its assets include property subject to a floating charge created after the statutory commencement date for the prescribed-part regime. There are unsecured creditors and the statutory conditions for a prescribed part are met.\n\nWhich is the best general statement?","options":{"A":"A floating-charge holder necessarily takes every penny of floating-charge realisations before unsecured creditors can receive anything.","B":"The prescribed part is paid only to shareholders.","C":"The regime applies only to fixed-charge assets.","D":"A prescribed part of qualifying net property may be set aside for unsecured creditors before the floating-charge holder takes the balance to which it would otherwise be entitled.","E":"A prescribed part automatically converts unsecured creditors into secured creditors."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 176A requires a prescribed part of qualifying net property subject to floating charges to be made available for unsecured debts, subject to statutory limits and exceptions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “A floating-charge holder necessarily takes every penny of floating-charge realisations before unsecured creditors can receive anything” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. The prescribed-part regime carves out part of qualifying floating-charge realisations for unsecured creditors.","B":"Incorrect. “The prescribed part is paid only to shareholders” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. The prescribed-part regime carves out part of qualifying floating-charge realisations for unsecured creditors.","C":"Incorrect. “The regime applies only to fixed-charge assets” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. The prescribed-part regime carves out part of qualifying floating-charge realisations for unsecured creditors.","D":"Correct. Section 176A requires a prescribed part of qualifying net property subject to floating charges to be made available for unsecured debts, subject to statutory limits and exceptions.","E":"Incorrect. “A prescribed part automatically converts unsecured creditors into secured creditors” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. The prescribed-part regime carves out part of qualifying floating-charge realisations for unsecured creditors."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, s 176A; Insolvency Act 1986 (Prescribed Part) Order 2003, as amended.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The prescribed-part regime carves out part of qualifying floating-charge realisations for unsecured creditors.","common_trap":"Treating floating-charge priority as absolute without considering the statutory unsecured-creditor carve-out.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"44978f8a5640ab46"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0645","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Allocation — tracks","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defended County Court claim has completed the directions questionnaire stage. The court is considering the appropriate procedural track by reference to value, complexity, remedy and other relevant features.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The claimant has an absolute right to choose the track.","B":"Every claim below a monetary threshold must be allocated solely by value regardless of complexity.","C":"Track allocation occurs only after trial.","D":"The defendant alone selects the procedural track.","E":"The court allocates the claim to the appropriate track using the CPR allocation criteria; value is important but is not the sole consideration."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"CPR Part 26 governs allocation. The court considers the applicable track scope and factors including financial value, nature of remedy, complexity, number of parties and other circumstances.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “The claimant has an absolute right to choose the track” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Track allocation is a judicial case-management decision based on the CPR criteria rather than party choice alone.","B":"Incorrect. “Every claim below a monetary threshold must be allocated solely by value regardless of complexity” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Track allocation is a judicial case-management decision based on the CPR criteria rather than party choice alone.","C":"Incorrect. “Track allocation occurs only after trial” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Track allocation is a judicial case-management decision based on the CPR criteria rather than party choice alone.","D":"Incorrect. “The defendant alone selects the procedural track” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Track allocation is a judicial case-management decision based on the CPR criteria rather than party choice alone.","E":"Correct. CPR Part 26 governs allocation. The court considers the applicable track scope and factors including financial value, nature of remedy, complexity, number of parties and other circumstances."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 26.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Track allocation is a judicial case-management decision based on the CPR criteria rather than party choice alone.","common_trap":"Treating monetary value as the only possible determinant of allocation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"684b979821e13a76"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0646","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Interim payments","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A personal-injury claimant has an urgent financial need while proceedings continue. Liability has effectively been admitted, but quantum remains disputed. The claimant seeks part of the likely damages before final judgment.\n\nWhich procedural mechanism is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"An application for an interim payment under CPR Part 25, if the relevant conditions are satisfied.","B":"A charging order against the claimant.","C":"Default judgment on damages regardless of the unresolved quantum dispute.","D":"A witness summons.","E":"A declaration of incompatibility."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"CPR Part 25 permits interim payments where specified conditions are met, including circumstances involving admissions, judgments for damages to be assessed, or a court being satisfied that the claimant would obtain judgment for a substantial amount.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. CPR Part 25 permits interim payments where specified conditions are met, including circumstances involving admissions, judgments for damages to be assessed, or a court being satisfied that the claimant would obtain judgment for a substantial amount.","B":"Incorrect. “A charging order against the claimant” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Interim payment can provide part of likely monetary recovery before final quantification where the CPR conditions are met.","C":"Incorrect. “Default judgment on damages regardless of the unresolved quantum dispute” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Interim payment can provide part of likely monetary recovery before final quantification where the CPR conditions are met.","D":"Incorrect. “A witness summons” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Interim payment can provide part of likely monetary recovery before final quantification where the CPR conditions are met.","E":"Incorrect. “A declaration of incompatibility” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Interim payment can provide part of likely monetary recovery before final quantification where the CPR conditions are met."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, rr 25.20–25.24.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Interim payment can provide part of likely monetary recovery before final quantification where the CPR conditions are met.","common_trap":"Confusing an advance on likely damages with post-judgment enforcement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"31590eea927bac06"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0647","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Enforcement — third-party debt orders","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A judgment creditor learns that a bank owes money to the judgment debtor in an account. The creditor wants an enforcement method directed at that debt rather than the debtor's physical assets.\n\nWhich remedy is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"A charging order over land is the only available enforcement method.","B":"A third-party debt order under CPR Part 72.","C":"A freezing injunction automatically transfers the bank balance to the creditor.","D":"A Part 36 offer.","E":"An order for specific disclosure."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A third-party debt order can require a third party who owes money to the judgment debtor, commonly a bank, to pay the judgment creditor subject to the procedural rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “A charging order over land is the only available enforcement method” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Third-party debt orders target qualifying debts owed to the judgment debtor by another person.","B":"Correct. A third-party debt order can require a third party who owes money to the judgment debtor, commonly a bank, to pay the judgment creditor subject to the procedural rules.","C":"Incorrect. “A freezing injunction automatically transfers the bank balance to the creditor” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Third-party debt orders target qualifying debts owed to the judgment debtor by another person.","D":"Incorrect. “A Part 36 offer” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Third-party debt orders target qualifying debts owed to the judgment debtor by another person.","E":"Incorrect. “An order for specific disclosure” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Third-party debt orders target qualifying debts owed to the judgment debtor by another person."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 72.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Third-party debt orders target qualifying debts owed to the judgment debtor by another person.","common_trap":"Confusing preservation remedies or security over property with enforcement against a debt in a third party's hands.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"640b7eadbdeccb80"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0648","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Client care — complaints","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm is instructed by a new client. The client asks how complaints about the firm's service will be handled.\n\nWhich is the best general professional position?","options":{"A":"A firm need not tell clients anything about complaints until a dispute arises.","B":"Only the SRA can consider service complaints, so the Legal Ombudsman is irrelevant.","C":"The client should receive information about the firm's complaints procedure and about how and when a complaint may be made to the Legal Ombudsman where applicable.","D":"A firm may prohibit clients from complaining as a term of engagement.","E":"Complaints information is required only for criminal matters."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The SRA Code requires firms and solicitors to provide clients with information about their complaints handling and, where relevant, rights concerning the Legal Ombudsman.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “A firm need not tell clients anything about complaints until a dispute arises” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Client-care information includes accessible routes for raising complaints and external redress where applicable.","B":"Incorrect. “Only the SRA can consider service complaints, so the Legal Ombudsman is irrelevant” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Client-care information includes accessible routes for raising complaints and external redress where applicable.","C":"Correct. The SRA Code requires firms and solicitors to provide clients with information about their complaints handling and, where relevant, rights concerning the Legal Ombudsman.","D":"Incorrect. “A firm may prohibit clients from complaining as a term of engagement” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Client-care information includes accessible routes for raising complaints and external redress where applicable.","E":"Incorrect. “Complaints information is required only for criminal matters” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Client-care information includes accessible routes for raising complaints and external redress where applicable."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, paras 8.2–8.5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Client-care information includes accessible routes for raising complaints and external redress where applicable.","common_trap":"Treating complaints information as optional or available only after dissatisfaction has crystallised.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f7ff74654f6fc9ec"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0649","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Referrals — commissions and client information","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor recommends a financial service provider to a client and will receive a referral commission if the client proceeds. The arrangement is lawful and does not compromise independence.\n\nWhich professional issue remains particularly important?","options":{"A":"The solicitor may secretly retain any commission because referrals are outside professional regulation.","B":"Any commission automatically belongs to the solicitor regardless of client agreement and disclosure.","C":"Receiving any referral benefit is always a criminal offence.","D":"The solicitor must deal transparently with the financial benefit and comply with applicable SRA requirements concerning referrals, commissions and the client's best interests.","E":"The client's informed position is irrelevant if the service provider is reputable."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"SRA requirements demand transparency concerning financial or other interests in referrals and prohibit arrangements that compromise independence or client interests; accounting to the client for commission may also be required unless properly agreed otherwise.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “The solicitor may secretly retain any commission because referrals are outside professional regulation” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Referral benefits require transparency and must not distort independent advice or the client's interests.","B":"Incorrect. “Any commission automatically belongs to the solicitor regardless of client agreement and disclosure” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Referral benefits require transparency and must not distort independent advice or the client's interests.","C":"Incorrect. “Receiving any referral benefit is always a criminal offence” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Referral benefits require transparency and must not distort independent advice or the client's interests.","D":"Correct. SRA requirements demand transparency concerning financial or other interests in referrals and prohibit arrangements that compromise independence or client interests; accounting to the client for commission may also be required unless properly agreed otherwise.","E":"Incorrect. “The client's informed position is irrelevant if the service provider is reputable” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Referral benefits require transparency and must not distort independent advice or the client's interests."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, referral and financial-benefit provisions; SRA Principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Referral benefits require transparency and must not distort independent advice or the client's interests.","common_trap":"Assuming a lawful commercial referral removes professional duties concerning disclosure and financial benefit.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"362843829e794560"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0650","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Offer and acceptance — invitation to treat","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A shop displays a standard consumer product on a shelf with a price label. A customer takes it to the till and says the shop is already contractually bound to sell at the displayed price.\n\nWhich is the best general contract-law analysis?","options":{"A":"Every price display is automatically an offer accepted when the customer sees it.","B":"The display creates a deed.","C":"A contract arises before the customer selects the goods.","D":"Invitation-to-treat analysis applies only to auctions.","E":"A shop display is ordinarily an invitation to treat rather than an offer, so the contractual offer and acceptance must be identified at the point of sale."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Ordinary retail displays are generally invitations to treat, allowing the customer to make an offer to buy which the retailer may accept or reject, subject to consumer and pricing regulation outside the formation issue.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Every price display is automatically an offer accepted when the customer sees it” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. A displayed price does not ordinarily constitute the contractual offer in a self-service retail transaction.","B":"Incorrect. “The display creates a deed” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. A displayed price does not ordinarily constitute the contractual offer in a self-service retail transaction.","C":"Incorrect. “A contract arises before the customer selects the goods” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. A displayed price does not ordinarily constitute the contractual offer in a self-service retail transaction.","D":"Incorrect. “Invitation-to-treat analysis applies only to auctions” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. A displayed price does not ordinarily constitute the contractual offer in a self-service retail transaction.","E":"Correct. Ordinary retail displays are generally invitations to treat, allowing the customer to make an offer to buy which the retailer may accept or reject, subject to consumer and pricing regulation outside the formation issue."},"primary_legal_authority":"Pharmaceutical Society of Great Britain v Boots Cash Chemists [1953] 1 QB 401.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A displayed price does not ordinarily constitute the contractual offer in a self-service retail transaction.","common_trap":"Assuming commercial advertising or display language necessarily demonstrates an intention to be immediately bound.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"13b4e9fac7c5caa5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0651","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Acceptance — communication and conduct","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A supplier sends an offer that does not prescribe an exclusive method of acceptance. The buyer does not expressly reply but, with the supplier's knowledge, begins the requested performance in circumstances objectively indicating assent.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Acceptance may in appropriate circumstances be communicated by conduct objectively demonstrating assent to the offer.","B":"Acceptance can never occur by conduct.","C":"Silence and conduct are legally identical in every case.","D":"Only a signed document can accept a commercial offer.","E":"Performance always amounts to a counter-offer."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Contract formation is assessed objectively. Conduct can amount to acceptance where it objectively communicates assent and satisfies the offer's requirements, even though mere silence ordinarily does not.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Contract formation is assessed objectively. Conduct can amount to acceptance where it objectively communicates assent and satisfies the offer's requirements, even though mere silence ordinarily does not.","B":"Incorrect. “Acceptance can never occur by conduct” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Acceptance can be inferred from unequivocal conduct where the circumstances objectively demonstrate agreement.","C":"Incorrect. “Silence and conduct are legally identical in every case” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Acceptance can be inferred from unequivocal conduct where the circumstances objectively demonstrate agreement.","D":"Incorrect. “Only a signed document can accept a commercial offer” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Acceptance can be inferred from unequivocal conduct where the circumstances objectively demonstrate agreement.","E":"Incorrect. “Performance always amounts to a counter-offer” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Acceptance can be inferred from unequivocal conduct where the circumstances objectively demonstrate agreement."},"primary_legal_authority":"Brogden v Metropolitan Railway Co (1877) 2 App Cas 666.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Acceptance can be inferred from unequivocal conduct where the circumstances objectively demonstrate agreement.","common_trap":"Treating the rule that silence is generally insufficient as a prohibition on acceptance by conduct.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1a649664fce77ac7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0652","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Misrepresentation — rescission bars","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A buyer discovers an actionable misrepresentation but, with full knowledge of the right to rescind, expressly confirms the contract and continues performance on the basis that it will remain binding. The buyer later seeks rescission.\n\nWhich bar to rescission is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Frustration.","B":"Affirmation.","C":"Remoteness.","D":"Contributory negligence.","E":"The postal rule."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Rescission is an equitable remedy and can be barred where, after discovering the truth and the right to rescind, the representee unequivocally elects to affirm the contract.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Frustration” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Knowing affirmation can extinguish the right to rescind for misrepresentation.","B":"Correct. Rescission is an equitable remedy and can be barred where, after discovering the truth and the right to rescind, the representee unequivocally elects to affirm the contract.","C":"Incorrect. “Remoteness” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Knowing affirmation can extinguish the right to rescind for misrepresentation.","D":"Incorrect. “Contributory negligence” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Knowing affirmation can extinguish the right to rescind for misrepresentation.","E":"Incorrect. “The postal rule” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Knowing affirmation can extinguish the right to rescind for misrepresentation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Long v Lloyd [1958] 1 WLR 753; rescission principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Knowing affirmation can extinguish the right to rescind for misrepresentation.","common_trap":"Confusing the substantive cause of action with equitable limits on the remedy of rescission.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0d5c45b68d2d547c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0653","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — omissions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A passer-by sees a stranger in danger created entirely by natural causes. The passer-by did not create the danger, has assumed no responsibility and has no special relationship or statutory duty to the stranger.\n\nWhich is the best general negligence position?","options":{"A":"Every person who sees danger owes an automatic duty to rescue.","B":"A duty arises whenever rescue would be morally desirable.","C":"English negligence law ordinarily imposes no general duty to rescue or prevent harm caused by a third party or natural event absent a recognised basis for a positive duty.","D":"Omissions are always treated identically to positive acts.","E":"A passer-by is strictly liable for harm not prevented."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The common law generally distinguishes causing harm from failing to confer a benefit. Positive duties can arise from recognised relationships, assumption of responsibility, creation of danger, control and other established categories, but there is no general duty to rescue strangers.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Every person who sees danger owes an automatic duty to rescue” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. A negligence duty for an omission requires a recognised basis for imposing positive responsibility.","B":"Incorrect. “A duty arises whenever rescue would be morally desirable” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. A negligence duty for an omission requires a recognised basis for imposing positive responsibility.","C":"Correct. The common law generally distinguishes causing harm from failing to confer a benefit. Positive duties can arise from recognised relationships, assumption of responsibility, creation of danger, control and other established categories, but there is no general duty to rescue strangers.","D":"Incorrect. “Omissions are always treated identically to positive acts” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. A negligence duty for an omission requires a recognised basis for imposing positive responsibility.","E":"Incorrect. “A passer-by is strictly liable for harm not prevented” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. A negligence duty for an omission requires a recognised basis for imposing positive responsibility."},"primary_legal_authority":"Stovin v Wise [1996] AC 923; Michael v Chief Constable of South Wales Police [2015] UKSC 2.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A negligence duty for an omission requires a recognised basis for imposing positive responsibility.","common_trap":"Turning moral expectations into a universal common-law duty of rescue.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"adb9c0d0bb6a9a01"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0654","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Trespass to land — intention","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A delivery driver intentionally walks onto land believing, reasonably but mistakenly, that the land belongs to the intended customer. The owner did not consent.\n\nWhich mental element is generally required for trespass to land?","options":{"A":"The defendant must intend to commit a legal wrong.","B":"Trespass requires proof of negligence only.","C":"Mistake as to ownership always prevents trespass.","D":"An intention to enter the land is sufficient; the defendant need not intend to trespass or know that entry is unlawful.","E":"Trespass requires physical damage to the land."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Trespass to land is actionable per se and generally requires an intentional act of entry; a mistake about ownership or entitlement does not necessarily negate the intention to enter.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “The defendant must intend to commit a legal wrong” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. The relevant intention concerns the physical act of entry, not knowledge of unlawfulness.","B":"Incorrect. “Trespass requires proof of negligence only” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. The relevant intention concerns the physical act of entry, not knowledge of unlawfulness.","C":"Incorrect. “Mistake as to ownership always prevents trespass” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. The relevant intention concerns the physical act of entry, not knowledge of unlawfulness.","D":"Correct. Trespass to land is actionable per se and generally requires an intentional act of entry; a mistake about ownership or entitlement does not necessarily negate the intention to enter.","E":"Incorrect. “Trespass requires physical damage to the land” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. The relevant intention concerns the physical act of entry, not knowledge of unlawfulness."},"primary_legal_authority":"Conway v George Wimpey & Co Ltd [1951] 2 KB 266; trespass principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The relevant intention concerns the physical act of entry, not knowledge of unlawfulness.","common_trap":"Assuming an honest mistake about title eliminates the intentional entry required for trespass.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5c551ae900f44e6f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0655","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Defamation — serious harm","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An individual claimant proves publication of a defamatory statement but produces no evidence that the publication caused or is likely to cause serious harm to reputation.\n\nWhich statutory threshold is directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Any defamatory meaning automatically establishes actionable damage regardless of seriousness.","B":"Serious harm applies only to corporate claimants.","C":"A claimant must prove physical injury instead of reputational harm.","D":"The serious-harm requirement was abolished in 2013.","E":"Section 1 of the Defamation Act 2013 requires serious harm, or likely serious harm, to the claimant's reputation."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Section 1 imposes a serious-harm threshold. For bodies trading for profit, serious harm requires serious financial loss; individual claimants must establish the statutory reputational threshold on the evidence and circumstances.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Any defamatory meaning automatically establishes actionable damage regardless of seriousness” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Defamatory meaning alone is not enough if the statutory serious-harm threshold is not met.","B":"Incorrect. “Serious harm applies only to corporate claimants” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Defamatory meaning alone is not enough if the statutory serious-harm threshold is not met.","C":"Incorrect. “A claimant must prove physical injury instead of reputational harm” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Defamatory meaning alone is not enough if the statutory serious-harm threshold is not met.","D":"Incorrect. “The serious-harm requirement was abolished in 2013” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Defamatory meaning alone is not enough if the statutory serious-harm threshold is not met.","E":"Correct. Section 1 imposes a serious-harm threshold. For bodies trading for profit, serious harm requires serious financial loss; individual claimants must establish the statutory reputational threshold on the evidence and circumstances."},"primary_legal_authority":"Defamation Act 2013, s 1; Lachaux v Independent Print Ltd [2019] UKSC 27.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Defamatory meaning alone is not enough if the statutory serious-harm threshold is not met.","common_trap":"Stopping the analysis after identifying defamatory meaning and publication.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b4d645720c16186a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0656","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Courts — precedent and ratio decidendi","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A later court is considering an earlier binding case. The earlier judgment contains both the legal proposition necessary to decide the case and additional observations not necessary to the result.\n\nWhich part ordinarily carries binding precedential force?","options":{"A":"The ratio decidendi: the legal reasoning necessary for the decision, rather than merely obiter observations.","B":"Every sentence in every judgment is equally binding.","C":"Only the factual narrative is binding.","D":"Obiter dicta always override the ratio.","E":"Headnotes written by law reporters constitute the binding rule."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Under the doctrine of precedent, the ratio decidendi of a decision from a binding court has authoritative force; obiter dicta may be persuasive but are not binding merely because they appear in the judgment.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Under the doctrine of precedent, the ratio decidendi of a decision from a binding court has authoritative force; obiter dicta may be persuasive but are not binding merely because they appear in the judgment.","B":"Incorrect. “Every sentence in every judgment is equally binding” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Binding precedent attaches to the ratio rather than all judicial commentary.","C":"Incorrect. “Only the factual narrative is binding” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Binding precedent attaches to the ratio rather than all judicial commentary.","D":"Incorrect. “Obiter dicta always override the ratio” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Binding precedent attaches to the ratio rather than all judicial commentary.","E":"Incorrect. “Headnotes written by law reporters constitute the binding rule” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Binding precedent attaches to the ratio rather than all judicial commentary."},"primary_legal_authority":"Doctrine of precedent; ratio decidendi and obiter dicta.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Binding precedent attaches to the ratio rather than all judicial commentary.","common_trap":"Treating persuasive observations or editorial headnotes as the binding legal rule.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1bec8e9311e07f46"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0657","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Human Rights Act — section 3 interpretation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A statutory provision is capable of more than one interpretation. One reading is compatible with Convention rights and another is not. The compatible reading does not contradict a fundamental feature of the legislative scheme.\n\nWhich Human Rights Act duty is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Section 4 requires the court immediately to invalidate the statute.","B":"So far as possible, legislation must be read and given effect in a way compatible with Convention rights under section 3.","C":"Section 6 applies only to Parliament's drafting process.","D":"Courts must always choose the ordinary literal meaning even if a lawful compatible interpretation is possible.","E":"Convention rights have no relevance to statutory interpretation."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 3 requires primary and subordinate legislation to be read and given effect compatibly with Convention rights so far as it is possible to do so, while authority recognises limits where a proposed reading would contradict a fundamental feature of the legislation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Section 4 requires the court immediately to invalidate the statute” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Section 3 creates a strong interpretive obligation but not an unlimited power to rewrite legislation.","B":"Correct. Section 3 requires primary and subordinate legislation to be read and given effect compatibly with Convention rights so far as it is possible to do so, while authority recognises limits where a proposed reading would contradict a fundamental feature of the legislation.","C":"Incorrect. “Section 6 applies only to Parliament's drafting process” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Section 3 creates a strong interpretive obligation but not an unlimited power to rewrite legislation.","D":"Incorrect. “Courts must always choose the ordinary literal meaning even if a lawful compatible interpretation is possible” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Section 3 creates a strong interpretive obligation but not an unlimited power to rewrite legislation.","E":"Incorrect. “Convention rights have no relevance to statutory interpretation” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Section 3 creates a strong interpretive obligation but not an unlimited power to rewrite legislation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, s 3; Ghaidan v Godin-Mendoza [2004] UKHL 30.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Section 3 creates a strong interpretive obligation but not an unlimited power to rewrite legislation.","common_trap":"Confusing compatible interpretation with a section 4 declaration or judicial invalidation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"17ea86290496598b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0658","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"EU-derived law — assimilated law","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A dispute in 2026 concerns domestic legislation that originated in EU law and was retained after the UK's withdrawal, later becoming part of the statutory category now described as assimilated law. Counsel assumes that the old principle of supremacy of EU law automatically overrides every later domestic enactment.\n\nWhich is the best general approach?","options":{"A":"EU law retains unconditional supremacy over every UK Act enacted after withdrawal.","B":"All EU-derived domestic law ceased to exist automatically at the end of 2023.","C":"The post-withdrawal statutory framework must be applied; the former general supremacy principle was altered by legislation and cannot simply be assumed to override later domestic law as it once did.","D":"Assimilated law can never be amended by Parliament.","E":"The CJEU alone determines the validity of all UK legislation."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The European Union (Withdrawal) Act framework, as amended by the Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023, changed the status and interpretive rules for retained/assimilated law, including the former supremacy principle. The applicable domestic statutory rules and dates must be analysed.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “EU law retains unconditional supremacy over every UK Act enacted after withdrawal” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Post-Brexit questions require the current domestic statutory hierarchy rather than automatic application of pre-withdrawal EU supremacy.","B":"Incorrect. “All EU-derived domestic law ceased to exist automatically at the end of 2023” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Post-Brexit questions require the current domestic statutory hierarchy rather than automatic application of pre-withdrawal EU supremacy.","C":"Correct. The European Union (Withdrawal) Act framework, as amended by the Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023, changed the status and interpretive rules for retained/assimilated law, including the former supremacy principle. The applicable domestic statutory rules and dates must be analysed.","D":"Incorrect. “Assimilated law can never be amended by Parliament” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Post-Brexit questions require the current domestic statutory hierarchy rather than automatic application of pre-withdrawal EU supremacy.","E":"Incorrect. “The CJEU alone determines the validity of all UK legislation” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Post-Brexit questions require the current domestic statutory hierarchy rather than automatic application of pre-withdrawal EU supremacy."},"primary_legal_authority":"European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018, as amended; Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Post-Brexit questions require the current domestic statutory hierarchy rather than automatic application of pre-withdrawal EU supremacy.","common_trap":"Applying historic EU supremacy without accounting for the amended withdrawal legislation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b0b02dd001ee305d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0659","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — testamentary capacity","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An elderly testator understands that a will disposes of property on death, understands the broad extent of the estate and the claims of those who might expect benefit, and no disorder of mind poisons the dispositions. The testator has some unrelated memory difficulties.\n\nWhich is the best general approach to testamentary capacity?","options":{"A":"Any diagnosed memory problem automatically invalidates a will.","B":"The Mental Capacity Act 2005 test wholly replaced Banks v Goodfellow for wills in every context.","C":"Capacity depends solely on whether beneficiaries agree with the dispositions.","D":"Apply the Banks v Goodfellow criteria to the testator's decision-making capacity for the will; age or unrelated memory impairment does not by itself establish incapacity.","E":"A testator must remember the precise value of every asset."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Testamentary capacity remains assessed through the Banks v Goodfellow criteria, focusing on understanding the nature and effect of the will, the extent of property in a general sense, relevant claims and freedom from a disorder influencing dispositions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Any diagnosed memory problem automatically invalidates a will” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Capacity is decision-specific and does not require perfect memory or exact asset valuation.","B":"Incorrect. “The Mental Capacity Act 2005 test wholly replaced Banks v Goodfellow for wills in every context” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Capacity is decision-specific and does not require perfect memory or exact asset valuation.","C":"Incorrect. “Capacity depends solely on whether beneficiaries agree with the dispositions” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Capacity is decision-specific and does not require perfect memory or exact asset valuation.","D":"Correct. Testamentary capacity remains assessed through the Banks v Goodfellow criteria, focusing on understanding the nature and effect of the will, the extent of property in a general sense, relevant claims and freedom from a disorder influencing dispositions.","E":"Incorrect. “A testator must remember the precise value of every asset” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Capacity is decision-specific and does not require perfect memory or exact asset valuation."},"primary_legal_authority":"Banks v Goodfellow (1870) LR 5 QB 549; Clitheroe v Bond [2021] EWHC 1102 (Ch).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Capacity is decision-specific and does not require perfect memory or exact asset valuation.","common_trap":"Equating age, diagnosis or imperfect memory with automatic testamentary incapacity.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"88aa3c349e86349c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0660","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — undue influence","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A beneficiary persistently coerces a vulnerable testator until the testator makes a gift that reflects the beneficiary's wishes rather than the testator's own free intention. The issue is probate undue influence rather than mere persuasion.\n\nWhich is the best general statement?","options":{"A":"Any request by a beneficiary for a gift automatically invalidates the will.","B":"The presumption of undue influence used for some lifetime transactions automatically applies to wills.","C":"Undue influence is established whenever a will is unequal.","D":"A solicitor's presence conclusively prevents any finding of undue influence.","E":"Probate undue influence requires coercion that overbears the testator's free will; persuasion or appeals to affection alone are insufficient."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"In probate, undue influence requires proof of coercion sufficient to overpower the testator's volition. There is no general presumption equivalent to that applied in certain inter vivos transactions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Any request by a beneficiary for a gift automatically invalidates the will” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. The central probate question is whether coercion displaced the testator's free agency.","B":"Incorrect. “The presumption of undue influence used for some lifetime transactions automatically applies to wills” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. The central probate question is whether coercion displaced the testator's free agency.","C":"Incorrect. “Undue influence is established whenever a will is unequal” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. The central probate question is whether coercion displaced the testator's free agency.","D":"Incorrect. “A solicitor's presence conclusively prevents any finding of undue influence” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. The central probate question is whether coercion displaced the testator's free agency.","E":"Correct. In probate, undue influence requires proof of coercion sufficient to overpower the testator's volition. There is no general presumption equivalent to that applied in certain inter vivos transactions."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wingrove v Wingrove (1885) 11 PD 81; Edwards v Edwards [2007] WTLR 1387.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The central probate question is whether coercion displaced the testator's free agency.","common_trap":"Confusing suspicious circumstances or persuasion with the demanding probate standard of coercion.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2c11edb7dee5e145"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0661","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Estate administration — insolvent estates","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A deceased's estate is insolvent: the assets are insufficient to pay all enforceable liabilities. The personal representatives propose to pay a large legacy first because the beneficiary is a close relative.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The estate must be administered under the rules governing insolvent estates and the applicable priority of liabilities; beneficiaries cannot be preferred ahead of creditors merely because of the will.","B":"A legacy always ranks ahead of unsecured creditors.","C":"Personal representatives may choose any creditor or beneficiary they personally favour.","D":"Death extinguishes insolvency priorities.","E":"All creditors and beneficiaries necessarily receive equal percentages."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An insolvent estate is administered according to the statutory insolvency framework and prescribed order of payment. Testamentary beneficiaries take only after enforceable estate liabilities according to priority.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. An insolvent estate is administered according to the statutory insolvency framework and prescribed order of payment. Testamentary beneficiaries take only after enforceable estate liabilities according to priority.","B":"Incorrect. “A legacy always ranks ahead of unsecured creditors” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Insolvency changes administration from ordinary distribution of legacies to a creditor-priority process.","C":"Incorrect. “Personal representatives may choose any creditor or beneficiary they personally favour” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Insolvency changes administration from ordinary distribution of legacies to a creditor-priority process.","D":"Incorrect. “Death extinguishes insolvency priorities” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Insolvency changes administration from ordinary distribution of legacies to a creditor-priority process.","E":"Incorrect. “All creditors and beneficiaries necessarily receive equal percentages” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Insolvency changes administration from ordinary distribution of legacies to a creditor-priority process."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Insolvent Estates of Deceased Persons Order 1986; Insolvency Act framework.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Insolvency changes administration from ordinary distribution of legacies to a creditor-priority process.","common_trap":"Treating a testamentary gift as superior to liabilities of an insolvent estate.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0f905941a2498a76"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0662","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Client money — withdrawals","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor wants to withdraw money from client account to pay a third party. The amount is held for a particular client, but the client has not authorised that payment and there is no other legal or regulatory basis for it.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Any solicitor may withdraw client money for any firm-related purpose.","B":"The withdrawal should not be made: client-account withdrawals must be for the purpose for which the money is held or otherwise authorised by the client or applicable law/rules.","C":"A positive client-account balance is sufficient authority for every withdrawal.","D":"Client authorisation is never relevant to third-party payments.","E":"Money ceases to be client money once a third party requests it."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Accounts Rules tightly control withdrawals from client account. Money may be withdrawn only for the purpose for which it is held or on proper instructions/authority or another permitted basis.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Any solicitor may withdraw client money for any firm-related purpose” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Client money cannot be used merely because funds happen to be available in the aggregate account.","B":"Correct. The Accounts Rules tightly control withdrawals from client account. Money may be withdrawn only for the purpose for which it is held or on proper instructions/authority or another permitted basis.","C":"Incorrect. “A positive client-account balance is sufficient authority for every withdrawal” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Client money cannot be used merely because funds happen to be available in the aggregate account.","D":"Incorrect. “Client authorisation is never relevant to third-party payments” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Client money cannot be used merely because funds happen to be available in the aggregate account.","E":"Incorrect. “Money ceases to be client money once a third party requests it” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Client money cannot be used merely because funds happen to be available in the aggregate account."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, r 5.1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Client money cannot be used merely because funds happen to be available in the aggregate account.","common_trap":"Confusing pooled banking mechanics with entitlement to use an individual client's funds.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"837f479ba6189d45"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0663","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustee investment — advice and review","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Trustees exercising the statutory general power of investment propose a major portfolio change. They have taken no proper advice and do not intend to review investments after the change, although no exception makes advice unnecessary.\n\nWhich statutory duties are most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Trustees have an unrestricted investment discretion once a trust is created.","B":"Advice is prohibited because trustees must make every investment decision alone.","C":"The Trustee Act duties concerning proper advice and periodic review, alongside the standard investment criteria.","D":"Review is required only after a beneficiary complains.","E":"The statutory investment criteria apply only to charitable companies."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Trustee Act 2000 requires trustees, subject to exceptions, to obtain and consider proper advice when exercising investment powers and to review investments from time to time, considering suitability and diversification.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Trustees have an unrestricted investment discretion once a trust is created” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Trustee investment is an ongoing process involving criteria, advice and review rather than a one-time unfettered choice.","B":"Incorrect. “Advice is prohibited because trustees must make every investment decision alone” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Trustee investment is an ongoing process involving criteria, advice and review rather than a one-time unfettered choice.","C":"Correct. The Trustee Act 2000 requires trustees, subject to exceptions, to obtain and consider proper advice when exercising investment powers and to review investments from time to time, considering suitability and diversification.","D":"Incorrect. “Review is required only after a beneficiary complains” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Trustee investment is an ongoing process involving criteria, advice and review rather than a one-time unfettered choice.","E":"Incorrect. “The statutory investment criteria apply only to charitable companies” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Trustee investment is an ongoing process involving criteria, advice and review rather than a one-time unfettered choice."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 2000, ss 4–5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Trustee investment is an ongoing process involving criteria, advice and review rather than a one-time unfettered choice.","common_trap":"Treating the general power of investment as eliminating the safeguards attached to its exercise.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d3b72ed16b688e58"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0664","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Breach of trust — limitation and fraud","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A beneficiary discovers that a trustee fraudulently appropriated trust property for the trustee's own use many years earlier. The trustee argues that an ordinary six-year limitation period necessarily bars the claim.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Every breach-of-trust claim is absolutely barred after six years.","B":"Limitation never applies to any trust claim.","C":"Fraud is irrelevant to limitation.","D":"The Trustee Act contains important exceptions to ordinary limitation for claims involving trustee fraud or fraudulent breach of trust and certain claims to recover trust property retained or converted by the trustee.","E":"Only criminal proceedings can recover misappropriated trust property."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 21 of the Limitation Act 1980 provides that no limitation period applies to specified beneficiary actions concerning trustee fraud/fraudulent breach and recovery of trust property retained or converted by the trustee, while other trust claims may be subject to limitation.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Every breach-of-trust claim is absolutely barred after six years” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Trust limitation requires identifying the statutory category; fraud and trustee retention/conversion can disapply the ordinary period.","B":"Incorrect. “Limitation never applies to any trust claim” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Trust limitation requires identifying the statutory category; fraud and trustee retention/conversion can disapply the ordinary period.","C":"Incorrect. “Fraud is irrelevant to limitation” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Trust limitation requires identifying the statutory category; fraud and trustee retention/conversion can disapply the ordinary period.","D":"Correct. Section 21 of the Limitation Act 1980 provides that no limitation period applies to specified beneficiary actions concerning trustee fraud/fraudulent breach and recovery of trust property retained or converted by the trustee, while other trust claims may be subject to limitation.","E":"Incorrect. “Only criminal proceedings can recover misappropriated trust property” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Trust limitation requires identifying the statutory category; fraud and trustee retention/conversion can disapply the ordinary period."},"primary_legal_authority":"Limitation Act 1980, s 21.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Trust limitation requires identifying the statutory category; fraud and trustee retention/conversion can disapply the ordinary period.","common_trap":"Applying a blanket six-year period without checking the statutory trust exceptions.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"cabcc7490078db0e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0665","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Co-ownership — TLATA sale applications","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two co-owners hold a family property on trust of land. One seeks an order for sale; the other wants occupation to continue. They cannot agree.\n\nWhich statutory framework gives the court power to determine an application concerning sale?","options":{"A":"The Wills Act 1837.","B":"The Defamation Act 2013.","C":"The Partnership Act 1890 automatically requires sale.","D":"The court has no jurisdiction over disputes between co-owners.","E":"The Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996, under which the court considers the statutory factors on a section 14 application."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Under TLATA, a trustee or person with an interest in property subject to a trust of land may apply for an order concerning the trustees' functions. Section 15 identifies factors relevant to the court's exercise of discretion.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “The Wills Act 1837” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. TLATA supplies the principal statutory mechanism for co-owner applications about sale and related trust-of-land functions.","B":"Incorrect. “The Defamation Act 2013” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. TLATA supplies the principal statutory mechanism for co-owner applications about sale and related trust-of-land functions.","C":"Incorrect. “The Partnership Act 1890 automatically requires sale” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. TLATA supplies the principal statutory mechanism for co-owner applications about sale and related trust-of-land functions.","D":"Incorrect. “The court has no jurisdiction over disputes between co-owners” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. TLATA supplies the principal statutory mechanism for co-owner applications about sale and related trust-of-land functions.","E":"Correct. Under TLATA, a trustee or person with an interest in property subject to a trust of land may apply for an order concerning the trustees' functions. Section 15 identifies factors relevant to the court's exercise of discretion."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996, ss 14–15.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"TLATA supplies the principal statutory mechanism for co-owner applications about sale and related trust-of-land functions.","common_trap":"Assuming a beneficiary has an automatic entitlement either to immediate sale or indefinite occupation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b0ab4f67099dcace"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0666","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Leases — forfeiture for breach other than rent","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A commercial tenant breaches a repairing covenant. The lease contains a forfeiture clause. The landlord wants to forfeit for that breach, which is not non-payment of rent.\n\nWhich statutory procedural step is generally central before forfeiture?","options":{"A":"Service of a section 146 notice specifying the breach, requiring remedy if capable of remedy, and requiring compensation where appropriate, before enforcement after a reasonable time.","B":"Immediate peaceable re-entry is always lawful without notice for any covenant breach.","C":"A section 146 notice is used only to create a lease.","D":"The tenant must first serve notice on the landlord.","E":"Forfeiture clauses are unenforceable in all commercial leases."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"For most covenant breaches other than non-payment of rent, section 146 restricts enforcement of a right of re-entry or forfeiture until the landlord has served the prescribed notice and the tenant has had the required opportunity to respond/remedy.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. For most covenant breaches other than non-payment of rent, section 146 restricts enforcement of a right of re-entry or forfeiture until the landlord has served the prescribed notice and the tenant has had the required opportunity to respond/remedy.","B":"Incorrect. “Immediate peaceable re-entry is always lawful without notice for any covenant breach” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Forfeiture for non-rent covenant breach generally requires compliance with the statutory notice regime.","C":"Incorrect. “A section 146 notice is used only to create a lease” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Forfeiture for non-rent covenant breach generally requires compliance with the statutory notice regime.","D":"Incorrect. “The tenant must first serve notice on the landlord” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Forfeiture for non-rent covenant breach generally requires compliance with the statutory notice regime.","E":"Incorrect. “Forfeiture clauses are unenforceable in all commercial leases” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Forfeiture for non-rent covenant breach generally requires compliance with the statutory notice regime."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, s 146.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Forfeiture for non-rent covenant breach generally requires compliance with the statutory notice regime.","common_trap":"Applying the different treatment of rent arrears to other covenant breaches.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c571d080841bbc31"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0667","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Commercial leases — assignment and consent","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A commercial lease prohibits assignment without the landlord's consent, such consent not to be unreasonably withheld. The tenant makes a proper written application with sufficient information. The landlord simply ignores it for months.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The landlord may always ignore an application indefinitely.","B":"The landlord may be subject to the statutory duty to give consent except where it is reasonable not to do so, and to respond within a reasonable time with written reasons if consent is withheld.","C":"A qualified covenant gives the tenant an automatic right to assign without seeking consent.","D":"Consent can be withheld for any reason whatsoever.","E":"The statutory duty applies only to residential freeholds."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Landlord and Tenant Act 1988 imposes duties on landlords receiving qualifying written applications for consent, including dealing with the request within a reasonable time and giving written reasons for refusal.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “The landlord may always ignore an application indefinitely” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. A qualified alienation covenant is reinforced by statutory duties governing reasonable handling of consent requests.","B":"Correct. The Landlord and Tenant Act 1988 imposes duties on landlords receiving qualifying written applications for consent, including dealing with the request within a reasonable time and giving written reasons for refusal.","C":"Incorrect. “A qualified covenant gives the tenant an automatic right to assign without seeking consent” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. A qualified alienation covenant is reinforced by statutory duties governing reasonable handling of consent requests.","D":"Incorrect. “Consent can be withheld for any reason whatsoever” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. A qualified alienation covenant is reinforced by statutory duties governing reasonable handling of consent requests.","E":"Incorrect. “The statutory duty applies only to residential freeholds” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. A qualified alienation covenant is reinforced by statutory duties governing reasonable handling of consent requests."},"primary_legal_authority":"Landlord and Tenant Act 1988, ss 1–4; Landlord and Tenant Act 1927, s 19(1).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A qualified alienation covenant is reinforced by statutory duties governing reasonable handling of consent requests.","common_trap":"Assuming either unrestricted landlord discretion or an unrestricted tenant right to bypass the consent process.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e74f4c87f19f09b2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0668","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Conveyancing — requisitions on title","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"After exchange and before completion, the buyer's solicitor raises standard requisitions dealing with completion arrangements, discharge of mortgages and matters needed to complete the transfer.\n\nWhat is the principal function of requisitions on title in modern conveyancing?","options":{"A":"To renegotiate the purchase price automatically after exchange.","B":"To replace all pre-contract investigation of title.","C":"To resolve specified completion and title-administration matters before completion rather than reopen matters already accepted or governed by the contract without proper basis.","D":"To create planning permission.","E":"To register the buyer as proprietor before completion."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Requisitions are part of the pre-completion process and address matters necessary to complete safely, including execution, completion mechanics and discharge of charges; they are not a general opportunity to reopen the bargain.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “To renegotiate the purchase price automatically after exchange” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Requisitions focus on outstanding completion and title mechanics after the contractual stage.","B":"Incorrect. “To replace all pre-contract investigation of title” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Requisitions focus on outstanding completion and title mechanics after the contractual stage.","C":"Correct. Requisitions are part of the pre-completion process and address matters necessary to complete safely, including execution, completion mechanics and discharge of charges; they are not a general opportunity to reopen the bargain.","D":"Incorrect. “To create planning permission” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Requisitions focus on outstanding completion and title mechanics after the contractual stage.","E":"Incorrect. “To register the buyer as proprietor before completion” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Requisitions focus on outstanding completion and title mechanics after the contractual stage."},"primary_legal_authority":"Conveyancing practice; Law Society completion information and requisitions procedures.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Requisitions focus on outstanding completion and title mechanics after the contractual stage.","common_trap":"Treating post-exchange requisitions as a substitute for pre-contract due diligence or a general renegotiation device.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"aabe9c8d97616722"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0669","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Theft — belonging to another","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A receives money from V subject to a clear obligation to retain that specific money or its proceeds and deal with it in a particular way for V. A dishonestly appropriates it for personal use.\n\nWhich Theft Act concept is particularly relevant to whether the money can still 'belong to another'?","options":{"A":"Money handed over can never belong to another for theft purposes.","B":"Only legal title is relevant under section 5.","C":"A contractual obligation can never be relevant to belonging to another.","D":"Property can belong to another where that person has possession or control or a proprietary interest, and section 5 contains specific rules concerning property received subject to obligations to retain/deal with it or its proceeds.","E":"Cash is excluded from the definition of property."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 5 defines 'belonging to another' broadly and includes statutory provisions addressing property received subject to an obligation to retain and deal with the property or its proceeds in a particular way.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Money handed over can never belong to another for theft purposes” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Theft analysis of entrusted money requires the statutory belonging-to-another rules, not merely formal title.","B":"Incorrect. “Only legal title is relevant under section 5” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Theft analysis of entrusted money requires the statutory belonging-to-another rules, not merely formal title.","C":"Incorrect. “A contractual obligation can never be relevant to belonging to another” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Theft analysis of entrusted money requires the statutory belonging-to-another rules, not merely formal title.","D":"Correct. Section 5 defines 'belonging to another' broadly and includes statutory provisions addressing property received subject to an obligation to retain and deal with the property or its proceeds in a particular way.","E":"Incorrect. “Cash is excluded from the definition of property” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Theft analysis of entrusted money requires the statutory belonging-to-another rules, not merely formal title."},"primary_legal_authority":"Theft Act 1968, s 5, including s 5(3) where its requirements are satisfied.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Theft analysis of entrusted money requires the statutory belonging-to-another rules, not merely formal title.","common_trap":"Assuming transfer of possession or title necessarily ends all section 5 interests.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f77d11680c848ef1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0670","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Criminal evidence — adverse inferences from silence","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"During a properly conducted police interview under caution, a suspect fails to mention a fact later relied on in the defence at trial. The statutory conditions concerning questioning and later reliance are otherwise satisfied.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Silence automatically amounts to a confession.","B":"No inference can ever be drawn from failure to mention a later-relied-on fact.","C":"The suspect loses the right to a trial by remaining silent.","D":"An adverse inference requires the court to direct an automatic conviction.","E":"The court or jury may in appropriate circumstances draw an adverse inference under the statutory regime, but silence does not itself automatically prove guilt."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Section 34 permits adverse inferences in specified circumstances where an accused later relies on a fact that, in the circumstances existing at the time, they could reasonably have been expected to mention when questioned or charged. Safeguards and proper directions apply.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. “Silence automatically amounts to a confession” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Silence can have evidential consequences under defined statutory conditions but cannot substitute automatically for proof of guilt.","B":"Incorrect. “No inference can ever be drawn from failure to mention a later-relied-on fact” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Silence can have evidential consequences under defined statutory conditions but cannot substitute automatically for proof of guilt.","C":"Incorrect. “The suspect loses the right to a trial by remaining silent” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Silence can have evidential consequences under defined statutory conditions but cannot substitute automatically for proof of guilt.","D":"Incorrect. “An adverse inference requires the court to direct an automatic conviction” does not state the governing rule for this scenario. Silence can have evidential consequences under defined statutory conditions but cannot substitute automatically for proof of guilt.","E":"Correct. Section 34 permits adverse inferences in specified circumstances where an accused later relies on a fact that, in the circumstances existing at the time, they could reasonably have been expected to mention when questioned or charged. Safeguards and proper directions apply."},"primary_legal_authority":"Criminal Justice and Public Order Act 1994, s 34; applicable direction and fair-trial safeguards.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Silence can have evidential consequences under defined statutory conditions but cannot substitute automatically for proof of guilt.","common_trap":"Treating the right to silence as either consequence-free in every circumstance or as an automatic admission.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"021feb7056c54f09"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0671","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — written resolutions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company circulates a written special resolution to amend its articles. Members holding 72% of the total voting rights of eligible members signify agreement before the circulation period expires.\n\nHas the written resolution passed?","options":{"A":"No. A written special resolution requires the same 75% majority applicable to a special resolution, calculated under the statutory written-resolution rules.","B":"Yes. Any written resolution passes with a simple majority.","C":"Yes. A written special resolution requires only two-thirds of eligible votes.","D":"No. Articles can never be amended by written resolution.","E":"Yes, provided every director also signs the resolution."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A private company can generally use the statutory written-resolution procedure, but a written special resolution still requires not less than 75% of the total voting rights of eligible members.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A private company can generally use the statutory written-resolution procedure, but a written special resolution still requires not less than 75% of the total voting rights of eligible members.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that yes. Any written resolution passes with a simple majority. That does not match the controlling rule here. Using the written procedure does not reduce the statutory majority required for a special resolution.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that yes. A written special resolution requires only two-thirds of eligible votes. That does not match the controlling rule here. Using the written procedure does not reduce the statutory majority required for a special resolution.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that no. Articles can never be amended by written resolution. That does not match the controlling rule here. Using the written procedure does not reduce the statutory majority required for a special resolution.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that yes, provided every director also signs the resolution. That does not match the controlling rule here. Using the written procedure does not reduce the statutory majority required for a special resolution."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 21, 283, 288 and 296.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Using the written procedure does not reduce the statutory majority required for a special resolution.","common_trap":"Confusing the availability of written resolutions in private companies with the voting threshold needed for a special resolution.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"eeeb5a403e91b6ff"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0672","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — dividends and unlawful distributions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company has cash in its bank account but accumulated losses mean its relevant accounts show no profits available for distribution. The directors nevertheless propose a dividend.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Any positive bank balance can lawfully be distributed as a dividend.","B":"Cash availability alone is insufficient; a company may make a distribution only out of profits available for the purpose under the statutory capital-maintenance rules.","C":"A private company may distribute capital whenever all directors agree.","D":"Only public companies are subject to restrictions on distributions.","E":"A dividend is lawful whenever shareholders unanimously request it."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Companies may make distributions only out of profits available for distribution, broadly accumulated realised profits less accumulated realised losses, subject to the statutory rules. Liquidity is not the same as distributable profits.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that any positive bank balance can lawfully be distributed as a dividend. That does not match the controlling rule here. A company's ability to pay cash does not establish that the amount is legally distributable.","B":"Correct. Companies may make distributions only out of profits available for distribution, broadly accumulated realised profits less accumulated realised losses, subject to the statutory rules. Liquidity is not the same as distributable profits.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that a private company may distribute capital whenever all directors agree. That does not match the controlling rule here. A company's ability to pay cash does not establish that the amount is legally distributable.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that only public companies are subject to restrictions on distributions. That does not match the controlling rule here. A company's ability to pay cash does not establish that the amount is legally distributable.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that a dividend is lawful whenever shareholders unanimously request it. That does not match the controlling rule here. A company's ability to pay cash does not establish that the amount is legally distributable."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 829–830.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A company's ability to pay cash does not establish that the amount is legally distributable.","common_trap":"Confusing liquidity with the accounting and capital-maintenance test for lawful distributions.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e58599b5e76339ee"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0673","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Business taxation — VAT output and input tax","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A VAT-registered trading business makes taxable supplies and incurs VAT on purchases used for its taxable business activities. No exemption or special restriction applies.\n\nWhich is the best general description of the VAT mechanism?","options":{"A":"VAT on business purchases can never be recovered.","B":"The customer, rather than the registered supplier, files the supplier's VAT return.","C":"The business generally accounts for output tax on taxable supplies and may credit recoverable input tax, paying HMRC the net amount due for the period.","D":"A VAT-registered business pays corporation tax instead of VAT on taxable supplies.","E":"Input tax is automatically refunded in cash on every individual purchase."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A VAT-registered taxable person ordinarily charges output tax on taxable supplies and deducts allowable input tax incurred for business purposes, with the return determining the net amount payable or repayable.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that vAT on business purchases can never be recovered. That does not match the controlling rule here. VAT operates through periodic accounting for output tax less allowable input tax.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that the customer, rather than the registered supplier, files the supplier's VAT return. That does not match the controlling rule here. VAT operates through periodic accounting for output tax less allowable input tax.","C":"Correct. A VAT-registered taxable person ordinarily charges output tax on taxable supplies and deducts allowable input tax incurred for business purposes, with the return determining the net amount payable or repayable.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that a VAT-registered business pays corporation tax instead of VAT on taxable supplies. That does not match the controlling rule here. VAT operates through periodic accounting for output tax less allowable input tax.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that input tax is automatically refunded in cash on every individual purchase. That does not match the controlling rule here. VAT operates through periodic accounting for output tax less allowable input tax."},"primary_legal_authority":"Value Added Tax Act 1994; general VAT accounting principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"VAT operates through periodic accounting for output tax less allowable input tax.","common_trap":"Treating VAT as a tax borne administratively only by the final customer without supplier accounting, or assuming all input tax is irrecoverable.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"38a396f12b61d109"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0674","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — statutory demand and winding up","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A company indisputably owes a creditor a presently due debt above the statutory minimum. The creditor is considering a statutory demand as evidence that the company is unable to pay its debts for a winding-up petition.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Service of a statutory demand itself immediately liquidates the company.","B":"A statutory demand conclusively proves insolvency even where the debt is genuinely disputed on substantial grounds.","C":"Only a shareholder may present a winding-up petition.","D":"Failure to satisfy a qualifying statutory demand within the statutory period can provide evidence of inability to pay debts for a winding-up petition, subject to insolvency law and the court's discretion.","E":"A company has no opportunity to pay or challenge the basis relied upon."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The Insolvency Act provides routes by which inability to pay debts may be established, including failure to comply with a qualifying statutory demand. A genuinely disputed debt is ordinarily unsuitable as the basis for insolvency proceedings.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that service of a statutory demand itself immediately liquidates the company. That does not match the controlling rule here. A statutory demand can support a winding-up case but does not itself place the company into liquidation or bypass genuine debt disputes.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that a statutory demand conclusively proves insolvency even where the debt is genuinely disputed on substantial grounds. That does not match the controlling rule here. A statutory demand can support a winding-up case but does not itself place the company into liquidation or bypass genuine debt disputes.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that only a shareholder may present a winding-up petition. That does not match the controlling rule here. A statutory demand can support a winding-up case but does not itself place the company into liquidation or bypass genuine debt disputes.","D":"Correct. The Insolvency Act provides routes by which inability to pay debts may be established, including failure to comply with a qualifying statutory demand. A genuinely disputed debt is ordinarily unsuitable as the basis for insolvency proceedings.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that a company has no opportunity to pay or challenge the basis relied upon. That does not match the controlling rule here. A statutory demand can support a winding-up case but does not itself place the company into liquidation or bypass genuine debt disputes."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, ss 122–123.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A statutory demand can support a winding-up case but does not itself place the company into liquidation or bypass genuine debt disputes.","common_trap":"Treating the demand as an automatic winding-up order or as a substitute for resolving a substantial debt dispute.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3fecbaebbfc454c2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0675","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Admissions — judgment on admission","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant makes a clear admission of the whole monetary claim in accordance with the CPR. The claimant wants judgment without proving the admitted liability at trial.\n\nWhich procedural route is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"The admission must be ignored until trial.","B":"The claimant must apply for summary judgment because admissions have no independent procedural effect.","C":"An admission automatically becomes a Part 36 offer.","D":"The claim must be discontinued once liability is admitted.","E":"The claimant may seek judgment on the admission under CPR Part 14, subject to the applicable procedure."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"CPR Part 14 regulates admissions and provides mechanisms for obtaining judgment on admitted claims or parts of claims.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that the admission must be ignored until trial. That does not match the controlling rule here. A formal admission can permit judgment without litigating the admitted issue through trial.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that the claimant must apply for summary judgment because admissions have no independent procedural effect. That does not match the controlling rule here. A formal admission can permit judgment without litigating the admitted issue through trial.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that an admission automatically becomes a Part 36 offer. That does not match the controlling rule here. A formal admission can permit judgment without litigating the admitted issue through trial.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that the claim must be discontinued once liability is admitted. That does not match the controlling rule here. A formal admission can permit judgment without litigating the admitted issue through trial.","E":"Correct. CPR Part 14 regulates admissions and provides mechanisms for obtaining judgment on admitted claims or parts of claims."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 14.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A formal admission can permit judgment without litigating the admitted issue through trial.","common_trap":"Confusing admissions with summary judgment or settlement offers.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"87b88ac95a0b3b76"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0676","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Counterclaims","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant has a substantive claim against the claimant arising from the same commercial relationship and wants it determined within the existing proceedings.\n\nWhich is the most directly relevant procedural mechanism?","options":{"A":"A counterclaim under CPR Part 20, ordinarily included with the defence where brought without separate permission at that stage.","B":"The defendant must always commence wholly separate proceedings.","C":"A counterclaim is available only after final judgment.","D":"The defendant must convert the defence into a Part 36 offer.","E":"Counterclaims are limited to claims against witnesses."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"CPR Part 20 permits a defendant to bring a counterclaim against the claimant. A counterclaim filed with the defence ordinarily does not require permission, while later counterclaims are subject to the rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. CPR Part 20 permits a defendant to bring a counterclaim against the claimant. A counterclaim filed with the defence ordinarily does not require permission, while later counterclaims are subject to the rules.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that the defendant must always commence wholly separate proceedings. That does not match the controlling rule here. A counterclaim allows a defendant's own cause of action against the claimant to be determined within the proceedings.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that a counterclaim is available only after final judgment. That does not match the controlling rule here. A counterclaim allows a defendant's own cause of action against the claimant to be determined within the proceedings.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that the defendant must convert the defence into a Part 36 offer. That does not match the controlling rule here. A counterclaim allows a defendant's own cause of action against the claimant to be determined within the proceedings.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that counterclaims are limited to claims against witnesses. That does not match the controlling rule here. A counterclaim allows a defendant's own cause of action against the claimant to be determined within the proceedings."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 20.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A counterclaim allows a defendant's own cause of action against the claimant to be determined within the proceedings.","common_trap":"Assuming a defendant can only resist the claim and cannot advance affirmative relief in the same action.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4e07ccb6dc18b44f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0677","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Costs — qualified one-way costs shifting","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant brings a personal-injury damages claim to which the ordinary QOCS regime applies. The claimant loses after trial. There is no finding of fundamental dishonesty and no special exception applies.\n\nWhich costs concept is particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"A losing personal-injury claimant must always pay every penny of the defendant's costs without restriction.","B":"QOCS restricts enforcement of costs orders against the claimant to the extent permitted by CPR 44.14 and related provisions.","C":"QOCS means no costs order can ever be made against a claimant.","D":"QOCS applies to every commercial contract claim.","E":"QOCS automatically makes the defendant pay the claimant's costs after defeat."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The QOCS regime modifies enforcement of costs orders against claimants in proceedings within its scope. The current CPR provisions govern the extent of permissible enforcement and specified exceptions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that a losing personal-injury claimant must always pay every penny of the defendant's costs without restriction. That does not match the controlling rule here. QOCS principally concerns enforcement of adverse costs orders, not whether an order can be made at all.","B":"Correct. The QOCS regime modifies enforcement of costs orders against claimants in proceedings within its scope. The current CPR provisions govern the extent of permissible enforcement and specified exceptions.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that qOCS means no costs order can ever be made against a claimant. That does not match the controlling rule here. QOCS principally concerns enforcement of adverse costs orders, not whether an order can be made at all.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that qOCS applies to every commercial contract claim. That does not match the controlling rule here. QOCS principally concerns enforcement of adverse costs orders, not whether an order can be made at all.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that qOCS automatically makes the defendant pay the claimant's costs after defeat. That does not match the controlling rule here. QOCS principally concerns enforcement of adverse costs orders, not whether an order can be made at all."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, rr 44.13–44.17.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"QOCS principally concerns enforcement of adverse costs orders, not whether an order can be made at all.","common_trap":"Confusing the existence of an adverse costs order with the separate limits on its enforcement under QOCS.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"dc3bcc013cad0433"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0678","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Equality and diversity — unlawful discrimination","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor refuses to accept an otherwise suitable client solely because of a protected characteristic, in circumstances where no statutory exception applies.\n\nWhich is the best professional position?","options":{"A":"Solicitors may discriminate freely when choosing clients because professional services are outside equality law.","B":"Only courts, not law firms, are subject to equality obligations.","C":"The solicitor must comply with equality law and professional obligations and should not unlawfully discriminate in providing legal services.","D":"A firm's commercial status removes SRA equality duties.","E":"Discrimination is permitted whenever another client would be available."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Solicitors and firms are subject to equality legislation and SRA professional obligations concerning equality, diversity and inclusion. Unlawful discrimination in providing services is not permitted.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that solicitors may discriminate freely when choosing clients because professional services are outside equality law. That does not match the controlling rule here. Professional autonomy in accepting work operates within equality law and regulatory duties.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that only courts, not law firms, are subject to equality obligations. That does not match the controlling rule here. Professional autonomy in accepting work operates within equality law and regulatory duties.","C":"Correct. Solicitors and firms are subject to equality legislation and SRA professional obligations concerning equality, diversity and inclusion. Unlawful discrimination in providing services is not permitted.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that a firm's commercial status removes SRA equality duties. That does not match the controlling rule here. Professional autonomy in accepting work operates within equality law and regulatory duties.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that discrimination is permitted whenever another client would be available. That does not match the controlling rule here. Professional autonomy in accepting work operates within equality law and regulatory duties."},"primary_legal_authority":"Equality Act 2010; SRA Principles and Codes of Conduct.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Professional autonomy in accepting work operates within equality law and regulatory duties.","common_trap":"Treating legal services as exempt from generally applicable anti-discrimination requirements.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"40847aec93cf4ec2"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0679","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Confidentiality — disclosure required or permitted by law","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor holds confidential client information. A valid statutory obligation requires specified information to be disclosed to a public authority, and no legal privilege prevents disclosure.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Confidentiality is absolute and overrides every statutory disclosure obligation.","B":"A solicitor may disclose the client's entire file whenever one item is legally required.","C":"Only the client can ever authorise disclosure in any circumstances.","D":"The duty of confidentiality is subject to disclosure required or permitted by law, so the solicitor should comply to the extent legally required while avoiding unnecessary disclosure.","E":"The existence of a legal duty automatically destroys privilege over unrelated material."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The SRA confidentiality duty is subject to disclosure required or permitted by law. The solicitor should identify the legal basis and scope and disclose no more than is justified.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that confidentiality is absolute and overrides every statutory disclosure obligation. That does not match the controlling rule here. Confidentiality is fundamental but operates alongside lawful disclosure obligations and legal professional privilege.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that a solicitor may disclose the client's entire file whenever one item is legally required. That does not match the controlling rule here. Confidentiality is fundamental but operates alongside lawful disclosure obligations and legal professional privilege.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that only the client can ever authorise disclosure in any circumstances. That does not match the controlling rule here. Confidentiality is fundamental but operates alongside lawful disclosure obligations and legal professional privilege.","D":"Correct. The SRA confidentiality duty is subject to disclosure required or permitted by law. The solicitor should identify the legal basis and scope and disclose no more than is justified.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that the existence of a legal duty automatically destroys privilege over unrelated material. That does not match the controlling rule here. Confidentiality is fundamental but operates alongside lawful disclosure obligations and legal professional privilege."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, para 6.3; applicable statutory disclosure regime.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Confidentiality is fundamental but operates alongside lawful disclosure obligations and legal professional privilege.","common_trap":"Treating confidentiality as absolute, or treating a narrow disclosure duty as permission for wholesale disclosure.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f0db68644adee95c"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0680","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Formation — certainty","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two businesses sign a document stating that one 'may supply goods in the future on prices and quantities to be agreed later', with no objective machinery for resolving those essential matters and no course of dealing that supplies them.\n\nWhich formation issue is most directly raised?","options":{"A":"Whether consideration must always be monetary.","B":"Whether every commercial agreement must be executed as a deed.","C":"Whether frustration occurred before formation.","D":"Whether the postal rule applies.","E":"Whether the purported agreement is too uncertain or incomplete to create an enforceable contractual obligation."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"An enforceable contract requires sufficient certainty and completeness. An agreement to agree essential terms in the future may be unenforceable where no objective standard or machinery supplies the missing content.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that whether consideration must always be monetary. That does not match the controlling rule here. Courts seek to uphold bargains where possible, but cannot create essential contractual terms the parties left genuinely unresolved.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that whether every commercial agreement must be executed as a deed. That does not match the controlling rule here. Courts seek to uphold bargains where possible, but cannot create essential contractual terms the parties left genuinely unresolved.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that whether frustration occurred before formation. That does not match the controlling rule here. Courts seek to uphold bargains where possible, but cannot create essential contractual terms the parties left genuinely unresolved.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that whether the postal rule applies. That does not match the controlling rule here. Courts seek to uphold bargains where possible, but cannot create essential contractual terms the parties left genuinely unresolved.","E":"Correct. An enforceable contract requires sufficient certainty and completeness. An agreement to agree essential terms in the future may be unenforceable where no objective standard or machinery supplies the missing content."},"primary_legal_authority":"May & Butcher Ltd v R [1934] 2 KB 17; certainty principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Courts seek to uphold bargains where possible, but cannot create essential contractual terms the parties left genuinely unresolved.","common_trap":"Assuming commercial intention alone cures an agreement that lacks sufficiently certain essential obligations.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"970803bc829a8d6a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0681","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Breach — anticipatory repudiation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contractor unequivocally tells the customer, one month before performance is due, that it will not perform any of its contractual obligations. The threatened non-performance would be repudiatory.\n\nWhich choice is generally available to the innocent party?","options":{"A":"The innocent party may ordinarily accept the anticipatory repudiation and terminate, claiming damages, or in appropriate circumstances affirm the contract and keep it alive.","B":"The innocent party must always wait until the contractual performance date before taking any action.","C":"Anticipatory repudiation automatically rescinds the contract without any election.","D":"The innocent party can never claim damages until actual non-performance occurs.","E":"Any advance statement of difficulty is necessarily repudiatory."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"An unequivocal anticipatory repudiation gives the innocent party an election: accept the repudiation and treat the contract as terminated for breach, or, subject to limits, affirm and maintain the contract.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. An unequivocal anticipatory repudiation gives the innocent party an election: accept the repudiation and treat the contract as terminated for breach, or, subject to limits, affirm and maintain the contract.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that the innocent party must always wait until the contractual performance date before taking any action. That does not match the controlling rule here. Repudiation before the due date can trigger remedies before actual performance falls due if the innocent party accepts it.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that anticipatory repudiation automatically rescinds the contract without any election. That does not match the controlling rule here. Repudiation before the due date can trigger remedies before actual performance falls due if the innocent party accepts it.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that the innocent party can never claim damages until actual non-performance occurs. That does not match the controlling rule here. Repudiation before the due date can trigger remedies before actual performance falls due if the innocent party accepts it.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that any advance statement of difficulty is necessarily repudiatory. That does not match the controlling rule here. Repudiation before the due date can trigger remedies before actual performance falls due if the innocent party accepts it."},"primary_legal_authority":"Hochster v De La Tour (1853) 2 E & B 678; White & Carter (Councils) Ltd v McGregor [1962] AC 413.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Repudiation before the due date can trigger remedies before actual performance falls due if the innocent party accepts it.","common_trap":"Confusing repudiation with automatic termination; termination generally depends on the innocent party's election.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8a24ac8877f795f6"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0682","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Consumer Rights Act — services","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A consumer pays a trader to provide a service. The written contract says nothing about the standard of performance.\n\nWhich term does the Consumer Rights Act 2015 ordinarily imply?","options":{"A":"The trader guarantees a perfect result in every case.","B":"The trader must perform the service with reasonable care and skill.","C":"No quality obligation exists unless expressly written.","D":"The consumer must prove the trader promised to use the highest skill available in the market.","E":"The service provisions apply only to sales of physical goods."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Consumer Rights Act implies a term that a trader performing a service for a consumer must exercise reasonable care and skill.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that the trader guarantees a perfect result in every case. That does not match the controlling rule here. Consumer service contracts carry a statutory reasonable-care-and-skill term even if the contract is silent.","B":"Correct. The Consumer Rights Act implies a term that a trader performing a service for a consumer must exercise reasonable care and skill.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that no quality obligation exists unless expressly written. That does not match the controlling rule here. Consumer service contracts carry a statutory reasonable-care-and-skill term even if the contract is silent.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that the consumer must prove the trader promised to use the highest skill available in the market. That does not match the controlling rule here. Consumer service contracts carry a statutory reasonable-care-and-skill term even if the contract is silent.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that the service provisions apply only to sales of physical goods. That does not match the controlling rule here. Consumer service contracts carry a statutory reasonable-care-and-skill term even if the contract is silent."},"primary_legal_authority":"Consumer Rights Act 2015, s 49.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Consumer service contracts carry a statutory reasonable-care-and-skill term even if the contract is silent.","common_trap":"Confusing a duty of reasonable care and skill with a strict guarantee of outcome.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4c90bc723786cb2d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0683","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — intervening acts","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant negligently injures a claimant. During reasonable medical treatment for that injury, an ordinary treatment complication worsens the claimant's condition. The treatment was not so independent or extraordinary as to eclipse the original wrongdoing.\n\nWhich causation principle is most relevant?","options":{"A":"Any medical intervention automatically breaks causation.","B":"The original defendant is liable only for harm occurring before treatment begins.","C":"The medical treatment will not ordinarily break the chain of causation merely because it contributes to the eventual harm.","D":"A claimant's decision to seek treatment is always a novus actus interveniens.","E":"Causation in tort depends solely on the chronology of events."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A subsequent act breaks legal causation only where it has the necessary independent and overwhelming character. Reasonable medical treatment and ordinary complications commonly remain within the causal sequence initiated by the defendant.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that any medical intervention automatically breaks causation. That does not match the controlling rule here. Later events do not automatically sever causation; the court assesses whether the intervening event is sufficient to break the chain.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that the original defendant is liable only for harm occurring before treatment begins. That does not match the controlling rule here. Later events do not automatically sever causation; the court assesses whether the intervening event is sufficient to break the chain.","C":"Correct. A subsequent act breaks legal causation only where it has the necessary independent and overwhelming character. Reasonable medical treatment and ordinary complications commonly remain within the causal sequence initiated by the defendant.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that a claimant's decision to seek treatment is always a novus actus interveniens. That does not match the controlling rule here. Later events do not automatically sever causation; the court assesses whether the intervening event is sufficient to break the chain.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that causation in tort depends solely on the chronology of events. That does not match the controlling rule here. Later events do not automatically sever causation; the court assesses whether the intervening event is sufficient to break the chain."},"primary_legal_authority":"Webb v Barclays Bank plc [2001] EWCA Civ 1141; causation principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Later events do not automatically sever causation; the court assesses whether the intervening event is sufficient to break the chain.","common_trap":"Treating every subsequent contribution to harm as a superseding cause.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b7e6a569b6c5681d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0684","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Nuisance — remedies","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A continuing private nuisance substantially interferes with a landowner's use of land. Liability is established. The claimant asks what remedies may be available.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Damages are the only possible remedy for private nuisance.","B":"An injunction must automatically issue in every nuisance case.","C":"Nuisance law provides criminal punishment but no civil remedy.","D":"Depending on the circumstances, remedies can include damages and an injunction, with equitable relief governed by judicial discretion.","E":"The claimant can recover only nominal damages even for proven substantial loss."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Private nuisance may support damages and equitable relief. Injunctions are discretionary, and modern authority emphasises consideration of the circumstances rather than an automatic rule.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that damages are the only possible remedy for private nuisance. That does not match the controlling rule here. Established nuisance can attract compensatory and, where appropriate, injunctive relief.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that an injunction must automatically issue in every nuisance case. That does not match the controlling rule here. Established nuisance can attract compensatory and, where appropriate, injunctive relief.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that nuisance law provides criminal punishment but no civil remedy. That does not match the controlling rule here. Established nuisance can attract compensatory and, where appropriate, injunctive relief.","D":"Correct. Private nuisance may support damages and equitable relief. Injunctions are discretionary, and modern authority emphasises consideration of the circumstances rather than an automatic rule.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that the claimant can recover only nominal damages even for proven substantial loss. That does not match the controlling rule here. Established nuisance can attract compensatory and, where appropriate, injunctive relief."},"primary_legal_authority":"Coventry v Lawrence [2014] UKSC 13; Senior Courts Act 1981, s 50.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Established nuisance can attract compensatory and, where appropriate, injunctive relief.","common_trap":"Treating equitable relief as either unavailable or mechanically automatic.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"10cd7ee9f7c84dc7"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0685","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Defamation — privilege","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A witness makes a defamatory statement while giving evidence in court, and the statement is relevant to the proceedings. The claimant seeks to sue the witness in defamation because the allegation was false.\n\nWhich defence is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Only honest opinion can protect evidence given in court.","B":"Judicial proceedings attract no special defamation protection.","C":"Privilege applies only if the witness proves the statement true.","D":"Qualified privilege is necessarily defeated merely because the statement is defamatory.","E":"Absolute privilege ordinarily protects statements made in the course of judicial proceedings within the scope of the privilege."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Statements made by judges, advocates, parties and witnesses in the course of judicial proceedings attract absolute privilege within its scope, reflecting the public interest in uninhibited administration of justice.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that only honest opinion can protect evidence given in court. That does not match the controlling rule here. Absolute privilege can bar a defamation action even where the statement cannot be justified as true.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that judicial proceedings attract no special defamation protection. That does not match the controlling rule here. Absolute privilege can bar a defamation action even where the statement cannot be justified as true.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that privilege applies only if the witness proves the statement true. That does not match the controlling rule here. Absolute privilege can bar a defamation action even where the statement cannot be justified as true.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that qualified privilege is necessarily defeated merely because the statement is defamatory. That does not match the controlling rule here. Absolute privilege can bar a defamation action even where the statement cannot be justified as true.","E":"Correct. Statements made by judges, advocates, parties and witnesses in the course of judicial proceedings attract absolute privilege within its scope, reflecting the public interest in uninhibited administration of justice."},"primary_legal_authority":"Judicial-proceedings absolute privilege; Defamation Act 1996, s 14 and common-law principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Absolute privilege can bar a defamation action even where the statement cannot be justified as true.","common_trap":"Confusing absolute privilege with truth, honest opinion or qualified privilege.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"055853239ac9459d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0686","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Statutory interpretation — presumption against ouster","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A statutory clause is argued to exclude all judicial review of a public body's decisions. The wording is disputed.\n\nWhich constitutional interpretive principle is particularly relevant?","options":{"A":"Courts traditionally require clear statutory language before concluding that Parliament has excluded or substantially restricted supervisory judicial review.","B":"Any reference to a decision being 'final' always eliminates judicial review.","C":"Courts may ignore clear statutory language whenever they prefer review.","D":"Ouster clauses are interpreted solely by the public body concerned.","E":"Judicial review is a contractual remedy unaffected by statute."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The rule of law and supervisory jurisdiction underpin a strong interpretive approach to purported ouster provisions. The effect ultimately depends on the statutory language and current authority.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The rule of law and supervisory jurisdiction underpin a strong interpretive approach to purported ouster provisions. The effect ultimately depends on the statutory language and current authority.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that any reference to a decision being 'final' always eliminates judicial review. That does not match the controlling rule here. Restrictions on judicial review require careful construction and cannot ordinarily be inferred casually from general finality language.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that courts may ignore clear statutory language whenever they prefer review. That does not match the controlling rule here. Restrictions on judicial review require careful construction and cannot ordinarily be inferred casually from general finality language.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that ouster clauses are interpreted solely by the public body concerned. That does not match the controlling rule here. Restrictions on judicial review require careful construction and cannot ordinarily be inferred casually from general finality language.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that judicial review is a contractual remedy unaffected by statute. That does not match the controlling rule here. Restrictions on judicial review require careful construction and cannot ordinarily be inferred casually from general finality language."},"primary_legal_authority":"R (Privacy International) v Investigatory Powers Tribunal [2019] UKSC 22; R (Oceana) v Upper Tribunal [2023] UKSC 39; statutory construction principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Restrictions on judicial review require careful construction and cannot ordinarily be inferred casually from general finality language.","common_trap":"Assuming either that every ouster is automatically ineffective or that ordinary finality wording necessarily excludes review.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7a1d97f1bfbc127b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0687","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — legitimate expectation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A public authority publishes a clear and unambiguous promise that a defined procedural consultation will occur before a benefit is withdrawn. An affected person relies on that representation. The authority proposes to withdraw the benefit without consultation and gives no overriding justification.\n\nWhich doctrine is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Parliamentary privilege.","B":"Procedural legitimate expectation.","C":"Promissory estoppel as a complete substitute for public law.","D":"Vicarious liability.","E":"The doctrine of frustration."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A clear representation by a public authority about procedure can create a legitimate expectation that the promised process will be followed unless lawful overriding reasons justify departure.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that parliamentary privilege. That does not match the controlling rule here. Public-law fairness may protect a sufficiently clear procedural promise made by an authority.","B":"Correct. A clear representation by a public authority about procedure can create a legitimate expectation that the promised process will be followed unless lawful overriding reasons justify departure.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that promissory estoppel as a complete substitute for public law. That does not match the controlling rule here. Public-law fairness may protect a sufficiently clear procedural promise made by an authority.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that vicarious liability. That does not match the controlling rule here. Public-law fairness may protect a sufficiently clear procedural promise made by an authority.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that the doctrine of frustration. That does not match the controlling rule here. Public-law fairness may protect a sufficiently clear procedural promise made by an authority."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v North and East Devon Health Authority, ex p Coughlan [2001] QB 213; legitimate-expectation principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Public-law fairness may protect a sufficiently clear procedural promise made by an authority.","common_trap":"Confusing public-law legitimate expectation with private-law contractual or estoppel doctrines.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d716220da8e83186"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0688","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Courts — civil appeal permission","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A party loses a civil case and wants to appeal. The party assumes there is an unrestricted automatic right to a full rehearing.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Every civil litigant has an automatic right to a complete rehearing.","B":"Permission is required only for appeals to the Supreme Court.","C":"Civil appeals ordinarily require permission, applying the relevant CPR test, and an appeal is generally a review rather than a rehearing unless the rules or court provide otherwise.","D":"An appeal can never challenge an error of law.","E":"The trial judge's decision is immune from appellate review."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"CPR Part 52 generally requires permission to appeal and provides that appeals are normally by way of review unless a practice direction or court orders otherwise.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that every civil litigant has an automatic right to a complete rehearing. That does not match the controlling rule here. Civil appellate procedure is permission-based and ordinarily reviews the lower court's decision rather than rerunning the entire case.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that permission is required only for appeals to the Supreme Court. That does not match the controlling rule here. Civil appellate procedure is permission-based and ordinarily reviews the lower court's decision rather than rerunning the entire case.","C":"Correct. CPR Part 52 generally requires permission to appeal and provides that appeals are normally by way of review unless a practice direction or court orders otherwise.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that an appeal can never challenge an error of law. That does not match the controlling rule here. Civil appellate procedure is permission-based and ordinarily reviews the lower court's decision rather than rerunning the entire case.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that the trial judge's decision is immune from appellate review. That does not match the controlling rule here. Civil appellate procedure is permission-based and ordinarily reviews the lower court's decision rather than rerunning the entire case."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, Part 52.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Civil appellate procedure is permission-based and ordinarily reviews the lower court's decision rather than rerunning the entire case.","common_trap":"Assuming an appeal is an automatic second trial.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"d1f2d67f40b9ddde"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0689","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — burden of proof and suspicious circumstances","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A will appears duly executed, but the principal beneficiary prepared it and the surrounding circumstances create serious doubt that the testator knew and approved its contents.\n\nWhich is the best general approach?","options":{"A":"Due execution conclusively proves knowledge and approval in every case.","B":"The challenger always bears every evidential burden regardless of suspicious circumstances.","C":"A beneficiary who helped prepare a will is automatically guilty of undue influence.","D":"Although due execution can support a presumption of knowledge and approval, suspicious circumstances may require the person propounding the will to satisfy the court that the testator knew and approved its contents.","E":"Suspicious circumstances automatically invalidate the will without evidence."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Probate law distinguishes formal execution, capacity, knowledge and approval, and undue influence. Where circumstances excite the court's suspicion, the propounder may need to dispel that suspicion and establish knowledge and approval.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that due execution conclusively proves knowledge and approval in every case. That does not match the controlling rule here. A formally executed will is not immune from scrutiny where the circumstances cast genuine doubt on the testator's knowledge of its contents.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that the challenger always bears every evidential burden regardless of suspicious circumstances. That does not match the controlling rule here. A formally executed will is not immune from scrutiny where the circumstances cast genuine doubt on the testator's knowledge of its contents.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that a beneficiary who helped prepare a will is automatically guilty of undue influence. That does not match the controlling rule here. A formally executed will is not immune from scrutiny where the circumstances cast genuine doubt on the testator's knowledge of its contents.","D":"Correct. Probate law distinguishes formal execution, capacity, knowledge and approval, and undue influence. Where circumstances excite the court's suspicion, the propounder may need to dispel that suspicion and establish knowledge and approval.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that suspicious circumstances automatically invalidate the will without evidence. That does not match the controlling rule here. A formally executed will is not immune from scrutiny where the circumstances cast genuine doubt on the testator's knowledge of its contents."},"primary_legal_authority":"Barry v Butlin (1838) 2 Moo PC 480; Gill v Woodall [2010] EWCA Civ 1430.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A formally executed will is not immune from scrutiny where the circumstances cast genuine doubt on the testator's knowledge of its contents.","common_trap":"Treating the presumption arising from due execution as irrebuttable or treating suspicion itself as automatic invalidity.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"edfeb548451d5ff3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0690","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Administration — devastavit","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A personal representative knowingly distributes the entire estate to beneficiaries without retaining enough to pay an enforceable estate debt of which the representative had notice. The creditor cannot recover from remaining estate assets because none remain.\n\nWhich personal-representative risk is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"The creditor's debt is automatically extinguished by the distribution.","B":"Personal representatives can never incur personal liability when acting in that capacity.","C":"Beneficiaries always bear exclusive liability even where the representative knowingly misadministered the estate.","D":"The distribution converts the debt into inheritance tax.","E":"Personal liability for maladministration or devastavit arising from improper administration of estate assets."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Personal representatives must administer estate assets properly and meet liabilities before distributing the residue. Improper distribution with notice of liabilities can expose a representative personally for loss caused by the maladministration.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that the creditor's debt is automatically extinguished by the distribution. That does not match the controlling rule here. Distribution does not protect a personal representative who improperly dissipates estate assets needed to satisfy known liabilities.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that personal representatives can never incur personal liability when acting in that capacity. That does not match the controlling rule here. Distribution does not protect a personal representative who improperly dissipates estate assets needed to satisfy known liabilities.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that beneficiaries always bear exclusive liability even where the representative knowingly misadministered the estate. That does not match the controlling rule here. Distribution does not protect a personal representative who improperly dissipates estate assets needed to satisfy known liabilities.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that the distribution converts the debt into inheritance tax. That does not match the controlling rule here. Distribution does not protect a personal representative who improperly dissipates estate assets needed to satisfy known liabilities.","E":"Correct. Personal representatives must administer estate assets properly and meet liabilities before distributing the residue. Improper distribution with notice of liabilities can expose a representative personally for loss caused by the maladministration."},"primary_legal_authority":"Administration of Estates Act 1925; personal-representative duties and devastavit principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Distribution does not protect a personal representative who improperly dissipates estate assets needed to satisfy known liabilities.","common_trap":"Assuming representative status gives immunity from consequences of maladministration.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"403a10c982d50dcb"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0691","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Inheritance tax — normal expenditure out of income","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A donor makes regular gifts from surplus income as part of an established pattern. After the gifts, the donor retains sufficient income to maintain the donor's usual standard of living.\n\nWhich IHT exemption may be relevant?","options":{"A":"The exemption for normal expenditure out of income, if the statutory conditions are satisfied.","B":"The spouse exemption automatically applies to gifts to any recipient.","C":"Every regular gift is exempt regardless of its source.","D":"The annual exemption is the only lifetime-gift exemption recognised by IHT law.","E":"A gift can qualify only if made from capital rather than income."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Section 21 can exempt transfers shown to form part of the transferor's normal expenditure, made out of income, where after allowing for the transfers the transferor retains sufficient income to maintain the usual standard of living.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Section 21 can exempt transfers shown to form part of the transferor's normal expenditure, made out of income, where after allowing for the transfers the transferor retains sufficient income to maintain the usual standard of living.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that the spouse exemption automatically applies to gifts to any recipient. That does not match the controlling rule here. Regular qualifying gifts from surplus income can be exempt independently of the ordinary annual exemption.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that every regular gift is exempt regardless of its source. That does not match the controlling rule here. Regular qualifying gifts from surplus income can be exempt independently of the ordinary annual exemption.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that the annual exemption is the only lifetime-gift exemption recognised by IHT law. That does not match the controlling rule here. Regular qualifying gifts from surplus income can be exempt independently of the ordinary annual exemption.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that a gift can qualify only if made from capital rather than income. That does not match the controlling rule here. Regular qualifying gifts from surplus income can be exempt independently of the ordinary annual exemption."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984, s 21.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Regular qualifying gifts from surplus income can be exempt independently of the ordinary annual exemption.","common_trap":"Assuming regularity alone suffices without examining source of funds and maintenance of the donor's living standard.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"1388509a8f79294e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0692","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Client account — correcting breaches","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A firm discovers that, because of an accounting error, it has improperly withdrawn £2,000 belonging to a client from client account.\n\nWhich is the best immediate regulatory approach?","options":{"A":"The firm may wait until the client notices the shortage.","B":"The firm should promptly correct the breach, including replacing improperly withdrawn client money from its own resources where necessary.","C":"No correction is needed if the mistake was accidental.","D":"The firm should take the same amount from another client's ledger.","E":"The shortage can be written off as a business expense without restoring client funds."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Accounts Rules require firms to correct breaches promptly upon discovery. Where client money has been improperly withdrawn, correction ordinarily requires restoration from the firm's own resources rather than using another client's money.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that the firm may wait until the client notices the shortage. That does not match the controlling rule here. Prompt remediation protects the affected client's funds and the integrity of the client account.","B":"Correct. The Accounts Rules require firms to correct breaches promptly upon discovery. Where client money has been improperly withdrawn, correction ordinarily requires restoration from the firm's own resources rather than using another client's money.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that no correction is needed if the mistake was accidental. That does not match the controlling rule here. Prompt remediation protects the affected client's funds and the integrity of the client account.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that the firm should take the same amount from another client's ledger. That does not match the controlling rule here. Prompt remediation protects the affected client's funds and the integrity of the client account.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that the shortage can be written off as a business expense without restoring client funds. That does not match the controlling rule here. Prompt remediation protects the affected client's funds and the integrity of the client account."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, r 6.1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Prompt remediation protects the affected client's funds and the integrity of the client account.","common_trap":"Treating absence of dishonesty as eliminating the duty to correct an accounting breach.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3f1df7ec7725bc8e"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0693","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustees — appointment and retirement","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A sole trustee of land wishes to retire, but doing so would leave no continuing trustee and no replacement is proposed.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Any sole trustee may retire unilaterally at any moment and leave the trust without a trustee.","B":"Retirement automatically transfers legal title to the beneficiaries.","C":"A trustee cannot simply use the statutory retirement mechanism if its conditions are not met, including the requirement concerning sufficient continuing trustees or appointment of a replacement where applicable.","D":"A trustee can retire only with a court order in every case.","E":"Trustees can never retire."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Trustee Act provides mechanisms for retirement and replacement. Statutory retirement without replacement is subject to conditions designed to ensure proper continuation of the trusteeship.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that any sole trustee may retire unilaterally at any moment and leave the trust without a trustee. That does not match the controlling rule here. Retirement must preserve a legally workable trusteeship and comply with the applicable statutory or trust-instrument mechanism.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that retirement automatically transfers legal title to the beneficiaries. That does not match the controlling rule here. Retirement must preserve a legally workable trusteeship and comply with the applicable statutory or trust-instrument mechanism.","C":"Correct. The Trustee Act provides mechanisms for retirement and replacement. Statutory retirement without replacement is subject to conditions designed to ensure proper continuation of the trusteeship.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that a trustee can retire only with a court order in every case. That does not match the controlling rule here. Retirement must preserve a legally workable trusteeship and comply with the applicable statutory or trust-instrument mechanism.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that trustees can never retire. That does not match the controlling rule here. Retirement must preserve a legally workable trusteeship and comply with the applicable statutory or trust-instrument mechanism."},"primary_legal_authority":"Trustee Act 1925, ss 36 and 39.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Retirement must preserve a legally workable trusteeship and comply with the applicable statutory or trust-instrument mechanism.","common_trap":"Assuming a trustee can abandon office and trust property without satisfying formal requirements.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b4046c6d5235fd2a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0694","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Tracing — mixed bank account","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trustee wrongfully pays £20,000 of trust money into the trustee's personal bank account, mixing it with £20,000 of personal money. The trustee then makes withdrawals. The beneficiary seeks to trace into the mixed fund.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Trust money becomes irretrievably untraceable the instant it is mixed with the trustee's money.","B":"The trustee automatically acquires beneficial ownership of the trust money by mixing.","C":"Tracing is available only into physical cash kept separately.","D":"Equitable tracing rules can identify the beneficiary's interest in a mixed fund and apply presumptions governing withdrawals; mixing does not itself destroy the tracing claim.","E":"The beneficiary can recover only if the bank itself committed the breach of trust."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Equity permits tracing through mixed funds and uses presumptions, including rules developed in Re Hallett's Estate and Re Oatway, to prevent a wrongdoing trustee from defeating beneficiaries merely by mixing and spending funds.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that trust money becomes irretrievably untraceable the instant it is mixed with the trustee's money. That does not match the controlling rule here. Wrongful mixing by a trustee does not by itself defeat the beneficiary's proprietary tracing exercise.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that the trustee automatically acquires beneficial ownership of the trust money by mixing. That does not match the controlling rule here. Wrongful mixing by a trustee does not by itself defeat the beneficiary's proprietary tracing exercise.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that tracing is available only into physical cash kept separately. That does not match the controlling rule here. Wrongful mixing by a trustee does not by itself defeat the beneficiary's proprietary tracing exercise.","D":"Correct. Equity permits tracing through mixed funds and uses presumptions, including rules developed in Re Hallett's Estate and Re Oatway, to prevent a wrongdoing trustee from defeating beneficiaries merely by mixing and spending funds.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that the beneficiary can recover only if the bank itself committed the breach of trust. That does not match the controlling rule here. Wrongful mixing by a trustee does not by itself defeat the beneficiary's proprietary tracing exercise."},"primary_legal_authority":"Re Hallett's Estate (1880) 13 Ch D 696; Re Oatway [1903] 2 Ch 356.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Wrongful mixing by a trustee does not by itself defeat the beneficiary's proprietary tracing exercise.","common_trap":"Assuming tracing requires continuous physical segregation of the original asset.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7feb09e50b11f67d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0695","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Registered land — notices","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A purchaser's solicitor discovers an agreed notice protecting a third party's registrable interest on the charges register of a registered title.\n\nWhat is the principal priority significance of the notice?","options":{"A":"A notice transfers legal ownership of the registered estate to the third party.","B":"Every notice is proof that the protected interest is substantively valid.","C":"Notices are irrelevant to priority.","D":"A notice is the same thing as a restriction regulating registration.","E":"A notice can protect the priority of the interest to which it relates against a later registrable disposition, subject to the Land Registration Act."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Under the Land Registration Act, entry of a notice protects the priority of an interest affecting a registered estate. Registration of a notice does not itself guarantee the substantive validity of the underlying interest.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that a notice transfers legal ownership of the registered estate to the third party. That does not match the controlling rule here. A notice is principally a priority-protection entry, distinct from a restriction and from proof that the underlying right is valid.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that every notice is proof that the protected interest is substantively valid. That does not match the controlling rule here. A notice is principally a priority-protection entry, distinct from a restriction and from proof that the underlying right is valid.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that notices are irrelevant to priority. That does not match the controlling rule here. A notice is principally a priority-protection entry, distinct from a restriction and from proof that the underlying right is valid.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that a notice is the same thing as a restriction regulating registration. That does not match the controlling rule here. A notice is principally a priority-protection entry, distinct from a restriction and from proof that the underlying right is valid.","E":"Correct. Under the Land Registration Act, entry of a notice protects the priority of an interest affecting a registered estate. Registration of a notice does not itself guarantee the substantive validity of the underlying interest."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002, ss 32–33 and 29.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A notice is principally a priority-protection entry, distinct from a restriction and from proof that the underlying right is valid.","common_trap":"Confusing priority protection with creation or conclusive validation of the underlying interest.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"32718be53a87d26b"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0696","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Leases — assignment of old and new tenancies","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A commercial lease granted after 1 January 1996 is assigned by the original tenant to an assignee. The lease is a 'new tenancy' for the purposes of the Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995, and no authorised guarantee agreement or other continuing liability applies.\n\nWhich is the ordinary effect on the outgoing tenant's tenant covenants?","options":{"A":"On a lawful assignment of a new tenancy, the outgoing tenant is generally released from tenant covenants under the statutory regime, subject to exceptions such as an authorised guarantee agreement where validly required.","B":"The original tenant remains liable for all tenant covenants forever in every lease.","C":"Assignment automatically releases the landlord from all landlord covenants but never the tenant.","D":"The 1995 Act applies only to residential licences.","E":"Assignment terminates the lease."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The 1995 Act introduced automatic release of outgoing tenants from tenant covenants on assignment of new tenancies, subject to statutory qualifications including authorised guarantee agreements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The 1995 Act introduced automatic release of outgoing tenants from tenant covenants on assignment of new tenancies, subject to statutory qualifications including authorised guarantee agreements.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that the original tenant remains liable for all tenant covenants forever in every lease. That does not match the controlling rule here. The liability regime for post-1995 new tenancies differs materially from the historic privity-of-contract position.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that assignment automatically releases the landlord from all landlord covenants but never the tenant. That does not match the controlling rule here. The liability regime for post-1995 new tenancies differs materially from the historic privity-of-contract position.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that the 1995 Act applies only to residential licences. That does not match the controlling rule here. The liability regime for post-1995 new tenancies differs materially from the historic privity-of-contract position.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that assignment terminates the lease. That does not match the controlling rule here. The liability regime for post-1995 new tenancies differs materially from the historic privity-of-contract position."},"primary_legal_authority":"Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995, ss 5 and 16.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The liability regime for post-1995 new tenancies differs materially from the historic privity-of-contract position.","common_trap":"Applying the old-tenancy rule of continuing original-tenant liability to every modern commercial lease.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6115ab9853d32022"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0697","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Pre-completion — apportionments","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A commercial property completes halfway through a rent period. Rent payable by an occupational tenant has been paid in advance to the seller for the whole period. The sale contract requires income to be apportioned at completion.\n\nWhat is the conveyancing purpose of the apportionment?","options":{"A":"To alter the legal title before completion.","B":"To allocate the relevant periodic income between seller and buyer by reference to their respective periods of entitlement around completion.","C":"To calculate SDLT by ignoring the purchase price.","D":"To discharge every registered charge automatically.","E":"To replace the completion statement."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Completion apportionments allocate recurring income or outgoings between the parties so that each bears or receives the amount attributable to the appropriate ownership period, according to the contract.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that to alter the legal title before completion. That does not match the controlling rule here. Apportionment adjusts the financial position between seller and buyer at completion without changing the underlying title mechanics.","B":"Correct. Completion apportionments allocate recurring income or outgoings between the parties so that each bears or receives the amount attributable to the appropriate ownership period, according to the contract.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that to calculate SDLT by ignoring the purchase price. That does not match the controlling rule here. Apportionment adjusts the financial position between seller and buyer at completion without changing the underlying title mechanics.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that to discharge every registered charge automatically. That does not match the controlling rule here. Apportionment adjusts the financial position between seller and buyer at completion without changing the underlying title mechanics.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that to replace the completion statement. That does not match the controlling rule here. Apportionment adjusts the financial position between seller and buyer at completion without changing the underlying title mechanics."},"primary_legal_authority":"Standard Conditions of Sale/Standard Commercial Property Conditions; conveyancing completion practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Apportionment adjusts the financial position between seller and buyer at completion without changing the underlying title mechanics.","common_trap":"Confusing completion accounting adjustments with title registration or tax calculation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"23695a2067834d65"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0698","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Lease assignment — licence to assign","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A tenant proposes to assign a commercial lease containing a qualified covenant against assignment without landlord consent. The landlord agrees in principle subject to documented conditions permitted by the lease and law.\n\nWhich document is commonly used to record the landlord's formal consent and related obligations?","options":{"A":"An OS1 priority search.","B":"A transfer of freehold title by the landlord to the assignee.","C":"A licence to assign.","D":"A section 146 notice.","E":"A grant of probate."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A licence to assign commonly records the landlord's consent to a proposed lease assignment and may document permitted conditions, covenants and any authorised guarantee arrangement where applicable.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that an OS1 priority search. That does not match the controlling rule here. Formal landlord consent to a lease assignment is ordinarily documented through a licence to assign.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that a transfer of freehold title by the landlord to the assignee. That does not match the controlling rule here. Formal landlord consent to a lease assignment is ordinarily documented through a licence to assign.","C":"Correct. A licence to assign commonly records the landlord's consent to a proposed lease assignment and may document permitted conditions, covenants and any authorised guarantee arrangement where applicable.","D":"Incorrect. This option states that a section 146 notice. That does not match the controlling rule here. Formal landlord consent to a lease assignment is ordinarily documented through a licence to assign.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that a grant of probate. That does not match the controlling rule here. Formal landlord consent to a lease assignment is ordinarily documented through a licence to assign."},"primary_legal_authority":"Commercial conveyancing practice; Landlord and Tenant Act 1988 and lease alienation provisions.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Formal landlord consent to a lease assignment is ordinarily documented through a licence to assign.","common_trap":"Confusing consent documentation with title searches, forfeiture notices or a disposition of the landlord's freehold.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"6a9d6316123a4a12"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0699","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Fraud — failure to disclose information","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person is under a legal duty to disclose a material fact to another. Intending to make a gain, the person dishonestly fails to disclose that fact.\n\nWhich Fraud Act offence is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Fraud by false representation necessarily, even though no representation was made.","B":"Robbery.","C":"Burglary.","D":"Fraud by failing to disclose information where there is a legal duty to disclose it.","E":"Criminal damage."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 3 of the Fraud Act criminalises dishonest failure to disclose information where the defendant is under a legal duty to disclose it and intends thereby to make a gain or cause/expose another to loss.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option states that fraud by false representation necessarily, even though no representation was made. That does not match the controlling rule here. The section 3 offence specifically addresses dishonest non-disclosure in breach of a legal duty with the required gain/loss intent.","B":"Incorrect. This option states that robbery. That does not match the controlling rule here. The section 3 offence specifically addresses dishonest non-disclosure in breach of a legal duty with the required gain/loss intent.","C":"Incorrect. This option states that burglary. That does not match the controlling rule here. The section 3 offence specifically addresses dishonest non-disclosure in breach of a legal duty with the required gain/loss intent.","D":"Correct. Section 3 of the Fraud Act criminalises dishonest failure to disclose information where the defendant is under a legal duty to disclose it and intends thereby to make a gain or cause/expose another to loss.","E":"Incorrect. This option states that criminal damage. That does not match the controlling rule here. The section 3 offence specifically addresses dishonest non-disclosure in breach of a legal duty with the required gain/loss intent."},"primary_legal_authority":"Fraud Act 2006, ss 1 and 3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The section 3 offence specifically addresses dishonest non-disclosure in breach of a legal duty with the required gain/loss intent.","common_trap":"Assuming all fraud requires an express false statement rather than recognising the separate non-disclosure offence.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8429e0afb9f40783"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0700","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Sentencing — totality: concurrent or consecutive sentences","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A court sentences an offender for several offences arising from one incident. Individual sentences that are proper in isolation would produce an excessive overall punishment if simply added together.\n\nHow should the totality principle affect the court's approach?","options":{"A":"The court should consider whether sentences should be concurrent or consecutive and adjust the overall sentence so that it is just and proportionate to the offending as a whole.","B":"The court must always make every sentence consecutive.","C":"The court must always make every sentence concurrent.","D":"The longest individual sentence must automatically be doubled.","E":"Totality applies only to civil costs orders."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The totality principle requires the sentencing court to stand back from individual counts and ensure that the aggregate sentence is just and proportionate, including through the appropriate use of concurrent and consecutive sentences.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The totality principle requires the sentencing court to stand back from individual counts and ensure that the aggregate sentence is just and proportionate, including through the appropriate use of concurrent and consecutive sentences.","B":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. Totality concerns the overall punitive effect, not a mechanical rule that sentences must always run in one particular way.","C":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. Totality concerns the overall punitive effect, not a mechanical rule that sentences must always run in one particular way.","D":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. Totality concerns the overall punitive effect, not a mechanical rule that sentences must always run in one particular way.","E":"Incorrect. This incorrectly makes a non-exclusive factor the sole legal test. Totality concerns the overall punitive effect, not a mechanical rule that sentences must always run in one particular way."},"primary_legal_authority":"Sentencing Council, Totality guideline","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Totality concerns the overall punitive effect, not a mechanical rule that sentences must always run in one particular way.","common_trap":"Applying the headline doctrine without identifying the distinct consequence, exception or procedural stage tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ed16b85985409ad3"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0701","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — derivative claims: ratification and permission","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A member seeks permission to continue a derivative claim alleging breach of duty by a director. Before the permission decision, the company validly ratifies the relevant conduct under the Companies Act.\n\nHow is ratification relevant to the derivative-claim permission stage?","options":{"A":"Valid ratification is highly significant because the statutory permission regime requires the court to refuse permission where the relevant act or omission has been authorised or ratified in the circumstances specified by the Act.","B":"Ratification is legally irrelevant to derivative proceedings.","C":"Ratification automatically gives the member a personal damages claim.","D":"Any director accused of wrongdoing may alone ratify their own conduct for these purposes.","E":"Ratification converts the derivative claim into an unfair-prejudice petition automatically."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"The statutory derivative-claim regime expressly connects permission with authorisation and ratification. Where the statutory mandatory-refusal condition is met, permission must be refused.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. The statutory derivative-claim regime expressly connects permission with authorisation and ratification. Where the statutory mandatory-refusal condition is met, permission must be refused.","B":"Incorrect. This gives the wrong legal consequence for the facts and does not satisfy the governing test. Derivative claims protect the company's cause of action but remain subject to the Companies Act's corporate authorisation and ratification framework.","C":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. Derivative claims protect the company's cause of action but remain subject to the Companies Act's corporate authorisation and ratification framework.","D":"Incorrect. This gives the wrong legal consequence for the facts and does not satisfy the governing test. Derivative claims protect the company's cause of action but remain subject to the Companies Act's corporate authorisation and ratification framework.","E":"Incorrect. This is an overbroad automatic rule and ignores the qualification tested in the scenario. Derivative claims protect the company's cause of action but remain subject to the Companies Act's corporate authorisation and ratification framework."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 239 and 263","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Derivative claims protect the company's cause of action but remain subject to the Companies Act's corporate authorisation and ratification framework.","common_trap":"Applying the headline doctrine without identifying the distinct consequence, exception or procedural stage tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5558147824a43882"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0702","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — unfair prejudice","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A controlling shareholder causes a company to divert business to another company the controller owns, materially prejudicing the economic interests of the minority shareholder.\n\nWhich shareholder remedy is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"A derivative claim is the only remedy that can ever be used by a shareholder.","B":"A petition alleging that the company's affairs are being conducted in a manner unfairly prejudicial to the interests of members under section 994.","C":"Automatic dissolution without a court application.","D":"A criminal prosecution under the Theft Act is the statutory shareholder remedy.","E":"A petition under section 994 requires proof that the company is insolvent."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 994 permits a member to petition where the company's affairs are or have been conducted in a manner unfairly prejudicial to members' interests. The court has broad remedial powers, commonly including a share-purchase order.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a derivative claim is the only remedy that can ever be used by a shareholder. On these facts, the controlling point is: Unfair-prejudice proceedings protect members against qualifying unfairly prejudicial conduct in company affairs.","B":"Correct. Section 994 permits a member to petition where the company's affairs are or have been conducted in a manner unfairly prejudicial to members' interests. The court has broad remedial powers, commonly including a share-purchase order.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that automatic dissolution without a court application. On these facts, the controlling point is: Unfair-prejudice proceedings protect members against qualifying unfairly prejudicial conduct in company affairs.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a criminal prosecution under the Theft Act is the statutory shareholder remedy. On these facts, the controlling point is: Unfair-prejudice proceedings protect members against qualifying unfairly prejudicial conduct in company affairs.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a petition under section 994 requires proof that the company is insolvent. On these facts, the controlling point is: Unfair-prejudice proceedings protect members against qualifying unfairly prejudicial conduct in company affairs."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 994–996.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Unfair-prejudice proceedings protect members against qualifying unfairly prejudicial conduct in company affairs.","common_trap":"Assuming every shareholder complaint must be framed solely as a derivative claim for loss to the company.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"dfc8708e3f243bd5"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0703","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — removal of directors","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company's articles purport to state that a director can never be removed before expiry of a five-year appointment. Members holding a sufficient majority want removal at a properly convened meeting and comply with the special-notice procedure.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The articles can eliminate the statutory removal power entirely.","B":"Removal always requires a 75% special resolution.","C":"The statutory power permits removal by ordinary resolution notwithstanding anything in an agreement or the articles, while preserving any separate rights to compensation or damages.","D":"A director can be removed only by the board.","E":"A five-year appointment prevents removal for its entire term."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Section 168 gives a company power by ordinary resolution at a meeting to remove a director before expiry of office notwithstanding anything in an agreement or the articles, while section 168 does not deprive the director of contractual compensation rights.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the articles can eliminate the statutory removal power entirely. On these facts, the controlling point is: The statutory removal mechanism cannot simply be contracted out of through the articles.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that removal always requires a 75% special resolution. On these facts, the controlling point is: The statutory removal mechanism cannot simply be contracted out of through the articles.","C":"Correct. Section 168 gives a company power by ordinary resolution at a meeting to remove a director before expiry of office notwithstanding anything in an agreement or the articles, while section 168 does not deprive the director of contractual compensation rights.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a director can be removed only by the board. On these facts, the controlling point is: The statutory removal mechanism cannot simply be contracted out of through the articles.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a five-year appointment prevents removal for its entire term. On these facts, the controlling point is: The statutory removal mechanism cannot simply be contracted out of through the articles."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 168–169 and 312.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The statutory removal mechanism cannot simply be contracted out of through the articles.","common_trap":"Confusing removal from office with the separate contractual consequences of terminating a service arrangement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5f6db49af509e7ed"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0704","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — fixed-charge priority","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An insolvent company owns an asset subject to a valid fixed charge. The liquidator sells the asset and incurs properly attributable realisation expenses.\n\nWhich is the best general priority proposition concerning the net fixed-charge realisation?","options":{"A":"Ordinary unsecured creditors always rank ahead of a fixed-charge holder.","B":"A fixed charge becomes an unsecured debt on liquidation.","C":"Shareholders take fixed-charge proceeds before the secured creditor.","D":"Subject to applicable expenses and statutory rules, the fixed-charge holder ordinarily has priority to the proceeds of its charged asset ahead of ordinary unsecured creditors.","E":"The prescribed part is automatically carved out of every fixed-charge realisation."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A valid fixed charge gives proprietary security over the charged asset and ordinarily priority in its realisation proceeds, subject to relevant costs and statutory priority rules. The prescribed-part regime principally concerns qualifying floating-charge property.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that ordinary unsecured creditors always rank ahead of a fixed-charge holder. On these facts, the controlling point is: Fixed security ordinarily ranks against its charged asset ahead of the general body of unsecured creditors.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a fixed charge becomes an unsecured debt on liquidation. On these facts, the controlling point is: Fixed security ordinarily ranks against its charged asset ahead of the general body of unsecured creditors.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that shareholders take fixed-charge proceeds before the secured creditor. On these facts, the controlling point is: Fixed security ordinarily ranks against its charged asset ahead of the general body of unsecured creditors.","D":"Correct. A valid fixed charge gives proprietary security over the charged asset and ordinarily priority in its realisation proceeds, subject to relevant costs and statutory priority rules. The prescribed-part regime principally concerns qualifying floating-charge property.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the prescribed part is automatically carved out of every fixed-charge realisation. On these facts, the controlling point is: Fixed security ordinarily ranks against its charged asset ahead of the general body of unsecured creditors."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986; insolvency priority principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Fixed security ordinarily ranks against its charged asset ahead of the general body of unsecured creditors.","common_trap":"Applying floating-charge or unsecured-distribution rules indiscriminately to fixed-charge proceeds.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8cfd0eac11fc68b9"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0705","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Statements of case — defence","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant is served with particulars of claim and intends to contest the claim. No extension has been agreed and the defendant files an acknowledgment of service in time.\n\nWhich is the ordinary CPR consequence for the defence deadline?","options":{"A":"An acknowledgment of service eliminates the need for a defence.","B":"The defence is always due seven days after issue of the claim form.","C":"The claimant chooses the defence deadline without reference to the CPR.","D":"The defendant has six months to defend merely because an acknowledgment was filed.","E":"Filing a timely acknowledgment of service ordinarily extends the time for filing the defence to 28 days after service of the particulars of claim."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Under CPR Part 15, the ordinary defence period is 14 days after service of particulars, but a defendant who files an acknowledgment of service under Part 10 ordinarily has 28 days after service of particulars.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that an acknowledgment of service eliminates the need for a defence. On these facts, the controlling point is: A timely acknowledgment ordinarily extends, rather than removes, the defence-filing deadline.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the defence is always due seven days after issue of the claim form. On these facts, the controlling point is: A timely acknowledgment ordinarily extends, rather than removes, the defence-filing deadline.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the claimant chooses the defence deadline without reference to the CPR. On these facts, the controlling point is: A timely acknowledgment ordinarily extends, rather than removes, the defence-filing deadline.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the defendant has six months to defend merely because an acknowledgment was filed. On these facts, the controlling point is: A timely acknowledgment ordinarily extends, rather than removes, the defence-filing deadline.","E":"Correct. Under CPR Part 15, the ordinary defence period is 14 days after service of particulars, but a defendant who files an acknowledgment of service under Part 10 ordinarily has 28 days after service of particulars."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, rr 10.3 and 15.4.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A timely acknowledgment ordinarily extends, rather than removes, the defence-filing deadline.","common_trap":"Confusing acknowledgment of service with the substantive defence or with an indefinite extension.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"27156a659d84c384"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0706","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Witness statements — use at trial","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A witness has served a compliant witness statement and will give oral evidence at trial. No court order provides otherwise.\n\nWhich is the ordinary effect of the witness statement at trial?","options":{"A":"It generally stands as the witness's evidence in chief, subject to the CPR and the court's control.","B":"It has no evidential function once trial begins.","C":"The witness must ordinarily recite the entire statement orally before cross-examination.","D":"Serving a witness statement automatically makes all its contents admissible regardless of evidential rules.","E":"A witness statement can be used only for settlement negotiations."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"CPR 32.5 provides that where a witness is called, the witness statement ordinarily stands as evidence in chief unless the court orders otherwise, while admissibility and supplementary questioning remain controlled by the rules.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. CPR 32.5 provides that where a witness is called, the witness statement ordinarily stands as evidence in chief unless the court orders otherwise, while admissibility and supplementary questioning remain controlled by the rules.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that it has no evidential function once trial begins. On these facts, the controlling point is: A served witness statement ordinarily replaces lengthy oral evidence in chief rather than becoming irrelevant.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the witness must ordinarily recite the entire statement orally before cross-examination. On these facts, the controlling point is: A served witness statement ordinarily replaces lengthy oral evidence in chief rather than becoming irrelevant.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that serving a witness statement automatically makes all its contents admissible regardless of evidential rules. On these facts, the controlling point is: A served witness statement ordinarily replaces lengthy oral evidence in chief rather than becoming irrelevant.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a witness statement can be used only for settlement negotiations. On these facts, the controlling point is: A served witness statement ordinarily replaces lengthy oral evidence in chief rather than becoming irrelevant."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, rr 32.4–32.5.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A served witness statement ordinarily replaces lengthy oral evidence in chief rather than becoming irrelevant.","common_trap":"Confusing procedural use of the statement with automatic admissibility of every assertion it contains.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"91309d7bb47d95c4"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0707","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Dispute Resolution","subtopic":"Security for costs","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A corporate claimant appears unable to pay the defendant's costs if ordered to do so. The defendant seeks protection while the litigation continues.\n\nWhich procedural application may be relevant if the CPR conditions and discretion are satisfied?","options":{"A":"A third-party debt order before judgment.","B":"An application for security for costs under CPR Part 25.","C":"A declaration of incompatibility.","D":"An application for probate.","E":"A mandatory Part 36 offer."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"CPR 25.12–25.13 permit a defendant to seek security for costs where a specified condition is met and it is just to make the order. One statutory situation concerns certain companies where there is reason to believe they will be unable to pay the defendant's costs.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a third-party debt order before judgment. On these facts, the controlling point is: Security for costs can protect a defendant against a qualifying risk that an adverse costs order will not be met.","B":"Correct. CPR 25.12–25.13 permit a defendant to seek security for costs where a specified condition is met and it is just to make the order. One statutory situation concerns certain companies where there is reason to believe they will be unable to pay the defendant's costs.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a declaration of incompatibility. On these facts, the controlling point is: Security for costs can protect a defendant against a qualifying risk that an adverse costs order will not be met.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that an application for probate. On these facts, the controlling point is: Security for costs can protect a defendant against a qualifying risk that an adverse costs order will not be met.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a mandatory Part 36 offer. On these facts, the controlling point is: Security for costs can protect a defendant against a qualifying risk that an adverse costs order will not be met."},"primary_legal_authority":"Civil Procedure Rules, rr 25.12–25.13.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Security for costs can protect a defendant against a qualifying risk that an adverse costs order will not be met.","common_trap":"Confusing interim security for prospective costs with post-judgment enforcement.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3a3232b39f812433"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0708","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Professional conduct — reporting serious breaches","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A solicitor becomes aware of another regulated person's conduct that the solicitor reasonably believes amounts to a serious breach of regulatory arrangements. No legal restriction prevents reporting.\n\nWhich professional obligation is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"A solicitor must never report another solicitor because confidentiality between professionals is absolute.","B":"Only criminal convictions can be reported to the SRA.","C":"The duty to report promptly to the SRA or another approved regulator matters reasonably believed capable of amounting to a serious breach, subject to the applicable Code.","D":"Reporting is optional whenever the persons work at different firms.","E":"The duty arises only after a court has made a final finding."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The SRA Code imposes reporting obligations concerning serious breaches, alongside duties to cooperate with regulators. The precise obligation depends on the information held and applicable protections.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a solicitor must never report another solicitor because confidentiality between professionals is absolute. On these facts, the controlling point is: Serious regulatory concerns can trigger proactive reporting duties before a final judicial determination.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that only criminal convictions can be reported to the SRA. On these facts, the controlling point is: Serious regulatory concerns can trigger proactive reporting duties before a final judicial determination.","C":"Correct. The SRA Code imposes reporting obligations concerning serious breaches, alongside duties to cooperate with regulators. The precise obligation depends on the information held and applicable protections.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that reporting is optional whenever the persons work at different firms. On these facts, the controlling point is: Serious regulatory concerns can trigger proactive reporting duties before a final judicial determination.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the duty arises only after a court has made a final finding. On these facts, the controlling point is: Serious regulatory concerns can trigger proactive reporting duties before a final judicial determination."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, paras 7.7–7.8.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Serious regulatory concerns can trigger proactive reporting duties before a final judicial determination.","common_trap":"Assuming regulatory reporting is limited to proven criminal conduct.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"7ffe16edcde0430d"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0709","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Legal Services","subtopic":"Client identification — authority to act","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A person contacts a solicitor claiming to instruct the firm on behalf of a company, but the solicitor has no reliable information establishing that person's authority.\n\nWhich is the best general approach?","options":{"A":"The solicitor must accept the person's authority merely because a company name was mentioned.","B":"Authority is irrelevant if the matter is urgent.","C":"Only a court can verify who may instruct for a company.","D":"The solicitor should take appropriate steps to establish the client's identity and the purported representative's authority before relying on the instructions.","E":"A solicitor should take instructions from any employee without considering authority."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Client onboarding requires clarity about who the client is and, where instructions come through another person, whether that person is authorised. AML/customer-due-diligence obligations may impose additional requirements depending on the matter.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the solicitor must accept the person's authority merely because a company name was mentioned. On these facts, the controlling point is: A solicitor should know the identity of the client and have a proper basis for accepting instructions through a representative.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that authority is irrelevant if the matter is urgent. On these facts, the controlling point is: A solicitor should know the identity of the client and have a proper basis for accepting instructions through a representative.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that only a court can verify who may instruct for a company. On these facts, the controlling point is: A solicitor should know the identity of the client and have a proper basis for accepting instructions through a representative.","D":"Correct. Client onboarding requires clarity about who the client is and, where instructions come through another person, whether that person is authorised. AML/customer-due-diligence obligations may impose additional requirements depending on the matter.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a solicitor should take instructions from any employee without considering authority. On these facts, the controlling point is: A solicitor should know the identity of the client and have a proper basis for accepting instructions through a representative."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors; Money Laundering Regulations 2017 where applicable.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A solicitor should know the identity of the client and have a proper basis for accepting instructions through a representative.","common_trap":"Confusing access to the solicitor with legal authority to bind or instruct an organisational client.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4ee196206c7df762"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0710","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Consideration — part payment of debt","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A debtor owes an undisputed £10,000 immediately. The creditor agrees, without any additional consideration or deed, to accept £7,000 paid on the same date in full satisfaction. The debtor pays £7,000.\n\nWhich common-law issue is most directly raised?","options":{"A":"Any part payment automatically discharges the whole debt.","B":"Payment of a smaller sum is always fresh consideration because money has changed hands.","C":"The creditor's promise is enforceable only because every debt compromise is a deed.","D":"The rule applies only to tort damages.","E":"Part payment of an existing debt is generally not good consideration for a promise to forgo the balance, subject to recognised exceptions and equitable doctrines."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"The traditional rule in Pinnel's Case and Foakes v Beer is that payment of a lesser sum on the due date is not, without more, consideration for a promise to discharge the whole liquidated debt. Exceptions and promissory estoppel may require separate analysis.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that any part payment automatically discharges the whole debt. On these facts, the controlling point is: A debtor ordinarily needs some legally sufficient additional basis if a creditor's bare promise to accept less is to be binding at common law.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that payment of a smaller sum is always fresh consideration because money has changed hands. On these facts, the controlling point is: A debtor ordinarily needs some legally sufficient additional basis if a creditor's bare promise to accept less is to be binding at common law.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the creditor's promise is enforceable only because every debt compromise is a deed. On these facts, the controlling point is: A debtor ordinarily needs some legally sufficient additional basis if a creditor's bare promise to accept less is to be binding at common law.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the rule applies only to tort damages. On these facts, the controlling point is: A debtor ordinarily needs some legally sufficient additional basis if a creditor's bare promise to accept less is to be binding at common law.","E":"Correct. The traditional rule in Pinnel's Case and Foakes v Beer is that payment of a lesser sum on the due date is not, without more, consideration for a promise to discharge the whole liquidated debt. Exceptions and promissory estoppel may require separate analysis."},"primary_legal_authority":"Foakes v Beer (1884) 9 App Cas 605; Re Selectmove Ltd [1995] 1 WLR 474.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A debtor ordinarily needs some legally sufficient additional basis if a creditor's bare promise to accept less is to be binding at common law.","common_trap":"Applying the practical-benefit reasoning for contractual performance as though it had abolished the debt part-payment rule.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"4fda569941689c71"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0711","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Privity — Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A contract between A and B expressly states that C may enforce a specified payment obligation. C is identified by name but provides no consideration.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"C may be able to enforce the term under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999 despite not providing consideration, subject to the Act and the contract.","B":"C can never enforce because only a person who provided consideration can have statutory third-party rights.","C":"The Act applies only if C signs the original contract.","D":"C automatically becomes a contracting party for all purposes.","E":"Third-party enforcement is possible only after assignment by B."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Section 1 permits an identified third party to enforce a contractual term where the contract expressly provides for enforcement or the term purports to confer a benefit, subject to the statutory conditions and contrary intention.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Section 1 permits an identified third party to enforce a contractual term where the contract expressly provides for enforcement or the term purports to confer a benefit, subject to the statutory conditions and contrary intention.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that c can never enforce because only a person who provided consideration can have statutory third-party rights. On these facts, the controlling point is: The 1999 Act creates a statutory exception to common-law privity without requiring the third party to furnish consideration.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the Act applies only if C signs the original contract. On these facts, the controlling point is: The 1999 Act creates a statutory exception to common-law privity without requiring the third party to furnish consideration.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that c automatically becomes a contracting party for all purposes. On these facts, the controlling point is: The 1999 Act creates a statutory exception to common-law privity without requiring the third party to furnish consideration.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that third-party enforcement is possible only after assignment by B. On these facts, the controlling point is: The 1999 Act creates a statutory exception to common-law privity without requiring the third party to furnish consideration."},"primary_legal_authority":"Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999, s 1.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The 1999 Act creates a statutory exception to common-law privity without requiring the third party to furnish consideration.","common_trap":"Importing the common-law consideration requirement into the statutory third-party enforcement mechanism.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"fbd16846dbf561bd"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0712","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Contract Law","subtopic":"Remedies — expectation damages","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A supplier breaches a profitable contract. The customer seeks damages designed, so far as money can do it and subject to ordinary limits, to place the customer in the position it would have occupied had the contract been performed.\n\nWhich measure is being described?","options":{"A":"Restitution for every benefit ever conferred regardless of breach.","B":"The expectation measure of contractual damages.","C":"Punitive damages as the ordinary contract measure.","D":"Nominal damages only.","E":"Contributory negligence damages."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The orthodox contractual damages objective protects the expectation interest by placing the claimant, so far as money can, in the position performance would have produced, subject to causation, remoteness, mitigation and proof.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that restitution for every benefit ever conferred regardless of breach. On these facts, the controlling point is: Expectation damages protect the value of the promised contractual performance.","B":"Correct. The orthodox contractual damages objective protects the expectation interest by placing the claimant, so far as money can, in the position performance would have produced, subject to causation, remoteness, mitigation and proof.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that punitive damages as the ordinary contract measure. On these facts, the controlling point is: Expectation damages protect the value of the promised contractual performance.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that nominal damages only. On these facts, the controlling point is: Expectation damages protect the value of the promised contractual performance.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that contributory negligence damages. On these facts, the controlling point is: Expectation damages protect the value of the promised contractual performance."},"primary_legal_authority":"Robinson v Harman (1848) 1 Ex 850; contractual damages principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Expectation damages protect the value of the promised contractual performance.","common_trap":"Confusing compensation for the performance interest with punishment or an automatic restitutionary award.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9c0cb912c0506379"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0713","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Negligence — psychiatric harm","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A claimant directly involved in a serious accident caused by the defendant's negligence was within the range of foreseeable physical injury but escaped physical injury and instead suffered a medically recognised psychiatric illness.\n\nWhich category is most relevant to the duty analysis?","options":{"A":"The claimant must always satisfy every Alcock secondary-victim control mechanism.","B":"Psychiatric injury is never recoverable without physical injury.","C":"The claimant may be treated as a primary victim, for whom the control mechanisms applicable to secondary victims do not operate in the same way.","D":"Only rescuers can be primary victims.","E":"A primary victim must prove a close tie of love and affection with another victim."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A person directly involved in an accident and objectively exposed to foreseeable physical injury may qualify as a primary victim. If personal injury was foreseeable, recovery for recognised psychiatric injury is not governed by the secondary-victim controls in the same manner.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the claimant must always satisfy every Alcock secondary-victim control mechanism. On these facts, the controlling point is: Primary-victim analysis turns importantly on direct involvement and exposure to foreseeable physical danger.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that psychiatric injury is never recoverable without physical injury. On these facts, the controlling point is: Primary-victim analysis turns importantly on direct involvement and exposure to foreseeable physical danger.","C":"Correct. A person directly involved in an accident and objectively exposed to foreseeable physical injury may qualify as a primary victim. If personal injury was foreseeable, recovery for recognised psychiatric injury is not governed by the secondary-victim controls in the same manner.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that only rescuers can be primary victims. On these facts, the controlling point is: Primary-victim analysis turns importantly on direct involvement and exposure to foreseeable physical danger.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a primary victim must prove a close tie of love and affection with another victim. On these facts, the controlling point is: Primary-victim analysis turns importantly on direct involvement and exposure to foreseeable physical danger."},"primary_legal_authority":"Page v Smith [1996] AC 155; psychiatric-injury principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Primary-victim analysis turns importantly on direct involvement and exposure to foreseeable physical danger.","common_trap":"Automatically applying the secondary-victim relationship and proximity controls to a claimant personally endangered by the accident.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"5c5989214c59cf07"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0714","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Companies — written resolutions: ineligible resolutions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Members of a private company want to use the written-resolution procedure to remove a director before expiry of office.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"The issue is resolved solely by the parties' subjective preference, without applying the relevant statutory or common-law test.","B":"The opposite rule applies automatically in every case, regardless of the facts or any statutory exception.","C":"The matter is outside the court's or regulator's legal framework and therefore has no legal consequence.","D":"The relevant rule applies only after final judgment and can never affect the underlying transaction or proceeding.","E":"The statutory written-resolution procedure cannot be used for a resolution to remove a director under s 168; the matter requires the meeting procedure with the applicable special notice rules."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Certain resolutions are expressly excluded from the private-company written-resolution procedure.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. The rule is capable of affecting the present transaction or proceeding and is not confined to the post-judgment stage.","B":"Incorrect. Legal consequences are determined by the governing rule, not merely by the parties' subjective preference.","C":"Incorrect. This is deliberately overbroad: the question tests a qualification, exception or procedural condition to the headline rule.","D":"Incorrect. The facts fall within an established legal framework identified in the authority.","E":"Correct. Certain resolutions are expressly excluded from the private-company written-resolution procedure."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 168, 288(2)","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Certain resolutions are expressly excluded from the private-company written-resolution procedure.","common_trap":"Applying the broad headline rule without identifying the narrower statutory or doctrinal qualification tested by these facts.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"588d8a1a33af5730"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0715","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Tort","subtopic":"Occupiers' liability — warnings","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An occupier knows of a concealed danger and places a warning sign nearby. The sign is vague and does not enable a reasonable visitor to understand or avoid the risk.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"Any sign, however unclear, eliminates occupier liability.","B":"Warnings are legally irrelevant under the 1957 Act.","C":"An occupier must eliminate every danger physically and can never rely on warnings.","D":"Only written contractual exclusions can affect occupiers' liability.","E":"A warning does not automatically discharge the occupier's duty; it must in all the circumstances be enough to enable the visitor to be reasonably safe."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Section 2(4)(a) provides that a warning does not absolve the occupier unless, in all the circumstances, it was enough to enable the visitor to be reasonably safe.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that any sign, however unclear, eliminates occupier liability. On these facts, the controlling point is: The effectiveness of a warning is practical and contextual, not satisfied merely by displaying words near a hazard.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that warnings are legally irrelevant under the 1957 Act. On these facts, the controlling point is: The effectiveness of a warning is practical and contextual, not satisfied merely by displaying words near a hazard.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that an occupier must eliminate every danger physically and can never rely on warnings. On these facts, the controlling point is: The effectiveness of a warning is practical and contextual, not satisfied merely by displaying words near a hazard.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that only written contractual exclusions can affect occupiers' liability. On these facts, the controlling point is: The effectiveness of a warning is practical and contextual, not satisfied merely by displaying words near a hazard.","E":"Correct. Section 2(4)(a) provides that a warning does not absolve the occupier unless, in all the circumstances, it was enough to enable the visitor to be reasonably safe."},"primary_legal_authority":"Occupiers' Liability Act 1957, s 2(4)(a).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The effectiveness of a warning is practical and contextual, not satisfied merely by displaying words near a hazard.","common_trap":"Assuming the existence of a sign is itself conclusive discharge of the common duty of care.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"0b3393e26326a127"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0716","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Judicial review — mandatory relevant considerations","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A statute requires a minister to consider specified environmental factors before making a licensing decision. The minister deliberately ignores those factors and decides on an unrelated political convenience.\n\nWhich ground of judicial review is most directly engaged?","options":{"A":"Illegality through failure to take mandatory relevant considerations into account and reliance on irrelevant considerations.","B":"Procedural legitimate expectation only.","C":"Parliamentary privilege.","D":"The doctrine of precedent.","E":"Private nuisance."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A public decision-maker must direct itself properly in law, consider matters the statute requires it to consider and avoid reliance on legally irrelevant matters where these materially affect the decision.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A public decision-maker must direct itself properly in law, consider matters the statute requires it to consider and avoid reliance on legally irrelevant matters where these materially affect the decision.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that procedural legitimate expectation only. On these facts, the controlling point is: Ignoring mandatory statutory considerations is a classic form of public-law illegality.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that parliamentary privilege. On these facts, the controlling point is: Ignoring mandatory statutory considerations is a classic form of public-law illegality.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the doctrine of precedent. On these facts, the controlling point is: Ignoring mandatory statutory considerations is a classic form of public-law illegality.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that private nuisance. On these facts, the controlling point is: Ignoring mandatory statutory considerations is a classic form of public-law illegality."},"primary_legal_authority":"Associated Provincial Picture Houses Ltd v Wednesbury Corporation [1948] 1 KB 223; public-law relevant-consideration principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Ignoring mandatory statutory considerations is a classic form of public-law illegality.","common_trap":"Treating a statutory decision-maker as free to substitute unrelated considerations for those Parliament required.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"c4570df2342eb799"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0717","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Human Rights Act — remedies against public authorities","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A court finds that a public authority acted unlawfully under section 6 of the Human Rights Act by violating a claimant's Convention right.\n\nWhich is the best general statement about relief?","options":{"A":"A Convention violation automatically entitles the claimant to punitive damages.","B":"A court may grant such relief or remedy, or make such order, within its powers as it considers just and appropriate; damages are available only under the statutory conditions.","C":"The court has no remedial power once a section 6 violation is found.","D":"The only possible remedy is repeal of the underlying Act of Parliament.","E":"Damages must always equal those awarded in an ordinary tort action."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Section 8 empowers courts that find an unlawful act by a public authority to grant relief within their powers that is just and appropriate. Damages are discretionary and subject to statutory criteria, including Convention principles.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a Convention violation automatically entitles the claimant to punitive damages. On these facts, the controlling point is: HRA relief is flexible; damages are neither automatic nor the sole remedy.","B":"Correct. Section 8 empowers courts that find an unlawful act by a public authority to grant relief within their powers that is just and appropriate. Damages are discretionary and subject to statutory criteria, including Convention principles.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the court has no remedial power once a section 6 violation is found. On these facts, the controlling point is: HRA relief is flexible; damages are neither automatic nor the sole remedy.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the only possible remedy is repeal of the underlying Act of Parliament. On these facts, the controlling point is: HRA relief is flexible; damages are neither automatic nor the sole remedy.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that damages must always equal those awarded in an ordinary tort action. On these facts, the controlling point is: HRA relief is flexible; damages are neither automatic nor the sole remedy."},"primary_legal_authority":"Human Rights Act 1998, ss 6 and 8.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"HRA relief is flexible; damages are neither automatic nor the sole remedy.","common_trap":"Assuming every rights violation mechanically produces a damages award.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"54d1cd9ab8b2f817"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0718","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":2,"subject":"Legal System","subtopic":"Delegated legislation — parliamentary control","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An enabling Act provides that a statutory instrument becomes law only if both Houses expressly approve a draft before it is made.\n\nWhich form of parliamentary control is being described?","options":{"A":"The negative resolution procedure.","B":"Judicial review.","C":"The affirmative resolution procedure.","D":"Royal prerogative.","E":"A private member's bill."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Under an affirmative procedure, delegated legislation requires positive parliamentary approval in the manner prescribed by the parent Act. Negative procedure instead ordinarily allows an instrument to operate unless annulled within the relevant period.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the negative resolution procedure. On these facts, the controlling point is: Affirmative instruments depend on express parliamentary approval rather than merely surviving a period in which annulment could be sought.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that judicial review. On these facts, the controlling point is: Affirmative instruments depend on express parliamentary approval rather than merely surviving a period in which annulment could be sought.","C":"Correct. Under an affirmative procedure, delegated legislation requires positive parliamentary approval in the manner prescribed by the parent Act. Negative procedure instead ordinarily allows an instrument to operate unless annulled within the relevant period.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that royal prerogative. On these facts, the controlling point is: Affirmative instruments depend on express parliamentary approval rather than merely surviving a period in which annulment could be sought.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a private member's bill. On these facts, the controlling point is: Affirmative instruments depend on express parliamentary approval rather than merely surviving a period in which annulment could be sought."},"primary_legal_authority":"Statutory Instruments Act 1946; applicable enabling Act.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Affirmative instruments depend on express parliamentary approval rather than merely surviving a period in which annulment could be sought.","common_trap":"Confusing the two principal forms of parliamentary scrutiny of statutory instruments.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"9b5d53d34b509eba"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0719","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Wills — lapse","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A testator leaves a pecuniary legacy to a close friend. The friend dies before the testator. No substitution clause applies and the statutory anti-lapse rule for issue of the testator is irrelevant.\n\nWhich is the ordinary result?","options":{"A":"The friend's personal representatives automatically take every lapsed legacy.","B":"The gift is automatically divided among the testator's children.","C":"The will is revoked in full.","D":"The gift lapses and falls into residue, or into intestacy if there is no effective residuary disposition.","E":"The executor personally receives the gift."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Subject to exceptions such as section 33 for qualifying gifts to descendants, a testamentary gift ordinarily lapses if the beneficiary predeceases the testator. A lapsed specific or pecuniary gift normally falls into residue if effectively disposed of.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the friend's personal representatives automatically take every lapsed legacy. On these facts, the controlling point is: A predeceasing beneficiary ordinarily cannot take, so the destination of the failed gift depends on the will and applicable anti-lapse rules.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the gift is automatically divided among the testator's children. On these facts, the controlling point is: A predeceasing beneficiary ordinarily cannot take, so the destination of the failed gift depends on the will and applicable anti-lapse rules.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the will is revoked in full. On these facts, the controlling point is: A predeceasing beneficiary ordinarily cannot take, so the destination of the failed gift depends on the will and applicable anti-lapse rules.","D":"Correct. Subject to exceptions such as section 33 for qualifying gifts to descendants, a testamentary gift ordinarily lapses if the beneficiary predeceases the testator. A lapsed specific or pecuniary gift normally falls into residue if effectively disposed of.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the executor personally receives the gift. On these facts, the controlling point is: A predeceasing beneficiary ordinarily cannot take, so the destination of the failed gift depends on the will and applicable anti-lapse rules."},"primary_legal_authority":"Wills Act 1837, s 33; lapse principles.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A predeceasing beneficiary ordinarily cannot take, so the destination of the failed gift depends on the will and applicable anti-lapse rules.","common_trap":"Applying the statutory descendant anti-lapse rule to an unrelated friend.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2a27fbfaf13e2058"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0720","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Administration — disclaimer by beneficiary","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"An adult beneficiary, before accepting any benefit, does not want a testamentary gift and wishes to reject it.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"A beneficiary is legally compelled to accept every testamentary gift.","B":"Disclaimer transfers the gift automatically to the executor beneficially.","C":"A disclaimer rewrites the entire will.","D":"The beneficiary can disclaim only after spending part of the gift.","E":"A beneficiary can generally disclaim the gift, in which case the property devolves according to the will and succession rules as though the beneficiary had not taken it, subject to the nature and terms of the disposition."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"A beneficiary is not generally compelled to accept a gift and may disclaim before acceptance. The consequences depend on the terms of the will and the type of gift; the beneficiary cannot ordinarily redirect it by a mere disclaimer.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a beneficiary is legally compelled to accept every testamentary gift. On these facts, the controlling point is: Disclaimer rejects rather than assigns the beneficiary's entitlement.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that disclaimer transfers the gift automatically to the executor beneficially. On these facts, the controlling point is: Disclaimer rejects rather than assigns the beneficiary's entitlement.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a disclaimer rewrites the entire will. On these facts, the controlling point is: Disclaimer rejects rather than assigns the beneficiary's entitlement.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the beneficiary can disclaim only after spending part of the gift. On these facts, the controlling point is: Disclaimer rejects rather than assigns the beneficiary's entitlement.","E":"Correct. A beneficiary is not generally compelled to accept a gift and may disclaim before acceptance. The consequences depend on the terms of the will and the type of gift; the beneficiary cannot ordinarily redirect it by a mere disclaimer."},"primary_legal_authority":"Succession and disclaimer principles; Administration of Estates practice.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Disclaimer rejects rather than assigns the beneficiary's entitlement.","common_trap":"Confusing disclaimer with an assignment or variation directing the property to a chosen recipient.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"29ba117bbe7491a8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0721","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Wills and Administration of Estates","subtopic":"Inheritance tax — charitable exemption","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A will leaves £100,000 outright to a UK charity within the statutory charitable exemption.\n\nWhat is the ordinary IHT treatment of that transfer?","options":{"A":"The qualifying charitable transfer is exempt from inheritance tax.","B":"The charity must pay inheritance tax at the highest personal income-tax rate.","C":"Charitable gifts are treated as chargeable lifetime transfers by the deceased.","D":"The exemption applies only if the charity is also an executor.","E":"No testamentary charitable gift can qualify for IHT relief."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Transfers of value to qualifying charities are exempt from inheritance tax under the statutory charitable exemption, subject to the legislation's requirements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. Transfers of value to qualifying charities are exempt from inheritance tax under the statutory charitable exemption, subject to the legislation's requirements.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the charity must pay inheritance tax at the highest personal income-tax rate. On these facts, the controlling point is: A qualifying testamentary gift to charity is excluded from the charge by the charitable exemption.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that charitable gifts are treated as chargeable lifetime transfers by the deceased. On these facts, the controlling point is: A qualifying testamentary gift to charity is excluded from the charge by the charitable exemption.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the exemption applies only if the charity is also an executor. On these facts, the controlling point is: A qualifying testamentary gift to charity is excluded from the charge by the charitable exemption.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that no testamentary charitable gift can qualify for IHT relief. On these facts, the controlling point is: A qualifying testamentary gift to charity is excluded from the charge by the charitable exemption."},"primary_legal_authority":"Inheritance Tax Act 1984, s 23.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A qualifying testamentary gift to charity is excluded from the charge by the charitable exemption.","common_trap":"Confusing the charity's exempt receipt with taxable transfers to non-exempt beneficiaries.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"8a038c5523bc9f75"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0722","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Solicitors Accounts","subtopic":"Client money — residual balances","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A matter has ended and a small amount of client money remains. The client cannot immediately be located.\n\nWhich is the best general regulatory approach?","options":{"A":"The firm may automatically treat every residual balance as profit.","B":"The firm should take appropriate steps to return client money promptly and deal with any residual balance only in accordance with the Accounts Rules and SRA requirements, documenting the steps taken.","C":"Residual client money can be divided among partners after the file closes.","D":"The firm should transfer the balance to another client's ledger.","E":"No record is needed for small residual balances."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Client money must be returned promptly as soon as there is no proper reason to retain it. Residual balances require proportionate efforts to locate the owner and compliance with the SRA framework before any alternative disposal.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the firm may automatically treat every residual balance as profit. On these facts, the controlling point is: Closure of a matter does not convert unclaimed client money into the firm's money.","B":"Correct. Client money must be returned promptly as soon as there is no proper reason to retain it. Residual balances require proportionate efforts to locate the owner and compliance with the SRA framework before any alternative disposal.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that residual client money can be divided among partners after the file closes. On these facts, the controlling point is: Closure of a matter does not convert unclaimed client money into the firm's money.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the firm should transfer the balance to another client's ledger. On these facts, the controlling point is: Closure of a matter does not convert unclaimed client money into the firm's money.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that no record is needed for small residual balances. On these facts, the controlling point is: Closure of a matter does not convert unclaimed client money into the firm's money."},"primary_legal_authority":"SRA Accounts Rules, r 2.5; SRA guidance on residual client balances.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Closure of a matter does not convert unclaimed client money into the firm's money.","common_trap":"Assuming administrative inconvenience changes beneficial ownership of client funds.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a9bc4275145a5f23"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0723","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Constitution — transfer of shares","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A settlor intends to make an outright gift of registered shares. The settlor executes the proper stock transfer form and does everything within the settlor's power to effect the transfer, delivering the documents required for registration, but registration is completed only later.\n\nWhich equitable principle may be relevant to whether the gift is treated as complete before registration?","options":{"A":"Milroy v Lord means every unregistered share gift necessarily fails even after the donor has done everything required personally.","B":"Registration is irrelevant even if the donor has done nothing to transfer the shares.","C":"The Re Rose principle, under which equity may treat a transfer as effective once the donor has done everything necessary within the donor's power to transfer the property.","D":"A gift of shares can be completed only by will.","E":"Equity automatically perfects every imperfect gift whenever intention is clear."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Although equity will not ordinarily perfect an imperfect gift, Re Rose recognises that where the donor has done everything required of the donor to transfer the property, equity may regard the beneficial interest as having passed pending registration.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that milroy v Lord means every unregistered share gift necessarily fails even after the donor has done everything required personally. On these facts, the controlling point is: The exception depends on completion of all steps within the donor's own power, not intention alone.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that registration is irrelevant even if the donor has done nothing to transfer the shares. On these facts, the controlling point is: The exception depends on completion of all steps within the donor's own power, not intention alone.","C":"Correct. Although equity will not ordinarily perfect an imperfect gift, Re Rose recognises that where the donor has done everything required of the donor to transfer the property, equity may regard the beneficial interest as having passed pending registration.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a gift of shares can be completed only by will. On these facts, the controlling point is: The exception depends on completion of all steps within the donor's own power, not intention alone.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that equity automatically perfects every imperfect gift whenever intention is clear. On these facts, the controlling point is: The exception depends on completion of all steps within the donor's own power, not intention alone."},"primary_legal_authority":"Re Rose [1952] Ch 499; Milroy v Lord (1862) 4 De GF & J 264.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The exception depends on completion of all steps within the donor's own power, not intention alone.","common_trap":"Treating Re Rose as a general power to cure any incomplete gift.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"37ad17048ed30a42"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0724","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":1,"subject":"Trusts Law","subtopic":"Trustee remedies — equitable compensation","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A trustee commits a breach of trust that causes a quantifiable loss to the trust fund. The beneficiaries seek restoration of the trust estate.\n\nWhich remedy is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Punitive damages are the automatic remedy for every breach of trust.","B":"The beneficiaries can recover only nominal damages.","C":"A trustee can never be personally liable for loss to the fund.","D":"Equitable compensation requiring the trustee, subject to equitable principles of causation and loss, to restore the trust fund for loss caused by the breach.","E":"Rescission of the beneficiaries' wills."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"A trustee who causes loss through breach of trust may be required to restore the trust fund by equitable compensation. Modern authority distinguishes compensatory restoration from punishment and applies equitable causation principles.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that punitive damages are the automatic remedy for every breach of trust. On these facts, the controlling point is: Equitable compensation addresses loss to the trust estate attributable to the trustee's breach.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the beneficiaries can recover only nominal damages. On these facts, the controlling point is: Equitable compensation addresses loss to the trust estate attributable to the trustee's breach.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a trustee can never be personally liable for loss to the fund. On these facts, the controlling point is: Equitable compensation addresses loss to the trust estate attributable to the trustee's breach.","D":"Correct. A trustee who causes loss through breach of trust may be required to restore the trust fund by equitable compensation. Modern authority distinguishes compensatory restoration from punishment and applies equitable causation principles.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that rescission of the beneficiaries' wills. On these facts, the controlling point is: Equitable compensation addresses loss to the trust estate attributable to the trustee's breach."},"primary_legal_authority":"Target Holdings Ltd v Redferns [1996] AC 421; AIB Group (UK) plc v Mark Redler & Co Solicitors [2014] UKSC 58.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Equitable compensation addresses loss to the trust estate attributable to the trustee's breach.","common_trap":"Confusing restorative equitable relief with automatic punitive damages.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"b3ecf39df38d4708"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0725","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Co-ownership — overreaching","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Two trustees of land sell registered property to a purchaser for capital money. A beneficiary has an equitable interest under the trust and is in actual occupation. The purchase money is properly paid to both trustees.\n\nWhich is the best general priority analysis of the beneficiary's equitable interest?","options":{"A":"Actual occupation always prevents overreaching.","B":"Overreaching can occur only if the beneficiary signs the transfer.","C":"A purchaser can never take free of a beneficial interest under a trust.","D":"Payment to two trustees extinguishes the beneficiary's interest entirely rather than transferring it to the proceeds.","E":"Payment of capital money to at least two trustees can overreach the beneficial interest, transferring it from the land to the proceeds so that actual occupation does not preserve that overreached interest against the purchaser."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"Where the statutory requirements are satisfied, overreaching detaches beneficial interests under a trust from the land and transfers them to the capital money. The House of Lords confirmed that actual occupation does not defeat a properly overreached interest.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that actual occupation always prevents overreaching. On these facts, the controlling point is: Proper overreaching changes the asset to which the beneficial interest attaches and therefore precedes the overriding-interest analysis.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that overreaching can occur only if the beneficiary signs the transfer. On these facts, the controlling point is: Proper overreaching changes the asset to which the beneficial interest attaches and therefore precedes the overriding-interest analysis.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a purchaser can never take free of a beneficial interest under a trust. On these facts, the controlling point is: Proper overreaching changes the asset to which the beneficial interest attaches and therefore precedes the overriding-interest analysis.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that payment to two trustees extinguishes the beneficiary's interest entirely rather than transferring it to the proceeds. On these facts, the controlling point is: Proper overreaching changes the asset to which the beneficial interest attaches and therefore precedes the overriding-interest analysis.","E":"Correct. Where the statutory requirements are satisfied, overreaching detaches beneficial interests under a trust from the land and transfers them to the capital money. The House of Lords confirmed that actual occupation does not defeat a properly overreached interest."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, ss 2 and 27; City of London Building Society v Flegg [1988] AC 54.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Proper overreaching changes the asset to which the beneficial interest attaches and therefore precedes the overriding-interest analysis.","common_trap":"Starting with actual occupation without first asking whether the equitable interest was overreached.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"2b604d19f8ceffcf"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0726","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Land Law","subtopic":"Mortgages — power of sale","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A legal mortgagee's statutory power of sale has arisen. The mortgagee now considers exercising it after the statutory conditions governing exercisability are satisfied.\n\nWhich is the best general statement about the mortgagee's conduct of the sale?","options":{"A":"The mortgagee must act in good faith and take reasonable care to obtain the true market value or proper price at the time of sale, though it need not postpone indefinitely in hope of a better market.","B":"The mortgagee owes a duty to obtain the highest price imaginable at any future date.","C":"Once the power arises, the mortgagee may deliberately sell at an undervalue to an associate.","D":"The mortgagee acts as the mortgagor's fiduciary in every respect.","E":"A mortgagee can exercise a power of sale only with the mortgagor's consent."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A mortgagee exercising a power of sale may choose the timing for its own legitimate purposes but must act in good faith and take reasonable precautions to obtain the proper market value at the chosen time.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A mortgagee exercising a power of sale may choose the timing for its own legitimate purposes but must act in good faith and take reasonable precautions to obtain the proper market value at the chosen time.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the mortgagee owes a duty to obtain the highest price imaginable at any future date. On these facts, the controlling point is: The mortgagee has latitude over timing but not over careless or bad-faith disposal at an undervalue.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that once the power arises, the mortgagee may deliberately sell at an undervalue to an associate. On these facts, the controlling point is: The mortgagee has latitude over timing but not over careless or bad-faith disposal at an undervalue.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the mortgagee acts as the mortgagor's fiduciary in every respect. On these facts, the controlling point is: The mortgagee has latitude over timing but not over careless or bad-faith disposal at an undervalue.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that a mortgagee can exercise a power of sale only with the mortgagor's consent. On these facts, the controlling point is: The mortgagee has latitude over timing but not over careless or bad-faith disposal at an undervalue."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property Act 1925, ss 101 and 103; Cuckmere Brick Co Ltd v Mutual Finance Ltd [1971] Ch 949.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The mortgagee has latitude over timing but not over careless or bad-faith disposal at an undervalue.","common_trap":"Confusing freedom to choose when to sell with freedom to disregard the property's proper market value.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a880573a11a563c1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0727","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"Conveyancing — title guarantee","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A seller transfers a registered freehold with full title guarantee.\n\nWhich is the best general description of the significance of the title guarantee?","options":{"A":"It guarantees that the property can never have any physical defect.","B":"It implies statutory covenants for title of the scope prescribed by the Law of Property (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1994.","C":"It replaces investigation of registered title entirely.","D":"It is a warranty that planning permission exists for every use.","E":"It means the seller will remain legal owner after completion."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Full title guarantee implies statutory covenants concerning the transferor's right to dispose and freedom from specified encumbrances, subject to the 1994 Act. It is not a general guarantee of physical condition or planning status.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that it guarantees that the property can never have any physical defect. On these facts, the controlling point is: Title guarantee concerns statutory covenants about title, not every characteristic of the property.","B":"Correct. Full title guarantee implies statutory covenants concerning the transferor's right to dispose and freedom from specified encumbrances, subject to the 1994 Act. It is not a general guarantee of physical condition or planning status.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that it replaces investigation of registered title entirely. On these facts, the controlling point is: Title guarantee concerns statutory covenants about title, not every characteristic of the property.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that it is a warranty that planning permission exists for every use. On these facts, the controlling point is: Title guarantee concerns statutory covenants about title, not every characteristic of the property.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that it means the seller will remain legal owner after completion. On these facts, the controlling point is: Title guarantee concerns statutory covenants about title, not every characteristic of the property."},"primary_legal_authority":"Law of Property (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1994, ss 1–3.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Title guarantee concerns statutory covenants about title, not every characteristic of the property.","common_trap":"Treating a conveyancing title covenant as a comprehensive warranty about condition, use and value.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"f0f1747d7f805fc8"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0728","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Property Law and Practice","subtopic":"New leases — registration","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A landlord grants a registrable legal lease out of a registered title for a term of ten years. Completion has occurred.\n\nWhich post-completion step is central to perfecting the tenant's registered legal title?","options":{"A":"No registration is relevant to a ten-year lease.","B":"Register the lease at Companies House instead of HM Land Registry regardless of the tenant's identity.","C":"Apply to HM Land Registry to register the registrable disposition within the applicable priority and registration framework.","D":"Only the landlord can ever apply for registration.","E":"The lease automatically becomes a registered freehold on completion."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A grant of a lease for a term exceeding seven years from the date of grant is a registrable disposition under the Land Registration Act. Registration is required to complete the legal title to the registrable disposition.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that no registration is relevant to a ten-year lease. On these facts, the controlling point is: A registrable lease grant requires post-completion Land Registry registration; execution alone does not complete the registered legal estate.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that register the lease at Companies House instead of HM Land Registry regardless of the tenant's identity. On these facts, the controlling point is: A registrable lease grant requires post-completion Land Registry registration; execution alone does not complete the registered legal estate.","C":"Correct. A grant of a lease for a term exceeding seven years from the date of grant is a registrable disposition under the Land Registration Act. Registration is required to complete the legal title to the registrable disposition.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that only the landlord can ever apply for registration. On these facts, the controlling point is: A registrable lease grant requires post-completion Land Registry registration; execution alone does not complete the registered legal estate.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the lease automatically becomes a registered freehold on completion. On these facts, the controlling point is: A registrable lease grant requires post-completion Land Registry registration; execution alone does not complete the registered legal estate."},"primary_legal_authority":"Land Registration Act 2002, ss 27(1) and 27(2)(b).","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"A registrable lease grant requires post-completion Land Registry registration; execution alone does not complete the registered legal estate.","common_trap":"Confusing contractual completion of the lease with completion of the registrable legal title by registration.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"efd10535579103bf"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0729","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Liability","subtopic":"Homicide — murder mens rea","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"D intentionally inflicts serious bodily harm on V without lawful justification. V dies from the injury. D did not specifically desire V's death.\n\nWhich is the best general statement about the mens rea for murder?","options":{"A":"Murder always requires proof that death was D's sole purpose.","B":"Intent to cause serious injury can never support murder.","C":"Recklessness as to any minor injury automatically constitutes murder.","D":"An intention to cause grievous bodily harm can satisfy the mens rea for murder; an intention to kill is not the only sufficient intent.","E":"Murder is an offence of strict liability."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Under established English law, the mens rea for murder is an intention to kill or to cause grievous bodily harm. The prosecution need not prove that death itself was the defendant's purpose if the requisite intent to cause really serious injury is established.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that murder always requires proof that death was D's sole purpose. On these facts, the controlling point is: Intent to cause GBH is sufficient mens rea for murder where the remaining elements, including causation and absence of a defence, are proved.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that intent to cause serious injury can never support murder. On these facts, the controlling point is: Intent to cause GBH is sufficient mens rea for murder where the remaining elements, including causation and absence of a defence, are proved.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that recklessness as to any minor injury automatically constitutes murder. On these facts, the controlling point is: Intent to cause GBH is sufficient mens rea for murder where the remaining elements, including causation and absence of a defence, are proved.","D":"Correct. Under established English law, the mens rea for murder is an intention to kill or to cause grievous bodily harm. The prosecution need not prove that death itself was the defendant's purpose if the requisite intent to cause really serious injury is established.","E":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that murder is an offence of strict liability. On these facts, the controlling point is: Intent to cause GBH is sufficient mens rea for murder where the remaining elements, including causation and absence of a defence, are proved."},"primary_legal_authority":"R v Vickers [1957] 2 QB 664; R v Cunningham [1982] AC 566.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Intent to cause GBH is sufficient mens rea for murder where the remaining elements, including causation and absence of a defence, are proved.","common_trap":"Assuming murder always requires a specific intention to cause death.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"73b6c6cb376c5d8f"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0730","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK2","session_2027":2,"subject":"Criminal Law and Practice","subtopic":"Appeals — Magistrates' Court to Crown Court","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A defendant convicted in the Magistrates' Court after a not-guilty plea wants to challenge both conviction and sentence using the ordinary appeal route to the Crown Court.\n\nWhich is the best general position?","options":{"A":"There is never an appeal from the Magistrates' Court.","B":"The only route is directly to the Supreme Court.","C":"An appeal to the Crown Court is confined to judicial review of procedural illegality.","D":"Only the prosecution can appeal a Magistrates' Court conviction.","E":"A defendant may ordinarily appeal conviction and/or sentence from the Magistrates' Court to the Crown Court, where the appeal is generally by way of rehearing."},"correct_answer":"E","explanation":"A person convicted by magistrates may ordinarily appeal to the Crown Court against conviction if pleading not guilty and against sentence. The Crown Court appeal is generally a rehearing before the appropriate bench.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that there is never an appeal from the Magistrates' Court. On these facts, the controlling point is: The ordinary Crown Court appeal from magistrates provides a rehearing rather than merely supervisory judicial review.","B":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that the only route is directly to the Supreme Court. On these facts, the controlling point is: The ordinary Crown Court appeal from magistrates provides a rehearing rather than merely supervisory judicial review.","C":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that an appeal to the Crown Court is confined to judicial review of procedural illegality. On these facts, the controlling point is: The ordinary Crown Court appeal from magistrates provides a rehearing rather than merely supervisory judicial review.","D":"Incorrect. This answer would apply the proposition that only the prosecution can appeal a Magistrates' Court conviction. On these facts, the controlling point is: The ordinary Crown Court appeal from magistrates provides a rehearing rather than merely supervisory judicial review.","E":"Correct. A person convicted by magistrates may ordinarily appeal to the Crown Court against conviction if pleading not guilty and against sentence. The Crown Court appeal is generally a rehearing before the appropriate bench."},"primary_legal_authority":"Magistrates' Courts Act 1980, s 108; criminal appeal procedure.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The ordinary Crown Court appeal from magistrates provides a rehearing rather than merely supervisory judicial review.","common_trap":"Confusing the statutory appeal route with judicial review or appeal by case stated.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"3f4a6bed07f5f717"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0731","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — reduction of capital","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company limited by shares wants to reduce its share capital without applying to court. Its articles do not prohibit the reduction and the directors are prepared to make the required statutory solvency statement.\n\nWhich route may be available?","options":{"A":"The private-company solvency-statement procedure, combined with the required special resolution and statutory filings.","B":"A board resolution alone can reduce share capital without further formalities.","C":"Only a court-confirmed reduction is ever possible.","D":"An ordinary resolution alone is sufficient whenever all shares are fully paid.","E":"Capital can be reduced merely by paying money to shareholders and recording it later."},"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A private company limited by shares may use the statutory solvency-statement route for a reduction of capital, requiring compliance with the Companies Act including directors' solvency statement, special resolution and registration requirements.","why_each_option":{"A":"Correct. A private company limited by shares may use the statutory solvency-statement route for a reduction of capital, requiring compliance with the Companies Act including directors' solvency statement, special resolution and registration requirements.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that a board resolution alone can reduce share capital without further formalities. The controlling rule here is: The Companies Act provides a non-court capital-reduction route for qualifying private companies, but it is formal and condition-dependent.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that only a court-confirmed reduction is ever possible. The controlling rule here is: The Companies Act provides a non-court capital-reduction route for qualifying private companies, but it is formal and condition-dependent.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that an ordinary resolution alone is sufficient whenever all shares are fully paid. The controlling rule here is: The Companies Act provides a non-court capital-reduction route for qualifying private companies, but it is formal and condition-dependent.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that capital can be reduced merely by paying money to shareholders and recording it later. The controlling rule here is: The Companies Act provides a non-court capital-reduction route for qualifying private companies, but it is formal and condition-dependent."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 641–644.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The Companies Act provides a non-court capital-reduction route for qualifying private companies, but it is formal and condition-dependent.","common_trap":"Assuming the existence of a non-court route means directors can alter capital informally.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"194e2d9b569d2492"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0732","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Companies — directors' loans","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"A private company proposes to make a substantial loan directly to one of its directors. No statutory exception applies.\n\nWhich corporate approval issue is most directly relevant?","options":{"A":"Director loans are absolutely prohibited in every company.","B":"Member approval is generally required before the company enters into the loan transaction with its director.","C":"Board approval alone necessarily satisfies the statutory regime.","D":"Member approval is relevant only after repayment defaults.","E":"The Companies Act regulates loans only to shareholders who are not directors."},"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Companies Act generally requires member approval for loans, quasi-loans and certain credit transactions involving directors, subject to detailed provisions and exceptions.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that director loans are absolutely prohibited in every company. The controlling rule here is: Transactions providing credit to directors can require prior shareholder approval even if the board supports them.","B":"Correct. The Companies Act generally requires member approval for loans, quasi-loans and certain credit transactions involving directors, subject to detailed provisions and exceptions.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that board approval alone necessarily satisfies the statutory regime. The controlling rule here is: Transactions providing credit to directors can require prior shareholder approval even if the board supports them.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that member approval is relevant only after repayment defaults. The controlling rule here is: Transactions providing credit to directors can require prior shareholder approval even if the board supports them.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the Companies Act regulates loans only to shareholders who are not directors. The controlling rule here is: Transactions providing credit to directors can require prior shareholder approval even if the board supports them."},"primary_legal_authority":"Companies Act 2006, ss 197–214.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Transactions providing credit to directors can require prior shareholder approval even if the board supports them.","common_trap":"Treating board authority as sufficient where Parliament has imposed a separate member-approval safeguard.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"ab99c7b77d900f6a"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0733","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Partnership — sharing losses","difficulty":"Medium","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"Three partners have no agreement dealing with the division of profits or losses. One contributed more capital than the others and argues that this alone entitles that partner to a larger share of profits and a smaller share of losses.\n\nWhich default rule ordinarily applies?","options":{"A":"Profit and loss shares always follow capital contributions automatically.","B":"The oldest partner takes half the profits.","C":"Partners are entitled to share equally in capital and profits and must contribute equally towards losses, subject to contrary agreement.","D":"Losses are borne solely by the partner who contributed least capital.","E":"A partnership cannot exist without an express profit-sharing clause."},"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Section 24 supplies default terms where partners have not agreed otherwise, including equal sharing of capital and profits and equal contribution towards losses.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that profit and loss shares always follow capital contributions automatically. The controlling rule here is: The statutory defaults govern internal financial sharing unless the partners have agreed different terms.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the oldest partner takes half the profits. The controlling rule here is: The statutory defaults govern internal financial sharing unless the partners have agreed different terms.","C":"Correct. Section 24 supplies default terms where partners have not agreed otherwise, including equal sharing of capital and profits and equal contribution towards losses.","D":"Incorrect. This option proposes that losses are borne solely by the partner who contributed least capital. The controlling rule here is: The statutory defaults govern internal financial sharing unless the partners have agreed different terms.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that a partnership cannot exist without an express profit-sharing clause. The controlling rule here is: The statutory defaults govern internal financial sharing unless the partners have agreed different terms."},"primary_legal_authority":"Partnership Act 1890, s 24.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"The statutory defaults govern internal financial sharing unless the partners have agreed different terms.","common_trap":"Assuming unequal capital contributions automatically displace the Partnership Act's default allocation.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"a1a6ce377e538bb1"},{"question_id":"SQE1-2027-0734","bank_version":"2026-09-20.1","target_sitting":"January 2027","law_cutoff":"2026-09-11","flk":"FLK1","session_2027":1,"subject":"Business Law and Practice","subtopic":"Insolvency — liquidation and dispositions","difficulty":"Hard","source_type":"ORIGINAL_SQE_STYLE","source_name":"Consolidated audited SQE-style bank","source_url":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","law_verified_date":"2026-09-20","question":"After a winding-up petition has been presented against a company, but before a winding-up order is made, the company disposes of an asset in the ordinary course. A winding-up order is subsequently made.\n\nWhich statutory issue should the recipient consider?","options":{"A":"Every disposition before the winding-up order is automatically immune from challenge.","B":"Presentation of a petition immediately transfers all company property to the petitioner.","C":"Only transactions made after appointment of a liquidator can ever be affected.","D":"A disposition of company property made after commencement of the winding up may be void unless the court validates it, with commencement generally relating back to presentation of the petition in a compulsory winding up.","E":"The rule applies only to distributions to shareholders."},"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Section 127 generally renders dispositions of company property made after commencement of a winding up void unless the court otherwise orders. In compulsory winding up, statutory commencement rules can relate commencement back to presentation of the petition.","why_each_option":{"A":"Incorrect. This option proposes that every disposition before the winding-up order is automatically immune from challenge. The controlling rule here is: Transactions in the petition-to-order period can be vulnerable and may require a validation order.","B":"Incorrect. This option proposes that presentation of a petition immediately transfers all company property to the petitioner. The controlling rule here is: Transactions in the petition-to-order period can be vulnerable and may require a validation order.","C":"Incorrect. This option proposes that only transactions made after appointment of a liquidator can ever be affected. The controlling rule here is: Transactions in the petition-to-order period can be vulnerable and may require a validation order.","D":"Correct. Section 127 generally renders dispositions of company property made after commencement of a winding up void unless the court otherwise orders. In compulsory winding up, statutory commencement rules can relate commencement back to presentation of the petition.","E":"Incorrect. This option proposes that the rule applies only to distributions to shareholders. The controlling rule here is: Transactions in the petition-to-order period can be vulnerable and may require a validation order."},"primary_legal_authority":"Insolvency Act 1986, ss 127 and 129.","secondary_verification":"https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification","third_verification_if_required":null,"exam_skill":"Apply functioning legal knowledge to a realistic client-based scenario.","key_rule":"Transactions in the petition-to-order period can be vulnerable and may require a validation order.","common_trap":"Assuming the winding-up order date is always the first legally relevant date for dispositions.","notes":"Original training item; not represented as a past live SQE question.","content_hash":"e364e858432ae39d"}]);
